[
    {
        "review": "Great value and good sound it's not a high end pair of headphones but definitely a superior value for the money. They look good and the case is small for being in your pocket. They are the most comfortable headphones I have used. I got this pair for my wife but I've been using a pair for over a year and they work great still great battery life and they are good on the bike for noise cancelling"
    },
    {
        "review": "1) The sound of the WF-C510 is really good right out of the box without EQ. I listen to a lot of music, various genres, and everything is balanced and in control. Punchy bass, treble that's crisp and doesn't make my ears bleed, and decent mids. I definitely trust Sony when it comes to audio products.2) Fit and comfort are amazing! I can leave these in all day. No joke, after 5+ hours of use, I feel zero discomfort. Not only are these earbuds the most comfortable earbuds of all time, but getting a good in-ear seal is effortless. Just set them and forget them, zero hassle. Just to note, there's no ANC but I don't mind; I use these at home where it's already quiet. Further, there's a feature called Ambient Sound Control, kind of the opposite of ANC, that lets outside noises in (press button on left earbud). Obviously, the volume has to be pretty low for this feature to be useful.3) I rarely ever use playback controls on a pair of earbuds, as I just use my phone for that stuff. However, the physical buttons on the WF-C510 work like they should and it's always nice to have actual buttons over touch controls, mainly because there's less chance for accidental inputs, a common occurrence on my other earbuds.4) The battery life seems to jive with Sony's claims of 11 hours in the buds and another 11 in the case. I'm getting a 50% battery level after 5+ hours at a volume level above half with the \"Sony Sound Connect\" app installed on my phone. I'll admit, the 11 hour battery claim was the #1 factor for my purchase.5) The app gives you a ton of stuff to play around with. There's a bunch of EQs, 360 audio (more app downloads and payment required, I think), information regarding battery levels, and more.6) A couple of oddities: The charging case confuses me. Does the orange light turn off when charging is complete or does it stay lit? I've experienced both scenarios and both times the app said the earbuds and case were at 100%. The instruction manual was no help. Also, for some reason I had to re-pair the earbuds to my phone's Bluetooth after shutting it off, which was super annoying. I was testing to see if an older device (without the app) would pair, so to avoid interference I turned off my phone's Bluetooth. The older device paired with the earbuds just fine without the app, so it isn't required. I plan on deleting it; it's probably a major battery hog anyway.7) Minor issues aside, the Sony WF-C510 delivers premium comfort and pleasing sound. and they're super affordable. Get yours today! lolUpdate 3/25 -- Checking in to mention that the orange charging light on the case does in fact shut off to indicate that charging is complete and I'm still very happy with my purchase."
    },
    {
        "review": "I don't understand the logic behind other earbuds that claim water resistance with capacitive controls. I have tried almost a dozen across various brands and NONE of them work properly when wet. It's not that the water damages them (it doesn't), it's that the capacitive buttons can't tell a water drop from a finger, so they're constantly registering false touches, jumping tracks, changing volume. CAPACITIVE EARBUDS ARE USELESS WHEN WET. (Fine once they dry off.)This WF-C510 pair from Sony, with physical buttons instead of capacitive touch, is the FIRST SET EARBUDS I'VE FOUND THAT ACTULLY WORK IN THE SHOWER or heavy rain.If you're just going for a jog in light mist or some drops of sweat run down your ears, those others do OK, but in a heavy rain or if you want to listen in the shower, go for these. I use earbuds in the shower so I don't wake up my spouse or kids if I get up early.  I used to use a suction cup Bluetooth speaker, but that's loud and disturbs the family.Another problem with many capacitive earbuds: if you don't have any audio playing for a few minutes, they'll turn off to save battery... but because the capacitive touch requires power to work, there's often no way to turn them back on without putting them in the case and then removing them. This means if you don't have the case with you, you can't use them at all. I have actually bought extra cases just to leave in the places I often get stuck with powered-off earbuds, but what a waste of money that is!But note that where JBL, for example, sells just extra cases for their capacitive buds, I don't see that Sony does that. While you don't really need an extra case for these because you can turn them on and off via buttons, in the event you do want an extra case, be aware that your only option might be used on eBay.Also, these Sony WFD-C510 earbuds are small (compact, in the best possible way). These are some of the few earbuds I've found that I can wear under ear protection when mowing or doing other loud equipment work. Most other earbuds are bulky in comparison and ear protection pushes against them directly, really digging into the ear. These are so slim, you barely feel them even while wearing ear protection over them.On sound quality, these are great. They're not the best overall, but I think they are the best per dollar (and no earbuds are as good as real headphones if sound quality is the primary goal). They sound amazing for $50 buds.So, yes, overall, I would highly recommend them to anyone, but especially if you care about using them when very wet or under ear protection. Best I've found for those scenarios by a wide margin. To address both issues with a single pair of earbuds is incredible. One of my favorite bits of tech ever."
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased the Sony WF-C510 for around $50 or less, and considering that price point, I am generally satisfied with the product. Below is my experience after a few weeks of use.Sound:For the price, the sound profile is good and well balanced. The bass is present, though not very powerful compared to more expensive models. As a user who has owned higher-end earbuds, I do notice a lack of depth in the low frequencies, but at this price range, that is acceptable. You cannot expect premium bass performance from a budget model, and within those limits, the sound is decent.Comfort:The earbuds are small and lightweight, and after some time you barely notice you are wearing them. They are comfortable enough to use while lying in bed, and if they had touch controls, you could even listen while resting on one side without discomfort. That said, the fit is not perfect for me. They do not feel 100% secure at all times, and I occasionally need to readjust them manually. It is not frequent, but it is an area that could be improved.Buttons:For the price, I did not expect touch controls, but the physical buttons can be inconvenient at times. Pressing them often causes the earbud to move out of position, which then requires readjustment.Microphone:Based on feedback from my wife during phone calls, there was noticeable background noise on my end. I cannot provide a very detailed review of the microphone, but this was the main comment I received during the three weeks of use.Would I recommend them?It depends on the use case. I would not recommend them for intense workouts. If you are very demanding about sound quality or expect a high-end audio profile, these will likely disappoint you. However, for walking, office use, or casual listening—and if you are not very demanding about sound quality—they are a good option for the price.Note:I have used the Jabra Elite 75t for about two years (originally $199) and have been using the Sony WH-1000XM3 for four years. Obviously, this is not a fair comparison, as the difference in sound quality is significant. Still, considering the price of the WF-C510, they are worth it if you are not overly demanding about audio performance.============================Update after a few months of use:============================After a couple of months with the Sony WF-C510, my overall opinion remains mostly the same, but I can now add a few clearer conclusions.The biggest downside is definitely the microphone. Call quality has consistently been poor. On several occasions during meetings, I had to disconnect them and switch to other headphones to continue the call. Background noise isolation for the mic is weak, and for professional use, they are simply not reliable.The sound profile is still good for the price. It remains balanced and acceptable within its budget category. However, if you are used to earbuds in the $150+ range, these will likely feel underpowered and lacking depth. The difference in presence, clarity, and overall audio richness becomes more noticeable over time.Would I buy them again? Probably not. While they are not bad for the price, I personally prefer stronger audio performance and more depth, even if that means paying more.Overall, my experience is neutral — not bad, not exceptional. They deliver what you would expect from a $48 pair of earbuds, but they do not meet my personal expectations, which are influenced by years of using higher-end models."
    },
    {
        "review": "The box was ripped open right where the earbuds were placed in the package. I don’t know if it was someone being curious, or just a shipping machine rip. Either way, I took the earbuds out of package, charged them fully and put them on for a trail.I would put a “mind blown” emoji here. The sound right out of the box was good. Then I downloaded the “Sound Connect” app and I was lost for a good hour and a half, just having fun and listening to music.I was able to quickly use “short cut” way to find what kind of sound I like, as the app just walked me through. I now know, ( by this apps definition) that I like clear bass. There is an equalizer where anyone whom has the knowledge and the skill to set their music to sound just like they want to hear. I have an eclectic range of music. Billy Joel ( older version cd) that didn’t give me the strong clear bass I like, yet it was quite balanced. I listened to Tchaikovsky‘s Swan Lake and that just made me smile. Enter P!nk with “Truth About Love” cd and I could hear parts of the music that I have not heard before. Amazing!! And to end the night with a bang  I switched to “Disturbed” Down with the Sickness and I was banging. The sound never got distorted. Everything was clear and I actually had to turn the sound down, as I like it loud!! Mind you, I am not a audiophile, but I do love music, so take my opinion as you choose.The buttons on the buds are easy to use. I used with Alexa, phone call, next song, previous song, and it was seamlessly easy. Functionality is A+.Instructions have a QR Code to take you to a users manual. The buds come with a one year warranty. Just a quick scan tells me I need to keep the box it came in and I am sure there is more that is needed for the warranty. Just read to be safe. The package comes with different size buds inside the package.Now, I can not say enough about how comfortable these buds feel. I have purchased sooooooo many types of earbuds and have NEVER felt buds this comfortable. Every pair of buds, I will not name them, but seriously a LOT, has been uncomfortable. These are just so comfortable and even though I have no noise cancellation, they are sealed so well in my ear that it doesn’t feel like I am missing anything.Long story short, get these!!!!! Sony has always come through and these are no different. Be well."
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased these a little under a year ago, and up until about a month ago, I absolutely loved them. Honestly, I would have easily given these 4-5 stars if it weren’t for the charging case issue.Unfortunately, the case has taken a pretty sharp nosedive lately. I now have to charge it almost daily just to use one earbud at a time... which kind of defeats the whole “wireless earbuds” concept.For the price, I expected a bit more longevity. I usually buy cheaper earbuds because I have a talent for leaving them in pockets or sacrificing a single earbud to the outdoors forever, but I decided to splurge for better sound and call quality - and to be fair, these definitely delivered on that front.I also have smaller ears, and these have been some of the most comfortable earbuds I’ve used. They’re lightweight, not made of super hard plastic, and don’t feel enormous or awkward after wearing them for a while.So overall: excellent sound, calls, and comfort... but the charging case aging this badly in under a year is really disappointing."
    },
    {
        "review": "Bought these to replace my favorite earbuds of the past few years, the KZ SKS and was a little disappointed at first. That was until I tweaked the EQ in the SONY app, which these responded wonderfully to. The bass is very lacking out of the box, but it makes for balanced tone. I like a lot of bass and bright highs, so I moved the slider to 7 on bass and adjusted the others accordingly, with no loss in sound quality. The biggest change though, was the exceptional battery life and amazing passthrough mode that I did not expect to like as much as I do. It allows conversations to be carried out while on the phone or listening to music. Even though these are roughly double the price of my $30 KZs, I would actually say they're an even better value with the features and battery life. They're also super light and relatively small, so they won't fall out of your ears. For a 6mm driver (which is supposedly shared with some of their more expensive models), these have an excellent soundstage and really can thump. The ONLY drawback (if you want to call it that), is the buttons are not capacitive, and must be physically clicked. This can be helpful in preventing accidental presses though, which is a plus. Build quality is what you'd expect from Sony. Very good. Will update if these have any drawbacks in the future."
    },
    {
        "review": "Perfect earbuds for just a normal clear sound. Nothing special like noise cancelation but the do have pass thru that work kimda well. The sound of the music and phone calls are great. All in all a great product for the price. Fits really well in ear."
    },
    {
        "review": "⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Excellent Earbuds!Really happy with these earbuds! The sound quality is clear, the bass is good, and they are comfortable to wear for a long time. Bluetooth connectivity is quick and stable, and the battery backup is impressive. The microphone quality is also good for calls. Overall, great quality at this price. Definitely worth buying! 👍🔥..install sound connect App & choose correct equalizer.."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have the Sony WH-1000XM4 (with a headband) and the Xiaomi Redmi Buds 3 Lite, so these will be my point of comparison. I have read opinions that these Sonys do not have a very high volume but for me the volume is enjoyable. The bass sounds very good for the price, nothing to ask for from the low frequencies of the 1000XM4 (which to be fair, the bass of these headphones, the 1000XM4, does not stand out much even with the equalizer). The audio in general is cleaner and of better quality compared to the Xiaomi ones. The material does feel cheap, both in the headphones and the case but nothing to worry about, they are cheap but the construction is good and comfortable. They feel very light and do not bother you at all after hours. The battery of the headphones is very good, it takes a long time for it to drop below 100% (unless you activate the ambient mode) and although they do not have noise cancellation, I personally feel that in this type of headphones it is not really necessary because the rubber closes the ear very well. The case on the other hand... doesn't last long. In 2-3 days it had already dropped to 50%, but I will continue checking (I clarify that I use them for a long time a day, when I don't use them much the battery in the case does not drop as quickly). If you see them for $800 MXN or less, they seem like a good option to me, especially because of how light and comfortable they are and the audio quality in general. If you see them as more expensive, I would look for other options.Finally, two pieces of advice:1. Download their app, 'Sound Connect' (from Sony) and use the equalizer. The audio that comes from the factory does feel very flat (maybe that's why they complain about the volume), so using the equalizer substantially improves the sound quality. If you don't want to rack your brains too much, just open the app, register the headphones and in the equalizer section choose the 'Excited' profile (or some other profile that has at least +7 Clear Bass).2. I've read on some forums that some people had a problem with the case: if you let the battery in the case reach 0%, it won't charge again (the headphones do, but the case doesn't). In the same app you can monitor the battery of the headphones and the case. Check it from time to time and try to ensure that the battery in the case does not drop below 20-30%. I haven't had any problems and it doesn't happen to everyone, but it's better to prevent anything. Update: after two months of use, the case has completely discharged more than once and it continues to work, don't be scared about that issue, but yes, its battery lasts three to four days, sometimes a little less."
    },
    {
        "review": "I thought I needed noise-cancelling earbuds for the gym, but these do the trick at a fraction of the price. Since they fit snugly with the silicone tips (and I have small ear canals), they block out a lot of noise passively. Noise-cancelling probably wouldn't do much better, because it's not optimized for voices and other higher frequencies. If you want earbuds and don't want to pay $300, then these are a great value. Note that I bought them on sale for $68; the current price of $100 is pushing it.The sound is clear and balanced, no excessive treble or bass. No connectivity issues using a Samsung phone and MacBook. Battery life shouldn't be an issue for most people, they last forever.If you haven't used this type of earbud before, just note that when you first put them in, they might not seal well, but just give the silicone a minute to warm up and it will sit better (provided you have the correct tip size installed)."
    },
    {
        "review": "Have not been able to use as per instructions, have asked for refund but have heard nothing so far"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I used the Razer Mamba Elite for many years and absolutely loved it, so I was hesitant to switch. This mouse was recommended to me as a replacement, and I have been pleasantly surprised. It feels comfortable in the hand, responds quickly, and the customizable buttons and RGB lighting are excellent. The scroll wheel is especially smooth and versatile. Overall, it has been a great change and I am very happy with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good god, what a mouse. I mean, it's not perfect; nothing is. But it's so good.I came from years of the G502 Hero; the grips on it were wearing out. I first decided to try a G502 X, as it was supposed to be an improved version of the Hero, essentially. For $25 more than the Basilisk, it pretty much sucked in my opinion. The only thing I could imagine picking it over the Basilisk for is weight. But if you care that much about weight, there are much lighter mice on the market.---I'll review it as compared to the Hero (and a bit to the X), as that's what I know. They're pretty common, so maybe this will help people out if they're thinking about converting:Initially I wasn't so sure about the grip and the main mouse buttons. The mouse buttons have a rough texture, and I found with my Hero that when my fingers were slightly tacky/damp, they would stick slightly to the surface of the buttons in the same way a clean basketball shoe does to a court (i.e., not \"sticky\", but grippy). I liked that. But after using it for a while, I think it's totally fine and maybe even better.The feet seem the same to me as the G502 X, which is good. They're white PTFE. Very smooth.The scroll wheel is *fantastic*. Like, it's perfect. It seems to control the mode with an electromagnet, so it makes a cool click sound when the computer is restarted and such. And it even has a setting in the software where you can have it tactile by default, but with a quick flick, it switches to free-spin temporarily. Awesome. And when I move the mouse quickly side to side, there is absolutely no play in the wheel. Nothing. That was one of my annoyances with the Hero. The scroll wheel would wobble audibly and tactilely when in free spin mode with even moderately fast mouse movements.The click noise isn't as nice as I expected, but it's totally fine and better than the X (which was super loud and piercing). I'd put it somewhere between the X and the Hero. It's kind of like if someone built the X and then was like, oh, that sucks, and then made adjustments like a sane human. I believe the Basilisk switches are optical (the Hero is mechanical and the X is hybrid mech/optical), and so maybe that's approximately what happened. In short, I think the Hero is a pretty quiet click, and I think the Basilisk is a very standard click. Sounds like what I'd use as a mouse click if I were a foley artist.The Basilisk's weight is listed at 101g, and it feels quite nice. Not hard to control. I'm sure I'll get even better as we go, though. The palm section is actually quite high! Like, even more than the Hero. It feels pretty good so far.The Basilisk's cable is an absolute work of art. I mean, it's perfect. It's got a soft woven sleeve and is not stiff at all. I had the same kind of reaction to it as I did when I got my current massage shower head and found that no, the tube connecting the head to the spigot doesn't actually have to be stiff at all to do its job.The software is slightly annoying because there's a separate program for the lighting and the mouse (though they're styled identically and could ostensibly be packaged in one app). But its functionality is nice. There are 9 RGB zones around the bottom edge of the mouse, plus one on the scroll wheel and the logo under the palm. They seem rather infinitely adjustable. Neat!---Hope this helps somebody!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve genuinely liked using the Razer Basilisk V3. The first thing I noticed was how comfortable it felt. Some mice are fine for about 20 minutes and then they start feeling like a weirdly shaped brick. This mouse has been much better for longer use, whether I’m gaming or just clicking around online doing whatever nonsense I got distracted by.What I like most is that it feels like someone actually thought about how a hand works. The buttons are responsive, the movement is smooth, and the scroll wheel feels so nice that it almost made reading comment sections worth it. Almost. While this mouse has not been life changing in a dramatic movie soundtrack kind of way, it has changed my expectations enough that going back to a cheaper mouse would feel like using a shopping cart with one bad wheel.Oh, and the customization. I am not out here trying to launch a space mission from my desk, but it is nice being able to set things up in a way that actually fits how I use it. Once everything is dialed in, it just feels easy.Overall, it has been a genuinely solid mouse and one of those upgrades you stop thinking about because it just quietly does its job well. Which, for a mouse, is honestly the dream. It just sits there, minds its business, and makes me slightly more powerful than I probably deserve."
    },
    {
        "review": "i have owned like 8 mouses from $20-$120. i got this for $35 and this is absolutely the best mouse i ever owned. the lighting, feel, shape, customization, etc is perfection for me. this mouse has 26k DPI, the mouse wheel is amazing and smooth to spin and has grip to it and the wheel can click side to side. it has grip on the sides and my hand never slips. to customizable buttons on the side and a big side button to instantly change mouse sensitivity. the app is a breeze to use which you can customize the color of the rgb lighting. no lie playing Apex with this mouse is 10x better than playing with my other mouse. i will will buy this model from now on"
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased the Razer Basilisk V3 for my son after he specifically asked for it, and so far, he’s been pleased with it. As a parent, I was initially a little hesitant due to the price, but after seeing how well it performs, I think it was a solid investment.One of the main features my son really appreciates is the customizable options that the Basilisk V3 offers. From programmable buttons to adjustable sensitivity settings, he can really fine-tune the mouse to fit his personal preferences and gaming style. He uses it for everything from casual gaming sessions to more intense, competitive play, and it handles everything smoothly. The ergonomic design is another major plus. The shape and weight feel great in his hand, and he says it’s comfortable to use for extended periods—important for those marathon gaming sessions.The speed and accuracy of this mouse are really impressive. The optical sensor provides pixel-perfect precision, and the fastest gaming mouse switch delivers great feedback and response time, something that’s crucial for high-speed gaming. While he’s not a professional gamer, my son definitely noticed the difference in performance compared to his previous, more basic mouse.The RGB lighting is a fun touch, too. The Chroma RGB lighting system allows him to personalize the mouse with a variety of colors and lighting effects, which adds a cool flair to his gaming setup. It's also fun to change up the lights depending on his mood or the game he’s playing.The only reason I gave it four stars instead of five is that, while it's an excellent mouse, it might be a little on the larger side for kids with smaller hands. My son has no trouble with it, but younger children or those with smaller hands might find it less comfortable.Overall, if you’re looking for a high-performance gaming mouse with lots of customization options and a comfortable ergonomic design, the Razer Basilisk V3 is a great choice. It offers precision, speed, and the customizability needed for serious gaming, all while looking stylish with its RGB lighting. My son is happy with it, and I can see why—it’s a top-tier mouse at a reasonable price point for what you get!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This mouse is just incredibly smooth. Even with its high weight the skates make it super easy to move around and the weight gives it control, resulting in superb aim. The material can make your hands a bit sweaty when death gripping it, but it’s very solid matte surface. You can grip test and throw this mouse across a room without it breaking. The shape this mouse has just fits right in your hand, and the rubber side grips make it super comfortable and easy to use, it’s basically great with any grip type. The skates are super glidey and quiet even when under the weight of this chunker of a mouse. It’s better for bigger hands of course, it’s harder to use with smaller hands but it’s doable. The scroll wheel is the best of any mouse, even the Logitech g502. It has a setting in the software where it can be a tactile scroll wheel when scrolling slowly, but becomes a freely spinning wheel when flicked fast. You can even switch between spinny wheel mode and tactile mode with the click of a button. I loved this mouse so much I upgraded to the wireless version, and I wasn’t sacrificing much leaving wired because the battery life was great on that one. 10/10 mouse."
    },
    {
        "review": "This mouse is punching way above its weight class. Here’s the breakdown of why I’m not hitting \"Return\" just yet:The SpecsWeight: It’s so light I’m convinced it’s mostly made of hopes and dreams.Speed: Zip-ier than a caffeinated squirrel.Price: My wallet didn’t even feel the impact. It’s basically a steal without the legal fees.The ErgonomicsI have hands that look like they belong to a retired yeti, and I usually prefer a mouse with a bit more \"girth.\" Despite that, this little guy is surprisingly comfortable. It’s like driving a Mini Cooper when you’re used to a Silverado—compact, but surprisingly ergonomic.The Warzone Stress TestI spent several hours in Call of Duty getting absolutely dismantled by \"sweats\" who clearly haven't seen sunlight since 2022. For a solid three hours, I was convinced this mouse was defective because it wasn’t magically granting me 30-k!lls.Pro Tip: Apparently, the mouse doesn't come with an \"Auto-Win\" button. Trust me, I checked the box twice.Final DecisionJust as I was about to pack it back into the box and send it back to the abyss, I finally caught a dub. Was it my skill? Was it the mouse? Or was it the fact that the enemy team finally fell asleep at their desks? Doesn't matter. A win is a win.The mouse stays."
    },
    {
        "review": "Best mouse I've ever used. Every surface is comfortable to touch & adds to its controllability. The RMB & LMB are soft & responsive, and have a quick reset. Easy to hold & thumb rest allows for greater control during long use."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good quality"
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm an avid PC user and rarely replace my accessories. My mouse especially was hard to replace, as every top end mouse seems to get lighter and lighter. However, after using the Basilisk V3, I can safely say this mouse has been a perfect upgrade from my 2013 Deathadder. It weighs roughly the same, being on the heavier side for a mouse, and that's perfect for me. It's built great, has a fantastic braided cable which is smooth to the touch, it doesn't catch on corners like the older Razer cables, and the RGB is stellar. I really like the ring of RGB that goes around the base, and it's incredibly easy to edit in Razer Synapse.The mouse is also extremely comfortable. I didn't expect a thumb rest to do much, but I'll honestly miss it if I ever have to upgrade.For the price, you really can't go wrong. This mouse is incredible for its price point and well worth investing in if you like to 'feel' your mouse movements."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been using this mouse for about a month now.It's my first gaming mouse.It's lighter than I expected but that does not impair the use at all.The greatest strength is it's smoothness of operation. The cursor glides smoothly with small and large gestures."
    },
    {
        "review": "Ergonomic mouse with thumb slot, has texture on the thumb and sides for a better grip, it's large so you can use it if you have a \"claw\" grip or if you hold it with your whole hand, it works with both. It's heavier than other mice as I've read described in website reviews but it's an ideal weight, a light mouse is a bit strange, in my opinion. They come with buttons with various functionalities, such as the 2 buttons above the thumb, as well as the one in front of the thumb that serves to reduce the dpi for games FPS type, for example, use with sniperscope where you need to make smaller movements. It's useless for MOBA, but all the buttons are customizable with the Razer app, so you can configure them to use an item slot, or skill, etc. The app also allows you to make macros and configure the color pattern of the LEDs. The cable is of good quality and long. The scroll is very smooth, and has a button to switch between running mode (scrolls without stopping) and normal scrolling (with jumps/ticks). And the scroll itself also serves as 2 extra buttons when you push it to the sides. Overall, satisfied, especially with the ergonomics. It comes in a box that has a very explanatory multi-language manual, some stickers, etc. And placing the mouse on the PC has already activated Windows to want to install the software/app."
    },
    {
        "review": "My deathadder v2 pro broke down so I bought a new one. I generally don't play games, but I often switch DPI on a 4K monitor, so a gaming mouse would be perfect if I could switch between them at hand and have enough buttons to assign a certain amount of macros. Since it was originally a wireless mouse, I was reluctant to switch to a wired mouse, but the thick-looking cable is lighter and softer than I expected, so I don't feel any discomfort when operating the mouse. It has a moderate weight and is easy to hold, so personally, I made the right decision to buy a new one. Since there is one more button, I assigned Alt+F4 (close window) to the backmost button, which I personally find very convenient and use frequently. One negative thing I noticed right after purchasing it (which has already been resolved) is that the scroll wheel feels too light. Even when I turned the deathadder v2 pro, it felt like it was turning one step at a time, but this product spins so lightly that I thought it was almost free, so I thought it was a little strange, but I was a little impressed when I found out that you can change the feel of the wheel mechanically by setting it on the SYNAPSE side. I felt the evolution of the mouse."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I’ve had this mouse for almost a week now, and I can confidently say it’s hands down the best mouse I’ve ever used. It’s incredibly lightweight, the wireless performance has been flawless, and it just feels high quality overall.I am 35 years old and don't have the best reaction time but this mouse dropped my Human Benchmark reaction time average from 190 ms to around 160 ms. I wasn’t even planning on testing this, but one of my friends told me to try it and see if there was actually a difference. I tested on my old mouse, a Model O, and then back to the Superstrike X2 just to make sure it wasn’t in my head. The difference was still there. Everything just feels quicker and more in control, and the speed and click feel really make a difference.The feeling when you click it is incredibly responsive and smooth. The only thing I can compare it to is clicking an iPhone sleep button.Battery life has been very good also. After about six hours of gameplay, the battery only went from 82% down to 75%, which is impressive for a wireless gaming mouse.Can't recommend this mouse enough to everyone"
    },
    {
        "review": "The Logitech PRO X2 SUPERSTRIKE is easily one of the best gaming mice I’ve used. The first thing you notice is how incredibly lightweight it is—it glides effortlessly, which makes a huge difference during long gaming sessions. The responsiveness is top-tier, and the Lightspeed wireless connection feels just as fast and reliable as a wired mouse.The build quality is excellent, with a clean, minimalist design that looks great on any setup. The clicks feel crisp and precise, and the customizable haptics add a nice touch for those who want to fine-tune their experience. Battery life is also impressive, and the USB-C charging is a welcome upgrade."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been using the Superstrike X2 for gaming and I’m really impressed with it. The mouse is extremely lightweight, feels great in the hand, and the shape works especially well for my claw grip. The clicks feel fast and responsive, and the side buttons are easy to reach without accidentally pressing them.Tracking feels very smooth, and the mouse has been great for competitive games where quick movements and precise aiming matter. I also really like how easy it is to control without feeling like I have to put much effort into moving it around."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve owned and tested many gaming mice over the years, and the Logitech G Superstrike X2 is, without question, the best one I’ve ever used. I don’t play competitively—I mostly enjoy adventure games, action titles, and other immersive experiences—but this mouse has made every gaming session more enjoyable. It combines incredible comfort, premium build quality, and features that genuinely stand out.The biggest highlight for me is the haptic switches. They feel amazing, providing a tactile, satisfying click that makes every input feel deliberate and precise. The feedback is unlike anything I’ve experienced on another mouse, adding a level of immersion that you don’t realize you’re missing until you try it. Combined with the outstanding responsiveness, every click feels fast, consistent, and incredibly satisfying.Another thing I absolutely love is how lightweight it is. It glides effortlessly across the mousepad and never feels fatiguing, even after hours of gaming. The shape is comfortable, the weight is perfectly balanced, and it almost feels like an extension of your hand.The wireless connectivity has also been flawless. It’s fast, stable, and completely reliable, with no noticeable latency or dropouts. It truly delivers the freedom of wireless without any of the compromises that used to come with it.Logitech has once again delivered a premium product. From the build quality to the innovative haptic switches, lightweight design, and rock-solid wireless performance, everything about the Superstrike X2 feels refined and thoughtfully engineered. It’s a mouse that makes you appreciate the little details every time you use it.Overall, this is easily the best mouse I’ve ever owned, and I couldn’t recommend it more. Whether you’re a competitive player or, like me, simply want the best possible experience while enjoying your favorite games, the Logitech Superstrike X2 is absolutely worth it."
    },
    {
        "review": "I gave this mouse 5 stars simply because of the haptic triggers. Ive only ever used the razer viper pro mouse and for my large hands its the only one that fits comfortably. The superstrike is a bit smaller in my hand but nothing that would make me stop using it. Its heavier than the razer and to my surprise I actually like and prefer that. The battery life on the mouse is great. The settings you choose for the haptic triggers does affect battery life but its nothing major. Now the main reason I bought this mouse was for the haptic system it uses and ill be blunt and say its the best thing ever and I hope more companies adapt this. Being able to adjust the actuation points is fantastic. It will take a few days to get used to the sensitivity if you go all the way to one but once youre used to it you'll never want a regular mouse again. Overall I think its a fantastic mouse its a bit spendy but in my opinion the haptic system by itself is worth the price. The only issue i have with this mouse is the side buttons they are extremely squishy they barely click its very unsatisfying I would give the side buttons a half a star."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been using this mouse for a while now and honestly I’m really impressed. It’s super lightweight and feels very comfortable in hand, even during long gaming sessions. The response time is excellent — everything feels smooth and precise, especially in FPS games.Battery life is also really good, I don’t have to charge it often which is a big plus. The wireless connection is stable with no lag at all.The only small thing is that it might feel a bit too light if you prefer heavier mice, but for me it’s perfect.Overall, definitely worth it if you’re looking for a high-performance wireless gaming mouse."
    },
    {
        "review": "I tried this mouse out because I have frequent fingertip sensitivity and pain in my left index finger after long clicking sessions. At its lowest setting, its easier to click than an average mouse, and the haptic feedback feels very nice. When I switch back to a regular mouse such as the G502X Plus, I can notice a difference in force required to click the mouse and weight of the mouse. However, the scroll wheel on this one is not MagSpeed like other Logitech mice such as MX Masters and G502Xs. This would have been a nice addition as that type of scroll wheel also helps with my fingertip sensitivity.I also really like how lightweight this mouse is. It's very easy to move around and pick up.Overall an excellent mouse, but if I could have Logitech design my dream mouse which I'd pay $250+ for, I would just ask for this:- The adjustable actuation point and haptic feel from this mouse- The smooth MagSpeed scroll wheel from the Logitech MX Master 4- The extra programmable buttons from the G502X Plus- And miscellaneous features such as quiet clicks for all buttons, RGB, 60g of weight or less, USB-C dongle as well as bluetooth options for wireless connection, and an extra programmable button on the right side where the pinky goes.These would really help with my fingertip sensitivity and would overall be the perfect mouse for me. I'm disappointed that no Logitech mouse has all these features as they are all amazing additions to a mouse. I hope this message can find someone at Logitech for them to design the perfect mouse. For now, I will be sticking with the G502X Plus as it has the smooth scroll wheel and the multiple programmable buttons."
    },
    {
        "review": "I am 37, my reaction times aren't what they used to be. I have been using the Razer DeathAdder v4 Pro and Razer Viper V4 Pro. This is a noticeable decrease in my reaction time. I saw a lot of videos talking about issues with missclicks from side squeezing....i have had none of that. It is a sturdy mouse, that is barely heavier than my Viper v4 Pro. The \"front heavy\" feel is highly overblown imo. I play R6 Siege and it is incredibly noticable on DMRs in particular. On the lowest sensitivity with Rapid Trigger, I can easily pop off 5 shots in the same time i used to get off 3. My click reaction time went from 205ms to 177ms on the click tester. And I have had no accidental shots, even on lowest sesnsitivity....as for the clicks themselves (the haptic feedback)...it feel soooo clean, crisp and is tunable to be more or less. I have been a Razer guy for over 20 years and the Superstrike finally offered something I couldnt refuse. It def lived up to the hype. Especially for someone who isnt a 19 year old with insane twitch fiber and eyesight. Give it a tryYoull love it."
    },
    {
        "review": "I recently picked up this mouse as my first step into wireless gaming, specifically the Superstrike X2, after using a wired Razer Basilisk V3 for a long time. I’d been thinking about making the switch for a while, and overall I’m glad I did.The main buttons (left and right click) feel excellent — responsive, solid, and satisfying to use. This is clearly where the mouse shines, and it delivers exactly what you’d expect at this price point in terms of core performance.However, the side buttons (the two next to the left click area) are a noticeable weak point. They feel cheap and somewhat out of place, especially when compared to competitors like Razer. Considering the price of this mouse, I expected a higher-quality feel across all buttons, not just the main ones.That said, since the primary clicks and overall performance are what matter most in actual gameplay, I would still recommend this mouse. If you can overlook the side button quality, it’s a strong option for anyone looking to move into wireless gaming.Sorry for the messy mousepad :)"
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought it and the mouse had problems"
    },
    {
        "review": "A good mouse, encanto me"
    },
    {
        "review": "Mostly got for the reason that it’s basically silent in terms of clicking the main left and right mouse buttons. When paired with the mousepad Amazon recommends, this combo is great in terms of feels and responsiveness. Also comes with grip tape which has a nice texture but a really strong smell of fake cheap leather.The software is top tier you can customize a bunch of things, I have the haptics for the mouse (the vibration/feeling of when you press down a button) set to almost the lowest possible and it still feels better than any mouse I have used.I haven’t really messed with changing the speed of how fast the button presses down or whatever, but superb mouse although it is a tad overpriced ( kind of reasonable as this is one of if not the only mouse with this technology )"
    },
    {
        "review": "I spent a long time shopping around for the right mouse, and the description of this one immediately caught my attention. What ultimately convinced me was the adjustable haptic feedback.It feels wonderful in the hand, without being overloaded with unnecessary buttons. The scroll wheel feels excellent and reminds me of my old Microsoft Optical mouse, which I used for 22 years.I’m very happy with my choice. I would never go back—I’m definitely keeping it! JFN Quebec, CANADA"
    }
