[
    {
        "review": "The 2022 Logitech G502 X Plus was a very pleasant surprise.  It is replacing a Logitech G-Pro that is roughly 80 grams weight on a Logitech Powerplay mouse pad (expanded cover for extra space and non-stop charging), primarily used for Overwatch2.  Other gaming mice in my collection include earlier G502's, G903, Razer Death Adder + Copperhead, Steelseries Rival3 and Rival600, etc.  Generally the best performers in FPS games are the lightest units with the best optical sensors, and the G502 X Plus definitely fits that criteria.Pros:-Comfortable ergonomic shape for marathon gaming sessions-PTFE mouse feet for super-smooth glide across mouse pads-The new optical hybrid switches are incredibly fast compared to the G-Pro's older Omron switches, with a nice crisp click and improved actuation.  They will last much longer than conventional mechanical switches and correct double-clicking issues.  My last G-Pro's right rear thumb switch suffered from double-clicking issues since they were the old Omron mechanicals that wore out from constant use.-Nice RGB lighting if you are looking for that kind of bling-This mouse wakes from sleep mode instantly (like the G-Pro series).  The old G502 Hero Wireless required a full second to wake up, making the old sleep mode feature extremely inconvenient and annoying.-At 106 grams list weight, the G502 X Plus is a tad heavier for a gaming mouse. However, coming from an 80 gram G-Pro, I notice better aim stability without sacrificing flick aim consistency.  With the Powerplay charging puck attached and USB receiver stored inside, the G502 X Plus hits 110g on my postal scale.  The G502 Hero wireless is 120g with the same accessories.Cons:-No more adjustable weights like its predecessor G502, but to be fair, the whole e-sport industry has shifted competitive user demand to lighter weight mice for the fastest aim flicks possible.-Battery power drains faster with RGB lighting at full settings.  This is mitigated by reducing RBG brightness to 85% and shortening idle-mode entry from 2 minutes down to 1.  Using a Logitech Powerplay mouse pad, my mouse battery power hovers around 78% to 90% steady with RGB enabled.-The mouse's black finish tends to have shiny oil stains and wear marks over time.  This is why I apply blue painter's tape to the areas most prone to this issue.  EDIT Feb 2024 - I've replaced the blue painter's tape with automotive carbon fiber vinyl wrap for better visual appeal.  Not recommended if you do not like the feel of textured carbon fiber, but it definitely gets rid of those shiny oil stains.  Picture attached.-GHub software will not allow users less than 3 DPI presets, and all of these have to be different values at 50dpi intervals.  I tried to \"lock\" my mouse at 1000dpi to avoid accidental DPI switching, but the software would not allow me to do this, so I ended up moving my DPI cycling button to the default profile button up top and removing the up/down DPI adjustment buttons next to the left click.  This gave me two additional buttons for other programable functions (like Alt and middle-mouse click since pressing the mouse wheel down can feel awkward). EDIT -- GHUB finally fixed this issue.  I can now run with just 1000dpi single setting.-The new hybrid mechanical switches have a different click sound compared to conventional switches -- not really louder, but definitely more metallic.Yes, this mouse is expensive, but the combination of lighter weight + excellent ergonomics + fast/crisp long-wear switches makes this model very hard to beat.  If you are looking for a mouse with more weight and heft, then you may not be happy with the G502 X since the latest model has been redesigned for the e-sports crowd.  The G502 X now combines lighter weight with an ergo shape, something that most ultra-light mouse offerings lack.NOTE:  If you want the best wireless mouse experience, I recommend pairing the G502 X Plus with a Logitech Powerplay for non-stop wireless charging while using the mouse.  For those that want a larger mouse pad than what the default Powerplay size offers, check out \"Logitech powerplay mousepad expansion mod\" on youtube.  I have my set-up displayed in the attached images along with actual weight compared to the old G502 Wireless.  Update 4/26/2023:  The Glorious XL mousepad is the perfect thickness for this Powerplay expansion mod.EDIT:  I just read a few other reviews claiming that the G502 X feels \"cheap\" because it feels too light.  The whole point of lowering mouse weight is to minimize mass, thereby requiring less physical energy / effort to move the mouse quickly and accurately.  This is how modern e-sports mice are designed, because a lighter mouse is a faster mouse, while a heavier one is a slower boat anchor that is usually on the losing end of an FPS fire fight.  The G502 X Plus is only 10 grams lighter than the G502 hero, yet it is much easier to flick-aim than the older G502 Hero Wireless.Also, if you want RGB on the G502 X, you have to get the Plus edition, other wise you get no RGB.  Yet one newer review complained about missing RGB LED's on his G502 X non-Plus edition.  Going no-RGB is still a valid choice since it saves a few grams, extends battery life, and costs less, but just make sure you know what you are buying ahead of time.  ;)"
    },
    {
        "review": "TBH if your looking for a G502/HERO/Lightspeed replacement there is no complaints in my opinion.Its just a hair bit wider then the previous 502s almost unnoticeable but if you hold them together there is a slight difference.The battery life for it so far has been good for me only been  having to charge it like once a week it feels like.It feels like a upgraded 502 lightspeed which is what is.I like that they gave us a option to replace the thumb button with a flat cover that matches the mouse curve so you dont have to have the thumb button for those of us like myself who accidentally press it and never actually use it.Compared to what we paid at launch for the g502 it feels comparable for value and performance.The scroll wheel feels like a nice upgrade from the deep groves to more of a slightly grooved surface thats easy to catch with your finger without issues.The only thing could probably complain about is lack of weights for those who like the weighted options is not super light but its definitely not as heavy as the previous models so people who like the weight of the 502 hero or lightspeed with weights it is gonna be lighter.I like how they changed the charging cable over to the C type charger just like the G733 headphones it still slides it like before but its alot easier to slide in and out and the cable has a good hold when plug in without that feeling like you might rip it if you pull to hard.Other than that software wise minus the RBG change on the body of the mouse it does everything the same as the HERO and Lightspeed models so its pretty much a direct upgrade and replace without having to relearn or figure new stuff out."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been using the Logitech G502 X Plus for about a week now, and it's been excellent so far. I came from an older gaming mouse, and the difference was noticeable right away. Everything feels incredibly smooth and responsive, whether I'm gaming or just using my computer for everyday tasks.Setup couldn't have been easier. I plugged in the wireless receiver, turned the mouse on, and it worked immediately. It was truly plug and play. Installing Logitech G HUB was simple too, and customizing the buttons and RGB lighting only took a few minutes.The mouse feels very well built and comfortable to use, even during longer gaming sessions. The clicks are crisp, the scroll wheel feels solid, and the sensor tracks flawlessly. I also really like having the extra programmable buttons. They've already become useful for both gaming and general productivity.While I can't speak to the long term durability yet, my first impression has been extremely positive. I honestly don't have any complaints so far. If it continues performing this well over time, it'll easily be one of the best gaming mice I've owned. If you're looking for a high quality wireless gaming mouse that's easy to set up and performs exactly as you'd expect, I'd definitely recommend giving this one a look."
    },
    {
        "review": "Ive been with logitech since 5th grade in 2005 when my mom bought me a logitech extreme 3d joystick for microsoft flight simulator on our crappy home computer. Since then I have probably bought a few mice and controllers from logitech. Mainly I enjoy logitech's mice the most, theyre just good, they never break (from QC issues)or have software issues. The only time these mice break are after years of heavy use and it usually results in misclicking or double clicking and that will happen to any brand of mice as nothing mechanical lasts forever under use..At one point I think logitech upgraded me like 2-3 times over the years for free. I bought a G700 in 2012 and got a double click a lot sooner than it should've been. They gave me a G700S (upgrade) then, I think that didn't last more than a year or 2 as well and then they sent me the G502 proteus core (the OG G502) in 2014. That G502 lasted me till 2021!!!! even though the plastic was warped from my sweaty hands and the blue LED lights were no longer all blue but some were kinda greenish blue due to the LED reaching end of life (very very impressive). I actually only threw that mouse away last year and it still worked perfectly, it was just beyond dirty, warped plastic from thousands of hours of use and had a frayed cable (wired).Then I got a G502 lightspeed in 2021 and it started to misclick.. like it started to let go of a click that I was still holding a few months ago. I ended up just buying this one because they have the best mice, the least intrusive software and good customer service.Logitech used to replace a lot of products WELL after warranty. I don't think they are that way anymore which is fine. Logitech deserves the money and I never feel that way about a big company but they really care and you get a quality product that's gonna last you at least 4-5 years nowadays. Personally I have been at 800dpi since 2012 when I got that G700 and haven't gone back. Sometimes I turn it up or down 100dpi but I dont see the need to advertise crazy mouse sensitivity when most just end up turning down the sens in game anyhow.This mouse has a better battery life than the G502 lightspeed. I always keep it on a lower RGB brightness setting 20-35% is enough to look cool but not be distracting and also not drain your battery. I kinda don't care now though because logitechs newer mice take usb-c where my older ones took micro usb which was annoying to find all the time when I needed to charge.This is the best fitting mouse you will probably ever use if you're a palm enjoyer like me. The software and mouse are beyond easy to use. You can adjust the dpi up and down with the buttons and the colors change when you do that so you know which mode you're in. Also it fits in your hand and is very portable if you wanted to use it on the go.Basically if you're a mouse palm user then you will likely be a lifetime logitech customer because they're just the best mouse you will ever use."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good mouse, i like it. the buttons are useful, its ergonomic for long sessions, the lighting is cool (even if you can’t see it while using it) and i put some glass skates on and some grip tape to protect the grey rubber grips it already has. the wireless or wired option is great and the battery genuinely lasts a really long time, i charge it like once a week and it charges quick too. only complaint is the software you need to use to set up your dpi settings and lighting and all that is GARBAGE it’s soo bad! they really need to improve the software so for that reason its 4 stars, its that bad."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this as a gift for a gamer who specifically requested this mouse, so expectations were already pretty high. It ended up being a great gift and they were very happy with it. They especially liked the feel and responsiveness for gaming, along with the wireless design and RGB lighting. When someone who games regularly asks for a specific mouse and loves it once they get it, I’d say that’s a win!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Title says it all. I bought 2 of these, figuring I could sync profiles between my desktop and laptop gaming systems. The mouse is mechanically well built, but there are plenty of mice that are mechanically just as good. If you're buying a gaming mouse with all the extra buttons, then you probably play more than one video game... which requires software to change the \"profile\" or button functions for each different game... and that's where this mouse falls apart. The G-hub software seems designed to do everything Except what you want to do:-You want to change button functions? By Yourself? Gosh, even if you do figure out the interface and psychically intuit that the keys have to be drag/dropped onto the buttons to change them, they still won't change. I mean, why would you need to? We scanned all software on your hard drive and sent the list to our marketing dept, and they auto-set profiles that work great! They promise!-You want to unlock the profile so you can change the button settings? Aw, too bad choom. It's locked because, um, we felt it was necessary to lock the buttons for some reason. Like you might accidentally open this software and accidentally drag/drop functions you didn't really mean to change. Best to leave it locked, for your own good.-You want to change profiles? Oh, sure you can do that. Until you navigate away from G-hub and then it \"helps\" by auto-changing the profile back to Default! No worries, our marketing team has your back. I mean, they are gaming experts! You should SEE their score on Candy Crush!-You still want to change profiles? Why? I mean, we auto-detect the application for you! Spent a lot of money on that, choom. Why you wanna mess that up?-Want to sync your gaming profiles across two computers? Yeah, we know you tied your G-hub software to your logitech account and thanks so much for that data pipe from your computer to the cloud, but really, our marketing department NEEDS that so they can gather data from your system. You know, to uh... track performance and issues and stuff. We're afraid there just isn't the bandwidth to move your settings to the cloud and over to a whole other device. Sorries, but like we said, our software auto-detects and auto-sets the prefect settinz 4 U! We are soo accurat, and xcelnt qualitee! Promize!So the G-hub software is crap designed by idiots, but don't listen to me. I only have a BA in Computer Science and 24 years experience working in IT, and played video games since Dungeons of Daggorath on the TRS-80. But be my guest and google \"Manual for G HUB?\" for yourself.Do yourself a favor - buy 3 mice for this price and throw the two worst ones away. You will STILL be money ahead, and have a way better experience. At least until Logitech hires an actual software interface designer."
    },
    {
        "review": "Alright, let's talk about the new star of my desk setup – the Logitech G502 X Plus Wireless Gaming Mouse. As someone who spends a good chunk of time in front of the PC, this isn't just a peripheral; it's practically an extension of my hand, and honestly, it feels like it was designed specifically for me.First off, the wireless freedom is a game-changer (pun absolutely intended). Ditching the cable is just chef's kiss for decluttering and those intense gaming sessions where every millisecond counts. And the LIGHTSPEED Optical connection? Flawless. There's no perceptible lag, no hiccups, just pure, unadulterated responsiveness. My old M720 Triathlon is still great for everyday use, and it's my go-to when this beast needs a charge, but for serious pixel-peeping and headshot hunting, the G502 X Plus blows it out of the water.The HERO 25K Sensor is no joke. I'm talking pinpoint accuracy that makes navigating my Alienware 38 Curved and 34 QD-OLED monitors feel like butter. Whether I'm trying to land that perfect shot or meticulously editing a document, this mouse tracks like a dream. And the LIGHTFORCE Switches? Every click feels crisp and satisfying, giving you that tactile feedback that just confirms your action. No mushiness here, just pure, responsive clicks.Then there's the LIGHTSYNC RGB. Look, I appreciate a bit of flair, and this mouse delivers without being over the top. It adds that perfect touch of gaming aesthetic, syncing up with the rest of my setup, like my Gimars RGB Gaming Mouse Pad. It just makes the whole experience feel more immersive and, frankly, cooler.This mouse just fits. The ergonomic design feels great in my hand, even during those extended sessions. It's not too light, not too heavy, and the button placement feels intuitive. It's built for comfort and performance, and you can really tell.In short, if you're serious about your PC experience, especially gaming, the Logitech G502 X Plus is an absolute winner. It's fast, accurate, comfortable, and looks damn good doing it. My gaming setup feels complete with this bad boy. Highly recommend it to anyone looking for a top-tier wireless gaming mouse!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I got this when it was on sale and boy oh boy I LOVE using this mouse for playing games!Very accurate, no lags whatsoever, easy to configure using the desktop app of Logitech. The rgb lights are just perfect adding accent to the mouse’s design - I hate too much lights in the mouse as if it becomes like a christmas light. It has on and off switch but it does go auto sleep if you left it on so another plus to that feature!Charging takes a bit but faster than other wireless mouse. Battery life is great (would last for more than a week playing games a couple hours a day)I have big hands and mouse felt nicely (not too small and not big either)Only comment I will add is that I don’t think it was worth the full price but if you see it go on sale for like 25% or more, heck yeah add to cart and checkout right away!!!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Accurate nice size for a palm grip especially for bigger hands, notice the button under the scroll wheel to switch the wheel's rigidity. The infinite scroll thing is a gimmick.Overall great mouse for gaming or productivity, I wish it had a bluetooth + wireless dongle option."
    },
    {
        "review": "One of the best Mouse I have bought, good quality of materials and functionality as promised"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I am coming back to write this review after putting this mouse through its paces for a solid 45 days. After upgrading my desktop with a premium 8K magnetic keyboard, I knew my mouse setup had to match that elite level of performance. That is why I pulled the trigger on the latest Razer Viper V4 Pro, and after a month and a half of intense daily use, it has completely redefined my gaming experience. If you are looking for the absolute pinnacle of symmetrical gaming mice, this is the endgame.First, let us talk about the design because there is a lot of confusion out there. This Viper V4 Pro is a flawless, perfectly symmetrical shape. It is a completely different beast compared to the older, bulky, asymmetrical right-handed design of the DeathAdder V4 Pro. As someone who prefers a claw/fingertip grip for high-precision micro-adjustments, this symmetrical shell melts into my hand perfectly, giving my thumb and fingers ultimate control.At a mind-blowing 49 grams, the weight distribution is pure wizardry. Combined with the native 8000Hz polling rate and the 50K DPI sensor, the tracking feels like an extension of my own nervous system. In high-APM gaming sessions and chaotic shooters like World War Z or CS2, swapping targets is instantaneous with zero initial drag. Furthermore, it is an absolute dream for massive open-world action games like Where Winds Meet. During high-stakes martial arts combat, dodging, executing flawless parries, and rapidly controlling the 3D camera feels incredibly smooth and responsive. There is absolutely no pixel-skipping or camera stutter, giving me perfect situational awareness.Out of the box and after 45 days of absolute abuse, the build quality feels incredibly solid despite being so incredibly light—no creaking, no flexing, and the Gen-4 optical switches offer a crisp, satisfyingly tactical click that hasn't degraded a bit.If I have to nitpick, running this monster at a full, unrestricted native 8K polling rate does drain the battery significantly faster than standard mice. It is a trade-off for infinite performance. However, the real sweet spot here is the 4K polling rate mode. With about 3 to 4 hours of gaming per day on the 4K setting, the mouse can easily coast for 8 to 10 days straight without even completely draining the battery to zero. This exceptional power efficiency completely eliminates any battery anxiety. Also, the premium price tag might make your wallet sweat, but for a top-tier esports weapon, you absolutely get what you pay for.To sum it all up, the Viper V4 Pro is the undisputed king of symmetrical lightweight mice. It delivers unrivaled performance, unparalleled speed, and an incredibly locked-in grip. It has officially earned a permanent spot as the perfect crown jewel for my high-end setup. Highly recommended! Money well spent!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Update: 6 months later and still my main mouse, feeling brand new. Have had zero issues. I've tried a couple of other mice since, and immediately went back to this. It just feels so much better than everything else out there.TLDR: This is the mouse I keep coming back to. Long story short, I want nothing more when using this mouse.I'm one of those that just collects mice. I have my favorites, and I have some that I have just used once and put away. This is one that I keep bringing out. Simply put, the latest Viper and Deathadder are just some of my favorite mice period. They are built very solid, light weight, and flawless sensors. They have an instant wake up, fantastic battery life, satisfying clicks, great scroll wheel, and great side buttons. They are pricey, but quality-wise, it really doesn't get better than Razer now. And I'm comparing to Zowie, Logitech, G-wolves, Finalmouse, Pulsar, etc etc... Every one of those other brands has something I don't like in the quality alone, a creak, spongy click, or something keeps me from wanting to use those brands since I've used the Viper. Obviously, the shape is subjective, so if you've used the Viper V3 and didn't like the shape, you're not gonna like this. But I love the size shape, and weight. I can't wait to see the next Deathadder shed some weight to around 49g, and maybe implement some analog switches like the Logitech Superstrike. If they ever did that, I just hope they do weight balancing better because the superstrike is pretty front heavy, but I'm perfectly happy with Razer's optical switches because they just feel sooooo good. But I think the haptic analog switches would be very interesting in the Deathadder shape. I really don't know how they could improve on this other than finding more ways to lighten the weight even more. Razer might've perfected the Viper with this iteration."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is the best gaming mouse as someone who upgraded from a regular stock mouse. The weight, the functionality, and the design of the mouse is perfect. In fact, this mouse, and it features had further fine-tuned my already decent aim. After using this mouse for a while, I have observed my aim became a natural aim bot, where I was even accused of potentially exploiting in a match of BF1.The mouse itself is light, and as someone who came from a heavier mouse it felt a bit weird at first. However, if you can manage it, then it becomes a benefit. It allows you to flick quickly, and track targets consistently, which is where my claim of \"natural aim bot\" came from. It's not too light where you might find yourself tensing up too much to keep it in place, but if you came from a heavier mouse, then you might overflick your target a few times.The features the mouse offered through Razer's Synapse software such as the DPI and mappings are very useful. You can set different profiles for different games, so there are different DPI's for each game. In addition, you can change the mouse's buttons to be mapped to keys on your keyboard. For example, you can have the side button mapped to 'R', so you can reload through the side button. You can also have the same side buttons run a macro, so if you're playing Helldivers 2, you can instantly input a stratagem through your side button. There are also features like letting the side buttons launch a program.The mouse also features polling rates up to 8k, where higher rates increases the consistency of your cursor's tracking across the screen, and decreases latency. HOWEVER, higher rates can drain the battery life faster, and require specific hardware. Increased polling rates will put higher loads onto your CPU, so ensure you have a strong enough CPU. Otherwise, CPU intensive games will suffer FPS drops, or in general, some games might have issues or do not even support said polling rates. In addition, to notice the different in polling rates, a monitor with a higher refresh rate will be required. Regardless, using 2k and 4k, I haven't really noticed a difference as someone who has a 180hz monitor. It feels the same as 1k polling rate, but if you have the hardware to support it, use it!Regarding the battery life, it's very very good. I have used 2k and 4k polling rates for hours in a day, and the battery only goes down from 100% to like 96%. Whereas if you use 1k polling rate, then the battery doesn't even drain at all till the next day or two maybe (I charge it overnight once the day ends).Upgrading from a wired mouse, and as someone who was a firm believer that wired mice was better, this mouse changed that. I found the wireless mode of this mouse more consistent, than my wired mouse, and offered more freedom in wrist/arm movement. Therefore, I was able to track better.  Also, the latency was small on it, perhaps even better. However, you can use the USB cable and plug it into the mouse if you'd like to use it wired.The design of the mouse is comfortable for those who use a claw grip. I typically have my palm touching the mouse, and my fingers in a claw like stance on it. I found no discomfort, and was able to consistently aim. As for other grips, I do not know.If you're upgrading from a stock mouse, then this is worth the upgrade. However, please read other reviews, if you're upgrading from like Razer's Viper V3 Pro mouse, then it might not be worth the money. Since the V3 shares many similarities to the V4 with a few new features.Overall, this might be one of the best mice on the market right now. This mouse ended up being the final piece in the puzzle for me towards perfect aim. Somehow, my peripherals were holding me back, where it is typically the opposite."
    },
    {
        "review": "I upgraded from Viper V3 Pro - my go-to mice for gaming. I do have other flagship mice but always find myself coming back to the Viper.I think the most noticable changes are the weight and battery.The shape remains the same, but now it only weighs about 48 grams as for the black variation, while the white variant weights 1-2 grams heavier. I love the design on the dome-shaped dongle, with the led indication on battery status.I went for black because it suits my set up better, but the white color will have better grip as from my experience with the previous versions from Viper and other razer mice. And the color black will leave a lot of smudges.Quality-wise, I think it is nice and sturdy, no creaking sounds at all. The stock skates are great, but I will have it replaced when it wears out. The clicks are light, responsive but hollow and loud. The battery time is INSANE, I feel like I barely ever have to charge the mouse, and I don't usually bother turning off the mouse after I'm done using.The other thing that I am super happy about is Razer now offers web-based software to make changes on your mouse, no more installing trashy Razer Synapse, YAY!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I don’t typically write reviews, but this mouse is hands down the best I’ve ever had the pleasure of using, so I feel like I must. The moment my hand gripped the mouse, I knew it was the one. The build quality is fantastic. Everything. From the clicks to the sensor, the scroll wheel and the side buttons. Everything is perfect. The coating on the mouse is high quality and the dongle that comes with it is nicely weighted so it doesn’t slip around on your desk. The stock skates are also wonderful and glide around on my Artisan Zero Soft so smoothly. The size and shape is perfect for my grip style (relaxed claw) so I can handle it comfortably. I recently just bought a GPX Superlight 2 as well as an X2 Crazylight Medium and neither felt quite right. So I decided to try this mouse and it did everything for me the other two couldn’t. Since I bought the Razer Viper V4 Pro, I’ve seen a massive improvement in my gameplay. And for that reason I can confidently justify the price of this product. 5 Stars. Genuinely amazing product across the board. Dare I say perfect. 👍 ⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️⭐️"
    },
    {
        "review": "The Razer Viper V4 Pro is a flagship-level esports mouse that doubles down on performance rather than reinventing the formula. It features an ultra-lightweight ambidextrous design at around 49–50g, paired with Razer’s Focus Pro 50K optical sensor capable of 50,000 DPI, 930 IPS tracking, and 90G acceleration. It also introduces Gen-4 optical switches, improved wireless performance with an upgraded dongle, and up to 180 hours of battery life. On paper, it’s one of the most technically refined mice available fast, consistent, and clearly built for competitive play.That said, if you already own the Razer Viper V3 Pro, there’s honestly no real reason to upgrade. The improvements here are incremental slightly better battery life, marginally upgraded switches, and small refinements that you’re not going to feel in-game. The V3 Pro is already insanely lightweight, responsive, and accurate, so the V4 Pro ends up feeling more like a spec bump than a meaningful evolution. Unless you’re chasing absolute top-end specs for the sake of it, you’re better off sticking with the V3 Pro. As a stand alone mouse, I give it 4 stars. As a \"upgrade\" to the previous generation I'd give it one star."
    },
    {
        "review": "This mouse took me by surprise.Coming from the Basilisk V3 Pro, this one is just so lightweight that I almost mistook it for a toy. I seriously can't believe how light this is.The battery life is phenomenal, and the controls are astoundingly tight. The buttons on my Basilisk always felt cheap and mushy, but every button on this Viper V4 Pro is  snappy and solid.Getting to the part people care about most, this is probably the most fluid mouse I've ever used. The 50K sensor is a marketing ploy, as I don't use anything over 6400DPI, but you can turn on the 8K polling with a high refresh rate monitor and you won't see any dragging of your cursor across your screen as you will with lower refresh rates, and the higher polling rate shows your mouse's location on the screen more times per second than traditional 1K polling rates. My eyes can see the difference, but only if I strain to see it.I run the mouse at 1K polling rate for optimal battery life and because most games are using that rate the most effectively. It's a sweet spot.So the reasons to get the mouse are simple: lightweight, long battery life, no skipping, truly ambidextrous.The cons: no wireless charging, and no RGB. But those are apparent tradeoffs for that impossibly light 49 gram feel."
    },
    {
        "review": "The box didn't have much protection, but it arrived in great condition. As for the mouse itself, it is amazing.I won't talk much about shape because that's highly subjective (I'll just say it fits my hand size and grip style), but everything else is excellent: coating is smooth and grippy, very nice to the touch; main clicks are light and tactile, tho they could have a little less post travel; side buttons are well tensioned and positioned; the wheel might be the weakest aspect, because the scrolling feels a bit off, but it's totally functional; M3 click is very tactile; skates are smooth and fast; build quality is perfect, solid as a rock; performance is top tier, no complaints; and finally, the battery is absolutely insane. I have been using the mouse for 12 days at 1000hz (sometimes 8000, but only for a couple of Apex matches) and it has only gone down to 60%.On top of all of that, Razer has finally made a web software. That means 2 things: 1) you don't have to deal with the bloatware that is Synapse (except for firmware updates); and 2) you can change your settings on Linux, which has been a game changer for me (I dual boot Windows 11 and Bazzite).I think it is worth the price, but only if you don't have a Viper V3 Pro (or even a Viper V3 Pro SE). On paper, the V4 Pro has many improvements, but the only real upgrade that a player will notice is the battery life and the web software. I recommend it to people who have never owned a Viper V3 and want the latest technology and power efficiency. I can recommend it to those who just want to try the shape, but they might also want to take a look at the V3 Pro, since it is cheaper right now."
    },
    {
        "review": "Awesome, but the sound of the buttons is very loud"
    },
    {
        "review": "Best in the market. As always razerr"
    },
    {
        "review": "I would argue this mouse is better than the superstrike 2 in the way a porsche 911 drives, it just does what you want. I’ve had zero issues, just a lightweight, easy to use mouse that works well on both cloth and glass mousepads. 49g seems like the perfect weight before getting too light for me, and it suits all three grip types well. Battery is genuinely insane, I can charge it fully and leave it at 2000hz polling rate for a month and a half of daily use (multiple hours) before I need to charge it again. The web software is reasonably clear and has good features too, letting you change things like liftoff distance, mouse tilt angle and so on."
    },
    {
        "review": "Lightweight, responsive, all you can ask from a gaming mouse. One of the best itw, if not the best."
    },
    {
        "review": "Banger of a mouse especially with the included optional rubber grips. Already loved my Viper V3 but this one is just simply better and not to mention the battery. I can go a whole week without charging the damn thing which is amazing. Only drawback is the clicking noise but honestly, you forget its even a thing."
