[
    {
        "review": "Best Razer mouse in a while.  First, I am a picky buyer, and I have owned many \"MMO\" mice over the years, starting with Razer's very first naga.  The 2014 Naga was the ideal setup in my opinion.  It was just missing the optical switches.  I have owned many competitor's mice, including the Logitech mouse (didn't fit my hand well), and the Corsair Scimitar series.  I want the 12 button side piece, wired connection, ease of setup, and durability.The Pros:* Quality seems fine to me.  I'm not sure why people are complaining about the sound of the click on the optical switches.  My Razer Naga Trinity made the same sounds.  I know the V2 had complaints about the scroll wheel, but this one seems fine so far with the settings that they give you in Synapse.* Optical switches feel great and responsive.* The placement of the keys is the same as previous Naga versions, which is fine with me.* Configurable: You can do everything you want in Synapse.  I am able to rebind any keys that I want.* Options: I like that they still give you a wired option, as opposed to making it wireless only.  I don't like wireless mice.  Also, the hypershift key (ring finger) would never have worked for me as I rest my finger rather heavily on that key, but Razer gives you the option of physically locking that button on the mouse, which was a smart move on their part.  If I ever decide I want to try it, it's waiting there for me with one click of a button underneath the mouse to remove that button lock.Cons:* Price: This mouse is expensive, even for the features they are giving you.  I think it's over-priced and wished there was something more like the Trinity or setup of the 2014 Naga for a cheaper price.* The wired USB-C cable is short.  I need a longer cable to reach my desktop, so I'm using the USB ports on the top of my PC for now until I can test out a longer cable.* The placement of the two buttons on left click are very odd.  The old Naga had these buttons, and I thought they were cumbersome back then.  They are still cumbersome.  I think they are useless and will not use them for anything, but at least they don't get in the way of my left click.* The design of the 2 buttons behind the screen wheel are terrible.  I liked the Naga 2014, Trinity, Chroma, etc. which had cleaner, symmetrical, easier to push buttons behind the scroll wheel.* I'm not a big fan of having tons of wireless signals all over, and it's not clear if the mouse is still trying to send out wireless or not when I'm using the mouse with a wired connection.  At least I'm not picking anything up on my wireless scanner, so that's promising.Overall, if you're looking for a feature rich mouse, this is it.  It has a lot of options and configurability, and the build quality seems good to me.  Time will tell how well the mouse stands up.  It is, however, over-priced, and a few of the button layout/design are a bit odd, though it still fits my needs overall, and sadly, there just aren't many other MMO mice that fit my hand or won't break in a year, so this is it."
    },
    {
        "review": "The mouse works great. 12 buttons for right thumb is helpful for gaming on mmo, and the 12 button plate can be swapped if less buttons are preferred which is nice. Had trouble logging into razer with Google account and discord account, continued as guest and it worked. Anyways, all the buttons working fine from the get go. In the customize tab on the software it allows you to record what you want when you press the number buttons or other buttons that the mouse has. Hypershift allows you to click hold with your ring finger and press the same number for a completely different action! The software also has lighting you can customize effects colors so on and so fourth. I haven't dived too deep into the software but just enough to get the hang of the basics. With my PC windows 11 it's working fine not complicated to set up.. I don't know about mac. The mouse is fairly comfortable as a palm gripper. I think it works for claw grippers as well. I bought the wireless charger for it so that's going to be a cool item to have to charge it. I researched whether blue tooth or 2.4 would be better, and 2.4 is better it's on the bottom of mouse you switch it to 2.4 and plug the USB in the back of your computer. The mouse is expensive, and it's an adjustment (I usually just spammed buttons with my left-hand on keyboard so switching to this getting used to it). Only con I would say is the mouse clicks are a little on the loud side. Nevertheless, razer did an outstanding job. If you play a lot of mmo this is a solid option, if you play other games and want to still use this mouse you can swap the side plates. Oh yes, one last thing battery life.. insane 280 hours for BT, 155 hours (2.4 GHZ) or wired mode which is unlimited play while charging USB."
    },
    {
        "review": "Heavier than the G600 (I removed the weights from the G600) due to the battery. There are no weights inside (two screws under each foot pad) and design seems solid. If you need to take it apart the side opposite of the swappable plate can be hard to wiggle out but there are no screws on that side.The weight most likely won't bother majority of people but I prefer my mouse extremely light so it took awhile to get used to the extra weight.Swapping plates is easy and the side button resistance is less than that of the G600 but more than the hedgehog. Side button tactile feedback is greater than that of either mouse. Click is louder and the feel of the press is similar to that of the G600.Middle Mouse Wheel is bigger than the G600 and has Mouse4/Mouse5 and can actually map both to Mouse4/Mouse5 (the hedgehog only has mouse 5 and cannot map to mouse 4/5 as of last use). Mouse wheel spins more freely in tactile mode than either G600 or Hedgehog which is still easy to control but you can more easily let it free spin. If you prefer no resistance the mouse can physically unlock (you can feel internals shift) the mouse wheel to allow zero tactile spinning.Ring Finger click is slightly farther to the right than the G600 so you might accidentally do right clicks with that for awhile.If you wanted this as a replacement for the G600 then this is the mouse.Spend the extra money to get this over the hedgehog which is not ergonomic and physically hurts my hand (I am disabled) where as this is pretty close to the G600 in terms of feel/size.The extra weight of the mouse vs the G600 is a downside for me but you may be able to take the battery out if you prefer a lighter corded mouse (I did not remove my battery so do this at your own risk!)If they had a wired version that came with removeable weights I would have preferred that over wireless but given that G600 alternatives are unicorns we take what we can get."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was really, really contemplating rating this less because of Razer Synapse 4.  I couldn't get button remapping to work in any game at the start, it would only work after I closed Synapse entirely.  Profile switching also didn't work.  The software took so much finagling and trial and error manually deleting Synapse and re-installing more than once--I even tried deleting Synapse data online from my account via the Privacy Center to no avail.  Midway through, I almost just resorted to keeping things on onboard memory and manually switching between games.  It was brutal.Thankfully profile switching started working and button remaps followed suit.  I don't know how I got it to work, but it works!  It handles so well that the pain setting up the software can be forgiven.  This is everything I wanted out of my old Logitech G600s, of which I've had several and daily drove for the better part of a decade.  I'd always try but ultimately return other MMO mice because I'd always miss ring-finger button.  I know I'm not the only one that felt this!It seems like Razer heard our cries and implemented it on this new Naga V3 Pro, even adding the two additional buttons by the left-click button like on the V2 Hyperspeed.  My only gripes are that the side buttons aren't as pronounced and contoured as the G600, and the left- and right-mouse clicks are just obnoxiously loud.  Travel distance and weight per click as well I'd say aren't great either--maybe better for older MMOs where each click needed to be more deliberate, but a lighter switch would be better for modern MMOs.  I guess I'll get used to them.Despite all of that, the fact that in addition to the extra buttons this is wireless AND I can swap plates for other non-MMO games?  I can learn to live with this.Logitech really missed out."
    },
    {
        "review": "Works great with 2, 6, or 12 side buttons. Razer Synapse customizes it with up to twice as many functions. Button on ring finger rest acts as trigger for second set on all Razer devices. Replaced my V2 with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Synapse 4 currently does not support Razer Naga V3 Pro for MAC. This is ridiculous, and should be specified for buyers."
    },
    {
        "review": "Pros:  Swappable sides.  Ring finger button (that you can disable when not needed!).  Free spin mousewheel.Cons:  Oddly positioned two buttons added to left click side.  Ring finger button/rest is insanely uncomfortable.  Loud, tingy clicks.  Plastic feels loose and cheap on the main click buttons.I used the V2 and G600 for years.  This seemed to be the best of both, but it didnt cut it for me.  The clicks are intolerable.  They have an annoying ting to them.  After using this mouse for a couple of hours, the ring finger rest started to hurt.  I noticed it's a very sharp design, that I just couldn't do.  I still have my G600 and it's so much more comfortable.  I really liked the free spin wheel.  But, the V2 would let you actually adjust the mouse wheel to your liking instead of just 3 presets.  Seems like a downgrade.  Then there's the two new buttons that my fingers cant reach well, so what's the point?  The plastic just also feels cheap and \"creaky\", which I never noticed on the V2.  This ended up going back the next day.  Bummer."
    },
    {
        "review": "Feels pretty much identical to the V2 Pro but it's far and away in my opinion the best MMO mouse available. The switches do feel ever so slightly better and the added buttons will be really nice for some users. Would recommend!"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "The Superlight 2 has easily become one of my favorite gaming mice. The first thing you notice is how light it is. At around 60g, movements feel incredibly quick and effortless, which has been especially noticeable for me in FPS games where fast flicks and precise adjustments matter.Tracking feels extremely accurate and consistent, and wireless performance has been flawless. I haven’t noticed any input delay compared to a wired mouse. Battery life is also excellent—I can use it for long gaming sessions without constantly thinking about charging it.I also really like the shape. It’s simple, comfortable, and doesn’t try to do anything weird ergonomically, so it works well for both gaming and everyday PC use. The white version looks especially clean paired with a white setup.It’s definitely expensive for a mouse, but you’re paying for the extremely low weight, excellent sensor, reliable wireless connection, and overall refinement. If competitive FPS games are a big part of what you play, the Superlight 2 absolutely lives up to its reputation."
    },
    {
        "review": "The G PRO X Superlight 2 is an excellent gaming mouse. It’s incredibly lightweight, tracks flawlessly, and feels very responsive in fast-paced games. The battery life is great, the shape is comfortable for long sessions, and the build quality feels premium. It’s expensive, but if you’re serious about gaming, it’s absolutely worth the investment."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have been using this mouse for a couple weeks now and honestly it has been amazing. The first thing you notice is how light it is. It almost feels like nothing in your hand which made a huge difference for me in FPS games and even just normal computer use. My aim feels smoother and my wrist does not get tired after long sessions. As someone who games in the mornings before studying or rotations, it has been a really nice upgrade.The clicks feel super crisp and responsive and the wireless connection has been flawless so far. No lag, no random disconnects, and the battery life has been better than I expected. The shape is comfortable even after hours of use and it fits well for both claw and palm grip. Setup was simple and the software was easy to use to adjust DPI and buttons.Overall this is easily one of the best mice I have owned. It feels premium, performs great, and actually made gaming more enjoyable for me. If you are thinking about upgrading your mouse, especially for competitive games, I would definitely recommend this one."
    },
    {
        "review": "The Logitech G Pro X Superlight 2 is easily one of the best mice I’ve used. It’s extremely light without feeling cheap, and the sensor performance is outstanding. When paired with a glass mouse pad, the glide is insanely smooth and tracking feels incredibly clean and precise.Because I have larger hands, I added grip tape and highly recommend it for anyone in the same boat. Without grip tape I had to grip the mouse tighter than I liked, but with it, control and comfort improved immediately. Combined with the crisp clicks, reliable wireless performance, and long battery life, this mouse delivers exactly what it promises. Pricey, but well worth it."
    },
    {
        "review": "As an avid gamer, I can confidently say that the Logitech G PRO X SUPERLIGHT Gaming Mouse is a game-changer. From its lightweight design to its impressive performance, this mouse has exceeded all my expectations.First and foremost, the weight of this mouse is a standout feature. Weighing in at just under 63 grams, it’s incredibly lightweight, allowing for swift and precise movements during intense gaming sessions. Whether I’m engaging in fast-paced FPS games or executing intricate maneuvers in strategy games, the mouse feels like an extension of my hand, offering unparalleled control and agility.The HERO 2 Sensor with a staggering 32,000 DPI ensures pixel-perfect accuracy, translating every subtle movement into smooth, responsive cursor action on the screen. Combined with the LIGHTFORCE Hybrid Switches, which deliver ultra-fast click response and exceptional durability, every click feels satisfyingly tactile and precise.The 4K polling rate further enhances the responsiveness of the mouse, minimizing input lag and ensuring that my actions are registered instantaneously. This is crucial in competitive gaming, where split-second decisions can make all the difference between victory and defeat.In addition to its performance, the design of the Logitech G PRO X SUPERLIGHT is ergonomic and comfortable, featuring a streamlined shape that fits snugly in the hand. The ambidextrous design ensures that both left-handed and right-handed gamers can enjoy the same level of comfort and control.Furthermore, the customizable button layout and onboard memory allow me to tailor the mouse to my preferred gaming style, with up to 5 programmable buttons for easy access to essential commands and macros.Overall, the Logitech G PRO X SUPERLIGHT Gaming Mouse has truly elevated my gaming experience. Its combination of lightweight design, exceptional performance, and ergonomic comfort make it a must-have for any serious gamer looking to gain a competitive edge. Whether you’re a casual gamer or a professional esports athlete, this mouse delivers in every aspect, making it well worth the investment. Highly recommended."
    },
    {
        "review": "Been using this mouse for about 6 months for work, school, and gaming. The mouse size is alright. I have small hands, and my hand doesn't get sore or strained using it for several hours. It has decent ergonomics. The clicks are much quieter than a Razer Viper V3 Pro. The sound clicks are as \"loud\" as linear switches (which are fairly quiet) if noise level is a concern for you. I prefer quieter, but tactile clicks, which this mouse does quite nicely. The mouse clicks don't bother me when I'm working at night.Battery life lasts quite a long time for this mouse. I can use this mouse for a couple of days (or even a week, if I'm taking breaks) without having to charge it. On Logitech G HUB, it says the battery life lasts for approximately 96 hours. You'd have to download Logitech G HUB to customize macros and set sensitivity on the mouse. If you're using this mouse for mainly gaming, make sure to go to \"Device Settings\" and under \"Lightforce Switch\" set it to \"Optical Only\". This will decrease battery life, but will utilize the full performance of the optical switch and has lower latency.My only gripes are the Logitech G HUB is glitchy sometimes and certain game profiles don't automatically switch over, like when I tab in and out between the desktop and Fortnite, and the software doesn't automatically switch to the \"Fortnite\" profile, and the side buttons feel a bit mushy when building and editing in Fortnite. The side buttons could be a bit crispier.Overall, this mouse does the job and excels for gaming, especially FPS and fast-paced games."
    },
    {
        "review": "Fav mouse. Just overpriced, only get it on sale. But probably gonna use these for the next 10 years (year 2 atm)"
    },
    {
        "review": "About 6 years ago I bought a g pro wireless, the first one had an issue with double clicking with the primary left click AND the middle click. That developed after ~4-7 days of use. Logitech RMA'd it and sent me a new mouse. This second mouse immediately had issues with the left click where it was mushy and had significant travel past the actual click sound. They RMA'd this one too. Finally by the third mouse I got one that worked good, no double clicking, no middle mouse button issues..... for maybe 2.5 years. 0.5 years past the warranty period. I lived with the mouse button issues for a while until it became unbearable to the point where I can't reliably move the camera anymore in league of legends, or rotate the camera in many 3rd person games/3d software. I decided to get this mouse (G PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2) to replace the aged g pro wireless. It seems logitech has learned absolutely nothing in their quality control department over the years. This mouse that I just got ALREADY has scroll wheel decoder issues where it scrolls in the opposite direction briefly before scrolling in the correct direction. Secondly, it has a squealing noise as a scroll upwards that is already unbearable to listen to. I've had enough of logitech mice. I hate the situation because I love the G pro's shape and feel. The silver lining is that there are MANY competing mice now that offer great performance for less, or about the same amount of money which copied the shape and feel. This will likely be the last logitech mouse I buy and am returning it. Razer Viper V3 pro here I come.These issues I have with quality aren't the only things that are a negative about this mouse. Compared to its predecessor, the g pro wireless, the mouse buttons are much louder and slightly heavier. I didn't mind the noise nor the heavier clicks as they were only marginally heavier to press, but this is a big complaint many gpw/gpx1 -> gpx2 users have complained about. Maybe I don't mind it because I tried putting TTC gold 80M switches in my old GPW but promptly switched back to my broken middle mouse button because the TTC gold 80M's are massive (and LOUD) BRICKS you have to press down to actuate the button. Insanely heavy, horrible switches, absolutely horrible. The worst switches the world has ever seen. I challenge anyone complaining about the GPX2's switches to try those absolute monsters out. But I digress. Another issue I had with the mouse was the removal of the DPI button, which wasn't the end of the world because I created a macro I found on reddit to cycle through them. It worked fine for the small number of occasions I needed to switch the DPI but the one button was easier."
    },
    {
        "review": "Using this for work and various video games (cs). Very light mouse, almost feels like air. Build quality is excellent, the materials are pleasant to the touch. The sensor is very precise and smooth, this mouse wont let you down in a clutch. No issues with battery life (multiple full days), just keep a usb-c cable nearby. My phone charger is multipurpose now, phone/headset/mouse.The only issue is the middle click (mouse3): when held down, it can register multiple clicks when slightly changing pressure. For gaming, no big deal; for working, very lame (can close/open multiple tabs by accident). From forums/reddit, this is common to most (if not all) superlights.I've used the superlight for several months now with no new issues (other than the middle button).Before this, I've had at least seven wired and later wireless g703s. Something usually breaks within a year or so (left/right double clicking, mouse wheel issues, etc.). I expect the same for the superlight. That's gaming, cant help it. Might sound bad, but both g703 and superlight are excellent mice. With heavy use, it is inevitable that something breaks eventually. If looking for cheaper mice, g703 is a heavier alternative.pros:-\"super light\"-great sensor-no issues with logitech drivers-logitech good customer service (can RMA, I got like 3 free g703 after issues with the purchased ones)cons:-no dpi shift button-inconsistent middle mouse, can register multiple clicks while holding m3 down (without letting go)-scroll wheel feedback is somewhat weak"
    },
    {
        "review": "I have tried various types of gaming mouse set-ups and I have found this mouse to be almost perfect from the ergonomics, battery life, charging, and practicality. I have been using it for a couple of months for various tasks such as work, studies, gaming, and general use. Currently I have no complaints other than that price is high. Finally what made buy this version compared to the superlight 1 is the type-c charger which is worth it."
    },
    {
        "review": "I got the G PRO X SUPERLIGHT 2, and it's pretty great. First off it's insanely lightweight, which is a great touch if you're into quick movements. Feels good in the hand and is super responsive. The battery life is solid too, it holds charge for a decent amount of time, and when you do need to charge it, it's fast. Build quality seems sturdy, like it won’t just fall apart if you’re a bit rough with it.That said, for how good it is, there are a lot of other mice out there offering similar stuff at cheaper prices now. I still like it, but honestly, it might be time for Logitech to consider bringing the price down a notch with all the competition around. It’s still worth it, just... a little less of a splurge would be nice."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "now for my background, i play r6 and csgo, and occasionally roblox shooters. i went from originally a 60g razer orochi v2 using a fingertip grip, and i went to this mouse with a traditional claw. i’ve had this mouse for a few months and i can say that this is a very good and universal shape for all sorts of grips. i have a medium size hand and i can comfortably fingertip and claw this mouse.the coating is lovely and the best i’ve personally felt on a mouse, and i tried the grips but they eventually wear out over time so i don’t recommend using those (the actual mouse texture is better than the grips anyway). now i know this sounds a little ridiculous but moving from a 60g small mouse not even including the batteries, i played a LOT better with this bigger one after adjusting and i’m honestly happy with my purchase (even though it cost quite a bit). it feels light in the hand for the size and the weight is flawlessly distributed. the skates are solid for being stock but you can always upgrade, the scroll wheel is pretty normal and has distinctive bumps per scroll but personally i like more smoother ones (i am probably just being nitpicky). the main clicks are lovely to hear and the little dongle included doesn’t take up too much space, i’d say only the actual cable itself does but this isn’t actually an issue. the dongle can also support up to 8k hz which isn’t necessary but it’s a “nice to have” feature and i use 2k hz myself to save battery life;speaking of battery life it’s pretty good and i suggest not using 8k since it drains it pretty fast but it lasts up to a week or two for me with regular use not charging (just remember to plug it in after use) the side buttons are a DEFINITE improvement from my old mouse and that was a perfect feature on the mouse. now, razer’s synapse software can be a little finicky sometimes but i suggest just configuring your settings and uninstalling it since it can take up some of your ram and overall performance.in conclusion, this is a really good option for gaming mice and it can fit a LOT of playstyles. this has all of the latest mouse tech (might be a little outdated for me to be saying that in the big 25 now) and each part of the mouse is very pleasant to use. i suggest this mouse if you have the money (it is pretty ehh value for money but as long as you have the funds it’s a s tier mouse)"
    },
    {
        "review": "The Razer Viper V3 has been an excellent mouse for both gaming and everyday use. It feels very lightweight and comfortable in hand, making long sessions easy with no fatigue. The shape is simple but very ergonomic, and it works well for both claw and fingertip grip styles.Performance wise, the sensor is extremely accurate and responsive. Tracking is smooth, clicks feel crisp, and there is no noticeable delay. The wireless connection is stable and reliable, with no lag or dropouts. Battery life has also been great, lasting several days of heavy use without needing frequent charging.Build quality feels solid, and the mouse buttons and scroll wheel all feel well made. Overall, the Viper V3 delivers excellent performance, comfort, and reliability. It is a great choice for anyone looking for a high quality lightweight gaming mouse."
    },
    {
        "review": "(Review from my 15 year old son)Honestly this mouse felt SO much lighter than I thought it would. I just upgraded from the Glorious Model O- Wireless Gaming Mouse, and let me say... the upgrade was WORTH it for sure. I pretty much only play Fortnite on Keyboard and Mouse, and I was having issues with my side buttons on my old mouse. I decided it was time to upgrade since I can't have those issues happening mid-game. The Razer Viper V3 Pro, is definitely a solid upgrade. The side buttons are so much more solid than the side buttons on my old mouse. Not to mention, the response time of the clicks was faster, and the accuracy of my aim with the better sensor was so much better. The scroll wheel is also very responsive and fast which is important for Fortnite. (I was able to easily quad edit in Fortnite when using this new mouse)The wireless connectivity isn't even noticeable at all. The response time is insane for a wireless mouse, and it surprised me a lot. I don't have a lot to say about the battery life yet, as this is my first day using it. I will say though, I charged the mouse to 68%, used it for an hour in Fortnite, and the battery was only 67% afterwards.Here are the few cons I can think of:When switching from whatever mouse you currently use, it will take a few hours or even a day to adjust to the lightness of the mouse. (mainly if you played on heavier mice before) It's not anything with the mouse itself, but just a matter of adjusting to the feel of the new mouse.The mouse comes with some grip tape you can put on the mouse, which honestly looks really cool in my opinion, and it really helps with making sure your aim is as good as it can be, since your hand won't slip off the mouse. (See image) (Note: mouse is charging)Razer also has a good software which allows you to adjust the mouse to match the feel of your old one which is really helpful to make sure you don't have to COMPLETELY start over with learning a sensitivity.Overall a really good mouse, no complaints to be honest."
    },
    {
        "review": "I had a Logitech superlight and moved to the Viper v3 pro. It was the best decision that I have ever made. The mouse sensor in my opinion is more accurate the Logitech mice. The feel and texture and material of the mouse feels very nice especially the white coating option is phenomenal and has really good grip for a very low amount of weight gain. The size definitely favors mid-big hands and excels in the Claw grip. It isn't the lightest mouse on the market but it still in my opinion is more than light enough for most. The scroll wheel actually feels quite nice and i prefer it over the Logitech one. Though this mouse supports 8k polling I rarely find myself using it as it drains battery too fast, I would just use 4000/2000 if you want a high enough speed with a very long battery life. If you are looking for a high quality claw grip mouse then this is the mouse for you, if you don't have a claw grip I would recommend looking at other mice that might be for you."
    },
    {
        "review": "As someone who loves the Superlight and has been jamming out with version 2 of it along with a Logitech Pro X TKL paired with it, it's been nice to swap back to my Black Widow V4 along with the Viper Pro 3.Right out of the box, and I am someone with medium to large hands, this mouse is just a pure delight to hold; it's not often I can say that my hands feel like they disappear when using a peripheral, but this mouse and the Superlight from Logi are the two mice that achieve that for me. Razer knew what they were doing here and it shows in the product; this will be my go-to mouse and I will alternate my Razer setup back to the Logitech set up as the mood strikes me to change things up week by week.When it comes time for the Wooting 80HE to arrive, I will most likely be pairing the Viper Pro 3 with that board due to just how consistently good it feels; I am 3 hours deep into a play session and the battery is down to 98% and I'm using the 4K polling rate; not going to bother with 8K just yet.It's hard to put into words here just how good the slight ergo shape of this mouse feels; I really have tried a number of different mice and this could be \"the one\"; I don't say this in a hyperbolic fashion either. It's rare that you use a gaming device where you just forget what you're using because it seamlessly blends into the play session.For anyone looking to try this out, the asking price is steep. For a low frills mouse in the aesthetic department it really isn't going to be winning wards for it's design change to be not as sharp as it's predecessor. I for one really dig the smoother design and the light hump towards the back of the mouse, the finger ridges, or whatever you want to call them, on the L + R mouse clicks, all come together to suit whatever combination of grip you want to employ here. I am using a light palm grip and find that going claw and then relaxing my fingers feel natural.The side buttons deserve a special award here; they are separated ever so slightly and their clicks are immediate / responsive. Truly the only gripe I have with my Superlight are the side buttons; Logitech really needs to take the oatmeal mush out of those and it will be a near perfect mouse for me.Really curious to see what kind of competition this will spur with other manufacturers; that said, and for now, this is the best of the best for something that is here, on Amazon, that you can order and have by tomorrow while it's in stock. To whoever nabs one, enjoy; curious to see other reviews pop up on here."
    },
    {
        "review": "So far so good. I was impressed on how light this mouse felt when I used it for the first time. You can definitely feel the extra accuracy when you aim, by that I mean every small movement you make on your mousepad translates exactly as you would expect on the screen. My router and modem are right under my desk and I don't get any interference.  I don't open the program that comes with it (synapse) because I used a wired headset with it (razer blackshark v2 X) and I don't want synapse messing the audio settings of my headset since the default option is pretty good. The battery works for a long time; I used it for like 3 consecutive days and when I open synapse to check battery is about 70% (I use 2k polling rate). Clicking sounds satisfying as you would expect from an expensive mouse. Your forearm gets considerably less tired. The side buttons and click buttons have not collected any dust or grime from my hands, unlike my old mouse did."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have had this mouse for about a year now and use it as my daily driver, and I love it.It is really light and glides across the mouse pad. The mouse wheel is not noisy at all and neither is the clicking of the mouse buttons imo. The battery life is good lasting more than a few all day play sessions, and a couple of weeks of light use.It comes with some little adhesive pads that you can place on the mouse which I would recommend using as the mouse is very smooth.My complaints are that the mouse gets clammy feeling after a while. Not a huge deal, but I have thought of buying another adhesive pad to go over the emblem because it gets to feel gross. I do clean it, but this is the only mouse I have ever had this issue with. Also the dongle is kind of a pain if you have OCD, it is hard to get it to lay flat down.Overall I love the mouse and think it is worth the money. I would just recommend buying an adhesive patch (I'm not sure what they are really called) to go over the razer emblem as that part can start to feel gross."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is by far the best mouse I’ve ever owned, and I’ve owned several, including the Razer DeathAdder, Mamba, Mamba Elite, and most recently, the SteelSeries Aerox 5 Wireless. I switched to this mouse because the scroll wheel on the Aerox breaks far too quickly. Initially, I was hesitant to get the Viper due to the Aerox 5's five side buttons but I’m so glad I made the switch!This mouse is incredible. The response speeds are super fast, it’s insanely light, and it glides effortlessly across my mouse pad. The battery life is also outstanding. I used it for four days straight before putting it on the charger as I was going to bed, and it still had 37% left. Switching between wired and wireless modes is instant, which is a huge improvement over my Aerox 5, where connections sometimes took minutes. The scroll wheel feels high quality and has deep notches.I’m not sure why I see people hating on Razer Synapse. Maybe I'm a little biased since I've always used razer keyboards, and am Using the Huntsman pro rn but I've never had issue and I always found it pretty convenient for making macros and chroma effects and the features Synapse has for this mouse is pretty Dope. For example, you can create your own sensitivity response curve, similar to how controller settings work in many games. Additionally, if you grip your mouse at an angle, as I do with a left leaning grip, you can tell Synapse your grip style using a slider for better horizontal accuracy. You can also have synapse auto switch polling rates depending on if your in a fullscreen game or just browsing the web which makes the battery last ages. I use 2000hz in-game and 1000hz out.Overall, I don’t usually write reviews, but this mouse has me blown away. It’s significantly better than my previous one in every way. Highly recommend it to anyone considering an upgrade or looking for a premium wireless mouse. For real this thing is AWESOME."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good mouse, one of the best I have ever had, I only charge it every 2 weeks for about 4 hours and it works 100%."
    },
    {
        "review": "Light and feel nice"
    },
    {
        "review": "Very comfortable mouse. The design feels a lot better than the previous v2 for medium/large hands using claw/relaxed claw grip. High quality feeling mouse with a very nice coating also. It’s certainly expensive, but well worth it in my opinion. The only downside for me personally is the little gap between the two thumb buttons, which is mainly because i have shorter thumbs. Other than that though, it’s the perfect mouse for me"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great mouse but higher polling rates affect fps depending on your pc"
    }
]

