[
    {
        "review": "I recently purchased the Razer Basilisk V3 Pro 35K Wireless Gaming Mouse from Amazon, and I couldn’t be happier with my choice. This mouse has exceeded my expectations in terms of quality, performance, and ergonomics.Exceptional QualityThe build quality of the Basilisk V3 Pro is outstanding. It feels solid and well-constructed, which is essential for a gaming mouse that’s meant to endure hours of use. The materials used are top-notch, providing a premium feel that enhances the overall experience.ErgonomicsOne of the standout features of this mouse is its ergonomic design. It fits perfectly in my hand, making long gaming sessions comfortable. The shape and grip are well thought out, allowing for precise movements without causing fatigue.PerformanceI’ve been using this mouse for some time now, and I’ve encountered no issues with response time. The tracking is incredibly accurate, which is crucial for competitive gaming. I’ve noticed a significant improvement in my gameplay, thanks to the reliable performance of this mouse.Battery LifeThe battery life is impressive as well. Depending on my usage, it lasts up to two days or even three, which is fantastic for a wireless device. I appreciate not having to constantly recharge it, allowing me to focus on my gaming sessions.Features and SoftwareThe Basilisk V3 Pro comes packed with features that enhance its functionality. I downloaded the Razer software, which was straightforward and user-friendly. Customizing settings and adjusting performance options is a breeze, allowing me to tailor the mouse to my preferences.Final ThoughtsOverall, the Razer Basilisk V3 Pro 35K Wireless Gaming Mouse is a quality investment for any gamer. With its excellent ergonomics, reliable performance, and impressive battery life, it has become an indispensable part of my gaming setup. I highly recommend this mouse to anyone looking for a high-performance gaming accessory!"
    },
    {
        "review": "So far, so good.  I got this to replace my old Logitech M720, that until about a month ago was working just fine.  Through some combination of updates, either Windows, Logitech, or both, it started giving me issues with click/drag and selection, so the hunt was on for a new mouse.  This one is expensive AF, but it had the fastest delivery option.  The Razer Synapse software is nightmarishly complicated to figure out.  You will need ChatGPT to help you, but it is posible to configure program specific functions for all the buttons.  And once you do figure it out, it's easy to use.  I have one set to do a Ctrl+click, one to do Ctrl+W, and one to do Enter in chrome, and it works flawlessly.  There is zero lag, and I can once again select text with ease, and slide the time bar in Wondershare Filmora to precisely seek to points in videos I'm editing.  You can set up multi-step button/mouse actions to happen with a single click with a macro.  And if set up properly, the mouse just knows what program it's being used in.  You don't have to manually switch button profiles each time you open a different program.  That was a major non-negotiable thing for me.  I love this thing.  No more dropped selections and executions of click commands that I did not initiate.  The Logitech is getting the hammer.  I'll probably keep this, even though I have two other cheaper ones coming to test.  It fits my hand well, and the button placement is equal to, or more convenient than, my old one.  It is possible to turn off all of the lights, though I don't know how I did it exactly.  I'll have to ask ChatGPT the proper way, but the childish light show is off, which is all that matters."
    },
    {
        "review": "I love this mouse, it feels very durable, sturdy, and gives you a lot of ability to customize the mouse bindings. I really like how they did the RGB by having the light bar wrap around the base of the mouse. You get a nice under-glow effect while you use it rather than have RGB on the top and your hand ultimately cover it up during use. The only downside is the side thumb button sits slightly too far forward so it’s not quite as ergonomic of a key press as the other buttons. It’s not that big of a deal to drop the rating but in future models I hope they address this. It’s probably a little heavy to some, but for me that is a benefit and makes it feel premium. I was using a G502x which is a great mouse in its own right but I feel this one is overall better."
    },
    {
        "review": "The Razer Basilisk V3 Pro 35K is hands-down one of the best wireless gaming mice I’ve ever used. The build quality feels premium the moment you pick it up, and the 35,000 DPI sensor is incredibly precise—perfect for fast-paced games or detailed work. The wireless connection is rock solid with zero noticeable lag, making it feel just as responsive as a wired mouse.The ergonomics are outstanding. It fits naturally in the hand, and the customizable buttons make it easy to set up for any game or workflow. The RGB lighting is clean and vibrant without being over the top, and the battery life is excellent, especially considering all the features packed into this mouse.If you’re looking for a top-tier wireless gaming mouse that performs flawlessly and feels amazing to use, the Basilisk V3 Pro 35K is an easy 5-star recommendation."
    },
    {
        "review": "Finding a mouse that will work well without loading down the computer with excess software can be a challenge. I replaced my logi gaming mouse with this one so I could offload the logi software. I does mouse things well. I can use this mouse for extended periods in comfort. I don't play competitive PVP games and have noticed no lag or response times issues in any of the games I do play. I can't say much about using it wireless for competitive shooters.PROS: ergonomic enough, good response time with no noticeable lag in wireless modeCONS: battery life is acceptable, not great.I do recommend this mouse. Good value and I would buy it again."
