        "review": "Works fine. Adding three more connections is a big advantage when needed."
    },
    {
        "review": "Often many products advertised on Amazon are exaggerated, or wrong, but not this one.  Other reviews cover the packaging, and build so I'll focus on the function.  Because of the renaming of USB 3.2.1 vs 3.1.2 speed is confusing, its best to specify and compare it to the raw speed it delivers rather than the USB version name.Pros10Gbits/s or 1GByte/s read, for each port tested individually using Crystaldiskmark.  The crystal image shows one run of 3, which were roughly identical, for all ports and to speed without a port, plugged directly. The file transfer is a 7GB file transferred from one t7 Shield to another on the same hub, its roughly sustained until near the end.  This produces collisions and tests the latency from transfers within the port itself, that is from one port to another.case is plastic and lightweight, there is no need for metal as its not a power port to need a heatsink.  There is a nearly identically spec'd model sold for $10 more with a metal case, if buyers prefer.ports are well made, click properly and lock into place, all workbuild quality is excellent for plastic mounted component with a high quality USB C cable; it wouldn't work at speed otherwise.packaging is mostly cardboard, paper and minimum plastic wrap, mostly recyclable, as reviewed by othersthis is the port to get for USB C 3.2 Superspeed 10Gbpsso good I bought two of these hubsConsnone.  I will update this review if the port prematurely dies.  There is no way to tell except by using it for some time.  The build quality, packaging and meeting its spec would suggest it would last far beyond a year."
