        "review": "I have three of these.  They are rock solid and very well built.  Tough, look great, and works perfectly.  great port selection, perfect speed and great color."
    },
    {
        "review": "I thought this device worked great...until it didn't. I have not tested on my old MacBook Pro, but with my brand MBP M5 Max, the Ethernet only works if something else is connected, such as a USB HDD or the PD. As soon as neither of those are present, the Ethernet connection disappears. I have reported to the seller for support. I debugged this with Apple and they concluded it's the device, not MacOS, but it's possible it is in fact MacOS. Either way, it does not work properly with the latest MacOS 26.5 unless something else is also connected. Will update based on response from vendor.The seller refused to replace with a properly working device. They told me to contact Amazon who told me it doesn't qualify for a refund or replacement. Lowered to 1 star as a result. Bought in Feb, discovered issue in May, so only 3 months old. This is the only such device I've bought to have an issue like this (i've bought. like 10 various models). Fundamentally flaw IMO and I'm very disappointed."
    },
    {
        "review": "A very good adapter, affordable cost and very good quality"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "So far, works fine. Tha material seems ok, I like the size & design of it (small, unassuming at the back of my desk but angled so easy to plug in things), it has the configuration and number of plugs I needed. All good so far.It does say in the little instructions that it will power ONE external HD, hopefully I can use a second one at the same time but I haven't yet had a chance to test that out."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a well-made powered hub.  Delivery was same day (awesome) and arrived undamaged due to excellent packaging.  Upon testing, all of my ports worked with no problem. The silver color matches perfectly with my iMac (see photo) and it sits very nicely under the iMac screen on the pedestal.  I like the slanted design, which makes it easy to plug and unplug your USB cables. There are plenty of ports, including 6-USB and 3-USB C ports. All USB ports can charge as well as be used for data.  3 of the USB ports move data at 5 GBS and 2 of the USB ports move data at 10 GBS.  2 of the USB-C ports are for charging only and 1-USB C port is for charging and data.  I plugged in a USB portable thumb drive, and it was recognized immediately with no issue.  I am not running any other disks off of this hub at this time.  Not only that, but I was pleasantly surprised by post sales support.  I had a question on submitting my warranty registration using their website.  I emailed them and got a response within 2 hours…very good considering this unit is from China. They only design issue I have is that the power plug and USB C connection cable comes out the side of the unit.  I would have preferred it to be off the back of the unit in order to hide the cables better. I would purchase this Hub again."
    },
    {
        "review": "I had a problem.  My monitor array at home includes a Thunderbolt hub that includes two DisplayPort and one VGA connector, and my laptop at home includes one HDMI and one additional VGA connector, but this totals only 6 screens when my laptop's primary screen is added in.  My monitor array, on the other hand, includes yet another 6 screens (all but two of them are 1080p).  These screens are driven by a set of mostly DisplayLink display adapters, although I have a few others that required manually installed drivers (with varying success), and some that don't advertise DisplayLink but whose drivers installed automatically anyways.Six USB-A Display adapters require a lot of power, and I ran into the limit of my workstation and Thunderbolt hub's power capacity at only 4 additional displays, and every single USB-A and USB-C port on my laptop and my dock were used up, leading to a messy snarl of wires and cables that wire ties just didn't resolve.What I like:1. It allows me to connect my entire array of video extenders and power my entire array of monitors.It substantially cleans up the mess of cables connected to my laptop and hub by putting many of them in one place.2. Its aluminum finish looks good on my desktop.3. Its power supply is rated for 65w, telling me I can connect more devices before its capacity is exhausted.4. It has a sturdy construction.What I don't like:a. Ummm, that's a tough one.  Maybe it takes up additional desk space and there are a lot of cables connected to it, but hey, that's kind-of the intent.  Nah, I can't say I dislike that.Caveats:- Your results may vary if you plan to use this to drive a large array of screens.  My laptop has 64GB RAM and a second video card with another 6GB video RAM.  I have a newer laptop at work with 128GB RAM and a second video card with 16GB video RAM that can't run my home array without overheating."
    },
    {
        "review": "Edit: as of 12/13/25, they have made things right and replaced my product with one that works as intended. It is nicely made, and fits nicely on the desk. They only reason it now has 4 stars instead of 5 is because the ports, primarily the USB-C ports are a little sunk in. Word well, and worth the money though.Unfortunately does not work. After getting this I was happy to see that it had a great design and features that I was looking for. It only worked for about 1 day though before it stopped, now none of the ports work to connect to my computer. The only thing that does work is the charging, which is nice but I needed this to make connections to the computer easier at work. I will be returning this product."
    },
    {
        "review": "This USB hub is absolutely excellent. After trying several USB hubs before, I can confidently say this one has been the best so far.It works flawlessly, handles my devices reliably, and has performed exactly as expected. The connection is stable, the ports work perfectly, and the overall quality feels much better than other hubs I've used.I'm very impressed with the performance and reliability of this product. If you're looking for a powerful, dependable USB hub with plenty of connections, I highly recommend this one.A fantastic product that truly delivers!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Well made!  The USB hub is weighty enough to keep the cables from pulling it away from where you setup it. I also like the wedge design, which angles the ports towards me. The amount of power delivery is more than adequate to power up all of my devices while allowing them to send and receive data."
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased this for a few reasons. I liked the fact that it has 4.4 out of 5-star reviews. I read some of the 1-star reviews and I can see their point, however, I mainly use USB-A devices. I will probably someday have to get another USB hub to handle USB-C devices, but for now this works for me. My old one was a low profile where I had to place the cables or USB thumb drives almost flat next to the desk top. It was difficult to plug anything in unless I slightly pick up the hub. This one, the ports point up at a 45-degree angle which makes it a lot easer to plug in the cables and USB devices. I’m not sure I would by this again anytime soon though, just because of the limited USB-C ports where only one passes data. I agree with the one-star reviews, it makes no sense to only have one of the three ports only pass data. All three of them should. It is disappointing."
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased this USB hub to help organize my workspace, and it has been a great addition to my desk. It provides plenty of ports for my devices and helps reduce cable clutter, making my work area much cleaner and more efficient. The build quality feels solid, the connections are reliable, and it’s very easy to use. I especially like how it keeps everything in one place instead of having multiple adapters scattered around. Overall, it’s a practical and useful accessory that has made my workspace more organized and productive."
    },
    {
        "review": "This hub has a very Apple-esque design and appears to be very solidly built.  So far, it seems to perform well and as expected based on its specs.A couple of things though that are not made clear to purchasers based on the description provided:1. The hub is provided only with a US 2-pin mains power lead.  In other countries you’ll need to use a plug adaptor, else provide your own native mains cord with an IEC-C7 “figure 8” connector on the other end.  At least the power brick uses that style of connector, the mains plug is not hard-wired.2. The instructions warn you that WiFi dongles (such as those used to communicate with wireless mouse and keyboards) should not be plugged into the hub due to the risk of interference.  Meaning one port on your laptop/PC will have to be used for such dongles if you have them.  Not a dealbreaker, but it would have been nice to know this prior.3. The instructions say only one HDD or two SSDs can be connected to the hub (presumably for power draw reasons).  The maximum load is 900 mA (4.5 W) but it’s not clear if that is per data port (the USB standard) or in total across all data ports.This hub provides only one USB-C and two USB-A 3.1 Gen 2 data ports (10 Gbps).  That’s a bit light-on these days.  The remainder are USB-A 3.0 ports (5 Gbps).The two “power delivery” (non-data) USB-C ports are handy for charging devices, total power delivery max 45 W.So all up, a rating of 4 stars."
    },
    {
        "review": "Versatile and Elegant USB Hub - Top Purchase! I recently purchased this Intpw USB hub and I am absolutely delighted with my choice! After numerous searches on the Internet and comparisons, I fell for this model which offers truly impressive value for money. With its 9 ports (5 USB 3.0 and several USB-C), it perfectly meets my daily connection needs for my laptop, tablet and other peripherals. The design is truly modern and elegant - we feel a careful product with quality finishes. Installation is child's play: just plug it in and that's it! The power of 45W in Power Delivery is a real plus, allowing me to efficiently charge my devices. My only small downside concerns the cable which seems slightly short to me, but this remains really marginal compared to the many advantages. I absolutely recommend this USB hub to anyone looking for a practical, high-performance accessory at a very competitive price. It’s really a smart purchase that simplifies daily life!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great looking USB Hub with plenty of data and charging ports.  I deducted 2-Stars because:1. The listing does not mention that it only comes with a U.S-Style power plug, which means I cannot use it until I source another figure-8 lead at my expense.2. The connections for power and USB to PC are on the side.  This is an aesthetic-only issue but for such a sleek-looking device, having the cords sticking out the side is just a little issue."
    },
    {
        "review": "Looks cool works as advertised. Feels solid and well built. Aluminum body is nice"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "So many times I buy these and they half work, power is not enough for the entire hub to share or it is just junk. I bought this one out of need and to my surprise this one has held up, powers all the devices that can connect to it, and provides great data speeds to my computer. So if you asked me would you buy this again, my answer would be a YES!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This hub is pleasing beyond expectations.  I bought one six weeks ago, and when it didn't fail during my 30 day return window I bought another.  It's now a week later and all is well, and if nothing breaks, my opinion is that this is the best thing out there.It's got 8 USB-C ports, 7 USB-A ports, a massive power supply, and switches that are not mechanical on/off, in/out push-buttons but light-press switches (no clicks) with blue indicator lights.  The chassis is metal and you feel that you own a quality device.  I'm using one on a Mac mini and the other on a big gaming PC.  On both machines I went from messy spaghetti to nice and tidy.I just re-read the 1-star reviews and I haven't experienced any of those issues, USB-PD issues and ports not working.  But I do have several USB-powered MIDI devices that small, un-powered USB hubs could not handle at all (I bought five, hoping one would work), presumably because they couldn't supply enough power, and this one was the fix. It works fine for \"normal\" devices, like external drives and SSDs, other hubs, and my MIDI stuff. Devices requiring massive power I haven't tried but my neither do I need them on my desk.  They have their own power supplies."
    },
    {
        "review": "It does what it is adverised to do and is easy to set up"
    },
    {
        "review": "I have been waiting for this USB hub.  I have the other version from the same manufacturer that only has about 2 USB C ports and they finally made one with more USB C ports.  I use these hubs for music and attach my external music equipment, Scarlett units, MIDI controllers, external storage drives, etc.  USB C is very popular, and most of my devices use USB C.  This hub has worked great for me.  When I saw they finally made a version with 50/50 outlets, I immediately bought one.  This is my go-to manufacturer:  Press the button just long enough and it powers down the port; it also doesn’t power cycle when you shut your laptop down (or desktop) - other manufacturers I had DID power cycle (and they are also powered hubs) as my laptop was shutting down and, when you have a connected external storage device, that’s not good for a storage drive.  To date, I have about 4 of these units from the same manufacturer, but will start swapping them out in favor of the high USB C port count.  These units have never failed me during music performances.  I’ve had other manufacturers, but this has been my best and I have ZERO reason to look for another.  You won’t be disappointed!"
    },
    {
        "review": "It is too soon to give a fair review.  But right out of the box the power cord that goes from the outlet to the power block has a connector that is not a good fit.  After several minutes of trying to jam the connector into the socket on the power block, I settled for an imperfect connection.  We will see if that will be adequate over time.It is still early but I have had an opportunity to enjoy its' performance now for a little while and it is more than I hoped for.  I still worry about the power connection.  But I am pleased with its' performance."
    },
    {
        "review": "I hate to say it, but this hub (at least the one I received) is garbage.I will give the manufacturer points for build quality, the hub seems to built well and using quality materials. Unfortunately, that's about where the positive news ends and as far as usability goes I cannot recommend this device. I've listed below the problems I experienced within the first 24 hours of usage:1) The switches outright just do not work for many devices. I tried several devices which seem to somehow just \"ignore\" the switch being turned off. I put ignore in quotes, because the device should not be built in a way that would allow any attached device to communicate with the host while it's off. For some devices, the switch does work correctly. However, for other devices, I can plug a USB cable into the hub and have it persistently connected to the host no matter how many times I toggle the switch on and off. This is a key indicator that the device was not designed in a secure way. When the toggle switch is off, power and connectivity should be completely disconnected to the port. The fact that I am able to bypass the toggle switch is an indicator that some sort of firmware workaround is being used. Bad news here.2) Power delivery does not seem to work correctly on the hub. I'm using the power adapter that was delivered with the device which is plugged into a stable power source. Some devices which require power delivery (USB-PD) get stuck in an initializing state when drawing power from the hub. They get enough power to appear powered on, but the device never initializes and the host system can detect something was plugged in but cannot interface with the device. When I switch these devices to a different hub (or directly to the host), the issue is not present and the device works as expected.3) Even devices that do connect, seem to have issues with performance and/or connectivity. When some devices are attached to the hub, the host operating system seems to hang while the device is connecting. I experience this on about 1/3 of my attempts with any mass storage device, and some devices fairly consistently. When I do get a device to connect to the host, my transfer speeds are considerably lower than if I had used a different hub or connected directly to the host.All in all, I absolutely do not recommend this device. There are several clear indicators of poor and unsafe design when it comes to this hub, and I would not trust that it delivers power cleanly or that there's any consistent integrity on file transfers.Again, all of these issues were experienced within the first 24 hours of usage. I tested over a dozen different devices with this hub, of varying types and manufacturer. If I had this many issues straight away, I can guarantee it's not an isolated incident. Even if my hub was \"defective\", there's no way it would have been defective in a way that would selectively allow some devices to connect while toggled off.Let this be a warning to you!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Haven’t had to use all the ports yet but the ones I have seem to work fine. No problems with the on/off switches that others have reported.  Will have to see how robust it is after longer use."
    },
    {
        "review": "Works great. I purchased it for use with Mac and Linux computers to have multiple peripherals. It’s well built and well packaged."
    },
    {
        "review": "great mfg bruno weber"
    },
    {
        "review": "This powered hub is one of the best i've ever used. The build quality is tuff & sturdy. All good here!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This powered hub works really well. That each port can be switched on or off individually was not one of my requirements, but it’s certainly a good thing, and a little blue LED next to each port lights up to show when it’s turned on.The sheer number of ports is the extreme advantage with this device, together with the fact that eight of them are USB-C and eight are USB-A. This makes it easy to have both older and newer USB devices attached on the desktop via a single hub. If I should need more USB connections, I will get another one of these. This is the best and most useful USB hub I have ever had the pleasure of using."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Technically this is plug and play for most of us. It does mention needing more power if you connect 3 or more hard drives to it. I am not sure but this likely would be a very small % of users. If you read the instructions, this is plug and play. The USB C end goes to the USB 3 input on rear. That part got me a bit as I would have thought that would have been an output read by computer. Input/output, write or read, it goes both ways unless charging only.The other end is a USB A but you can pull this connector to expose a USB C. C will be faster than A and should be used if possible. If you use USB A, please make sure to connect to a USB A 3.0 or better terminal on computer. See your documentation for this location or color. The wire diameter was an impressive 4.5ish mm and has a reach of about 4 feet.The device has 7 USB ports, of which 3 are C and 4 are A. The unit is not heavy but it is heavy enough that the anti-slip rubber strips under the unit keeps it in place. Connection for data such as a card reader was really fast and it has no problem charging other devices while connecting as a drive. This is a very functional unit that can accommodate many tasks simultaneously. It is slick and aesthetic on a desk. For 7 ports, I found the price for the functionality and quality to be really good."
    },
    {
        "review": "I picked up this 7-port USB C hub because I had simply run out of USB ports on my docking station. Between my mouse, keyboard, speakers, webcam, and all the newer devices using USB-C, I needed an easy way to expand what I could connect without replacing my existing setup. The 4-foot cable is particularly useful on my desk because I can position the hub where it is convenient instead of having everything crowded around the back of the docking station.I have been using it for the wireless dongles for my mouse and keyboard, along with my small Polycon speakers and webcam, and everything has worked perfectly for me. I like having four USB-A ports and three USB-C ports available in one place, especially as I am accumulating more USB-C devices. The aluminum housing feels sturdy, the non-slip base keeps it from sliding around, and the small LED lets me know it is powered. One thing to pay attention to is that the USB-C port marked 5V is for power input only, not data transfer, so it is not an additional data port. That is clearly stated in the description, but it is worth knowing before plugging something into it and wondering why it isn't recognized.I think this is a practical solution for anyone who needs more USB ports without rearranging their entire computer setup. The long cable and combination of USB-A and USB-C ports make it especially useful on a desktop workstation. For my particular setup, it has done exactly what I needed and saved me from constantly swapping devices between limited ports."
    },
    {
        "review": "The XIAOBI USB 3.0 7 Port Hub looks really nice. It is aluminum and has 7 ports, 3 USB C and 4 USB A all rated at 5Ggps. It does require a 3 Amp 5VDC power supply (not included -1 Star) of some sort if you are going to be using external hard drives and the like.I am glad I got this hub because I had two other 10 port USB hubs connected I thought were connected to USB 3.0 ports but when I started speed testing this hub, I noticed they were only transferring at USB 2.0 speeds. I connected all hubs to legit USB 3.0 ports and the speeds increased by 5 times. Almost all the things I have connected to the USB hubs work fine at USB 2.0 speeds, dongles, wireless headset etc. but if you transfer files on to a thumb drive or an external hard drive; you want the USB 3.0 speed.With the other two 10 port hubs connected; when I connected this hub to my system it started to do squirrely things. The Logitech mouse & keyboard didn’t work and thumb drives would not register on some hubs but would on other hubs. I think this is a problem of my system; too many USB ports connected so I removed this one.I connected this hub to my windows 11 laptop as the only hub and it worked perfectly with no problems. So, if you have a power supply and only use this hub; you should be fine. I really do like the looks and speed of this USB hub."
    },
    {
        "review": "First thing I noticed was how nice the exterior construction of this hub is. Stays in place on my desk too which is my number one ask of a desktop hub. The mix or ports is great and allows me to plug in nearly everything I need without having to jockey cords around. Included cable is great and has an adapter on it. I'm using an existing power adapter as well to give my devices a bit more juice. Even fully loaded, I'm getting good throughput on my storage devices and I have not had any connectivity issues. I like that the cords are positioned in the back too which allows me to put this next to other things without the cord getting in the way. Additionally the rubber on the bottom keeps it from sliding around on my desk. It also doubles as a way of keeping it from scratching the surface.A very good USB hub that works as well as it looks and isn't overly expensive. Definitely worth checking out if you need a few more USB and USB-C ports within easy reach."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very pleased with this USB-C hub. Having seven ports in a compact enclosure is extremely convenient, especially when connecting multiple peripherals to a laptop or desktop with limited USB connectivity.The four USB-A and three USB-C ports provide a useful mix for both older USB devices and newer USB-C peripherals. USB 3.0 speeds are also fast enough for transferring files to external storage and for everyday devices such as keyboards, mice, USB adapters, and other peripherals.I particularly like the straightforward plug-and-play operation. There was no complicated setup or additional software required—just connect it to the computer and the ports are immediately available. The compact design also makes it easy to keep on a desk or take along with a laptop, its heavy weight keeps it from moving around when attached devices move around, a problem I've had with lighter-weight models.Overall, this is a very practical USB expansion solution at a great price. The combination of seven ports, a good mix of USB-A and USB-C connectivity, compact size, and USB 3.0 performance makes it a useful addition to any computer setup.  Highly recommended!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I didn't realize how much I needed to clear up my messy living room hub center for cords until I plugged in this 7-port USB 3.0 hub from Xiaobi. The solid aluminum alloy build feels genuinely sturdy and doesn't slide around thanks to the non-slip rubber base, which also keeps my TV stand safe from annoying scratches. With seven ports available, I can easily leave the grandkids' Xbox, PS4, external media drives, and peripheral receivers plugged in all at once without constantly swapping cables. Transferring large video files and photos takes just seconds with the speedy 5Gbps data transfer, and the 4-foot cable gives me plenty of slack to route it neatly behind my entertainment center. It even comes with a handy dedicated 5V power input port on the back to keep all my devices running super stable under a heavy workload. At this price point, getting a slim, travel-friendly, and wide-compatible hub that works seamlessly across multiple systems is an absolute steal for the value."
    },
    {
        "review": "For being a USB 3.0 rating, this is a good hub.  Solid metal construction with a tapered angle front for easier access.  Rubber strips on the bottom of the HUB keep it in place.  It has a tight form factor, up to seven devices without over crowding while keeping the overall width to a minimum.The input USB C port is on the rear of the chassis, along with an external power input (optional).  For my desk design, the rear ports don't work out well, plugging the cables into the rear of the HUB chassis, pushed it out away from the rear wall of my desk.  Side input ports work out better for me.Throughput speed is what you would expect for a 3.0 USB rating, there is no discernable loss of speed using the hub, it seems to pass data without any loss of packets or bandwidth.For a 3.0 hub, the price point makes it a good value."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a really cool design. I was actually surprised by how small and compact it is when it arrived, but in this case that’s definitely a good thing. It manages to pack a lot of utility into a very small footprint without taking up a bunch of unnecessary space.The design makes really efficient use of the available room, and I like that you get quite a bit of functionality without having another bulky item sitting around. The speeds also seem nice and fast in my use, which makes it feel even more capable for something this compact.Overall, I’m impressed with how much they managed to fit into such a small design. It’s clever, space-saving, fast, and practical. A lot of utility in a surprisingly small package."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I have had no issues running this with my work laptop HP Laptop running Windows 10 and my Dell Inspiron 17 running Ubuntu 20.10/Windows 10. Both laptops charge, and all ports seem to work well. In general, the worst part of the USB-C format is the confusing amount of different laptops that seem to implement it in different ways. Not this product's fault; as I said, it works well with Dell and HP while my HP Laptop dock runs with both laptops, but under Ubuntu or Windows 10 on my Dell, it falls short. The HP Dock is a Thunderbolt dock, and my Dell is a Display port marked with a DP; the DP Port on my Dell does charge and allows all ports to work with this dock. I mention it because USB-C gets confusing, and mileage may vary depending on the laptop itself. My Hp is marked as Thunderbolt, and my Dell as Display Port, and this product runs wonderfully with both. I found I could get a 4k Display using the DP port on the back of the dock in Ubuntu, but I found it a little problem some and instead switched to using the two HDMI ports instead. Sometimes 4k would flicker, but that can be my older KVM Switch (1080P KVM). I don't mind the two 1080p screens as the dock makes it so easy to switch computers. I wonder why the HDMI port only seems to pump out 1080P and the display port 4k from my experience. Still, my HP dock didn't do 4k at all, so this dock outperformed it. I have even booted my Dell and HP Laptops with a live USB of MX Linux, and with no configuration, it worked. I just changed the display settings a bit.So to sum it up.1) Easy to use - NO Drivers to install!2) Works across Windows and Linux.3) Affordable and better than my work HP Thunderbolt dock.4) Works with USB-C marked PD or Thunderbolt.Finally, I would like to say the PD port can get confusing another port format for USB-C. At first, I thought it did not work as I plugged in USB-C devices, and it was not recognized. Then I upgraded to a new Samsung phone with USB-C PD charging support, and it charged and was recognized from the PD port on the dock. So the device has to support USB-C PD. As I said, USB-C is confusing. Now, what are you waiting for? Buy this best Dock on Amazon; it is excellent for Windows and, more importantly, great with Linux. If you have Thunderbolt, the big thing you get from a Thunderbolt dock is the quicker data transfer speeds, but you need to know your laptop supports it. For me, I don't care about my work laptop having maximum data transfer speed. As I said, Thunderbolt does work with this also from what I can tell.1/26/2024 - I am updating this because I also wanted to note that this works great with the Surface Pro 7. The only exception is the built in wired network does not seem to get recognized, but I don't care. I am using WIFI anyway. So Sound, video, and all other devices work perfectly."
    },
    {
        "review": "With this dock I can connect my laptop with a single cable to connect to a second monitor, ethernet, and power. It also has additional USB ports and an SD card reader, but I personally don't use any of those.Overall, it's super convenient. It's even better if you add a magnetic USB-C adapter, just make sure to get one that supports both high speed data and high power charging. I got a right-angle 240W+40Gb/s connector from Magtame (https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B0CWGHXVFK), and it works great with this dock.My one complaint with this dock is that every now and then, video out doesn't work. My laptop says it can see the monitor, but my monitor doesn't see the laptop. When this has happened, disconnecting my laptop, turning the dock off and then on again, and then reconnecting my laptop fixes the issue. It's only happened 5 or 6 times, and I have used the dock hundreds of times. (On a typical day I connect my laptop to the dock 2 or 3 times, and I've had it for almost a year and a half.) Overall, it's a huge timesaver."
    },
    {
        "review": "**UPDATE**  Now my rear USB port has died--the only one of two standard ports that didn't hiccup and glitch.  This thing worked FAIR (front USB port always glitchy) for about a year and a half--MINUS any Mac capacities--and now is essentially an aftermarket power brick.  The combination of misrepresented functionality (no Mac support despite showing Mac use), faulty design (front USB unreliable, irregular power supply to dual monitors), and poor reliability (multiple ports and functions dead within 18 months) have serious undermined my former respect for this brand.DO NOT BUY THIS PRODUCT.(ORIGINAL REVIEW:)Disappointing.First, it was not clear to me when I purchased this that it would not allow me to extend screens from my MacBook. There is now a slide that shows that all Mac screens will be identical. It’s possible I missed that a year and a half ago at purchase, but I think I would have noticed. In any case, claiming this is Mac compatible is inane. I don’t know anyone who purchases a docking hub so they can see 3+ screens of the SAME THING.Mac users, beware.I was willing to attribute that to careless comparisons on my part—partly based on a very high opinion of Anker in general. BUT unfortunately the problems do not end there.Though there are two standard USB ports, the way in which this device is wired makes it difficult to use them. If I place my keyboard dongle in the front USB port, and insert a flash drive into the USB-C port next to it, there is suddenly an irregular lag to my mouse movements. I cannot work all day with my mouse lagging.Of course I can move the dongle to the rear of the unit, but now my printer cable is projecting from the front and that’s the whole point of having rear USB ports. In short, why design the ports in a way that they interfere with each other?ANOTHER ISSUE is flickering in my monitor screen sometimes. I looked online and the only solution I found is turning down the refresh rate because “60 Hz on each screen is a lot for the hub to handle”. I thought 60 Hz was pretty much standard so again I’m not sure why a device would be advertised as handling up to three screens if two was going to tax it. This problem also seems intermittent but it ought to happen never.LASTLY, just this past week, the device suddenly can no longer extend more than one screen on my Windows work computer. I’ve tried two computers and multiple cables but whatever I do Windows will not recognize that I have two monitors attached and allow me to extend them. Both monitors are actually live and connected but show the same image, so somehow the hub has lost the ability to differentiate between them.Now the hub is useless. I have disconnected my second monitor from the hub and connected it directly to my laptop. I have actually moved my keyboard/mouse dongle and attached it directly to the laptop as well. This removes all purpose for having a hub at all. And again, this is for my Windows work computer—my MacBook has all these issues but would not extend screens even when the hub was fully functional.I bought this because I had faith in the Anker brand and because it was slightly less expensive than the hub Apple promoted on its website. I regret this purchase. Now I have a $150 hub that combines only two of the five cable connections I connect and disconnect every time I move my computer from work to home.Very, very disappointed. Pay more to get a better and more reliable product."
    },
    {
        "review": "I wanted to get rid of my desktop and just use a laptop. I'm used to using a desktop at home and wanted to keep using my external keyboard, mouse, and two large monitors when I'm home. I bought an Asus laptop that is fast and has lots of storage. With this docking station, it works perfectly for me.I keep the monitors, keyboard, and mouse plugged into the Anker docking station. I have one USB-C cable that connects to my laptop. This one cable charges the laptop and connects it -- via the docking station -- to the monitors, keyboard, and mouse.When I travel, I unplug the one USB-C cable and I'm ready to go. When I return, I plug in the one USB-C cable, turn on my docking station and monitors, and I'm up and running as a desktop.I tried a cheaper docking station (LIONWEI USB C Hub) first. It mostly worked but required two USB-C cables (one to charge the laptop, one to connect to the docking station) and it was not stable. It would often glitch out and require me to reconnect or reboot.The Anker docking station has been very solid and, in 4 months has never glitched out or required any type of rebooting.Pro tip: I also use an \"aucun Magnetic USB C Adapter\" for the USB cable. This connects via a magnet so I don't even have to plug and unplug the USB cable, I just pull off the magnet."
    },
    {
        "review": "Original 2 star review:I don't have a lot of experience with docking stations like this, outside of the ones I've used with my work laptops. But this one has generally been a disappointment. My keyboard regularly has problems when I switch computers or even turn off the docking station and turn it back on. It's 50/50 whether it will turn on my keyboard, or my keyboard will detect it, or it will detect my keyboard, whatever. I often have to mess with it for a second to get everything to work. But today it seems to have given up on my keyboard for good. No USB port on this unit will detect my keyboard. I plug it directly into the computer, and it's fine, back to the docking station, nothing. I have Kinesis Freestyle RGB keyboard.UPDATE: Anker reached out to me and sent a replacement and this one does seem to work better with my keyboard. I have not had the issue mentioned above in the week or so that I’ve been using it."
    },
    {
        "review": "when i bought this hub i had a couple of problems with the hub right out of the box. i was busy fixing other things on other machines and missed the Amazon exchange window and reached out to Anker via their email providing them with a description of the problem, it was dropping a monitor and eventually was cycling my ethernet off and on constantly.their customer service returned my email on the same day and flawlessly provided me with things to check and eventually sent me a new device and a shipping label for the bad one. just hooked up the new hub and it's running like a new buick. this is my second all i one hub i bought from Anker and i will continue to buy their products in the future for the quality of their products and that of their customer service.Thank you Anker!"
    },
    {
        "review": "FINALLY!  I have a lenovo laptop for work.  I have a different lenovo laptop for personal use.  My old dock was a thunderbolt 3 and with the latest teams update on my work laptop my audio would cut out when I could screen share on teams.  I also noticed suddenly the keyboard and mouse got wonky on registering activity.  well turns out my old doc was just dying.  that's all there was to it.  so I bought an expensive $$$$$ lenovo universal dock.  and then all of a sudden the 2nd monitor would not display no matter WHICH laptop was plugged in.  Updated drivers.  Updated firmware.  Power cycled.  Nope.  Returned.  Bought another off brand dock.  Monitor would work with my personal lenovo laptop but not with my work laptop.  WTF am I going insane here?  What happened to plug and play.  Returned.  Bought another off brand and now the speakerphone for conference didn't work.  Bought a new conference speaker phone (which I was going to do anyways) but it still didn't work and it wouldn't charge if I hot plugged in.  Returned.  Bought this dock.  FINALLY EVERYTHING WORKS!  NO ONE BREATHE!  Although admittedly now the SD card is stuck in the SD card slot.. and I fear I may need a pair of pliers later to resolve that issue.. so maybe the SD slot is not okay.It's 135w power supply that comes with the dock (nice for the price!)the USB Ports all work and there is one on the front for your keyboard/mouse - the rest are on the back.it's quiet.  Anker is good about not having whining USB ports.  So if you're one of those that thinks you have tenitus all the time because your electronics whine... you'll appreciate that their USB ports do not.HDMI port works!!!!!!  So if you're using an old school cheap a$$ monitor that doesn't have Display Port Tech, you're good still.Power button is a nice touch because now you can power off the dock which will turn the external display off and any additional devices that have lights will turn off also.. so if you're home office is in your bedroom, this is a value add for you.It is compact enough to travel with but it's more block or brick style than slim pocket style.Rubber slip pad on the bottom is a nice touch to keep the power cord from pulling it off the back of your desk when you're not looking.Ethernet port also worksOh and my USB Hub extension that's plugged into the dock also seems to be working just fine although it has it's own external power supply so if you're daisy chaining just keep in mind you may need more than one power supply here."
    },
    {
        "review": "- edit -Anker reached out to offer a full refund after hearing about my issues even though it had been more than 30 days since my purchase. I'm hoping the issues I had are an isolated incident and it doesn't reflect every unitYou get what you pay for. You only need to hook up half of what it offers? Fine. More than one monitor? Not happening. Worked fine for about 2 weeks and then decided to give up. I can only run a single monitor at a time through it now. Replaced cables and didnt help. Display port works great until you hook up an hdmi and then they both go nuts bouncing back and forth and neither working correctly. Restart the system 37 times and it works. Turn on pc the next day and you have to start everything over again."
    },
    {
        "review": "It does not work 100%, it is not compatible with my HP PC, it does not work for the Monitor, everything else such as external keyboards, microphone works well, but the main thing is the connection between laptop and monitor does not work well."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good quality, fast and powerful for my laptop. As they say you get what you pay forIt would be awesome if it had 1 extra USB or USB-C, but that is just for my specific use.However in general it works great, no issues and replaces my laptop charger (meaning powerful enough to charge of the latest Asus laptops, and all the other devices connected to it"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is for the \"Anker Docking Station, PowerExpand 13-in-1 USB-C Dock, 85W Charging for Laptop\"** Update March 9th, 2023 **So pretty much everything in the original review stands, but there is one issue that I touched on originally about the display refresh rate and resolution that I can clarify now.To get the resolution how you want it, you need to set the monitors to the same refresh rate first, then once the resolution is set and applied, change the refresh rate. That being said, I'm not sure if the refresh rate actually changes or if it just says it does.** End of update **So I just got this about an hour ago, and everything just worked! Both in Ubuntu 22.04 and Windows 10.Here's my setup. Everything is plugged into the dock and only the single USB-C dock cable is plugged into the laptop.Laptop: Dell XPS 13 93701TB SanDisk USB-C SSD (for booting into Ubuntu)2x Monitors + laptop monitor (One monitor is Ultra Wide 2560x1080 144Hz, other is 1080p 60Hz)- Note: I'm using one DP port and one HDMI portWireless keyboard/mouseSpeakersEthernet connection for InternetFirst of all, I was using a Dell TB16 dock before this but I had multiple issues with it. Long story short, I don't recommend the Dell docks, especially if you use Linux.** Long story about Dell TB16 Dock **Using the Dell dock, my monitor would randomly go out, and the network only connected at 100mbps, the soundcard is super quiet (even at max volume), it's always on and can get noisy when it heats up (it has a fan), the cable is built into the unit (non-replaceable) and is only about a foot and a half long (coming out of the left side of the unit) and the unit itself is rather large. Also, I boot off a USB-C SSD drive for Linux (which allows me to plug the USB SSD into almost any computer and boot into my Ubuntu OS); For whatever reason, I could never get it to show up as a boot option when plugged into the dock, even with ThunderBolt boot support and pre-module stuff enabled.** End of the Long story about Dell TB16 Dock **Now the Anker dock on the other hand is about a 1/4 of the size of the Dell dock, connects @ 1GB with no problem, allows me to boot off my USB-C SSD, has a power button to turn it off when not in use, it's silent, works without having to install any drivers on either Windows or Ubuntu, comes with a decently long USB-C cable (3 ft?) to plug into the computer (which can also be replaced and yes it charges my laptop too) and the sound volume is great.There is one oddity that happened, but I was able to fix it easily enough and it's likely not a problem with the dock but a software issue in Ubuntu. When changing the resolution + refresh rate in Ubuntu, it didn't properly scale the resolution (the resolution was right, but much of it was cut off or shrunk down as if the monitor itself didn't change resolutions but the computer did), I had to change the resolution + refresh rate to something else, then to what I wanted to make it work. To be clear, it worked when I plugged it in initially, but changing it to my preference required multiple changes to set it in properly. Once set, it worked as normal.I highly recommend this dock!"
    },
    {
        "review": "1 StarI was very happy with this docking station when I first purchased it. It worked well for several months, charging my laptop and running my dual-monitor setup without any issues.Unfortunately, the dock developed what appears to be an internal hardware failure while still under warranty. It stopped charging my laptop properly, and my monitors constantly disconnected and reconnected.To rule out every other possibility, I purchased an Anker Prime 14-in-1 Docking Station using the exact same laptop, monitors, cables, and power outlet. The new dock worked perfectly immediately, confirming that the original 13-in-1 dock was defective.What turned this into a one-star review was not the product failure itself. Electronics can fail. It was the warranty experience. I have spent over a week providing proof of purchase, completing troubleshooting, answering repeated requests, and following up multiple times. The process has been slow, confusing, and frustrating, with incomplete return instructions and unnecessary delays.I expected Anker to stand behind its warranty. Instead, I have had to escalate my case and file a Better Business Bureau complaint in an effort to have my warranty honored.Based on my experience, I cannot recommend this product. A good warranty experience is just as important as a good product, and unfortunately Anker has fallen well short of my expectations."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've had this dock for almost a year now (a year this month). As with all the WFH situation, it was purchased for that scenario and did it's purpose. Though it did get hot, the functionality was still there and I had no complaints.A few weeks ago, as per my morning usual, I roll out of bed and log onto work, except I noticed nothing was on. The dock was powered off (which I never power off). Any attempts to power it back on was futile. I even disassembled it from my work setup to try another outlet, different host device, etc and nothing. What I did notice was that it when it was plugged in, it was emitting this high pitch sound. Yeah, it sounded dead.After mulling around a bit, I decided to reach out to Anker. I thought maybe it wouldn't go anywhere .. but seeing how I paid a hefty price for this, I wouldn't expect it to die within a year.Anker supper was amazing. After supplying the proof of purchase and proof of the defect (It turned out it was the power supply that gave out), they asked me to ship it back to them and refunded me the shipping cost.Once I did, a new one was dispatched and I received it today. Looks like a different power supply as well compared to the previous. I quickly hooked everything up and everything appears to work fine. I hope this one lasts longer!Everything was done quick and efficiently with no hassles. I appreciate the customer service, 5 stars for that."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "It is really tough to find a reasonably priced USB-C hub that can handle data throughput and a reasonable amount of power delivery, but thanks to UGREEN, I found exactly what I needed.Here's the deal- it's a simple hub. In order to get power to the hub you need to meet one of two conditions:1. The port you're expanding already supplies 100w of power.2. Plug in an external 100w USB-C power supply into the PD-IN port of the hub.Due to the laws of, well, physics, you won't get that 100w at every port if you only plug in a single 100w power supply. Supplying more than 100w of power would also fry this board, because it's just not rated to handle that much current. Designing circuits to handle that much wattage is expensive, and it's why other hubs are so much more expensive than this.Now that being said, for my use case, this little hub is perfect. I run a sound card, a phone charger, and an arduino off of it. It provides just the amount of ports I need in a compact package to make my desk space much more organized. It's a simple hub that does the trick for simple tasks quite beautifully.Enjoy!"
