    {
        "review": "This keyboard offers a truly premium feel at an excellent price. The build quality is solid, the keys are responsive and comfortable, and typing on it is a pleasure. Everything feels well made and reliable, making it great for both work and everyday use."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this keyboard after spending some time looking for a wireless mechanical keyboard that I could use for both work and gaming, and I’m really happy with my purchase.What I like most is the feel of the keys when typing and the sound. It has that characteristic mechanical keyboard sound that is very satisfying without becoming annoying when I’m working for several hours. I also think the 75% size is perfect: it takes up much less space on my desk without sacrificing the keys I actually use.I also really like the way it looks. It feels stylish, solid, and much more polished than I expected. The wireless connection has worked very well, and being able to switch between devices is really convenient.I mainly use it for work and do a lot of typing, so it was important to me that it be comfortable as well as nice-looking, and so far it has completely met my expectations.When I bought it, I didn’t realize that the letters were printed on the front of the keycaps rather than on top. It only took me a few hours to get used to it, though, and now I actually like it because I think the lettering may wear off more slowly.The only thing I didn’t like is that it doesn’t have a dedicated “Home” key. It has an “End” key, but not “Home.” That said, the software makes it incredibly easy to remap keys and customize their functions, so it works perfectly for me anyway.I also like that you can choose from several colorful lighting effects, although I usually keep the lights turned off because I find them a little distracting while working. The keyboard is somewhat heavy, so I would consider it more of a desk keyboard than something you would want to carry around, which is completely fine for my use."
    },
    {
        "review": "Honestly one of the best keyboards I’ve ever felt. It is weighted so it won’t slide around, all of the components feel solid and well made and the keys have that creamy cushioned feel to them. This is extremely high quality for the price point. I am extremely happy with this keyboard. The RGB light configurations are also touch sensitive for a couple of the settings and the knob seems solid, although I wish it clicked with the turns, it is all in all a beautifully minimalistic addition to any workspace!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this keyboard for my husband and switched the keycaps to fit his aesthetic as well as so he can read a bit better. (He’s new to the computer club used to be console and he’s got bad eyesight from the military.) so in the picture the keycaps are changed but not the switches! The original keycaps it comes with is a grey with the letters, symbols, and words on the side.Even though I swapped them out I want to state they are absolutely excellent quality, super soft matte feel, and low profile. I kept the keycaps for future use instead of tossing them or offering them up to my family.Which is what I usually do with manufactured keycaps. So, that’s how good they are! That and they honestly are an absolutely gorgeous color in person despite just being greytones.Now onto more of the benefits this keyboards the forth of fifth I’ve customized (I just customize as a hobby for friends and family)This one though is the first of this brand and oh my gosh do I regret not buying this brand sooner! Mainly for myself because of all the bonuses with this keyboard. Haha.Firstly, the keyboard came in awesome packaging and when I realized it can be used wirelessly too! Absolutely fantastic! I didn’t check for that before purchasing so it was a super awesome surprise!And the quality too my husband has absolutely loved getting to use his new keyboard and he got so giddy with the RGB too.I feel like building wise too this is a great keyboard for beginners. If you want a nice keyboard that has easy hot-swappable capabilities this is definitely where to start!And the volume button is such a bonus too. It makes accessibility count with this keyboard. That along with how compact it is too it takes such little space. To put into retrospect his desk is only I think 38 inches width and he still has plenty of room on each side of his desk for his snacks and drinks because of how little space the keyboard is.Literally no issues have come from the keyboard, it even with his wonky usb ports (a custom built computer composed of old/used parts.) it works well and doesn’t have any connectivity issues.So, to put all the perks together:-compact and comfortable-easy to swap keycaps around and switches—switches it came with are the perfect click for those who don’t like it to be an overbearing sound.—perfect for beginners of customizing keyboards.-Volume nob to control volume with easy access rather than fighting around your desk to get to the speaker control.-has wired, Bluetooth, and dongle capabilities which cleans up wire management.— Battery life is great so charging doesn’t happen as often and it lasts even longer with rgb turned off.- I almost forgot: also, has amazing presets of rgb colors that are fun and is perfect for zoning out to when you are having writers or artist block.Now, for the jealousy caption. I need to save up to get me one too! Because, this keyboard is everythingggg and I am definitely in need of an upgrade from my wired keyboard as well as all the perks of this keyboard.Definitely, looking forward to customizing one for myself down the line! This company is for sure my go too for keyboards. I highly recommend!For now I’ll just enjoy seeing the smile on my husband’s face every time he looks at his keyboard while we game together! The best Christmas purchase I’ve ever made!"
    },
    {
        "review": "The AULA F75 Pro isn’t just a keyboard — it’s a life upgrade. From the moment I unboxed it, I knew this thing was built different. The build quality feels premium in a way that makes everything else on my desk feel insecure. The weight, the finish, the layout — chef’s kiss.But the sound? Oh man… pure mechanical ASMR. Every keystroke is a buttery, creamy “thock” that makes typing feel less like a task and more like a treat. It’s so smooth and satisfying I found myself typing things I didn’t even need to type — emails, notes, grocery lists, lorem ipsum paragraphs — just to hear it sing.Key layout & switch orientation: One thing I absolutely love is how the keys are south-facing, sitting slightly inward instead of pointing outward toward you. It gives the whole board a cleaner, sleeker look and makes the RGB shine through in a softer, more premium way.Wireless performance: flawless. Zero lag, zero hiccups, zero drama. It connects instantly and stays rock-solid whether I’m gaming, working, or just vibing on YouTube comments.Battery life: absurdly good. I swear this thing runs on wizard energy because I keep checking the percentage and it keeps refusing to go down.RGB: The lighting is vibrant without being over-the-top, and the multi-function knob makes switching effects, brightness, and colors stupidly satisfying. One smooth twist and you can go from a calm ambient glow to full-on gamer neon. It’s intuitive, responsive, and makes controlling the RGB feel extra premium without the need of a software which is a plus.Overall? This keyboard is elite. Legendary. A masterpiece of clicky, thocky engineering. If you want something that feels incredible, sounds incredible, and looks incredible, the AULA F75 Pro is the one. I absolutely, genuinely loved this product."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm impressed. Coming from a 4 year old Corsair mechanical keyboard with cherry mx switches, I had high hopes that this keyboard would get the job done. It lacks dedicated media keys, but you still have media keys by using the function key.The battery is excellent to start; I hope it lasts just as long as my Corsair did. The weight is a little beefy despite it being mostly plastic and thicc. The size is perfect if you intend to use it on your lap or TV tray. There are many different LED modes as well, which is nice, as well as the fact that you can set these modes without having to install some sort of app to control them.While I primarily use the 2.4 connection, I do like the fact that it supports 3 bt devices. The response rate is very good and on par with the Corsair. You will have to get used to the arrow key layout with end, pg up, pg dn, and delete being in one single vertical row.Probably the most intriguing thing that made me want to even look at it was the front etched keys, which were perfect for my use case. I usually rest the keyboard on a shelf slightly higher than my shoulder when I sit so the keyboard is closer to my eye level than my hands. These keys make it easier to quickly see and press some custom commands I have instead of having to pick up the keyboard, sit it on my lap or tray, and then use my commands.The key presses are also very muted from the cherry mx switches, which is a plus and slightly more satisfying.Overall, I'm Impressed.(update as of 3-15-26)Nearly a year later, the battery's lifespan has decreased to about 50% of its original capacity."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've had this keyboard now over a year, having purchased it in April of 2025 (Review written in August of 2026).I've had virtually zero issues with this keyboard, I work daily on my computer for hours at a time and have likely logged close to 8-10 million keystrokes on select keys. All of the keys from the factory still function well and I have not modified or changed anything with the keyboard.The included dongle works well, I recommend the 2.4ghz mode as opposed to Bluetooth for decreased latency and range, although the Bluetooth functionality works well within a room. The battery life meets the companies claims, and charges within that spec as well. I recommend keeping some kind of type-c cable nearby to charge it, either directly from your computer or a wall-outlet.Typing on this is great, I can type at roughly 98 wpm consistently on this keyboard at roughly 97% accuracy. I find the typing experience to be enjoyable, but light. The keys have a marble-like sound to them, and are soft and easy to actuate. The travel time is fairly generous, but soft, if you have a propensity to \"fat finger\" keys you'll more than likely have some accidental presses if you rest your finger on certain keys. This tends to happen less with general typing and more when resting my fingers of the WASD keys as I play games often.For gaming I think this is great, but keep in mind that certain games require more keys (Star Citizen, Arma, or other complex sim style games). You will be making a sacrifice going with a 75% keyboard on accessibility. However for competitive titles, or most general games, this keyboard is a great addition. In games like Counter-Strike 2, Rematch, and Battlefield 6 I've been able to maintain my K/D and win percentages having come from a wired gaming keyboard previously. The included function keys are nice as well, which you can use to rebind or bind certain actions with if you're into that degree of customization. I've used this to extend the keyboard a bit having given up the keypad and other function keys.The design and build quality are good as well, I've dropped this keyboard twice during cleaning and noticed no visible damage from both 3-foot falls. Cleaning is simple as well, the included key-stripper is easy to use and pops the key-caps out without much issue.If you're into customization, you can replace the key-caps to your liking. I've recommended this to a friend who swapped his key-caps with metal ones, to which he's enjoyed. I prefer the feel and texture of the included keys. The plastic is generally strong and not-flexible, you won't notice any \"deck\" flex when pressing on the keyboard. The volume knob is a surprisingly nice feature, which you can turn to raise / lower the volume, as well as being able to push the dial in to mute / unmute. The included RGB lighting is bright and easy to read.The only pain point is sometimes the receiver will kick out, which has happened more than a handful of times across the year and some change I've owned this keyboard. Frankly the fix is simple, switching the keyboard on and off resolved the problem immediately.I think for the value this is a steal, I've recommended this keyboard to just about everybody and will stick with both the brand moving forward."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’m honestly really impressed with the AULA F75 Pro Wireless Mechanical Keyboard. Right out of the box, it feels like a premium product. The build quality is solid and has a nice weight to it, so it stays firmly in place and doesn’t slide around during use.One of my favorite things about this keyboard is the sound. The clicking is super satisfying without being overly loud—it just has that perfect mechanical feel that makes typing enjoyable.The RGB lighting is another standout feature. At first, I didn’t realize there was software available, but once I found the link at the bottom of the product page and installed it, the level of customization blew me away. You can really dial in the exact colors and effects you want.Overall, this keyboard exceeded my expectations. Great feel, great sound, and excellent customization options. Definitely a solid buy."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good"
    },
    {
        "review": "It’s awesome sounds good just very loud, and takes a bite to get used to"
    },
    {
        "review": "A legendary keyboard in its shape. Its price is excellent and its sound is awesome. Its presence, to be honest, feels heavy in it and feels tired. I strongly recommend it."
    },
    {
        "review": "First of all, if you don't have much knowledge of mechanical keyboards and want to start with this keyboard, it is a very good option. The quality is felt from the moment you take it out of the box, it is a heavy keyboard and that does not mean it is bad, it feels very good to type on it, plus the sound is quite different from a keyboard that normally comes with a new computer or that you simply bought a cheap one at a Walmart or on the internet, it sounds firm and good when typing. Compared to cheaper keyboards, these mechanical switches are very sensitive, I am not referring to their duration, but to the force necessary to press them since the force needed is minimal, which makes them less burdensome for long days at the computer. It has several RGB lighting modes, for my part I only have it in white and at a moderate brightness, because yes, the brightness of the backlight is quite a bit. Another thing I liked is that you can check the keyboard battery with the key combination: FN + B. Which is very useful since it does not have another battery indicator. Just to conclude, it is an excellent keyboard that is very worth it, and if you find it at a discount, do not hesitate to purchase it."
    },
    {
        "review": "As a developer, it is very important to have a good keyboard. It just arrived and so far the weight, feel, connectivity are excellent. I hope it lasts long enough."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Best keyboard I've ever owned. Sturdy case, sleek profile, the layout makes this keyboard incredibly easy to clean without the need to dismantle the keys in the process. The White LEDs are bright and easy to see in the dark, unlike many backlit keyboards that can make it harder to read the keys. The responsiveness both in typing and in the tactile feel of every key clacking on release is a delight. I was skeptical when the sale brought the price below $35, but I could not be more impressed with the quality upon arrival. As a friend has been using a slightly different G4 model for 5 years, I have every expectation that I will be using this keyboard for years to come. Very happy customer."
    },
    {
        "review": "I ordered one of these after my place of employment got tired of keyboards failing constantly. After several months of outright abusive use by my co-workers, they still performed flawlessly with a lovely sense of tactile feedback, soda spills aside. One failed only after half a thermos of coffee was spilled into it, but that's to be expected from anything not waterproof rated.After ordering one for myself to use at home, I LOVE typing on it. It's my first true mechanical keyboard, and it's pleasant to just tap away, typing whatever I feel like. Each key feels like it's got two levels of depression (almost like me): First a clear 'catch' when the key activates and types, and then another more firm one when you reach the bottom of the key well. I've yet to have it double-type anything even when my clumsy fingers wanted to, although it hasn't saved me from butterfingering my keystrokes in games.As far as gaming goes, it's not PERFECT. It does make an audible noise when typing, so when you are frantically spamming the strafe or jump keys, it's going to sound like clicking the mouse over and over, but it's really no worse than your average mouse. If you're wearing headphones and blasting your speakers, you won't notice it. If you're in a silent small room, you can't avoid hearing it. Find your medium.Its clearly more meant for office work, but I find it good for gaming, and it's sturdy and heavy enough it doesn't move around at all or feel like it'll die if I drop my phone on it."
    },
    {
        "review": "Satisfying but quiet clicks, decent lighting, solid construction, and amazing responsiveness give this keyboard a 5 ⭐ from me. I game pretty much everyday and Logitech is the only keyboard/mouse company I find worthwhile AND affordable. As a major plus, it's also very ergonomic and easy to use for long periods of time."
    },
    {
        "review": "Just got it so not a complete review. But so far, I really like it. I'm not a gamer, but just needed a keyboard upgrade and this is perfect. So far :)Note: True, there are no painted letters/numbers on the keys. Once usb plugged in, everything is seen with the backlight on the keys. I figured out the reason: painted letters and numbers wear off after a while. This see-through letters/numbers (presumably) will always remain. You don't use the keys unless plugged in anyhoo! So, I see no problem!It's very nicely weighted and has a nice feel on the keys. Great for the $35 price I paid!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I like it. Well lit for a dark room, responsive, a little loud in a quiet room but not too bad, lack of a volume control knocks off a star. That should be standard on a gaming keyboard. Not bad."
    },
    {
        "review": "Got tired of going through cheap $15 keyboards where I would wear off the lettering in a few months and indent the cheap plastic keys.  I haven't used a mechanical keyboard in seemingly forever, and I forgot how much I miss them.  The backlighting is not super bright or obnoxious at all (not sure what Dylan is talking about), even with the lights out.  Then again, I have a 43\" monitor, so even with the lights out in the room it's  still pretty bright.....either way, it's a non-issue, and welcome on the rare occasion where I have no lights on in my office.  The action isn't bad - not like a good 1980's/90's keyboard, but good enough.  Since I just got this, who knows about durability/longevity, but hopefully it lasts awhile.  Ergonomics seem to be decent too with my wrist pad.  If you primarily type in a library and aren't allowed to make any noise, then this might not be the keyboard for you."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been very happy with this keyboard for everyday work use. The mechanical keys have a great feel and make typing much more enjoyable, especially during long periods of writing and data entry. The build quality feels solid, and the keyboard has a clean, professional look that fits well in an office setup."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a mechanical keyboard. That means it is noisy where it has the traditional keyboard sound. I personally love it! The key are far more responsive than the soft-touch keyboards that are mostly used today. The clicking of the keys could be distracting to someone sharing an office, however, if you work in a private office, this keyboard is great. Also, the keyboard is backlit which makes it that much more useful when working late nights in a darker environment. It is constructed of metal and has a slip resistant base that stays put on my keyboard slide."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good"
    },
    {
        "review": "I am using this keyboard right now to type this review. I got one of these for work, and I like it so much I bought another one for home. The brown switches have tactile feedback, but no audible click. The feel and sound of these keys while typing is very satisfying. This keyboard is a bit noisy, but I haven't heard any complaints about that so far. I much prefer this mechanical keyboard to my old membrane keyboards. I am still getting used to it, so I make a few typos, but the frequency of them is diminishing. Build quality is excellent, the chassis is aluminum. There is no pointless, annoying RGB backlighting. Only the keys light up, which makes it easy to use in the dark. I would recommend this keyboard. It's more expensive than membrane keyboards, but I think it's worth the extra money."
    },
    {
        "review": "The person who gave it to him loves the Logitech brand and was very happy so I think it's very good"
    },
    {
        "review": "I wanted to wait a couple months before writing this review, give myself time to decide my opinion.Well, I would say it is excellent, one can usually rely on Logitech to provide a good product and this is a prime example.The noise level is not the loudest I have heard from mechanical keyboards. Besides, if you want the responsiveness of a mechanical keyboard, you have to expect and accept a certain level of click-clack.I use it mostly to play games and secondly to write stories, as such it works well. While I do not play professionally, or online, nor care to, during my usage I have not noticed any lag or ghosting.In particular, I like the while lighting, it is not strong, but not weak either, allowing me to see the keys in any lighting situation. Which I appreciate, as the popular RGB keyboards are a bit too \"loud\" for me.It is also very tight and small for a mechanical, and fits comfortably on a small desk.I highly recommend this keyboard!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent, smooth when pressing the keys. Some noise, but I like the sound. It does not have programmable keys. It feels durable."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "This is completely useless without the USB receiver. I checked everywhere for this piece and wasn’t in the box. I told my coworker that I was going to send it back, so they looked it over and found the receiver in the mouse! After adding batteries and plugging in the receiver it worked perfectly and only took two minutes to set up. I opted not to use the mouse in this package because I prefer the ergonomic one I already had. But the mouse works well and looks nice. The keys take a little getting used to and I’m not a fan of the caps lock placement. But once I get used to this keyboard, I don’t think I’ll accidentally hit the all caps button as much. Other than that, no complaints. I'm so relieved that the USB piece was found and that I can now sit anywhere in my office to work in comfort."
    },
    {
        "review": "Love the look, feel, and design of the keyboard.This is not a quiet keyboard. I keep this at home and not in the office for this reason. If you’re looking for that typewriter noise and feel this is your keyboard."
    },
    {
        "review": "I love my new keyboard ⌨️ perfect!!! 😍 very aesthetic 🤎🤍"
    },
    {
        "review": "Last week I ordered a different retro looking wireless keyboard to use with my MacBook Pro. When it came, I loved it. Since most workdays I have my company PC on one side of my workstation and my personal Mac sitting next to it, I decided I needed a similar keyboard for each. I selected this Jar-Owl set as the second one in part because this product listing said you could operate multiple computers near one another with no cross-interference. So far this seems to be working well.What came in this box was simply the keyboard and the mouse. When you turn the mouse over, there is a small USB receiver that you pull out of the mouse. The receiver is plugged into a standard USB port on your pc to provide connectivity to both the keyboard and mouse. If you don’t have a standard USB port on your pc, you will need to purchase an adaptor. The previous keyboard I purchased included a USB to USB-C adaptor, but I haven’t needed it.This keyboard and mouse each require insertion of one AA battery. The batteries were not provided, so make sure you have some available. There are no on-off switches. Startup was easy for me this time because the other keyboard and mouse combo worked in a similar way.The light colors on this keyboard are appealing, and I find it easier to read the lettering and symbols on the light-colored keys than I did the dark keys on my other keyboard. The dark keys look more like classic typewriter keys, however. There are many color combinations available so choose the colors you like best. I’m now using this light-colored keyboard and mouse with my silver MacBook and the dark combo with my black PC.Both the keyboard and mouse work very well and are enjoyable to use. I spend a lot of time at my computers so, for me, they are super fun."
    },
    {
        "review": "Only thing I love of this is the color and the sound of typing. It’s March and the keyboard is already giving out on me. I replaced the battery but still nothing, when I type. Honestly not worth it, so bummed it didn’t last me. The color and set up are perfect…"
    },
    {
        "review": "I love my keyboard, it is actually bigger than I thought. Love smoothness of the keys. And the idea that mouse turns itself off when not in use."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard makes typing a little nostalgic and just a bit more fun.  The keys are round and more spaced out than on a modern keyboard and it reminds me of my father's old-fashioned typewriter that he kept even after getting a computer. I got this to use when I'm working on some writing that I do as a hobby and I switch this keyboard out with my regular, everyday keyboard.  It might sound silly, but it helps me mentally switch from my official 'at work' mode into my 'writing for fun' mode.  Unlike an old typewriter, the keys on this keyboard only need a small amount of pressure to type, although you can 'feel' the keys more than you would on a regular keyboard.  It readily connected to my computer using the included USB receiver without needing any additional software and there's no lag time when typing or waking it up from sleeping.  Overall, it's a fun combo of modern technology with a retro feel."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is such a fun keyboard, and it was super easy to set up. Just install the batteries in the mouse and keyboard, attach the USB Receiver, and you are good to go! No downloading a driver messages on your computer. The keys are fun to use, but it will take some getting used to. Additionally, though the keyboard is of good quality, it is NOT whisper quiet. It will make soft clicking sounds. I like it, but if clicking sounds bother you then this isn't the keyboard for you. The plastic that the mouse is made of is OK, not top quality, which is surprising for the cost. However, it works great. Not sure yet how long the batteries will last, but I will update if I believe the keyboard and/or mouse go through them too fast. Overall, it is a fun accessory to have at work and you will get a lot of compliments!"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Easily the best keyboard I've ever bought, and I've bought some high-tier brands in the past for over a hundred dollars.It comes with a keyswitch tool, extra switches, and does not feel cheap in quality.It also sounds nice when typing. It's that kind-of \"muted clack\" sound. Hard to describe such things but that's my best shot at it.The size is standard array and my fingers feel good on it.I was able to switch out my keycaps for WASD (I prefer circular key caps for those) and the caps do not clash with the other caps what-so-ever.The wireless bluetooth and plugged in modes both work well; I was able to use it wirelessly for about 3 days of near-constant use before having to plug it in.Honestly, I just left it plugged in since then so not sure how well the battery is on a recharge.The lights are appealing, the keys look nice; the color scheme is kinda abstract but I'm not mad about it.Overall, great keyboard, and it's held up for a couple months now with no degradation.Thanks Redragon. Appreciate a good product."
    },
    {
        "review": "I would highly recommend this keyboard and company. It worked great for some time and then within the 2 year warranty period, my num1 key started acting up. I use my num pad just enough for it to be bothersome. I reached out to the company with the issue and supporting documentation and they were kind enough to get me a new one sent out, same model and everything. I love the sound of the keyboard, the ergonomics are fantastic, the size is comfortable, it responds that way you think it would/should and is compatible with every program I use. Great product! And YES, you should buy it!"
    },
    {
        "review": "(TLDR): The “LED” one (less expensive) works/sounds just as fine, the only real difference is the “QWERTY” one has brighter and more vibrant backlighting.If you just care about functionality/affordability, get the cheaper one. If you care more about aesthetics, spend a lil more!- - - - -I have two fellow gamer friends who desperately needed new keyboards, and since I already bought them keycaps, I decided to look for affordable mechanical keyboards for them. Amazon recommended these, reviews seemed promising, but I had another issue.. what’s the difference?? Why is one $10-15 more?? Nothing really explains the difference here and Google couldn’t help me with wtf LED vs QWERTY was (I thought they both were?? 🤨 Idk still learning about keyboards, bear with me here) A few reviews said one was brighter than the other, but other than that, I was unsure why one cost more.One friend only cared about functionality as they work from home, but the other friend very much cares about aesthetics and has a nice gamer setup. So figured, I’ll just get both!So here’s a breakdown of all the notable but pretty settle differences:RBG LED (White Logo)On Box: Sindri Red DragonPrice: Cheapest Option (great for what you pay for, best affordable option)Sound: Red Switches, not “silent” imo but not too loud either (I think this one sounded SLIGHTLY clackier, but friends couldn’t tell difference, maybe I just watch a lot of keyboard videos to notice lol)Base: Black “Metallic” appearance (bottom keyboard in light pic) Felt slightly lighter, not all that noticeable thoughLighting: Not nearly AS vibrant, but still bright enough (top keyboard in dark pic) When you first plug in, the light is stagnant.Functionality: No lag so far, smooth typing, perfect for casual gamers or work useRGB QWERTY (Red Logo)On Box: Rudra Red DragonPrice: Slightly Pricier (still great for what you pay for tbh)Sound: Red Switches, same, not “silent” imo but not too loud eitherBase: Black Plastic appearance (top keyboard in light pic) Felt slightly heavier.Lighting: Much more vibrant (bottom keyboard in dark pic, pic doesn’t do justice, it was significantly brighter) When you first plugin, the light is flowing.Functionality: No lag so far, smooth typing, perfect for gamers and work use- - - - -My review is already long af, but I’ll have my friends update me on overall usage overtime. The “aesthetics” friend plays way more games on their PC, so I’ll be able to tell heavy gamer use for the QWERTY one. While the “functional” friend will be using it for work, as they work from home, so I’ll tell more daily use for the LED one. 👍 I’ll ask more about the settings as well as time goes on, for now, they’re just happy with their new keyboards!"
    },
    {
        "review": "The Redragon mechanical keyboard is a well-built and sturdy piece of hardware. The 11 programmable backlit modes offer great customization for those who enjoy a personalized desk aesthetic, and the hot-swappable feature is a significant plus, allowing for easy maintenance or future switch upgrades. It feels durable and provides the tactile feedback many users look for in a mechanical setup.However, it is important to note that this keyboard is definitely not silent. Mechanical switches are known for their distinct sound, and this model is no exception—it produces a clear, audible \"clack\" with every keystroke. For someone specifically seeking a quiet or discreet typing experience to avoid disturbing others or for use in a focused environment, the noise level here may be a drawback.Bottom Line: A high-quality, customizable keyboard that performs well for gaming and heavy typing. It’s a solid choice if you enjoy the classic mechanical feel, but if a silent workspace is your priority, you will find this model much louder than expected."
    },
    {
        "review": "Works fine so far. Love me some red switches.  A nice, satisfying \"thock\".  It is not too big even though it has the ten key pad. Illumination options are fine.  Good quality and value if it holds up. Got it on sale for $25. That is the price of a decent membrane keyboard. This is mechanical switches so better value."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was on a tight budget and picked out this keyboard but man I did not expect for this keyboard to be THIS GOOD! The material of the keys feels great and they are fairly quiet. The RGB lights are a nice touch and something you wouldn’t expect from such a cheap costing keyboard that so functions great. I love this keyboard and plan to use it for a long time"
    },
    {
        "review": "Very nice bit of kit, I've used this keyboard for over a year and the only issue is one of the number keys stopped providing a backlight. It still functions as a number key but no light. At first, I thought I needed to replace the key connection. But after doing that. It still is not providing a backlight."
    },
    {
        "review": "I wouldn't buy this keyboard for more than thirty dollars, but it goes on sale enough that I believe it's worth buying for the twenty or so dollars the bring the price down to. I've had this keyboard for over a year, and it's worked flawlessly for most of that time. There's not much to say about it, functionally. It's a standard full-sized mechanical keyboard. It's not quiet, but I like how clicky it is, personally.Now, for the reason I don't recommend you buy this for full price. About nine or ten months into owning this item, the keys started to behave finicky. The 'R' key no longer lights up and the 'A' key has a problem with double inputs. I'll give the keyboard this, I use it a lot. I'm a writer and a gamer, so I've really put it through its paces. I'm also not as anal about maintenance as I probably should be. If you regularly dust and clean under the keycaps, it would probably last you a good deal longer.If your like me, someone who puts your keyboard through heavy use, I would go with something more durable. If you only intend to game with it, it should be fine for about a year, which for twenty dollars is a fine deal."
