[
    {
        "review": "I can’t recommend the kisnt KN85 enough. The RGB is bright (although the software isn’t great), It’s a good weight for a plastic keyboard, the colors it comes in are attractive, and the price is amazing. The most important part (to me) is the switches. They are Bsun purple switches, a smooth, creamy linear switch that sounds amazing. I genuinely use these in my other boards. It is made of plastic, but the plastic is sturdy. It is bluetooth, 2.4 ghz, and wired. Overall, this keyboard is worth it. I love this keyboard, i’ve bought two of them!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great board especially at this price point. This really raises the bar for entry level products and makes it much more accessible. This thing will impress most seasoned enthusiast. I could see this thing attaining a similar status to the Aula F75.You get really good acoustics, great switches (lubed BSUN Purples), gasket mounting AND hotswap.I've been typing on it for a few days and the sound tests you see online are very accurate, board sounds very creamy and poppy, which makes for a very satisfying typing experience.The RGB on this board is really bright and has some cool preset animations.Definitely NOT office friendly, I took this thing to the office one day and had to swap it for a normal keyboard because it is quite loud. I enjoy the sound but I don't think my coworkers did. lolUnfortunately I did notice that the space bar seems to make a really loud thunk which doesn't match the sound of the rest of the board, but you can fix this pretty easily with a rubber O-Ring or some foam inside the space bar.And sadly I did not get any spare keycaps with my order, as I've seen other buyers receive in their reviews :(Overall though, really good board and significantly raises the bar for entry level products. This thing will outperform boards that cost twice the price. I am very happy with my purchase and will be buying one for my gf too"
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm shocked at the quality of this keyboard for the price. The main case is plastic but a is very good quality plastic. Obviously, it's not going to be aluminum at this price. The quality of the keycaps is also excellent. They are the more premium PBT plastic rather than the cheaper ABS plastic and they feel great. It even has more features than my other higher end mechanical keyboard as this one not only has wired USB (obviously), but also 2.4 Ghz dongle (which secures nicely in a little cutout of the bottom of the keyboard and held in magnetically), and a whooping 3 Bluetooth channels so you can pair this keyboard with many different devices at the same time.I love the layout as well. It's a little smaller than a TKL layout keyboard, but without any compromises. Typically, I don't like 75% or less because you lose too many useful keys or they have them in a weird place, but this keyboard's layout is perfectly done. You still have all of your Home, End, PgUp, PgDn, Insert, Delete, and Print Screen and Pause/Break keys. The only key missing is the Scroll Lock key which is never used anyway so this is perfect.It has nice RGB that lights up under and around the keys. Also comes with some extra switches and even a decent keycap and switch puller tool.  Even comes a thin, cheap, plastic dust cover which I appreciate.My only complaint whatsoever about this keyboard is that I'm not too crazy about the switches, but that's really a personal preference thing. They're certainly not terrible and I can definitely see other people with different preferences liking these, as actually a lot of other reviewers did really like the switches. But I'll probably be swapping them out, which is another great thing about this keyboard is that they are hot swappable.For those wondering about the sound - the stock keyboard is clacky rather than thocky."
    },
    {
        "review": "Chances are, you've found this keyboard after reading a number of Reddit threads or watching a review or 2 on Youtube, and I can confirm they're all very correct to recommend this keyboard.It's kind of obvious that it's not trying to be high-end, as evidenced by the all plastic build, basic and other creature-comforts that are left out, like volume knobs and little near-useless LED screens, but it is still a solid, well built keyboard with a decent-enough feature set.But, it's a keyboard, so if we're being real, its the tactile feel of typing on this thing along with it's really nice, satisfying \"creamy\" sound (at least that's what I've heard it called, though I still don't quite get why) that makes you want mechanical keyboard like this and this one does the thing real well while also looking pretty dang nice at the same time (I got the gray gradient version with side-printed keycaps. The battery life using the included USB 2.4G wireless dongle is exceptionally good and it also has RGB backlighting with 20 preprogrammed lighting modes, if you're into that thing, but I've got it set to a static color and it looks good enough for me.All of that at this price point makes this a really, really, really good option and one that I can absolutely recommend."
    },
    {
        "review": "It's a truly amazing keyboard, it feels great, looks great, and sounds great. I have only one issue with it, it's loud, but that's only an issue at night. The ergonomics feel pretty good, and the switches are very nice and responsive. Everything feels high quality, especially for this price. Very worth it and affordable.The backlight is a nice touch, it can be disabled and enabled, along with changing the color and speed.Note:\"Mom if you're seeing this, I wrote this review\" -your son."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard has been an absolute dream, and the price cannot be beat. It uses bsun switches (I'm honestly not sure which, I've done some digging but can't find them), of which I got the linear version. These switches are nice, there is no noticeable scratch, a crisp bottom out, and the stabilizers are actually silent with no rattle or ticking. There is a variety of foam included, but they leave a bit of room in the case which keeps the board from sounding muted. There are NO SCREWS, so modding this thing is a breeze (might need a small flathead tho) and you have a lot of options straight out of the box. It also comes with a hotswap board, so you can lube the switches (already lubed, but do what you want) or change them out entirely. There are also FLIPPY FEET (2 STAGE!!) so you can adjust your typing angle. I have heard people complain about the software that comes with the board, but it turns out it is fully compatible with the Signal RGB app. Now I can assign any pattern I want, even outside of the presets! The lighting is very prominent since the LEDs are south-facing. 3 of the lights do not work in the software, but they are on the edge of the board with the modifier keys so they are not noticeable.My ONLY gripe is that the keycaps are not very durable. I have been using mine for a few months now and the tops of the WASD keys are starting to become shiny and smoother. These are likely not PBT, but they are a very thick ABS so the sound stays deeper and fuller. However, a PBT set from a name brand manufacturer costs more than this entire keyboard, so this is an incredibly minor complaint."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard sounds even better than the reviews claim. Before buying, I came across a few comments suggesting that some of the internal padding had been removed or that the overall quality had declined as the keyboard became more popular. After using it myself, I can confidently say that those concerns were unfounded.The build quality is excellent, the typing experience is incredibly satisfying, and the switches feel fantastic. Every keypress sounds and feels premium, making it a joy to use for both work and gaming.So far, I’ve only tested the 2.4GHz wireless mode, so I can’t comment on the wired or Bluetooth performance. However, based on my experience, I have no hesitation recommending it.For the price, this keyboard offers outstanding value. It was absolutely worth the wait, especially considering I ordered it from the US and had it shipped to South Africa."
    },
    {
        "review": "Set up was a breeze, simply plug in. It charges in a few hours.This keyboard is really nice. I like the “creamy” sound other viewers have described. A VERY light touch is all you need to type. I won’t say it’s sensitive, it just requires a light touch of each key. That is something I need to get used to.I have a laptop set up on a riser, and needed a keyboard that will fit in the small space beneath it. This was my choice after reviewing MANY keyboards. I also wanted a laptop with raised keys, a mechanical keyboard. I like the “old school” feel of it.Weight: it’s heavy. I won’t be traveling with it, so that doesn’t matter to me. But I love the heaviness of it. Feels secure in its spot.Ergonomics: there are two levels if you want to change the angle. Three is you count not using the little feet at the back. I also have a padded hand/wrist rest for extra comfort (NOT INCLUDED) but the keyboard itself is comfortable to use.Color: I would have preferred black to match my desk accessories and laptop, or blue my favorite color. I went for the least expensive option as the color doesn’t change the effectiveness of the device.Lights: it’s advertised as a gaming keyboard. I’m not a gamer, nor will I ever be. You CAN turn off the lights. Simply read the paperwork that comes in the package to know how."
    },
    {
        "review": "The keyboard exceeded my expectations in the essentials: very pleasant “thock” sound, firm keystrokes and three connection modes that work without problem. The construction feels solid; The plastic is thick and clean to the touch, of better quality than in other models in its range. Points in favor: Excellent feel and linear response. Bass-creamy sound from the factory, without the need for mods. Very good value for the price: it is close to “custom” performance of higher ranges. Points for improvement: The administration software was useless on two PCs. The page is confusing, and although the keyboard was recognized, it did not offer real configuration options. The back uses a simpler plastic, and the non-slip rubbers are glued with double-sided tape that began to peel off from the first day. I am worried that they will be lost or will need to be replaced soon. Does not include a numpad: ideal for gaming and writing, but can be limiting for those who use numeric input a lot. Conclusion: An excellent gateway to the “custom” world for its sound, touch and connectivity, as long as you do not depend on the software or need a full keyboard. Separate note (does not influence the product rating): tracking took four days to update; The package arrived without damage, but the logistics process, communication and professionalism on the part of the seller can improve."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great Product"
    },
    {
        "review": "Honestly, I am absolutely blown away with the quality of this kisnt KN85, it's hands down the best keyboard I have ever owned. First of all, the sound quality of the key click is insane, this thing is so thocky with the bsun switches, PBT double shot key caps, gasket mounts and multiple layers of dampening foam/silicone, genuinely such a pleasure to type with. It is even hot-swappable if you want to change or upgrade your switches. The battery life is amazing, first use never charged out of the box lasted over a full week of heavy daily use.I find it very hard to find any fault with this keyboard whatsoever. One note is that the LED's are south-facing, so shine-through backlit keycaps won't work, I like double-shot caps for the thock, so this doesn't bother me one bit. But seriously, I have never been so happy to be typing at my desk. It has changed my whole experience. I watch reviews of £200-£500 keyboards, and I still think this is better.All I will say is get it fast, as prices are constantly rising due to demand."
    },
    {
        "review": "looks and feels amazing, setup was easy and battery is lasting along time which is great.  but the sound of the keyboard is so creamy, it really does sound like a premium keyboard. I also love the beige color scheme its very clean yet retro and the letters are bolded and of course its not a lousy 60% so you can enjoy all the key functions without having to press FN all the time"
    },
    {
        "review": "Awesome keyboard that punches above its weight. Stock switches are louder than I'd like, but hey it's a custom keyboard, so just swap them out easily for anything you like. For the price it's great keyboard. ONLY major issue is I like separate numpad controllers (I put these on the right of my mouse so it's quicker) but Kisnt only make these seperate numpads for the two other colour schemes that exist. The beige and green scheme is missing."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "This is my first mechanical keyboard, and I finally understand what all the hype is about.The sound is incredibly satisfying. It has that nice mechanical sound without being overly loud or obnoxious, but it also isn't so quiet that you lose the satisfying typing experience. The typing feel is probably my favorite part—the keys feel incredibly smooth and satisfying, and so far everything has felt flawless.The build quality is also really impressive for the price. It has some nice weight to it and doesn't feel cheap or flimsy at all.The smart display is a fun addition. Is it necessary? Absolutely not. 😂 But it's definitely nice to have. You can use it for things like Bluetooth connection and volume settings, and you can also customize it with your own photo or GIF.The one area that could definitely be improved is the software. It's not particularly user-friendly, and finding the correct software to customize the keyboard took me longer than it should have. Once I actually found it, though, it wasn't too difficult to figure out with a little patience. The software itself also feels a little chunky and dated. That said, this has zero effect on the actual performance of the keyboard, so it's a very minor complaint.I've had absolutely no issues with wireless connectivity. I've used both Bluetooth and the included cable and haven't experienced any disconnections, lag, or other problems. One thing to note is that you'll need to use the included cable when connecting the keyboard to the software for customization.The battery life has also been surprisingly good so far. I haven't actually had to charge it yet, so I can't speak to the full battery life, but I work from home about 9 hours a day and also casually game in the evenings. After two days of that, the battery has only dropped about 5%.Overall, I have very little negative to say about this keyboard. The software could definitely use some improvement, and there could be better information provided about where to find and how to use it. But that's a very small complaint compared to everything else this keyboard does well.For my first mechanical keyboard, I'm extremely happy with it. Everything about it has exceeded my expectations. 5/5 and definitely recommend!"
    },
    {
        "review": "tl;dr: I am EXTREMELY happy with this keyboard. Build? Beautiful. Sound? Amazing. Functionality? PERFECT. Price? FANTASTIC. If this keyboard fits your specifications for your desk and you happen to work with two different computers at the same time, get it ASAP.More details:Previously I had a significantly smaller RK 68 which was great for me. It was perfect for what I wanted, a small profile and clacky keyboard. Unfortunately, I spilled soda all over it and it died so I had to get a new one. Fortunately, it was this keyboard.In my search for a new keyboard I had a couple things I was looking for:- A green color way to match my set up- A nice clack to it.- A numpad attached.Clearly it's not something very specific. But during my search I came across other features that I had no idea I wanted. But this one hit pretty much what I was looking for so I decided to give it a try and boy did it blow my expectations out of the water.== Design ==Pros:This keyboard looks sleek. Before, to build something like this I had considered paying my friend to build it from scratch but the colorway and the key placements are just so clean. With multifunctional keycaps, they've added the second function on the side of the keycap so you can easily see the primary and the secondary without it feeling too cluttered.The back lighting options really make it fun to customize. They give you default options you can run with and each of those options you can tweak to your liking in terms of color.The digital display seems more like a part of the design the way it links to the volume knob, rather than an after thought like some other keyboards. This provides another option for you to make the keyboard your own by letting you upload a gif to just loop by default.Cons:No complaints really, except the digital display occasionally pops off slightly, requiring you to press it back in but it hasn't become too much of an issue for me yet. Also, uploading the gif to the keyboard takes a good amount of time (something like 5 minutes) , so if they could fix that speed that would be amazing.Oh one more complaint is that it's too tall. I had to get a wrist mount just to feel comfortable typing on it.== Functionality ==Pros:MAN, this keyboard is PACKED with features I never thought I would need. Aside from the aforementioned backlight customization, something that has made my life so much better is hidden behind the digital display. It's base functionality of controlling the volume of your computer works like a charm but it's when you get into the submenus that you can really see where it shines.You see, I am someone who bounces between a macbook pro for work and a windows PC for play and so I'm constantly bouncing between the two. Before, I had a mac specific keyboard and a windows specific keyboard, both occupying space on my desk. This keyboard has eliminated that need in a sleek and elegant way by allowing me to hardwire into my desktop and bluetooth to my macbook. From there, I can easily use the digital display + volume knob to switch my connection between the two. Heck, if I needed to add another couple I could. It seems like I can have up to 5 different devices connected (3 bluetooth, 1 wired, 1 wireless dongle). So if for some reason I had another device, I could easily switch between the two.You might ask yourself, but what about the difference in layout between macs and windows machines? Oh my friend, you see... THEY THOUGHT OF EVERYTHING. Through the same digital display, you can swap the layouts between the two types. I was totally fine with that until I read the manual a bit more and found you could even use a hotkey JUST for that. Remember the secondary functions of the various keys? If you look on the side of the 'A' and 'S' keys, you can see that they read 'WIN' and 'MAC' respectively. So to switch formats, all you have to do is hit 'Fn' + 'A' or 'S' to switch between the two. This really pushed my like for this keyboard to love.Cons:The only thing I can really think of is a nitpick and is possibly even already a function, I'd just have to read through the manual a bit more. It would be nice to be able to use a shortcut to bounce between the different keyboard connections like you can switch formats.== Sound and Feel ==Pros:I am having a BLAST typing this review. Every keypress feels so satisfying as the switches engage and disengage making you really feel connected to the keyboard. The sound is more thocky than clacky which is extremely pleasing to my ears, especially after coming from a clackier keyboard.Cons:While this keyboard is probably 50 percent quieter than my last keyboard, it's still definitely louder than a membrane keyboard, so if you're looking for silent, this isn't what you want. But if you're looking at mechanical keyboards, then this most likely isn't you.== Price ==I feel like this keyboard is well worth the 89 dollar price tag for what you get. I feel like even a couple years ago, you'd have to pay 300 bucks to someone to custom make something like this with the design options. Sure, it's not as bespoke as the true customs, but I feel like it holds up just as well.Overall, I'd give this keyboard a 'have no fear, buy away' if your needs are similar to what I've mentioned above!"
    },
    {
        "review": "First mechanical keyboard I’ve owned, can’t imagine I’d need anything different. Bought two of these, one for my workspace and one for my wife. We both needed accurate and responsive keys for frequent data and password entry, the lights and display are just bonus points. I change out my display for random gifs and RGB weekly using the web portal, easy enough once you get the hang of it, takes 5-10 minutes to upload a gif. Can confirm battery lasts about a week with moderate workday use. Keys are on the quieter end of clacky, not as obnoxious as some mechanical keyboards I’ve used in the past. Key travel registers about 75% from fully depressed which took me a tiny bit of time to adapt to. More often now, I’ll think I missed a letter because I didn’t press quite hard enough, but look back to find it registered after all. Comes with a few replacement switches, key puller, and usb-c cable which is nice. I’m sure there is better quality for more money out there but I’m not the demographic for it, this is all the keyboard I think I’ll ever need."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my favorite keyboard that I own - I do wish that the whole body were aluminum, but for the price this is a fantastic keyboard. I've switched out the keycaps and am currently lubing the keyswitches to give them a slightly less tactile feel and the keyboard is holding up well to my beginner fumblings :) I'm glad I went with this over some of the other beginner, more affordable mechanical keyboards I was considering as I feel like this one is a bit sturdier and offers a more premium experience for the price. I also love the colors, I haven't had any issues with the connectivity, the charge lasts a long time, and the RGB is bright and has a lot of fun modes. I enjoy typing on this one a lot - it's a deeper creamy I would say (maybe on the thocky side I guess) so if you're looking for a more linear feel or a higher pitch you may consider getting this and doing what I did and lubing the switches or changing them up entirely (there are a lot of really affordable creamy switches out there)."
    },
    {
        "review": "Recently I got bit by the creamy keyboard bug and i started my search for a keyboard that would give me that sweet sound that everyone seems to love.After doing some research i came across this keyboard and decided to pull the plug on ordering it. When i received it in the mail the first thing i noticed was the weight. Previous keyboards i have purchased have all felt light and somewhat flimsy/thin. This keyboard is built solid! It has a great weight and quality feel to it as a whole. The keys are exceptional and have the perfect texture that my fingers really enjoy when typing.While i did find the manual somewhat lacking in helpfulness, I was easily able to figure everything out on my own. It was pretty much plug and play and i currently have it in wired mode. The trickiest part was figuring out how to display gifs with the software you download, but i got it. The keyboard screen display is gorgeous and functional, and i absolutely love that i can display my favorite gifs. The knob menu is a bit finicky but you get used to it very quickly.The sound of the keyboard is a true creamy dream to listen to, and makes typing so much more enjoyable. The added cover for when i amnot using the keyboard is a nice bonus. My only complaint honestly is i wish this keyboard was available in more colors! This company is definitely one to watch, I was overall extremely impressed with my purchase. The keyboard definitely looks and feels a lot more expensive than what it costs. Highly recommend if you are thinking of making a purchase!"
    },
    {
        "review": "My first mechanical keyboard and sounds exactly how I imagined! It’s nice solid , creamy click noise! So satisfying!!!I loved the key color combo!  The RGB lights are customizable, down to each key! Super cool. I am overall impressed with my purchase, as I was hesitant to buy something I wouldn’t like the sound of. I was also hesitant because there are so many options but I am glad I picked this! Good value for your money. The keyboard is heavy and feel super sturdy, doesn’t feel cheap. The keys were very responsive to touch and the Bluetooth was easy to setup with my windows laptop.The only thing I don’t like is the look of the keys, they do have a sheen/texture to them I don’t like but honestly, overall, I don’t mind it at all!Great buy!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Today, I initially wrote a negative review of this keyboard due to issues with missed or repeated keystrokes, or keys continuing to fire until another key was pressed. However, after extensive investigation (because I really wanted to keep this keyboard for its mechanical build), I finally discovered that the issues were caused by an obscure, lingering USB driver from a device no longer connected to my PC. That driver was behind all the trouble. I hope this revised review is published before the original one, which is no longer valid.After 15+ years of using Cherry MX Blue switches on a single keyboard that eventually got damaged, I was in search of a replacement. This keyboard seemed like a good option - more compact than my full-size board and featuring “tactile” pale green switches. I hoped they would resemble MX Blues in feel, just without the audible click. Unfortunately, these switches aren’t really that much (or at all) tactile. I don’t understand the claim. There is a point somewhere along the key travel where pressure slightly decreases, but you’d need to press very slowly and have an extremely sensitive touch to notice it. In actual typing, it feels - at least to me - indistinguishable from a regular linear switch. That said, I’ve gotten used to them and didn’t have much trouble adapting after so many years with MX Blues. And the 'thocky' sound still sounds nice.The build quality is solid. The board is sturdy, heavy, and a definite improvement over my old keyboard. I’m not particularly interested in the display or LED lighting. I don’t see the point - especially since the keycaps aren’t translucent, so the lights don’t help in the dark. Omitting those toddler-toy-like features and lowering the price - or populating that space with more keys - would be more useful, in my opinion.I used the included keycap puller to swap the positions of the 'Home' and 'Del' keys, then made the change in the RK software. It took just a few seconds. These settings are retained even if you unplug the keyboard or uninstall the RK software entirely from your PC.All things considered, in terms of mechanical construction, this is a very good keyboard. I do wish the switches were more tactile, but that’s just a matter of personal preference. These switches are still decent, and I’ll probably swap them out in the future for some Kailh clicky switches."
    },
    {
        "review": "When I first got a gaming PC, I figured I didn't really need a super expensive keyboard and that a cheap one that sounded \"clicky\" and had lights was fine enough. I also didn't want to bother with charging a wireless keyboard. However, I started to hear from YouTube videos and TikToks about \"creamy\" and \"thocky\" keyboards. I immediately started to do research, because I was obsessed with the sound. This is one of those rare purchases that I cannot stop thinking about how happy it makes me. I have absolutely no qualms with it. Here's what I love:The sound is excellent. Videos that I watched were a good indicator, but as other reviews have said, they really do not do the keyboard justice for how it actually sounds. The letters themselves are very creamy, while the function buttons (spacebar, shift, enter, backspace, etc.) have a much deeper \"thocky\" sound to them. If you're on the fence, at least try it and return if it's not for you.The functionality is incredible. There are a ton of lighting options that you can play with. You can mess around with them in the software on your PC. But because this keyboard has a small LCD screen, you can also change them directly on the keyboard itself. I have mine set to light up the entire keyboard cyan from whichever key I press, expanding out.Speaking of the LCD screen, you're able to upload GIFS or images to the screen, which is a really cool feature.As far as connectivity, you have every imaginable option available to you. I have my gaming rig connected via the 2.4USB wireless adapter. You can also connect up to three devices via bluetooth. And of course you can opt to hardware in.I love the the keyboard has both a USB-A and USB-C port. I am a huge advocate for technology having USB-C as an option, as it is much more convenient, provide faster transfer times, and faster charging times.Speaking of charging, the battery life on this keyboard is excellent. I seem to get about a week's worth of charge with extreme use daily.I know that the keyboard is hot swappable, meaning you can swap out the switches, which ultimately determine the sound of the keyboard, but personally, I haven't messed with that, as I love the way it sounds now.Overall, this is an excellent keyboard. If you're in the market for a wireless keyboard that sounds great, this is the best option out there! I typically don't like spending a ton of money on stuff unless I am absolutely sure of its value, and let me put your mind at ease that this keyboard is worth so much more than what the price tag shows."
    },
    {
        "review": "Introduction. The keyboard arrived to me new but clearly used. The package was sealed by a second sticker applied on the previous one, the keyboard had fingerprints, the booklet as in the photo, the power cable even pinched, all the bags ruined. In short, if I buy a new product it must be new, otherwise I would have bought a guaranteed used one for less. I can understand a possible return during the withdrawal period but at least the cable had to be replaced. Going to the top of the range Royal keyboard Kludge. The type of switches mounted are not declared, they arrived with the tactiles and they are still excellent, I hadn't had the opportunity to try them. Very pleasant and which cause a minimum of resistance and then lower decisively. I don't mind them at all. The surface of the keys is slightly reduced on the flat part, which helps to avoid accidentally pressing unwanted keys. The sound is quite pleasant, a little dry but not annoying, probably influenced by the rigid structure of the keyboard. Writing is facilitated by the reduced width of the surface of the keys even though they do not have the ergonomic inclination that other keyboards of the same range offer, but the tone on tone on some keys makes them not immediately identifiable. There is no backlight. An absence that I find inexplicable in keyboards that cost over €100 but I see that, excluding the classic gaming brands, there is little to be found. The keyboard has two height levels with rubberized feet, but it is quite high. Personally, I use it without a riser. Full RGB, totally modulatable via software but with the possibility of managing everything via the integrated screen. Good brightness and intense colours. Possibility of connection via cable, included in the package, via Bluetooth and with dongle. The latter with a lateral magnetic seat. It also has two physical buttons. On one corner we find the selection of connection between cable or key and a little further on the on and off button. The control of the small monitor is comfortable and easy to read, as are the LEDs for Black Num and Caps Lock, also present as LEDs near the Esc key. The signs on F, J and 5 are also easily identifiable by touch. The small monitor has a menu function for various settings which can be navigated via a practical wheel. Battery level, date, type of connection, caps lock and block num, if it is in Win or IOS mode. In short, it shows what is needed and is easily readable. It is also possible to upload GIFs limited to 60 images. A little bit and goes to remove the other info. Nice option but I remain of the opinion that, having a small additional monitor available, you should be able to make better use of it. For example with the operating temperatures of the components, the percentage of use, etc. The software is complete. It offers to save 3 different command mappings and fully manage the lighting. Not very intuitive, I have to admit. There is a menu whose use I am not clear about and no information is provided either in the paper manual or in the one available on the site (both in English). Excellent keyboard, overall the one I am finding better with than the others I have tried (and I have tried several in the €50/150 range). It is really a shame about the lack of backlighting as well as, seeing that it is present on other products, the lack of being able to combine the magnetic wrist rest with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "The keyboard works flawlessly and has a really great mechanical feel and sound. The keyboard delivery was very prompt and thank you to the team for providing a hassle free online purchasing equipment"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a great and reasonably priced keyboard. The key sound/feel is super pleasing, and the quick switch between devices/volume/effects with the dial and screen is awesome."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good keyboard, with nice click sounds and fun to type! The build is sturdy and the keyboard is heavy. I am expecting it to last long. I liked the small screen for configuration. There is a time display on the keyboard, which I don't find useful.Once charge last about two weeks with bluetooth connectivity."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my 2nd Royal Kludge keyboard and I am very impressed. I've had mechanical keyboards from Logitech, Keychron, Corsair, Drevo and Redragon and I like my RK ones more. This one in particular is just super satisfying to type on. I love the creamy sound from the keys, it's unlike anything else I've owned so far. The green and clay-red color scheme also looks awesome. Keycaps are soft touch but not shine-through which is a bit of a disappointment but not a deal breaker. The little screen and knob is easy to use and actually useful. It's nice to change settings in a menu vs using function keys, and being able to check the time when enjoying full screen entertainment is also handy to me. My audio setup is also able to use the knob as a volume control which is an added bonus. All three connectivity modes work well for me - Bluetooth is reliable, 2.4Ghz is fast and reliable, and wired mode is also fast and reliable. Range on BT/2.4Ghz is average, but since this isnt really an HTPC keyboard I don't think it matters much.My only complaint is the battery life. RK claims they have a gigantic battery in this keyboard but I only get 2-3 days of use between charges, and it takes a loooooong time to charge so it ends up being plugged in a little more than I'd like. If I keep the RGB lighting on the 2nd lowest setting, I can get another day or two of usage but I wish I didn't have to charge this keyboard once a week.I really like this keyboard overall and I'd definitely recommend it! It's a good keyboard for full price but RK has sales all the time so I'd wait for a 20-25% discount, where it becomes a killer deal in my opinion.Edit: 3 months in and still very fun to type on and 100% reliable. I just keep the backlighting off most of the time now to conserve battery and it lasts a while without the backlight on 2.4Ghz link (2-3 weeks light use). Only thing I don't like after a few months of use is having to press fn + PgDn for the End key."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I’ve been using the RK Royal Kludge R98 Pro for a little over a week, and I’m honestly impressed with how much value this keyboard delivers. The moment you take it out of the box, it feels solid and well-built definitely not your typical budget keyboard.Typing Experience (5/5)The gasket mount design combined with the pre‑lubed linear switches makes typing incredibly smooth and quiet. It has that “creamy” feel everyone talks about. No pinging, no rattling just clean, soft keystrokes. The MDA profile PBT keycaps are thick, durable, and comfortable to type on.Design & Layout (5/5)I love the 96% layout. It saves desk space but still includes a full number pad, which I use constantly for work. The addition of the volume knob is a nice touch and works great for quick adjustments.RGB & Customization (4.5/5)The RGB is bright, vibrant, and offers a ton of effects. Customizing it is easy once you get familiar with the shortcuts. I only wish the software was a bit more intuitive, but it’s definitely not a deal‑breaker.Performance (5/5)For gaming, it’s responsive and stable with zero noticeable latency. Hot‑swappable sockets are a big win if you ever want to switch to different switches later.OverallIf you’re looking for a keyboard that feels like a premium custom build for a fraction of the price, the RK R98 Pro is a fantastic choice. Great for both work and gaming, excellent build quality, and a super satisfying typing experience.Highly recommended!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I upgraded my home gaming setup with this keyboard after considerable research, and it’s been a joy to use for both gaming and typing.Strengths:Premium typing experience: The pre-lubed cream linear switches and gasket mount deliver a “thocky” feel and satisfyingly smooth keystrokes. Typing and gaming are both quiet and comfortable, with five layers of foam dampening any hollow sounds.Customization: The included software lets me remap keys, create macros, and adjust more than 20 dynamic RGB lighting modes quickly. I loved being able to easily tweak the look and functionality.Build quality: PBT double-shot keycaps with an MDA profile feel sturdy and resist wear; they’re grippy and comfortable for long sessions. The detachable aluminum volume knob is a treat for instant control.Layout: The 98-key layout keeps the numpad and arrow keys but eliminates wasted space—a space-saving solution perfect for both gaming and work.Universal compatibility: Works perfectly with Windows and Mac out of the box, and includes both a 2-in-1 keycap/switch puller and extra switches for future customization.Bottom Line:This is an exceptional value for anyone who wants a customizable, quiet, and responsive keyboard for gaming, work, and creative projects. The smooth switches, full-featured software, and premium build make it a standout in its price range. Highly recommended for enthusiasts and everyday users alike."
    },
    {
        "review": "So, i dont usually write reviews, but i felt i had to for this keyboards. the creamy clicks are AMAZING (its my first time having it so im super impressed.) the tiny screen is actually pretty simple and easy to use, you can customize the backlight color and style, speed, etc. the knob works perfect. feels great. it came with extra keys, a usb-c to plug in if you prefer wired, and a key remover. everything feels amazing, very high quality. its obviously not 10 pounds, but carries some heft that feels super secure. its got little folded stands to prop your keyboard up if desired, which i think is super nice. i love the color variety (i got the brown, for reference.), it can be hard finding a nice keyboard that is still stylish and pretty. im extremely satisfied, especially with the price being only 70 usd for this nice of a keyboard. i still have yet to use it, but im pretty confident it will be nice. ill update if anything happens!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I recently purchased this item a couple of weeks ago and so I think it was a good purchase. First of all, they keyboard is super smooth and because of the switches, you do hear some clicks but they're not that loud, just enough to be satisfying. The product feels well-crafted with quality in mind. The keyboard stands; are the perfect size, not freakish large so typing for long periods of time is comfortable. I also like how they send you the \"end\" key in the box so if you don't like the volume knob, you can put the \"end\" key back in. I type 80 words a minute, especially when I am writing programs, I pleased on how well the keys on this board respond and the entire feeling of when a key is pressed. When I type superfast on things that are super important, I don't want my keyboard to fail me.Now, there are a few things I don't really like about it but overall, like I said, this is a great keyboard.- The volume knob: Hey, it works but personally, I feel that it is too close to the \"pgdn\" key so you really can't grip it like a knob. I kind of wished it had groves or something on the dial making it easier to turn. Right now, I just got to try and turn it little by little by spinning the know from the top down to the \"-\" key. That's about all the room you got.- Key position: This one is more of my fault. I am more used to full sized keyboards and not the space saving ones with less keys. The part that messes me up is where the arrows, shift, and enter is all smushed together. If this might also, be you, check out the S98 model instead.- Drivers and App: I can't really download this because the website is blocked at work for gaming lol. I don't know if the site is listed as gaming or if it is just because of their name. I know, this is totally not their fault haha."
    },
    {
        "review": "What a treat! Didn't expect much, but got an amazing keyboard. Got it at a coupon price, but would gladly buy another at full retail.1. Ultra quiet, feels buttery smooth to type on while still acting like a mechanical keyboard.2. Heavy. Like, really heavy. Love it.3. Tons of LED effects.I game and I work from home. 10/10 for computer work, 8/10 for gaming (the keys are just a little bit too sensitive to rest my fingers on without slowly pressing them.Not taking away stars, but a nicer cable would be cool. Maybe something braided with a right angle plug would truly complete to package here.Also, Do Not ship it in just the manufacturers box, there is ZERO padding and its not taped closed. I'm really lucky this thing showed up unscathed."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was looking for a keyboard that had a nice compact profile, looked nice, has a volume control knob, AND sounds great when typing.THIS keyboard literally hits everything I was looking for.It's just the right size and weight - it looks and feels premium.The keys have that creamy/clacky sound I was looking for.The colors of the keys are great - but that being said - the backlights don't seem as bright as other keyboards I've owned, but that's not really a big deal to me - but it might be to some people.Overall - I love this keyboard - now I just hope it lasts.  I've read some reviews that make it seem like it might not, but I'm hoping it's durable.As of this review, I've only had it for about an hour, so I'll update this review if anything changes with time."
    },
    {
        "review": "This keyboard is great, little heavy and only connects via USB, but as far as everything else you'll be pressed to find a better keyboard at this price range that is as satisfying to type on, cause the \"creamy\" switches sound like something that punches WELL above its weight.Nothing necessarily ergonomic about it but it does have a satisfying color to it and the lights aren't exactly offensive in terms of \"too bright\"If you're looking for wireless don't get this one, look at RK's other offerings but if you're okay only hooking up wired for work or other reasons this is going to be the one you want."
    },
    {
        "review": "So I upgraded my desk setup to include this keyboard. I did minimal research but I knew I liked the color and the number pad.After having it for several months:1. The keyboard is indeed \"creamy\" and smooth2. The lighting is fun and the quality of the board is very nice.3. It has a good weight and the volume knob is very helpful.4. I am glad I didn't go smaller5. The key swap capability is nice but I don't use it a whole lot.Overall a good keyboard to replace my Bluetooth work one. I definitely think this a worthy upgrade if you're in need of one."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good & my son loves his new keyboard"
    },
    {
        "review": "\"+\"- Qualitative injection of key plastics,- Flattering appearance and robust chassis,- Not aggressive branding,- Super responsive,- The definition of the RGB backlighting is excellent and the LeD scenarios are good,- Really cool 98% format.- Little parasitic vibration,- Excellent quality/price ratio,- Replacement set of a few switches and wheel replacement key if necessary.\"-\"- Some keys (Shift, Caps Lock, Tab) wiggle a little,- Wheel its is smooth and difficult to turn. However, it is equipped with a high quality mechanism. - The keyboard is high, without wrist rest fatigue is inevitable. - Any attempt at cleaning is in vain: dismantling the keyboard by layer is difficult.\"i\" - The activation distance is very short, my hand resting above the keyboard can be enough to trigger commands. This is an effective way to make the keyboard responsive but reduces the comfort of use in my opinion. - The \"creamy\" sound is not to everyone's taste, but it is a good compromise between mechanical feeling and discretion. It is noisier than cherry brown or the \"brown\" keys of Logitech mechanical keyboards. I really like the aesthetics and sound of this keyboard, I recommend it to informed buyers! :)My rating was 4/5, but the brand spontaneously contacted me to better understand my feedback. I think they have absolutely exemplary customer service."
    },
    {
        "review": "The RK ROYAL KLUDGE R98 Pro convinced me from the first moment. The workmanship is of high quality, the keyboard appears stable and the compact 96% layout offers all the important keys including the number pad without taking up an unnecessary amount of space on the desk. I am particularly impressed by the pleasant typing experience. The already lubricated switches run smoothly and sound pleasantly rich. Thanks to hot swap, the switches can be easily replaced if necessary. The RGB lighting is bright, can be adjusted in many ways and ensures a great look. The rotary control is also very practical in everyday life, for example for controlling the volume. Overall, the R98 Pro offers features that are often only found on significantly more expensive keyboards. It is an excellent choice for gaming, home office and daily use. The price-performance ratio is excellent – ​​I would buy them again at any time."
    },
    {
        "review": "I used to type 60wpm, now its 90wpm in only 1 week of having this keyboard. Its amazing for work!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Let's get straight the points.Advantages:- PBT keycaps. I don't know how to describe it but it feels great. Oh, the colors are nice too.- Support both Windows and Mac- The sound is nice- The Space button feels nice, very stable- Have PrintScreen (PRTSC) button (because I use this button a lot so this is a pro for me)- Have Numpad keys. As an accountant, this numpad never fails me lol.Disadvantages:- Requires a great force to press, but you can enhance this by replacing to other keycap layouts. I'm a typist, therefore I really care about this.- The brightness is okay, but I expect to have brighter LEDs.Oh well, that's all. This is a decent product. I recommend people buying this product. Please make sure that you choose the right product for your use. Thanks for reading."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Very nice, super solid, not so loud with the brown switching. It took me a couple of hours to get used to it after so many years using membrane switch keyboards. Less than half a day later I had forgotten that it was a new keyboard.I am not a touch typist but thanks to 30+ years of muscle memory I can fake it. The spacing of the keys is great, the travel and the click action work really well to my typing style. And I can always switch to a different switch type if I need to.I have had a couple weird instances when keystrokes aren't registering but honestly these cleared before I could troubleshoot enough to blame either the keyboard or something going on with whatever app was acting out.I was also surprised that it shipped with 8 spare brown switches. I have a really bad luck with keyboards, I usually kill them after about a year and I always end breaking at least one key. It's nice to know that the key caps and switches are user replaceable and very affordable.Two microscopic  nags:1. No wrist rest area. This is not a deal breaker, and I never used that area to rest my wrist, but it was nice to use it to tilt the phone when I needed to do something like look up a 2FA code. Again, not a deal breaker.2. The sticker with the dragon above the arrow keys throws me off, every now and then I look towards the keyboard and that empty area looks like it's missing keys, then it takes me a split second to figure out that's where the sticker sits.That's about the only two things I can find to complain about it. The price was excellent, the keyboard and a new Bluetooth mouse cost me less than I what I paid for each of the last three keyboard/mouse combos that I have purchased.My biggest mistake was that I should have bought two on the spot and give one to my son, he is in the Autistic spectrum and he loves rainbows and was blown away when he saw the customizable lights show.90-day update: I have now recovered from three spills that would have ruined every single keyboard I have owned in at least a decade. I cannot stress enough the peace of mind that comes from being able to replace one compromised key switch instead of losing the keyboard.I also had an interesting, self-inflicted anomaly. During my last spill I accidentally hit the wrong thing and switched the keyboard to French Canadian layout. Then proceeded to waste an hour freaking out over why the switches themselves were registering properly, but some keys were off. I only got to calm down when I plugged in a spare and it started doing the same thing, which is what told me that I was troubleshooting Windows, not the keyboard. So yeah, if you want/need to clean the keyboard, unplug it first."
    },
    {
        "review": "The key-feel is just right for me, half way between shallow laptop \"Chicklet keys\" style and old-school tall ALPS clicky switches. Pretty quiet, and sort of \"buttery\", just the way I like it. I type very fast, and the keyboard has smooth action that works well with that, and doesn't require deep key presses. I previously had two very expensive gaming keyboards and both failed one way or another after only 1-2 years. In this case, I got Redragon K556 (brown switch version) on sale, and got two for less than the price of one of the \"fancy-pants\" keyboards I had before, neither of which quite suited my preferences anyway.I like that EVERY key is backlit; no dark ones, especially no F keys I can't make out when the lights are off. The caps-lock and num-lock indicator lights are unobtrusive, which I also like. I really have no complaints of any kind about this. The construction is very solid, especially for the price-point.  I would have appreciated USB and headphone ports, but such add-ons are not really necessary, and I would not subtract a star for the product lacking extra features that it didn't claim to have in the first place.I have not done any metrics on keyboard latency, or tried to do anything with lighting configuration. I haven't even been gaming lately, just typing a whole lot, and the board is doing very well. By default it cascades through colors in a rainbow-wave pattern, and that's fine. It's not excessively bright, so I don't find it distracting at all."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have only been using the Red Dragon K556 going on three days now but the seller is asking for an honest review. So far, I am very happy with the entire process. I had originally been looking for an office keyboard that had some sort of back lighting and I had gone through a couple of much cheaper options before upgrading to this one from Red Dragon.When I first opened the packaging it was shipped in, I was a little concerned due to the minimal packing materials but upon inspecting the keyboard itself, it was in perfect condition, not a dent or scratch to be found. This is the first keyboard I have used that has an aluminum casing, It is very sturdy and I like the heft of it when moving it around. It just feels like a well made piece of equipment. The keyboard stays in place on the desk and doesn't slide around. I read one reviewer who said that by sliding the keyboard on the desk, the plastic tilting tabs on the bottom tended to flip closed on one side or the other making the keyboard wobble. This, so far, has not been my experience. I am able to slide it around a bit without affecting the tilting tabs.When first plugging it in, I noticed the nice length of the USB cable. It is plenty long enough to reach my tower that sits about two feet over and one foot down and I still have a nice excess to move the keyboard around a bit.I knew before purchasing that there were many more lighting functions one the K556 but I don't think I appreciated just how many options you have with back lighting colors and patterns. There are 9 different solid color options and I have not even explored how many light pattern options there are. I can tell you that there seem to be many multi-colored options to choose from. I primarily like one solid color but it can be fun to explore all the different options when you just don't feel the work happening. All colors can be completely turned off or they can be set to any of five levels of brightness.This K556 came with brown switches which have proved to be exactly what I had been looking for previously. I didn't really know much about switch brands, such as outemu or cherry but this brown switch is much quieter than the previous blue I used. Mind you it is not silent but it doesn't make much more noise than a soft clicking. The keys are very responsive, being what I suppose is a tactile keyboard.  I notice that when typing faster, I don't have to mash the keys. It's almost as if they are two stage keys where you can feel the halfway mark which is as far as I need to push to get a response. The keys spring back instantly and smoothly. I had read another review where the reviewer experiences some sort or ringing or vibrating sound (I don't remember exactly) but they thought it may have been related to the aluminum casing or something. (Again, I think I am remembering this correctly) I do not experience any other sound other that the keys making their normal soft clicking.I have not used this for gaming, hence the three stars for the gaming feature but I can't imagine it being a poor gaming keyboard. Overall, I am very happy with this purchase but again, I have only been using it for three days now. Customer service instantly emailed me to ask for a review and to assure me that they were available within 24 hours should I have any problems. Hopefully it won't come to that. All of this was to say that so far, I am very happy with this purchase am am hoping for long usage."
    },
    {
        "review": "Only had it for a half of a day and it's pretty amazing. My old rosewill brown switch keyboard stopped registering keystrokes so after much research I decided on this K556 board. The price is right. I'm not a casual gamer and wanted something I could also use for regular office type work. This thing seems to be great. It's like a solid chunk of metal, quite heavy. Doesn't move around at all on the desk. Never had a keyboard with this much heft.Keys seem fine, they are quieter than my old brown switches on the Rosewill Neon K85 RGB BR. But still have a clicky and satisfying press. This is my second mechanical keyboard so not an expert but it likely would be fine at a cubicle, but I wouldn't want anything more clicky sounding.The only problem so far is I connect to my Dell Thunderbolt Hub ultrawide monitor and for some reason the software doesn't recognize there's a keyboard, probably because it's not plugged directly into the PC and thinks it's just a standard USB connected device. I'm too lazy unplug it and plug it into my PC directly. And even if I could get the software working, it probably wouldn't work anyway when I ultimately move it back to the monitor anyway.If you're not a pro gamer and need something to handle both office work and casual gaming, and want a full size keyboard layout, then this is a great option. But I cannot say anything about longevity, since it's only been a half of a day.Oh, and I decided to order the Redragon P037 Meteor L wrist rest separately because there wasn't one in the box."
    },
    {
        "review": "First off I really like this keyboard itself. It came on-time and well-packaged, complaints there. Before I say more, let me be clear I am not an avid video gamer; I mostly use my computer for 'regular' boring old stuff like emails, internet surfing, and document writing. I play the occasional video game but nothing intense that requires quick key presses. This will be important later.The keys feel great to type on, not terribly stiff but plenty of tactile feedback and I haven't experienced any sort of typing fatigue. It comes with a sample set of extra key switches so you could experiment with what you like best and I guess replace all the switches with a different type if desired. It's important to note that this keyboard comes with only one or two of each type of switch, not full sets. I like the switches included with the keyboard but the ability to swap switches may be important to some. The backlighting is plenty bright, even in daylight, and features a good range of brightness settings. The keyboard allows you to cycle through some preset colors and quite a few different lighting routines by using function keys alone.Now to the software. Unfortunately the software is pretty much unusable for a few reasons. One, all of the different lighting program names are mismatched. For example there's a setting named something like \"snowfall\" which lights random keys in a pattern that looks like raindrops hitting the ground, but in the software the \"snowfall\" name is tied to the setting that makes the keyboard cycle through a rainbow configuration of lights. Not a big deal on its own but annoying.Two, you can set up the lighting however you want within the software, but when you restart the computer the keyboard resets back to whatever settings it was on without the software. Meaning when you use the function keys on the keyboard without using the software, those settings are always preserved, but if you want to use a custom setting you've configured through the software, you'll need to open the software and select that setting every time you restart your computer. (Therefore it's easier just to use a preset within the keyboard itself and ignore the software entirely).Three, a big draw of this keyboard for gamers should be that the software has a section that allows you to set each individual key to a specific color and effect. For example you're supposed to be able to set the WASD keys to, say, blue, and maybe the number keys to yellow, and the shift and space keys to green, and so on and so forth. Unfortunately this section of the software is entirely non-functional. When prompted to select a key to program, the software doesn't allow you to do anything and clicking on keys does nothing. It's as if the developers had the idea to customize individual keys but never got around to actually including that functionality in the software.Overall, the product loses a star for really bad software, but I want to emphasize that the hardware itself is wonderful. Even without the software, for the price, this keyboard is well worth the money, so I do recommend purchasing it unless you absolutely must have the functionality of programming individual key colors."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've had this keyboard for almost a year and a half now, and I have a lot of positive things to say. It's comfortable to use, even with my bad/arthritic wrists. It's sturdy and does not wobble at all while using it. It's wired, so there are no connectivity issues. The keys make very enjoyable clacking noises while using them, which is a plus for me but may be a negative for someone looking for a super quiet keyboard.My only recent issue is the 'y' key has become a bit spotty. Sometimes it doesn't out a 'y' when I use it, and other times it puts two y's instead of one. Most of the time it's fine though. I highly recommend this keyboard and would buy it again."
    },
    {
        "review": "Typically, I'm too busy to take the time to write a review for stuff, but I enjoy this keyboard so much that I had to leave a review!I've owned so many keyboards over the past 30 years, but this one is THE BEST I've had.Other keyboards that got close to this one were literally five times the cost of this keyboard.What I like about this keyboard the most is the brown mechanical switches. They are the perfect combination of response, tactile feeling, and low noise level. Every stroke is wonderful!I use this keyboard for professional writing. I can't tell you what a pleasure it is to have a keyboard that makes my job easier and more effecient. I spend hours a day on this rig and it's a serious upgrade from the factory model I got with my new computer.The whole keyboard is heavy and feels substantial. The quality is top notch.The back-lighted keys are wonderful. I chose the right color and pattern that I wanted and I'm very pleased with how they help me find the right keys in my dark room.I will say that the app I downloaded from the manufacturer's website did not work to set up the lighting, but I looked up how to set it up properly from the keyboard itself and that's working fine.I can't testify to how long this keyboard will last (I'm pretty rough on keyboards because I'm active) but I'll update here if I have any problems.Right now, I'm very satisfied with what I have and wanted you to know about it!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I am not a gamer, but I do spend a lot of time typing on my computer.  I have put up with miskeying in less than ideal lighting conditions for a long time. The K556 is perfect to the task.The K556 presents an array of kinetic and multicolor options, but I like a static, subdued and monochromatic layout.  This is easy to achieve via keyboard controls (see my setup below), and settings are persistent across reboots.I initially had a question about settings and contacted Redragon support, which was very responsive.The K556 is solidly constructed, the keys have a good feel and they are quiet.Now for my boring settings (“+” and “/” are not keyed).Fn+Ins  Each 2-key press cycles to the next in this set of lighting modes(see the manual for other modes): Trial of Light, Breathing, Normally On(“Normally On” defaults to the color “Rainbow” – a static wash from side to side).Fn+→  (In case the ASCII glyph doesn’t render,  Fn+Right Arrow) Each 2-key presscycles thru Rainbow, Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Cyan, Blue, Purple, Violet).My choice was “Purple”, more of a fuchsia.Fn+↑/↓  (In case the ASCII glyphs don’t render,  Fn+Up Arrow or Down Arrow).  Each 2-key presscycles brightness Up or Down."
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love this. Very happy with this purchase. Sounds like a typewriter and not as loud as a lot of other keyboards. Full metal, heavier than you would think. And they keys light up for all those \"in the dark\" gamers. This is a brilliant, affordable option for a keyboard."
    },
    {
        "review": "It's a very good product. I can now sleep better"
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent product, I have been using it for several months and I am delighted, I totally recommend it"
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good quality"
    },
    {
        "review": "These are initial impressions only. I'm used to TVS keyboards in the office and the laptop keyboard. This one is upgrading for personal use. Below are the points worth mentioning1. Sturdy keyboard with lots of weight, as expected2. Updated detachable cable from earlier attached cable, type C USB connectivity, a welcome upgrade3. Spare keys are browns only, little disappointment that no other option to customise4. Keys are custom Redragon keys, not Outmeu , however very smooth with little bump, nice to type in5. RGB is great. However it is not of my interest, as I am going to use the keyboard for typing only.Overall satisfying purchase so far."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "If you are interested in transitioning from an old school to mechanical keyboard, want something with an absolutely sick looking design, is simple to use, and doesn't have insanely loud key press noises, this is definitely a top option! I have had this unboxed for barely 30 minutes and am already in love with it. It was super easy to plug-n-play and use the built in functions immediately.I have used old style, full-length keyboards for decades and was extremely hesitant to try out a mechanical keyboard, especially because of the noise I had heard from seeing video reviews and how unsure I was if I could deal with the loss of full buttonology. One major reason I looked into one of this type/size was that I realized one of the main reasons I have issues with soreness and pain in my left shoulder, neck, and upper arm is due to how I would position my left hand and tilt the keyboard in order to use the traditional movement keys WASX on a full sized keyboard for gaming. With this one, my arm is now in a natural resting position at 90 degrees against my side, negating the angular stress on all those muscle groups, and when I want to do some typing while gaming, I don't have to stretch across the board to my left as much with my right hand to get to home keys.Additionally, one big assumption killer was that, even though the size is cut down by a ton from a full-sized keyboard, the muscle memory transition to the slightly more compacted keys took hardly any time at all when typing with home keys. I type about 100 WPM, so I assumed I'd have a super difficult time with this aspect of the transition, but the spacing of keys doesn't have a huge difference of feel. The other big plus is that the noise level isn't huge on key press when gaming, and is not bothersome when typing; it's not that high pitched clickity clack you might get from many other types, rather a dull, thud-like sound.Now add in the amazing painted design (especially if you like Japanese wood block print/art), and the colored light functions, which mind you are accessible by using the keyboard and the built in mini screen aka not requiring you to download software, as well as the pre-programmed FN + <key> functions that replace the buttons you lost from a full sized keyboard, and this thing is just a dream.HIGHLY RECOMMEND to one and all, especially old folks like me that are afraid to make that leap into transitioning to a mechanical style board.P.S.: If you're looking for actual dimensions of the keyboard itself, which are not on this Amazon page nor on the company's item page, they are approximately as follows: 12.75\"x5.75\"x1.375\"-0.875\" (the last number is the height, without the height extenders being used, at the top of the keyboard vs. at the bottom. it slopes upward towards the back)"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a fantastic mechanical keyboard on a budget. This is my first keyboard I’ve purchased for my gaming PC after using the one it comes with, and I’ve really enjoyed it.The keyboard has drivers, but they aren’t necessary, it’s a good plug and play. The colors are cute and the lighting doesn’t detract from the design. I really love the sound - when I’m just typing and listening to music, it’s pretty easy on the ears, a good creamy click. The size is just perfect as my last keyboard was 100% and took up way too much space on my desk. Not too bulky or heavy. I’ve been using this to play games that require WASD pretty heavily and the keys have been holding up!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I love it! It's my first keyboard other than an old 2015 work keyboard. It's super smooth and the sound is very nice. It's totally asmr worthy. It was smaller than I expected but just because it doesn't have a number pad like my old keyboard did. It glows rainbow but I lowkey lost my manual so I don't know how to change the lighting, but I remember there being a bunch of combinations you can do to change them on either windows or mac. And it is so cute my fav color is green and it is adorable with the frogs. 10/10 would buy again if mine breaks (knock on wood)."
    },
    {
        "review": "I really liked the color and the frogs, and wanted to try a 75%. It was only $49 when I got it, and it has screen controls, really good RBG,  and is hot swappable.  The only thing that is holding this back is the fact that it is only a wired connection, which for just $10-$15 more you can get tri-connect. But that was my mistake. And if that doesn't bother you then there really is no reason not to get this fully customizable board over more higher priced optionsThe stock switches are very good if you like linear, but I switched them with some Kaihl Box Pink, and it was very easy. Keycaps shine through and print are both quality and very comfortable to type on.The board itself is solid with a good build, proper gasket mounts and sound dampening."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have only had this keyboard for a few days, but I will say that it is of very nice quality and works well with my Lenovo Yoga Laptop. The feel and size are great, the keyboard is a bit on the heavier side, which I don't mind. and it seamlessly connects while also super cute!The only reason I did not give the keyboard 5 stars is that the setup instructions were a bit vague and did not include how to download the software needed to set up and customize (I was able to figure it out, and the colors work great, and I can upload GIFs to the screen). I had to visit the brand's website to download and install the software (steps not included in the manual). I think if you have a general understanding of how to set up these types of keyboards and download software, it's fine, but adding these steps would make everything much more streamlined.Besides that, everything else works great so far, and I really like using the keyboard!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Love this keyboard! It’s compact (75%) and has a creamy sound when typing. It’s more of a dull and thumpy sound if that helps! I preferred this since I’m in an office and didn’t want to have a loud  mechanical keyboard clacking away.But aside from sound it’s also extremely cute with all of the pictures on it! It was easy to setup and the website to download the drivers is mainly in another language (possibly Chinese?) but again easy to find the correct keyboard. Now I have to setup the RGB that I prefer and what gif I’d like in the upper screen.The connection was easy and simple to setup and I love that there’s a reinforced port underneath the keyboard and not off to the side where it can get caught or snagged on stuff.Overall I highly recommend this keyboard!!!"
