    {
        "review": "Watch is Chinese garbage don’t buy it."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was a little tired of watches that barely last a week and which panic as soon as you shake them a little. Between the splashes, the knocks against the work surface or the often caught hands, I needed something that would follow without flinching. This one intrigued me with its robust side and its announced autonomy which seemed almost exaggerated. I figured it was worth a try, especially to avoid having to take out the phone every five minutes. From the first days, I felt that she had something to answer for. She takes it well, without making any fuss. And in the end, it quickly established itself as a discreet but useful little companion. What I like: The AMOLED screen is really bright, even in direct sunlight you can see everything without straining The battery life is amazing, you almost forget that you have to recharge it It is resistant to shock and water, it reassures on a daily basis Answering calls directly on your wrist, it's super practical when your hands are busy Health monitoring is complete and clear, no need to look for information customizable dials allow you to change styles easily It remains comfortable despite its sturdy side, you quickly forget it The handling is simple, no need to spend hours to understand The notifications are easily readable, without being intrusive Conclusion of the comment In the end, it is a watch that does the job without doing too much. It follows the rhythm of the day well, without requiring constant attention. We feel that it is designed to last, not just to look pretty at the moment. Autonomy really changes things, it brings real comfort. It remains simple to use, and it feels good. Not perfect for very thin wrists, but otherwise, it ticks a lot of boxes. A nice surprise, especially if you're looking for something solid and hassle-free 👍"
    },
    {
        "review": "This smartwatch delivers a great mix of durability, performance and everyday practicality. The aerospace‑grade aluminium casing feels solid, and the military‑grade testing gives confidence that it can handle outdoor use, dust and rough conditions. The battery life is a standout - lasting around a week with normal use and up to 30 days on standby, which makes it far more convenient than many daily‑charge models.The 1.45\" AMOLED display is bright, sharp and easy to read outdoors, and the option to customise watch faces adds a nice personal touch. Health tracking is detailed, with continuous monitoring for heart rate, SpO₂ and sleep, and the 114+ sports modes cover everything from running to hiking. The IP68 rating is suitable for swimming, adding to its versatility.Call handling works smoothly thanks to the upgraded Bluetooth and built‑in speakers, and notifications come through reliably. The AI voice assistant is a useful extra for quick commands without reaching for your phone. With additional tools like music control, weather, alarms and even a small flashlight, it’s well equipped for daily and outdoor use.A feature‑rich, tough smartwatch that performs well across fitness, communication and outdoor activities."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was fed up with these gadgets that we charge more often than we use them. The idea here was simple: find a watch that copes with everyday life, which remains readable in all circumstances, and which does not become another constraint. You don't want a piece of technological jewelry, just a tool that stands up, without too much glossy marketing to be honest.🔹 Main features We're on a connected watch designed to take cash. 1.44-inch AMOLED display, quite sharp, bright enough to avoid squinting in direct sunlight. Calls directly to the wrist, classic health monitoring (cardio, sleep, oxygen), various sports modes. All enclosed in a case that is intended to be robust, with a promise of autonomy that flirts with a full month. Customizable dials for those who like to change the atmosphere without changing objects.🟢 The positive points Impressive battery life: you almost forget the charger for weeks 🔋Readable and contrasting screen: even outside, it remains clear 👀Practical call function: no need to take out the phone at each vibration 📞Complete health monitoring: the minimum is there, and it runs without bugs ❤️Fluid interface: it responds quickly, not annoying latency ⚡Good resistance: rain, sport, light shocks... it doesn't flinch 🛡️Personalization of the dials: it avoids monotony 🎨Comfortable bracelet: even after a long day, nothing to report 👍Quick connection: simple pairing, without any hassle 📲⚠️The sound on calls remains correct without being bluffing, don't dream either 🎧The health data remains indicative, we are not in the medical business 📊🏭 ManufacturerThe manufacturer plays the card of accessibility and robustness, without seeking to compete head-on with the behemoths. Clear positioning, promise quite consistent with what we have on our wrist.🌟 ConclusionThis is typically the kind of object that does not seek to impress, and that is perhaps why it convinces. It does what is asked of it, without whims, without constant dependence on an electrical outlet. A watch that accompanies more than it invades. And actually, it's not bad."
    },
    {
        "review": "I received this military-style smartwatch through Amazon Vine and it’s a solid all-round option if you want a budget-friendly fitness watch with calling features.The 1.39” HD display is clear and easy to read, and the rugged “military” design gives it a durable, outdoorsy look. Watches in this category are typically built to handle daily wear and light outdoor use.One of the standout features is the Bluetooth calling function—you can answer and make calls directly from the watch, which is really convenient when your phone isn’t nearby. These watches usually use Bluetooth 5.3 with built-in speakers and microphones for this featureIt also covers all the key fitness tracking features, including:Heart rate monitoringSleep trackingStep counter (pedometer)Multiple sports modes (often 100+ options)"
    },
    {
        "review": "Setting this up took only a few minutes, which was a relief as I don’t enjoy fiddling with tech for too long. The display is clear and responsive, and swiping between functions feels smooth enough once you get used to it. The build feels light on the wrist but not flimsy, and the strap has enough give to wear all day without discomfort.I’ve used it mainly for walks and day-to-day notifications, and it handles both without much effort. Step counting seems reasonable, though it can vary slightly depending on activity. Sleep tracking gives a general overview rather than detailed insight, which is fine for me. Battery life comfortably stretches across several days before needing a top-up. The charging cable attaches magnetically and is easy to line up, even in low light."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love my Garmin watch! It’s sleek, comfortable, and has so many helpful features without feeling overwhelming. I especially love being able to track my workouts, steps, sleep, and daily activity all in one place. The battery life is also amazing - I don’t have to constantly worry about charging it. I also got a different band for it to give it a more elegant look, and I love how easy it is to dress it up or down. The display is bright and easy to read, and it’s comfortable enough to wear all day and night."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have tested numerous “smart watches” in my quest to find a decent replacement for my ever faithful and reliable, Fitbit Versa 4. I purchased it almost four years ago to replace the original Fitbit Versa. But then Google took over Fitbit, and I was looking for a reliable alternative to the Fitbit products. I tried a Samsung Galaxy watch 7 that lost steps by the thousands each day. I tried an Apple Smart Watch that sometimes had to be charged more than once EVERY DAY! I tried a Parsonver (less expensive) but even though it was advertised and labeled IP 68, (indicating that it would withstand submersion in water) I actually received an email from the company before the watch was even delivered advising me to be careful even showering or washing my hands with it on! SERIOUSLY???? And the step counts were not even close to correct!  Then I tried an Amazfit BIP6 (also advertised as IP 68, but clearly IS NOT) and thought I had finally found what I was looking for, until it completely malfunctioned after only 3 weeks of use. So Amazon replaced it with ANOTHER BIP6, and that one lasted almost 5 months before it began to seriously malfunction and the touchscreen no longer worked. So my new search began and I decided to give Garmin a try as my walking and swimming buddies swear by their Garmins, and the results that they get with them compare favorably to my old Fitbit. I have now only had the Garmin Vivoactive 5 for about a month, but so far, it is AWESOME! It tracks extremely well with the Fitbit, and I have had no issues whatsoever with water infiltration. If that changes, I will be back to downgrade my rating, but so far, so GREAT! This proves that you don’t have to buy the most expensive models of ANY brand of fitness watch to find one that does what you need it to do. Most of them have more functions than you will ever need or use anyway. I bought separate, different color bands to customize it and love that you have a lot of choices for watch faces, so pick the one that works best for you! (Although - once again, Fitbit has a much better and more user friendly watch face selection portal!)The one potential “glitch” I thought I had finally discovered with my Vivoactive 5, but which turned out not to be an issue, occurred when I was flying internationally. I was returning from Europe to the US on an evening flight where the time in the US was six hours earlier. So both my FITBIT and my Garmin thought that when enough flight time had elapsed, they went past midnight and started their metrics over as a new day - meaning steps completely zeroed out and started over. But not long after I landed at 7 pm US time, my Fitbit picked back up with the previous day’s step tally, but my Garmin still showed the reduced step count tallied as a “new day”. I was disappointed with this until I checked the metrics in the Garmin app and noted that the “two day tallies” indicated on the watch itself were actually (appropriately!) counted and displayed as a single day step counts in the app. So yes, I am sold on the Garmin. But I do still have to wonder why if FITBIT - even early versions - could have successfully addressed ALL of the issues I have found with so many competitor brands, why can’t all the others? Maybe I shouldn’t have been so wary of Google taking over Fitbit when I started investigating other Smart watches, but I learned a lot about different brands and their bogus IP 68 and 5 ATM ratings!So to reiterate, Garmin is exactly what it says it is, and Fitbit is as well. Do your comparisons!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been using Garmin for running for years now. They've improved a lot! This one does everything I need and more. I like the body battery, sleep info. The 5 syncs up to GPS much faster than my 4 to run or walk. I love the music, however, my headphones sometimes disconnect to the watch while running. Not sure if it's the headphones or the watch. It's pretty easy to use if you've had one before. It has changed on the strength where you hit the bottom button vs tapping the watch to enter weight/reps (don't have to do that anymore, not that it was a problem). Overall I'm super satisfied. Can see it better as well with the new display."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm coming from an Apple Watch Series 5. I had gotten frustrated with the battery life and sleep tracking and wanted to see if a more fitness-focused watch would be a better fit. I'm not a big runner or someone who does a lot of intense exercise, but I do like to track my exercise and sleep and get nudges to move when I've been sitting down for too long. I also like to get notifications on my wrist without needing to pull out my phone.Compared to the Apple Watch, the first major thing I noticed was this is nowhere close in terms of smarts. I had gotten used to Apple's smooth interface, built in microphone and speaker for voice commands, and often useful apps that can be used without needing to get my phone out. That being said, after a few days I can confidently say that I don't need them. I would argue that Garmin knows what this is and what it is not - it's a fitness device first and foremost, and everything else comes second. And on that basis they nailed it - the fitness tracking has been excellent and noticeably better than the Apple Watch especially in sleep tracking. I also much prefer their approach to goals, which tracks exercise over the course of a week rather than focusing specifically on individual days. Whenever I was sick or had a down day I would feel guilty if I didn't close all of my rings on my Apple Watch, but I don't feel that same pressure with the Garmin. I do miss being able to use voice commands with my watch, but I could have gotten a more expensive Garmin if I really wanted that, and I don't think it's worth it for me.The second thing I noticed: not having to charge your watch every day is a game changer. This was easily my biggest frustration with the Apple Watch since it's marketed as a device you wear 24x7 and yet needs to be charged every day. And considering how long it took to charge my Series 5, this was a major annoyance. I couldn't charge it overnight, because I wanted sleep tracking. I couldn't charge it in the morning, because I like to start off my day with exercise and it doesn't charge fast enough to do it after a workout and before work. And if I charged it in the evening, I would often forget to put it on before bed and miss out on the sleep tracking. The Garmin is a breath of fresh air by comparison - I charge it about once a week, and with my relatively light usage it can probably go close to two. I took a multi day trip recently and I didn't once have to worry about charging my watch, which was a pleasant change.Overall I think this watch is a great fit for a more casual user who isn't running marathons but wants to track fitness and sleep and get some extras like smartphone notifications and see the weather at a glance. If you want something with more smart features and better build quality (i.e. more metal and less plastic), then the Venu might be a better fit. Or they have the Forerunner if you do run marathons. Or a bunch of other models for more specific use cases. But I would highly recommend giving this watch a try if you're looking for the same things that I am."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my first Garmin. And I'm really liking all the features. Easy to set up, the style is nice, and accurate from what I can tell. Only down side, is the charge holds for a day and a half. A bit inconvenience for me. Otherwise I love it"
    },
    {
        "review": "This has been a really great fitness tracker so far.  After over a decade of using a Fitbit there was a bit of a learning curve, but I've been really happy with it.  My only complaint has to do with the app more than anything else.  Unless I pay for a subscription I can't track my food in the app (unless I'm just nuts).  Overall the vivoactive works well, looks good, and does exactly what I want it to do!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love this watch! I have had mine for about 6 months and got my husband the same one. I love that it gives me language and data behind how I am feeling. The body battery is incredibly validating. I am constantly exhausted even if I think I'm getting the proper amount of sleep. I love that it explains how my sleep isn't restorative enough to support full recovery from my stressful daily life. Contrasting this, my husband has a labor-intensive job but always feels fine. We will go to sleep and wake up around the same time, but my sleep scores are usually in the 50s-70s and his is almost always in the high 90s. I wake up with my body battery around 30-50%, and he wakes up fully recharged at 90-100%. It's really helpful to see how my nervous system impacts my energy levels. I can even see direct effects of mindfulness and meditation in my metrics so it helps me see progress even if I don't immediately feel it.I also do strength training, hike, walk, bike, and swim, and this is pretty good for all of those things! I like seeing what zone I am in for workouts, tracking my heart rate, and seeing my recovery data."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’m someone who really really did not want a watch of any sort, especially not an Apple Watch or anything else that is bloated with unnecessary hardware that needs to be charged every single day. Charging a phone every day is enough of a pain without needing to add any other junk, so no thanks to the useless gimmick-loaded “smart” watches.But I really needed to do fitness tracking because without some sort of gadget I just have zero idea how lazy or not lazy I’ve been lately and whether or not I need to get at least some minimal exercise.And my #1 must have feature is week-long battery charge life. This watch is good enough, even though the battery life might be more like 5 days in some cases if you’re using it for activities a lot where the gps is turned on. But for those not running with the gps every day or constantly tinkering with it a full battery charge seems to last a week. That is, charge it for an hour on Sunday and no need to charge it again until the same time next Sunday. You could get the battery to last longer if you’re willing to shut off absolutely everything that might use more power.I started out with a Garmin vivosmart 5 because I didn’t think I needed anything beyond that, but I have sleep problems and decided I needed better sleep tracking. So I ended up with this one even though I don’t really care about random silly “apps” on a watch.Overall this watch has been excellent. It has a very good UI and the fitness tracking features a quite good. The sleep tracking is decent, but still has its issues that hopefully Garmin will fix with software updates. In particular the “nap tracking” doesn’t seem fully baked yet, and things like laying back in a recliner reading a book or watching TV can get falsely detected as a “nap”. Then there’s no way to delete a false nap on the watch or in the Garmin Connect app.There are still a few minor bugs that some people will run into but they seem to be things that can be fixed with firmware updates and I haven’t run into anything completely feature-breaking.If you feel like messing with it you can also install some “apps” like custom watch face designs or sunset/sunrise times, more detailed weather and such. But even without installing anything the watch does everything needed for health and fitness stuff.Almost all of the apps that come with the device can be uninstalled, so if you don’t need “women’s health tracking” for example then you can just remove that to free up space.Their mobile app is pretty well designed and only awkward in some parts of the configuration.The watch reliably synchronizes as long as you leave the phone app running in the background and I’ve never had any Bluetooth connection failures, lag, or lost pairings.Things that the watch is not good for are things that you’d typically use a phone for like communication, turn by turn navigation, maps, games, and such. But I don’t want a watch to do any of that stuff and I’d rather have it save its battery charge so I don’t have to charge it constantly.So overall, this is “the” watch you want if you want as little nonsense as possible and don’t want to worry about battery charge all the time but still need full health and fitness tracking stuff beyond what the $75-150 models offer. And there are also no subscriptions required."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm writing this review about 3 weeks after purchase, so I've already gotten to know the watch well. The product is amazing and meets all my expectations and even more. Great workmanship, the watch is light and the strap is very soft, so you practically don't feel it on your hand. A fantastic display that shows everything perfectly, even in direct sunlight. With continuous heart rate measurement, smartphone notifications turned on, recording several activities and making several payments a day, the watch lasts 7 full days on a single charge and usually has about 20-30% of the battery left. For me, it's a fantastic result. Great customizable dials that can be downloaded for free from the Connect IQ store. The watch will be perfect for a person who wants a small and light watch with contactless payments, continuous health monitoring and activity recording. Of course, for professional athletes, Garmin has better devices, but for amateurs it is a very good choice."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been using the watch for a while now. It’s amazing and well worth the price. Highly recommend"
    },
    {
        "review": "So far good. Size just nice not too big or too small. comfortable to wear all day in and out. For health enthusiasts. Sufficient data work as it is. Not too many unwanted features which could confuse and hard to navigate around. My second simply watch for health tracking purposes"
    },
    {
        "review": "Good product."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "Great Value!I’m honestly very impressed with this smartwatch! The screen is beautiful and clear, it looks elegant on my wrist, and it has so many features that I expected to find only on much more expensive smartwatches.I love the heart rate, sleep, SpO2, steps, calories, and activity tracking. It also has tons of sport modes, and the battery life has been great so far. It feels comfortable and lightweight, too.For the price, I really don’t think you can ask for much more. It looks stylish, works great, and gives you many of the features you would expect from a high-end smartwatch without the high price tag.Very happy with my purchase and definitely recommend it! ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐"
    },
    {
        "review": "I would give it ten stars. I have had multiple Apple Watches and mine brocke , so I was looking into replacing it for another watch. The setup was tedious and none of the watch faces had the opinions that i wanted on my main screen. Frustrated with that watch i ordered this because i liked the shape and the price is super cheap . I use it mostly for fitness / music and some communication apps.I was impressed how easy this was to set up !!! The app itself is amazing and easy to use. There’s a lot of watch faces and the menu is super easy. Plus the battery life is amazing. I’m on day 4 and it’s only on 53% . So I can actually wear it overnight and get my sleep report. . It’s water resistant and very accurate. I wore the Apple Watch and this watch on the first day this arrived and this watch is more accurate regarding steps, workouts, sleep , calories etc .I’m blow away with the quality.I added a different band and I love it ! This picture is with the gold and silver band. I didn’t see that there’s rose gold bands till a few days after, so i ordered them. The gold/silver doesn’t really match but it’s still a pretty band"
    },
    {
        "review": "I wasn’t expecting much from this smartwatch since it’s not a big-name brand, but I’m honestly impressed with everything it can do for the price.The feature that surprised me the most is the Find My Phone function. I use it way more than I thought I would, and it’s already saved me from searching the house countless times. It works great!The watch also tracks my steps, heart rate, sleep, and workouts, and the battery lasts several days before I need to charge it. Notifications come through clearly, and it’s comfortable enough to wear all day and while sleeping.If you’re looking for an affordable smartwatch without paying for a premium brand, this is definitely worth considering. It has a lot more features than I expected, works reliably, and has been a great value for the money. I’m very happy with my purchase!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I was honestly considering returning this, but now I’m a bit undecided. Maybe I’ll write this review and it’ll help me decide.For context, I’m a big watch person - especially for workouts - so I set a budget of $50 max and decided to give this one a try. It was right on the money. When it arrived, I’ll admit the packaging made me a little skeptical. Usually, presentation says a lot about the product, and this didn’t give the best first impression. AT ALL.After opening it, the watch itself did feel a bit flimsy at first (see photos), but surprisingly, the actual build quality turned out to be better than expected. It feels fairly sturdy, which was a nice surprise.I tested it at the gym this morning for the first timr, and it definitely held its own. The workout tracking exceeded my expectations - it offers a wide variety of activities you can easily select, which is a great feature!The battery life is exceptional! It lasts for a very long time which is ALWAYS a plus!I also liked that I could customize the watch face with a personal photo. However, there’s a downside: if you upload your own image instead of using one from the app’s store, you’re very limited in what data can be displayed. Instead of showing time, date, steps, calories, etc. all together, it only allows you to display one or two items. In my case, I could only show the time and date, which was disappointing.Cons:The app is probably the weakest part of this product. There’s a “Find My Phone” feature, but it disconnects every few minutes. You have to reopen the app to reconnect before using it again, which defeats the purpose - especially if you’re trying to locate your phone when it’s lost. Not sure how someone is supposed to find their lost phone but need their phone and watch need to be connected to the app before using this tool. Make it make sense??? You can’t lol.It also claims to support notifications from apps like Snapchat, Facebook, and Instagram, but despite enabling everything, I haven’t received any of those. Not a single one. I only get basic notifications like calls, texts, and a few random apps.I work in software, so I’m prettyyyy comfortable with tech. If I’m having trouble getting these features to work properly, that’s something to keep in mind if those functions are important to you. Please be aware of this if you’re searching for a watch solely for this reasoning.Another downside is that the wristbands aren’t interchangeable - there’s no easy way to swap them out, which is limiting. Which really sucks.Overall, for $50, it’s decent. But honestly, for an extra $20–$30, you could probably find something much better without all the disconnection issues this one constantly gives you.I’ll give it a few more days before deciding whether to keep it or return it. More than likely I’ll be returning to get something more reliable but I’m writing this review for those who wants to stay in a reasonable price range, while giving them the honest review they deserve before spending their hard earned money.Hope this helps!!EDIT 5/24/26: I ended up keeping the watch. The more I used it, the more features I was discovering both in-watch, and in-app. However, I’m still keeping it as 3 stars because of the Cons I’ve listed still being an issue. I’d say give it a try yourselves and see how you like it. You can read all the reviews but it’ll affect everyone differently."
    },
    {
        "review": "This will be the only review needed to know this is the Smart Watch to purchase!!I have at this point probably purchased and returned more than 50 smart watches because I wanted notifications to have an audible tone and not just vibrate. With Bluetooth 5.2 it was an automatic with most of the accompanying apps you had to partner the watch with but when 5.3 came out, it became vibrate only on all apps with the exception of ONE…VeryFit. Additionally, VeryFit is the ONLY one which will register notifications from LITERALLY EVERY SINGLE APP you have which sends banner notifications to your phone, not just the 10-15 options typically seen in the other apps like Gloryfit, DaFit, FitCloudPro, HBand, etc.In order to receive these Push Notifications on an iPhone, which is what I have, you need to go into Settings, Bluetooth, touch the “i” in the circle and check off “Show Message Notifications” and “Share System Notifications”. You must then go to the App List, find VeryFit, make sure Background App Refresh is checked and Notifications are mirroring the ones I’ve captured in my images.The other important piece of info is the watch volume is set low when it first arrives. Make a phone call from the watch and touch the speaker icon key to allow for raising the volume.In the VeryFit app itself, go to Device, click Notifications, be patient and wait a few seconds and the screen will expand - check off “Sync Phone Notifications” and this is where all the excitement is!As notifications come to your phone, they will also become listed here on this page (and your watch will get the alert for them at the same time) and this is where you can Allow or Turn Off the specific notification. I have also captured this in my images for reference. As you can see, I get notifications for everything I receive on my phone. If you need more assistance, do as it says and tap the “i” in the circle on that page and you will get the detailed instructions just as I have given you now. I have also captured those in my images for you to reference as well.This tracks sleep, stress, energy, blood oxygen level, heart rate, gives you weekly averages on all. It can track your cycle for females and ones weight. It has suggested programs to help with better sleep habits, managing weight, stress, breathing exercises etc. It has every possible physical exercise activity one could imagine. It’s waterproof, has excellent options for watch faces - many are free or you can opt to pay for some if you choose not to become a Community member and if you do, those become free then as well. You can share your progress within the community to help stay motivated if needed. You can contact customer service if you have additional questions at anytime by tapping the little blue operator floating on the page within the app as well.There are screen protectors available, watch cases are shown available but they are too big, so don’t bother, you can change out the bands - there’s nothing you can’t do with this watch and the battery life is also fantastic, lasting me a week so far, even with all the many notifications I receive!!Obviously, I highly recommend it!! You won’t regret the purchase! The only thing I am now in need of is finding its twin in rectangular form because I like to switch between that and round; should I find it, I will update this review with the information! I hope this review has helped!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This watch exceeded all of my expectations on quality, looks, features. It even has a 5 year warranty. All the others  have a 1 year warranty.First, it looks beautiful, it look great, it is fully imersible in water, so I don't have to worry about getting in the shower with it by accident. I probably won't go swiming with it, but if I did, it has a \"water displacement\" option that makes a racket and scared the heck out of me when I selected it to see what it was.If you're like me, you want an expensive watch at a bargain price. And this is it. A similar watch would sell for 2 to 3 times more.The app that you have to install is called  VeryFit. Easy to install and use, about the only thing I didn't like is the ads.First, you CAN, but don't have too, subscribe or join a community. I'm to cheap, so I don't know how good the community features are. But they look compeling if you like people.It has a watch face marketplace, where you can get lots of FREE face plates. And also some more awsome ones for as little as $0.49 and $0.99. Lots and lots. Plus, if you are a subscriber (community member), all the watch faces are free. All of them. So, again, you don't have to spend a penny. It has beautiful free ones.Having said that, the app interface has ads to join, redeem cupons, etc. It has a floating \"talk to someone\" thing in case you feel the need.  I took me a while to get used to it and ignore them. I could almost join the community if they promised me to remove all the ads. But I didn't ask..So. It gets the full 5 stars. I love the watch. And yes, it monitors all the things it needs to monitor.I did not test step count, or spO2 levels, or heart beats per minute. It says it counted them. I get a number. That's all I wanted."
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love this smartwatch! It has a beautiful, elegant design that looks much more expensive than it is. The display is bright, crisp, and easy to read, and the touch screen is very responsive.It pairs with my phone quickly, the battery lasts a long time, and it tracks my daily activity accurately. I also enjoy receiving notifications right on my wrist without constantly checking my phone.Overall, it's stylish, comfortable to wear, and offers excellent value for the price. I highly recommend it to anyone looking for a reliable and attractive smartwatch!"
    },
    {
        "review": "i received mine 05/19 and have been wearing it ever since. i tested the phone call feature and my mom said she wouldn’t have even known i wasn’t using my phone ! you can’t reply to text messages through the watch but you can read them and other notifications. the watch does have to be pretty close to your phone to stay connected, i can’t go to the other side of the house without it disconnecting but it auto connects when it’s back in range.the activity tracking has been nice, i’ve done 4/5 workouts while wearing the watch and i love the way it tracks it. it has tons of different workout styles such as freestyle, outdoor/indoor walk/run, swimming, etc. and the app also has workout videos/training you can use if you’re new to it. while you’re working out it displays your HR, how long you’ve been training, calories burned, and aerobic training effect (no clue what that means) and if you’re doing a walk/run it tells you how far you’ve gone. when you’re done it tells you how long you’ve should take to recover as well. it keeps a log of all your previous workouts so you can look back on them.i have tried to use the AI voice assist a few times but can barely hear it when it talks back. i’ve tried to turn the volume up but can’t seem to figure it out. this ain’t a function i bought the watch for tho so i don’t really care.the display is really good, you can change the brightness depending on if you’re outside or not which makes it really easy to see. you can make it to where it turns on when you lift your wrist or to where you have to push the button to turn it on. i will say i usually have it to where it automatically turns on but somehow this feature keeps getting randomly turned off and i have to constantly turn it back on.the battery life i think is pretty good ? i usually put it on the charger when i shower for about 15-20 mins everyday but it hasn’t gotten below 70% so i think that’s pretty good for a full 24hr period of it being used.the app tells you a lot more info too. it tracks your sleep and tells you how long you’ve spent in REM, light, and deep sleep. it also tells you the quality of your sleep. it tells you you’re body’s energy level, your heart rate variability, your stress levels, spo2, respiratory rate, heart rate, and the ambient sound around you. you can also connect a BP cuff and scale to the app to track those and blood sugar for diabetics. overall it has TONS of info about your body on the app and watch.10/10 recommend !"
    },
