        "review": "Great for the price, I’m shocked actually! I got it for $17.99 and it works exactly how I wanted - measures steps pretty accurately, BPM, and blood pressure. There are some other cool features too but I appreciate the fitness stuff the most. It looks nice also, different faces available too. Battery life is looooong!Not really sure how the call feature works but I turned off notifications cause that’s not really what I bought it for."
    },
    {
        "review": "Not accurate enough for running. I did 2 runs with this watch, and both times, the watch was WAY off - adding 27% more distance than was actually run and randomly reporting on pace. For $17.99, I didn't expect much, but this is unusable.RUN 1Actual: 2 miles, pace 9:00 per mileReported: 2.62 miles, pace 6:52 per mileRUN 2Actual: 3 miles, pace 8:25 per mileRun 2 reported: 3.75 miles, pace 10:53 per mileTo test accuracy, I ran with this watch alongside my iPhone connected to a Garmin HRM 600 heart rate monitor. These are routes that I run weekly.For $17.99, it's an impressive piece of technology, but not really usable for anything. I did also test out the other features, such as blood pressure monitoring and all of the other health features, which could be as inaccurate as everything else. There's just no way to know. It was a fun experiment, and I was hopeful that it could be used as a fit gap until I replace my Apple Watch with a new one, but after two runs and a few days of use, it was obvious that this watch is not dependable and cannot be used for tracking fitness activities."
