[
    {
        "review": "This watch is honestly almost perfect. Literally like a 9/10. The design is sleek, the watch face is not too big for my tiny wrist, has a vibrant and attractive display, and the watch band is really nice and soft! I had a fit bit charge 4 before this one and it finally quit on me. I wanted a low budget option because I haven't been consistent and don't have 300 dollars to waste for it to sit around if I end up not staying consistent. For 50 dollars this beauty fits nearly all my needs. Paired to my samsung phone immediately when I pulled it out of the box. Super easy to set up, and tons of different ways to customize the watch face. Have I mentioned its so pretty?? I don't typically wear a watch but I wanted the health metrics to track my heart rate and steps. This is one of two mild caveats. It doesn't actively track your heart rate. Every couple minutes or so it pings your heart rate to get more of a time line than a live feed. No worries, it pings often enough you get a good idea. Only mildly sucks because I use the live feed heart rate monitor to help clue me in to if I might have a panic attack or if I'm overreacting. Another thing. I have been using google health to track my calories and set specific goals for some of my macros I'm trying to hit. Unfortunately, the samsung health app doesn't get this specific and just gives you the suggested daily intake as a metric rather than letting you set your own. What sucks more is that these two apps don't sync with each other. So now I have 2 seperate apps to track my health. All in all. Its really a fantastic little gadget. For $50 I would 10000% buy it again if they came out with a newer version. Will update again after I've had it a few weeks."
    },
    {
        "review": "Works great. 2 weeks in and am glad I got this. If your looking to step into a step tracker this budget smart watch is the entry level watch you may need. It has the basic features needed to keep track of light and heavy workouts without breaking the bank. As many others have noted the battery life varies with what features you enable. Sleep tracker take a decent chunk of battery 8-11 days per charge I'd guess. I recharge at 30%. Looks sleek and screen size is that of a standard non-flashy basic watch. With an added protector case the watch is slim and robust quality even without the protector."
    },
    {
        "review": "After testing several wearables, the Samsung Galaxy Fit3 has completely blown me away. If you are looking for a device that delivers premium health tracking without the bulk or price tag of a full-scale smartwatch, this tracker is an absolute home run.​Here is my breakdown after putting it through its paces daily:​Superior Sleep Analytics: The sleep tracking feature on this device is incredibly sophisticated for its price point. It doesn't just log when you fall asleep and wake up; it breaks down your sleep stages (REM, light, deep) and gives you a legitimate, actionable sleep score. It’s light and slim enough that it doesn't bother my wrist at night at all.​Flawless Menstrual Cycle Tracking: The health tracking integration is seamless. Logging and predicting periods is straightforward, reliable, and integrates perfectly with the Samsung Health app dashboard, keeping all my data in one clean, easy-to-read spot.​Accurate Step Counting & Daily Metrics: The pedometer is highly accurate and doesn't pick up phantom steps from just moving your arms around. The beautiful, crisp 1.6-inch AMOLED display makes checking your step goals mid-stride incredibly satisfying, even in direct sunlight. Unbeatable Battery & Value: Because it runs on a streamlined operating system rather than a heavy smartwatch OS, the battery life is spectacular. You can easily go days and days without even thinking about a charger, which completely outperforms premium smartwatches that require daily charging.Final Verdict: At this price tier, the build quality and premium aluminum frame feel luxurious, and the feature set rivals trackers double its price. It provides total reliability for daily health monitoring. If you want top-tier metrics on a budget, this is the one to get!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I just received my watch today 2/15 it came fast as promised. I can already say that I like the watch. The color, it was easy to setup and pair to my S24 Ultra with no problems. It's slim, sleek, and cute on my wrist. When I got the watch it was already at 76% I used my phone to charge it to 100% I love the different functions the watch offers. You can adjust the brightness to your liking. In will update on the battery life after a week. It has many different options face changes for the watch and it's easy to read. And for the price it's really affordable. I'm not disappointed at all about purchasing this watch. I'm a HUGE FAN of Samsung products.Update 2/17 - I wanted to vive an update after me having the watch since Sunday afternoon 2/15. The battery life does not live up to as it is advertised for 13 days. You'll probably get about 4 or maybe 5 days at best. Right now as I'm updating my review on this watch I'm at 39% and it's Tuesday evening 2/17 so I know maybe tomorrow I'll have to charge it. The accuracy of the sleep tracker is good. In the Samsung Health App it gives a breakdown of how many hours you slept, if you snoring it records your snoring and you can hear yourself snore. On \"smart thing\" samsung app it shows you where your watch is or you can go under the wear app and click on \"find my band\" and it'll take you right to the \"smart things\" app and it'll show you where your watch is located. Overall I'm satisfied with the watch. So many features. I changed my 5 star to 4 due to the misinformation on the battery life. Would I recommend it to someone that's looking for just a \"fitness\" app I definitely would. I how the review helps the next purchaser. 3/07/26 UPDATE: The charge on the battery last 6 days. I tracked how long the battery would last without notifications. I charged it last Sunday (afternoon) & this morning 3/07 it was at 3%. Another cool feature that I came across. The walking feature has a voice commentary that tells you how far you been walking, the length of time it comes on when you reach your 1st mile in thought that was cool that it actually talks to you."
    },
    {
        "review": "My wife is not a complete Luddite, but neither is she a techie.  She seemed content with her Seiko quartz watch with an analog dial, but frankly I got tired of having to manually reset the time every time we transitioned between daylight savings and standard time.I decided it was time to bring my wife fully into the 21st Century, so I started looking for a Smartwatch for her.  I wasn't looking to spend a lot, recognizing that she might hate it, so I searched for something with a price below $100.  I did not want to waste time with no-name brands and my wife absolutely hates generic anything.  Having enjoyed my Galaxy Watch 5 for the past couple years, I decided to see what Samsung offered at the lower end of their range and that led me to the Fit 3.  Wow!  A no-kidding Samsung watch for less than $50??!!!  I read the reviews and the one that really caught my attention called the Fit 3 a \"Fitbit Killer.\"  That's pretty much all I needed to know.  Before my Galaxy Watch 5, I had a Fitbit that died unexpectedly, which I have since learned is not at all uncommon.  Between the general reliability of Samsung products, a reasonable array of features and the price, I figured this was a safe choice, so I pulled the trigger on the purchase.So far, so good but there are a few downsides with most Smartwatches.  To be blunt, the watchbands generally suck.  Some folks like the silicone bands but I hate them and knew my wife would too.  So I bought a nice stainless-steel watchband that was compatible with the Fit 3 (you have to be careful about that since it doesn't use the typical pins to attach it to the watch).  The other challenge I anticipated was getting an almost useless charging cable included with the watch.  Sure enough, the cable that Samsung includes is only about 1.5ft long, but I had the foresight to buy a charging stand with a 3ft cable for about $14.  I was a bit underwhelmed with what's available on Amazon that's compatible with the Fit 3, but the stand I bought will suffice and the good news with the Fit 3 is you don't need to charge it that often.As it turned out, my wife was pretty happy with her new Fit 3 and immediately started showing it off to our kids and other relatives, so I'll call that a win.  I may get called in for tech support from time to time (as I do with her phone) but since I am very familiar with my Galaxy Watch 5, the Wear app and the Samsung Health app, that's no problem.  Best of all, since it syncs with her phone, I'm done having to adjust the time on her watch (with a microscopic dial I can barely read) twice a year or more.  Mission accomplished!So, if you are looking for a quality Smartwatch with basic features and not spending a lot, the Fit 3 is definitely worth a look."
    },
    {
        "review": "Solid watch. If you need a daily wear watch that's affordable, and still tracks your basic fitness like steps, calories, and some basic workouts I'd grab one. It still tracks steps, calories, all the basics. I get plenty of sweat and water on it and haven't noticed an issue. Functions like a watch with a little bit of fitness. I definitely enjoy using it so I can keep my other watch free from damage. Would recommend having this one as a wear it to work if you're worried about a different one getting damaged."
    },
    {
        "review": "Works great swimming under water! I use it in the pool and the shower. Excellent battery life. I charge it about once a week, and fast charges in about an hour. Tracks my heart rate, 02, stress, steps/workout, sleep, syncs phone notifications/texts, and of course shows the date/time. Cannot track blood pressure. The sleep tracker needs some improvement. Otherwise I love this watch and it's exactly what I needed and was expecting."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've had a long line of Fit Bits and when I found out Samsung has a non-smart watch tracker, I decided to give it a chance. (I don't want to pay smart watch prices.)Out of the box, the Fit 3 was in Spanish, but when I put it on the charger, it reverted to English. It stayed English until I got a new phone and had to reset the tracker in order to pair it with the new phone. The tracker reverted to Spanish and putting it on the charger did not change that this time. I pressed the top option on the device's screen and got to choose from a whole list of languages. I chose English, but this is where someone who wants another language will find it.The charge on this item is supposed to last up to 13 days. I've had this device for 3 months, and I got 6 days out of each charge (7 if I let the device die) for the first 2 months or so. Now, it last 4-5 days. I don't have the display always on and I don't consider my use of this device heavy by any means. 6 days is what I got out of my most recent Fitbit.My Fit 3 charges from 20% to 70% in about 20 minutes and to 100% in about an hour. The trick is to get the charger properly seated, because the first couple of times, I don't think it was on correctly, as I got the main display, not the charging percentage display, and it took about 2 hours to reach 100%.I wore the new Fit 3 side by side with my old Fitbit Charge 6 for 24+ hours to compare - 2 nights and one active day. The first night, the Fit 3 recorded 20 more minutes of sleep and the second night, the Fitbit recorded 20 more minutes. I don't know how either determines when I am asleep and not quietly reading, so I figure the results are comparable.The Fit 3, while behind on step count early in the day, ended the day with almost 1,500 more steps than the Fitbit. Both counted activity (cleaning, for instance), not just pure steps. I'm OK with this - I figure any movement is better than no movement.There are many more features that I don't use. I may in the future, but I'm happy with what I do use. I tried taking photos on my phone (also a Samsung) with my Fit 3 as a remote shutter button. I like that feature!Text notifications come to my Fit 3, but not all of them. When I am using my phone, notifications do not go to the tracker (I have found that this is the default and I can change it if desired). Group texts never do (they will go to my car, so I don’t know why they won't go to my Fit 3), and random regular texts don't. I found in the Health app where I can pick and choose which apps will send notifications.All told, I like this device. It’s not perfect, though. I miss the feature that Fitbit has that reminds me to move each hour. I'm now forced to choose between step count, floor count (I assume that means how many stairs I climb), or active hours. I like counting steps, so that's my main goal. I like seeing the hearts light up throughout the day as I get closer to my goals. I also have active minutes and active calories burned as goals, but I don’t care as much about those."
    },
    {
        "review": "nice"
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent smart watch to pair with your Samsung phone. Many functions such as monitoring sleep, heartbeat, stress etc."
    },
    {
        "review": "I picked up the pink one and it looks absolutely gorgeous on the wrist. The colour is a soft, subtle pink — not loud or plasticky at all — and the slim band sits really comfortably without feeling bulky. I've worn fitness trackers before that felt like a brick on my arm, but this one is so lightweight I genuinely forget I'm wearing it sometimes.The screen is bright and clear, easy to read even in sunlight, and swiping through the menus feels smooth and responsive. Setting it up with my phone was quick and straightforward — no technical headaches, just paired it and it was ready to go.For day-to-day tracking it covers everything I need — steps, heart rate, sleep, calories, stress monitoring. The sleep tracking in particular is really detailed and I've found myself actually paying attention to it and adjusting my routine. The battery life has also genuinely impressed me. I charge it maybe once a week which is so convenient compared to smartwatches that need charging every day or two.The band is comfortable enough to sleep in and the clasp is secure so I never worry about it coming loose during workouts. It handles sweat well too and I've worn it through some pretty intense sessions with no issues.For the price, this thing punches way above its weight. It's not trying to be a full smartwatch and it doesn't need to be — it does its job brilliantly. The pink colour just makes it even better. Highly recommend, especially if you want something stylish, lightweight and reliable without breaking the bank."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent battery life"
    },
    {
        "review": "So far so good not worn for very long yet so may change to 5 star after more use"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I've had Garmins and Fitbits only in the past and this is just a huge, massive upgrade. Love the customizations that can be done with the watch too with the cool looking bands and faces that I bought separately on Amazon. I've had it for a year without any screen protectors and it's been durable enough that it's held up well with very little wear and tear on it, but I'm wise enough to use protectors for it now. My only complaint is the battery. It's a bit disappointing that the battery barely lasts a day. I've had to buy 3 chargers for it. One charger for my car, one for work, and one for home because this thing dies fast if you plan to work out with it and use it all day. Still, I love this watch."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have had a Samsung Galaxy watch since they first came out. My first one is still going strong, but I needed one that would track my activity in the water, since the majority of my activity is water related. This watch is great for that! It has been in the water with me now almost daily for weeks and works like a charm! I have an app that will automatically set the watch to the water mode, or I can set it myself. It counts each step, stroke and workout moment - and seems to do so accurately. The size is a nice upgrade from the earlier versions, being slightly easier to read. All my apps and information were easily transferred to this new watch, making the transition seamless. It doesn't vibrate quite as strongly as my last one did, so I miss a notification here and there, but since my phone is never more than a few feet away from me, that really isn't an issue. Time will tell if it lasts as long as my first watch did, but I am really so impressed with this watch. To me, it is worth spending the cash to have the added assurance of counting my water activities."
    },
    {
        "review": "I'm normally a regular watch wearing person but have been contemplating on getting either an apple watch or samsung to switch things up, I finally switched from apple products to Samsung and decided to go all out with the samsung tech and I couldn't be happier. I love the size , the appearance,  battery life is good. I literally have no complaints. I love how sleek and elegant it looks regardless what band you have. I also love all the features it has. Calling, GPS, wifi ,accuracy , notifications and more. Super happy"
    },
    {
        "review": "I ordered this watch because my fitbit suddenly stopped working. I honestly was just going to get another fitbit but decided to look around at other options for smart watches. I primarily used my previous watch for tracking my steps, sleep, and golf play time, but the selling point to this watch was the additional apps that I get access to. I use 18 Birdies when I golf and this watch is compatible with that app. I haven't used it yet since I just got it last week, plus it's been raining every day, but I can't wait to test it out. I also go to the gym and listen to Spotify when I'm working out. I can connect my headphones to my watch and listen to music. I'm still learning so much about this watch but so far I love it. I did have to turn off the AOD after I got it because it was draining my battery faster than I wanted it to. The reason I dropped a star is because the battery life is decent but not great. I have to charge it daily, but I do like that when it's fully charged I get an alert on my Samsung phone to prevent overcharging. I'm a very petite woman and ordered the 40mm. I was afraid this might be too big for my tiny wrist but it really isn't. I honestly like the size and don't think it's too big at all. Overall, I'm really liking the watch and would highly recommend it to anyone who may be on the fence about getting one."
    },
    {
        "review": "Overall I'm happy with this watch that seems like they're phasing out some features for this particular device. But it tracks my heart rate throughout the day among other useful features. If you're wanting some of Samsung's more advanced features you should probably just go with the newer watch. Capable of Samsung wallet meal tracking water intake. Definitely get yourself screen covers and a case especially if you work Outdoors.Messaging it's kind of tedious using this I mainly just use it to look at incoming messages at a glance. Call qualities decent but the volume is kind of lacking in my opinion. As far as battery life goes I find myself charging in the morning and evening you just charging the evening but the battery get pretty low as I reach the end of my day and sometimes with die before I get home . Battery takes between 30 minutes to an hour to charge depending on how low you let it get. I'd wait for this one to go on sale if you're going to pay full price I'd get the newer versions."
    },
    {
        "review": "Watch is nicely designed and easy to use. The apps are great and the set up is seamless with the phone. Audio seems good. I wish there was a smaller watch face but overall its a great investment."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great watch a lot of information but you have to charge it once a day. Otherwise I love itI need to take the time to go through everything it does. Very comfortable to wear"
    },
    {
        "review": "BOTTOM LINE:  if you don't need the 3 Labs - Advanced Glycation End (AGE) product index, the  Antioxidant index (does not work anyway), or the Vascular Load, ***Go With the WATCH 7!***  If you do get a watch 8, return it to Amazon as soon as it screws up!Watch 8 #2 is now \"inching up\" on its O2 measurements.  At first, it was 2% points over my Innovo FDA-Approved O2 device.  That's okay, because as long as it was consistent, I could deal with it.  Now, it is 3% over my Innovo -- My Watch 8 #1 and Watch 7 did the same.  I hope this is not a harbinger of failure.  It also must be tightened down painfully snug at night.Oct 14, 2025.  Bought new watch 8, now Watch 8 #2I am riddled with inflammation, so pursue antioxidants.  I take 3 supplements twice a day.  Yet the Antioxidant index stays low.  Farce.******************************  Below is the Watch 8 #1Oct 10, 2025 - Samsung refunded costSent it in a second time.  Kept checking (two weeks) and nothing.  Finally, Samsung offered a refund.  Not for the tax, of course.  Awaiting refund.Sep 26, 2025  Already Failing with Same ErrorsWas on the phone forever with Samsung, being fobbed off on every department imaginable.  I bought a Brand-New Watch for $350, and they \"repaired it\" only to have the same problems!I am so mad I could chew a horseshoe and spit nails.  I wish I'd never bought this thing!  If you are stupid enough to buy it, return it to Amazon at the first fail!  It is not YOU!  The sensor array is P-poor and it fails.  I miss my Watch 4.Sep 25, 2025  Samsung repaired the watch under warranty.  It wiped out all trending in the app.\"New\" watch still has \"blanks\" in its monitoring (picture).  Samsung says, \"wear snugly\".  It is already so snug it hurts (picture).  Furthermore, the \"O2\" monitoring is 5% MORE than my commercial O2 monitor.  I guess I have to go back to wearing five different monitors that are correct.Sep 15, 2025.  The watch has become worse.  I will contact Samsung under the warranty.Anytime I get sweaty, the watch stops tracking on the wrist!  I'm skinny - BMI 20.2.  It's a fitness watch, for God's Sake!  One expects it to work when you are sweaty!  But no - it stops tracking the heart rate and the oxygen level.  This is not at all what one wants in a Fitness tracker!Aug 31, 2025.  The watch has developed issues with tracking heart rate.I was walking - spanking pace - and the watch stopped tracking my heart rate.  I stopped for a directed measure.  It said \"put your watch on\".  I took the watch off and cleaned it, and had same result.  I finally rebooted the watch and it worked again.  I DID NOT have this problem with the Watch 4.Aug 5th, 2025.  Anti-oxidant scan doesn't work right.The scan kept saying low - eat a carrot.  Three days of carrots and it is still low.Also, I tried to take another scan and the software kept saying \"Put your watch back on.\" - but the watch WAS on.  Too many bugs.Aug 2nd, 2025  My initial review wasn't accepted; I am unsure why.I hemmed and hawed about Watch7 and Watch8.  I went for this Watch 8 due to features.  I am very disappointed.First, I have many Samsung-branded chargers.  I was very disappointed that the Watch8 band would not allow the Watch 8 to work on the flat chargers.  I guess you could put a weight on the watch, like 2 lbs (!) to press it down, but I believe Samsung should have designed their Watch8 to be compatible with its supporting chargers.  See first pic.Second, this is a 40mm watch.  I compare it to the Watch4 and the Watch 7, the only others with which I have familiarity.  The Watch8 face measures 40mm, but the display is smaller by 2mm, making it a 38mm display.I cannot at this time comment on durability as I have had it three days.  Color is personal choice.  EDIT - Watch began failing before 30 days!  I thought it was me.Functions.  The Watch8 is extraordinarily bloated by location-tracking bloatware.  I do not do use email, bixby, wallet, etc. from the watch, but it will not allow me to uninstall these apps.  Furthermore, every app tracks your location with precision!  I have no idea why outlook would need location, for example.The Watch8 has the same processor as the Watch7.  Comparing it with the Watch4, I do not see any difference in performance.  I would have expected better.The new Health features are AGE - Advanced Glycation End products.  Not sure what the low - adequate - high means, and there isn't a lot of information on the web.  It also does the antioxidant index - very low - low - adequate.  I tried the recommendation --- eat carrots.  My value did not change.  Lastly, is vascular load.  It is supposed to an indication of lifestyle factors affect on your health.  Basically, get sleep, exercise, and good diet. Shrug.The Watch8 is supposed to have a better battery.  However, I do not see the Watch8 lasting any longer than my Watch4.  I still charge the Watch8 at my noon-ish nap time, and see the Watch 8 is down to 66% at noon, and again down to 66% at night, after having been charged at noon.  If I could uninstall some of the not-used apps, it may be better, and maybe not.All in all, after seeing all the additional tracking and inability to turn it off, the questionable value of the new \"features\", the failure to be compatible with industry and brand standard equipment, I wish I'd gone with the Watch7."
    },
