[
    {
        "review": "EDIT 2/26/24: I am now the blocks \"AAA\".  Been helping people start their cars when jump starts with cables have not worked.  Definitely handy to get other people out of an otherwise expensive pinch.I have a car battery that I purchased around 2016 (date of review 1/3/24).  The car itself does not get driven much, at most 2-3 times a week no longer than 15 minutes one way.Throughout the years I've drained it by leaving the car lights on.  Its always required a jump start after that to get going again.  Recently I've been an idiot and been leaving my lights on more frequently.  Eventually it required me to jump start everytime I wanted to turn on the car.  It was becoming a hassle and I was about to call it quits and purchase a new battery until I came across Project Farm's review on battery chargers.  This was at the top of the list so I figured to give it a shot.I was not disappointed!  I charged it for 4 days straight, put it in my car, and it started right up.  Not surprised considering it was just charged but the real test laid ahead.  I was going to be out of town for two weeks and leave the car sitting.  I came back two weeks later and the car started up, no hesitation whatsoever, as if it was a different battery!The NOCO Genius 10 saved me about $150-200 at least in a brand new battery.  On top of that, I can now use it for other batteries in the event of any similar issues.  The instructions on the other hand are a bit confusing and had to rely on YouTube comments to piece it together.For those who are confused at what to do, I did not do a 12V repair (function which this unit has) as it requires the battery to be completely charged first.  All I did was charge it straight for 4 days uninterrupted.When first put on battery to the charger it will be in standby mode.  You push (or hold depending on what you need) the MODE button to select your desired option.  I was charging a lead acid battery so I selected 12V.  The meter at the top slowly advanceD from each of the four bars until the final bar was pulsing green which indicates its charging to full (over 80% I believe).  The green charging light continued to slowly pulse even on the 4th day but I took a gamble and disconnected it from the battery.  For the battery to be 100% charged, the green light should stay solid.  That means my battery worked 2 weeks later after sitting and it wasn't even at 100%.The unit can also force a charge on batteries that are extremely under voltage.  Thats a separate setting that I haven't had to use yet.  It can also charge AGM and Lithium but I believe the 12V repair function is only for lead acid.Unfortunately I had to go through 3 separate orders because the first two were from Amazon Warehouse and I encountered issues with both, consecutively.  I ended up getting frustrated and just purchased a brand new charger.Pros:Solid build qualityExcellent cable connections with weather coversCompatible with a wide variety of batteriesRepair and Force charge batteriesCons:Poor instructionsCharge is slow BUT I think thats how these chargers workTL;DRTry this first before rushing to get a new battery it is a good investment that can save you a lot of money"
    },
    {
        "review": "I recently replaced my Schumacher battery charger with the **NOCO GENIUS10**, and it has been a great upgrade. I’m very impressed with the quality, compact design, and how easy it is to use. The 10-amp charging power works great, and I really like the smart charging and battery maintenance features.It feels well built and gives me more confidence when maintaining my batteries. So far, I’m extremely happy with the purchase and definitely recommend the NOCO GENIUS10 to anyone looking for a reliable, modern battery charger and maintainer.excellent upgrade! ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐️"
    },
    {
        "review": "I have a battery charger made in the 1970s, inherited from dad that passed away in 2011. It was time for another one, and I was going to buy another Schumacher that weights about 15 Lbs. Was ready to pull the trigger and decided to see if something better was out there, thus buying a Genius 10 instead based on YouTube reviews.The Genius 10 was much larger and heavier than how it looks in the picture. The build quality is really good in my eyes, looks like tough ABS polymer, very nice cords with that good feeling wire jacket insulation. The new clamps are nice looking and very practical to me. It's heavy and awkward to store, so I bought the Noco case as well; I thought the other case was too small in size.I ran some test with it, on a 12V Honda Lead Acid and Group H8 AGM battery and was happy the results. It was more intuitive than the 1970s version I have with 30 amp boost that is scary to use. What I like with Noco or Genius, it's several tools in one unit, so every 3 or 4 months, I can use the Genius to check the status of all my batteries, well that is the plan at least.  One neat thing, using the 12V output to keep a battery online while changing a battery, so the cars computer keeps running and doesn't reboot from removing the battery causing weird things to happen.I have no regrets in my purchase and bought the case to enhance it more. I think the price is reasonable as well considering what a $100 buys today."
    },
    {
        "review": "Purchased the NOCO Genius charger in hopes it would resurrect my battery.  Un fortunately, it did not save my battery, but I cannot blame that on the charger.The only complaint I have is the lack of instructions.  They were not minimal, there were none at all provided with the unit.  Luckily, able to look on line and find plenty of videos, however, this does not negate the need for an instruction and care pamphlet.The unit feels solidly built and works as expected.  While it did not resurrect my battery as it claims it can do (not in all situations), mine was 5 years old, lead acid and probably just to far gone for help.  However, it is doing a great job charging  my marine battery and using it to keep it charged until used again.I like it.  Much more compact than the older styles and easily able to mount to a wall or post as a charging station.  I would definitely buy again."
    },
    {
        "review": "Good charger but cheap cables. If you’re looking to instal a more permanent plug like I do instead of using the alligator clips. Just know that you can remove the terminals from the clips or buy an additional cord. The ones that come with the charger clips are the 10 mm. Notice is picture they have stepped spacer to make it the 6 mm. I ended up buying the 6 mm because I wanted to keep the alligator clips for other vehicles.The bad is that the cables are short, I had to add length to the wires for my application. But when I cut the ends I was really disappointed to find the wires are aluminum. Not some electroplating over copper, just solid aluminum wire. I feel at the price point they could have done copper wire and a few inches longer. I actually didn’t even end up using the 6 mm terminals from the extra cable I purchased. Really all I used was the plug and inline fuse.For the actual charger it does a pretty good job, I have had several some from a very well known brand think “battery tender” not sure if it will post this if I mention to many other brands.So in testing voltage and having the power on in vehicle (eng not running) I was a little disappointed in the power supply feature, I got a little more voltage if I just left it in the charger mode. It’s for an AMG battery. I do a lot of stereo or interior work so having at least a little power is necessary. This doesn’t provide enough amps for most larger stereos but it helps and gets you by for 5-10 minutes of use without tapping out your battery to hard.It does charge fairly quickly, I watched the different stages of voltage as it goes through the charging cycle. One example is I have it connected play around with stereo for 10 mins, probably pushing up to 1,500 watts and 100 amps. It helps back this up some, just don’t do it for long periods. But what helps when I’m done I unplug the charger, plug it back it and it will boost up the power and bring the voltage back up quicker compared to just leaving it plugged in. It’s not advisable to run a powerful stereo without the vehicle running unless you have something with least say 100 amps. But this will get you by for a min.The charger seems to be smarter than my other brands too. As in watching the voltage at stages and then float.Overall if you can get it on sale then it’s a good choice for 10 amps.But they really need to improve on their cables. And the cost of those extra cables. Really I just want to be able to buy the plug and make my own."
    },
    {
        "review": "I cannot recommend the NOCO Genius10 enough. After a truly awful experience with an expensive, useless charger (the JMR), the NOCO was an absolute lifesaver for my 2022 Porsche Cayenne.My car has a specialized Lithium (Li-ion) battery, which can be difficult to charge with standard equipment. The worst part? The dealer quoted me $2,000 to replace the dead battery, and because it was a special order, the part was on a 4-6 week back order! I was looking at a massive bill and weeks without my car.The overpriced competitor (JMR) completely failed to charge or repair my battery—even after days of being plugged in.The NOCO Genius10, however, handled the challenge with ease. It has modes to work with multiple battery types, including Lithium, Lead-Acid, AGM, and Marine, and successfully not only fully charged the battery but also successfully repaired it! This charger is proof that you don't have to spend a fortune to get top-tier performance.For less than half the price of the faulty unit I initially bought (it cost me around $80), the NOCO is incredibly versatile. In addition to its multi-chemistry capability, it serves perfectly as a trickle maintainer. This is ideal for keeping the battery topped up and healthy during the times my Porsche is parked in the garage for several days.Stop searching and buy the NOCO Genius10. It's affordable, reliable, and actually works on tough-to-charge batteries like Lithium. This brilliant piece of engineering saved me $2,000 and weeks of waiting!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Works fantastic! I had the Genius 5 and it worked very well also until my ex got away with it. So this time I ordered the ten and it didn't disappoint.  Had an Interstate Megatron battery that had sat dead for a few months. Charged it in a few hours and its running like new ever since. I also like the recondition setting.  Used it on a deep cycle battery and it brought it back to life as well."
    },
    {
        "review": "So far so good. I purchased the genius 10 in hopes to save some of my batteries around the house and save a little bit of money in the long run. I have everything from cars trucks, RVs, dirt bikes, generators, and everything in between. I use this specifically on my generator, which uses a 12V sealed lead acid battery. I had the generator sitting for a few months in the storage container where it went through some hot and cold Cycles needless to say my lack of maintenance the battery was dead and would not turn over the generator. The generator is a 9000 W champion. I took the battery out and I did not measure the voltage prior to, but I put it on another brand “smart charger”. That brand rhymes with “shoemaker”. That charger ran for a little bit, but then aborted the charging cycle because it said it was bad. I then put that battery on an old-school charger not a smart one one that’ll just run no matter what and left it on for a few hours and then reinstalled the battery again it would not turn the generator over. I pulled the battery out and it measured 11.5 V and I hooked it up to Topdon battery analyzer, and it said the battery was bad. I purchased the Noco genius 10 not just because I wanted to try it on this but because I know all around it seems to be a good charger. As the Noko instructions said, I hooked up the battery and ran it through a normal charging cycle prior to using the repair mode. The normal charging cycle only ran for about 10 minutes before it said it was fully complete. I hooked up my battery analyzer again and again it said it was bad. I then ran the 12 V repair mode on the Noco and it ran for about four hours. After the repair mode was complete I hooked it up to the battery analyzer again and it still said it was bad, but I wanted to give it a shot in the generator. Oh, and I forgot after the repair mode the voltage on the battery red 12.6 V and it held 12.6 V overnight so that was a positive sign. I put the battery in the generator and it started flawlessly. So I spent $99 on the Noco charger and saved myself $40-$45 from buying another battery for my generator so it seems to already be paying for itself."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good!"
    },
