[
    {
        "review": "I purchased the NOCO GENIUS5 to maintain the batteries in vehicles that don't get driven regularly, and it has been one of those tools that quickly proves its value.Setup was straightforward. The included connectors are easy to use, and the charger automatically detects the battery and selects the appropriate charging mode. I especially appreciate that it can be left connected for maintenance without worrying about overcharging, which gives me peace of mind when storing a vehicle for extended periods.One of the batteries I used it on had been sitting for quite a while and was struggling to hold a charge. After connecting the GENIUS5 and allowing it to complete its charging cycle, the battery recovered enough to reliably start the vehicle again. While no charger can fix a battery that's physically damaged or completely worn out, this unit did a good job recovering a battery that I initially thought might need replacement.The charger itself feels well-built and the display indicators are easy to understand. I also like that it supports both lead-acid and lithium batteries, making it useful for multiple vehicles and equipment around the house.A few things prospective buyers should know:The 5A charging rate is ideal for maintenance and general charging, but it isn't the fastest option if you're trying to charge a large, deeply discharged battery in a hurry.The indicator lights are simple and functional, though some users may prefer a charger with a more detailed digital display showing voltage and charging status.Overall, this is a dependable smart charger that is easy to use, versatile, and well-built. Whether you're maintaining a seasonal vehicle, motorcycle, lawn equipment, or simply want a charger that can safely stay connected long-term, the GENIUS5 is a worthwhile investment.Helpful tip: If you're using it for battery maintenance, consider installing the included quick-connect lead permanently on the battery. It makes future charging sessions much faster and more convenient."
    },
    {
        "review": "Easy to use and well built. I used it to charge the AGM battery on my Ducati and it worked great. The indicator lights are clear, and the connector makes it easy to plug in whenever needed. Great for keeping the battery maintained, especially if you don’t ride every day. Overall, I’m very happy with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "My interstate battery went completely when I went returned from a 6 week trip. Tried jump starting by using my other car but failed.Noco is one of the few chargers that can charge a completely dead battery. Watched a project farm youtube video on car battery chargers  ( see chart at 18m29s of the video) before buying. I purchased the 5A one due to time constraint ( charged 75% in 8 hours ). You can buy the 2A version or 1A version if you have all the time and just want to keep batteries fully charged. I let it charge overnight and had a fully functional battery in the morning.This happens to me whenever I go on long trips ( 4 weeks + ). With a rechargeable jump starter, you are limited by the battery life of the starter which is only 3-4 years after which most batteries fail. The wall charger hopefully will last a long longer.  Saved me a lot of time - No need to drive the car around after jump starting to get some charge into  the battery. Just charge overnight and done. Love it."
    },
    {
        "review": "11/17/2022: Saved me from my very tired self, once again... totally dead battery in my car after leaving the trunk open slightly for a week... hooked this up after giving it a cursory jump with the TackLife T8 and left it to do it's thing. My battery is now in maintenance mode. What a wonderful helper.4/29/2021: Every time I start my mower I am in awe all over again. The battery performs better than it did last year and the year before. My mower starts quickly now without hesitation and I am so thankful. For someone else, this may not be your solution, but for me, a senior on her own, this was far easier than replacing the battery, and now I have it to use for my auto battery that doesn't get that much use.4/20/2021: Took a bit of work to repair the damage to my lawn tractor battery that I caused, but I'll be darned, it worked! Ran it through a charge cycle that didn't seem to do anything and I wasn't optimistic, but I was persistent. Did another charge cycle that it completed, I think, and then the next day ran the repair. The following day I did yet another charge cycle and tried to start it up and I'll be darned, it worked! All the damage I did, my negligence, this great gadget fixed. Nothing else I own, Battery Tender, Jump n Carry, etc. would do anything. I bought a different jump starter, TackLife T8 Pro, that actually got it started but all that did was show me how truly dead the battery was. As soon as I turned off my mower it wouldn't start again. Noco Genius5, and the steps I took, solved that. It wasn't fun since I'm disabled, and it was time consuming, but the end result is what I wanted and needed. Very satisfied customer.A note about the manual... boy does it need to be fixed. Nothing matched what went on at any stage of my repair and charging. My persistence and a lot of finger crossing made up for really horrible instructions.4/11/2021: My lawn tractor battery is in a disgraceful state and it's my fault, combined with continual rain since October that prevented me, due to poor health, from taking the steps necessary for year-end maintenance. Big surprise, totally dead battery with 0 volts that nothing I had on hand could resurrect.Tried jumping it with my Jump n' Carry and it didn't. Next tried jumping with the TackLife T8 Pro and, boom, it started. Wouldn't restart and maintain the start so I grabbed the NOCO Genius5 that I bought at the same time as the TackLife. Yesterday I was very discouraged because lights on the Genius5 didn't appear to be working per the manual. Tried it just now and it's responding, led-wise, as it should and appears to be charging.Truth told, totally my fault, I should be buying a new battery, but, physically, I can't do what it takes to do that. Single senior here and my son is on the other side of the country under no-travel orders, so my life is what it is. I must get by whatever means. Really hoping this works. If I can get it charged again I'll ride this battery as long as I can and then get a new one."
    },
    {
        "review": "I will start this review by informing you that, although purchased,  I've not yet used this product, hence the \"four\" and not \"five\" stars. I will change the rating to \"five\" after I've put it through its paces. The information below reflects some thinking you should be aware of. Please read on.I've spent dozens of hours researching the Noco and took time to speak with one of their engineers. There are a few things to get clear in an effort to dispel a lot of the blah-blah garbage there is out there on smart-chargers. I will condense a bunch of sources and data into a few concepts you can \"bank\" on:1). Auto alternators will de-sulfate batteries of \"soft\" sulfate covering lead battery plates. This begins to happen whenever your alternator is not charging the battery.2) Alternators will NOT de-sulfate batteries where plates have become covered with \"hard\" (crystalized sulfate). It takes high levels of voltage, \"pulsed\" to accomplish this. This function is known as \"recovery\" or \"forced\" de-sulfation. Recovery de-sulfation must occur at voltages higher than 15 volts.3). The Noco chargers have a \"recovery\" or \"forced\" mode for a reason. It is a manual selection and before making that selection they recommend you disconnect BOTH battery terminals. Removing the battery might not be a bad idea, either. Performing a \"recovery\" or \"forced\" de-sulfation with terminals connected puts anything in your auto's electronics (CPU, sensors, etc) at HIGH RISK of being fried due to the high voltages over 15 volts. This data is not stressed anywhere on the Internet that I can find.4). The Noco chargers make the high de-sulfation modes \"selected\", rather than automatic (convenience) because you will know your car's electronics are not being exposed to voltages higher than those already being supplied by the alternator. That is a Noco design concept and a huge for \"Sleeping Well At Night\" (SWAN).5). What is NOT known by most of us and difficult to locate, is the \"de-sulfation\" voltage of any one of the other dozens of smart chargers out there, e.g., Battery Tender, Black & Decker. Yuasa de-sulfation voltage will not exceed 14.08 volts, according to a company support specialist. Still, is the de-sulfation mode supplying a voltage level higher than 15 volts? If it is, you're putting your auto electronics at risk without disconnecting the battery terminals -- which, of course, directly conflicts with the convenience you're seeking when \"setting and forgetting\". Does your smart charger tell you what THAT de-sulfation voltage is?6). So does your smart battery charger \"soft-desulfate\" or \"hard-desulfate\"? The difference is the voltage and duration of pulses.Most of us won't let our batteries get in such bad shape. We'll replace them rather than attempt to wring out every possible CCA or every last Reserve Capacity hour. But I HAVE lost some electronics in my 2007 Camry (climate control assy and peripheral computer connected to it) that had no explanation for failure. The car gets driven very little but I do leave a smart charger connected to it.  Did the charger cause the electronic failure? The mechanic sure couldn't determine the direct cause.But YOU should consider the facts as I've laid them out to you because none of the marketing material or \"specifications\" or \"data sheets\" disclose what the many battery chargers produce when they're in \"de-sulfation\" mode. And are they \"soft\" or \"hard\" de-sulfating? They all talk about \"battery\" health ... but NONE of them speak to protecting your auto's sensitive electronics. Ask yourself why.I was dad-gum impressed that a Noco engineer called me back within two hours of my voice-message and he was able to answer all of my technical questions. Very impressive indeed.This unit rates a solid \"buy\" based on \"superior\" technical support and the reviews of hundreds of buyers from a bunch of different sources .. many of which that've had one or more of these units for years.  *** Special Note: The Noco uses a proprietary connector that is NOT a SAE connector. If you have a bunch of SAEs connected to your batteries .. you will also need to order a Noco X-Connect GC009 SAE Adapter. This will let you use your Noco with all your other standard connectors. And I can verify the GC009 will function as expected."
    },
    {
        "review": "This charger/tender worked well to \"revive\" a ~80Ah lead acid battery that sat in a low state of charge for nearly a year. After an initial 2.5 hour cycle in repair mode, followed by a higher amp charge from an older/larger charger, a noticeable improvement in battery performance was observed. A shorter repair cycle, about 30 minutes total, restored some capacity on another lead acid battery still in use. There are cheaper desulfator-only options, of questionable reliability/longevity, so the additional features offered on this model were cost-effective for my intended usage.While I have not yet used this as a tender/maintainer for any length of time, the cabling to and from the charger are long enough to reach from one side of a car to the other. This unit did not seem to produce much heat in repair mode with ~75F ambient temp. In auto charge mode between 25% and 75%, it was warm to the touch. I'll have to try a longer charge period to see if it heats up further. UPDATE: after using repair & charge mode on a flat battery for a few hours, this unit can get fairly hot. Using this on a flat surface so air can get underneath is a good idea.This product did not come with a manual in the box, just the warranty booklet. Tracking down a user manual on the manufacturer's site did not require much effort. The 6V and repair mode are not activated by quick presses of the single button. Pressing & holding for a few seconds activates these extra modes from standby. Some form of documentation in the box would have been more convenient than going to the website, but I digress."
    },
    {
        "review": "I used this a lot over the last 4-1/2 years, had to repair the power cord twice because I damaged it, and had to chop off the quick connect connector on the DC output a few months ago because it failed. That was after a little over 4 years of use. It did what I needed it to well, and the ability to charge DEAD batteries was a lifesaver more times than I could count. Well, it has finally given up the ghost. Upon cracking the case open, there's a bit of char/soot on the inside, but I can't spot anything obvious that failed, so I suspect something shorted out and fried some other parts. There was slight corrosion, I might add, likely because of moisture ingress. For something that I left out in the rain a lot, that's not shocking. It seems the housing was not perfectly water tight though. However, I noticed a few things worth noting on the inside. Most everything in there was potted, and the PCB was potted onto the base of the housing to dissipate some heat. There is a small thermistor mounted in the strain relief boot on the DC output side, quite far from the battery... There were EMI filters on the DC output.I will likely be ordering another one."
    },
    {
        "review": "The NOCO Genius 5 is a very compact device that does a great job charging my car battery. There's no digital readout but it's easy to read that the battery is more than 25%, 50%, or 75% charged, and over time, 80%-90% charged and in trickle mode. At full charge the lights will indicate 100%. It's very easy to use. Select your battery type and attach the positive clamp to the positive battery terminal. Ideally you then connect the negative clamp to the car frame or a metal bracket for ground. In my case, though, doing so didn't work. The NOCO would not recognize and read the battery. That can happen when the parts you've grounded to are painted or coated, so the fix was to attach the negative clamp to the negative battery terminal. After doing so the charge worked fine.  There's built-in intelligence so it's hard to screw up.  You can't overcharge because the battery in trickle mode won't let you do so, and if you leave it connected long-term, it will continue to charge as needed to keep a full charge, but won't do any damage by overcharging. If you choose the wrong battery type, apparently it will know that too and it won't start charging at all. Also, btw, if you have a car battery that's fully discharged and therefore not visible to the NOCO, you can put the charger into a \"forced mode\" to start charging.  All pretty impressive features and, when you need it, it's a great device to own. Two final comments: the charger does not come with a carrying case but there are various options online that are sized to this particular model. I will post a separate review of the one I purchased. Lastly, I did have an issue with the first charger I received. It just would not recognize my car battery. My request to return it was quickly authorized and the replacement unit I purchased later on worked totally fine."
    },
    {
        "review": "What a good product, charges the battery, recovers it and then maintains it…"
    },
    {
        "review": "I've used a lot of battery chargers and maintainers over the years, and after going through at least four different brands that failed to live up to their promises, I finally bought the NOCO GENIUS5. I only wish I had done it sooner.NOCO is a trusted brand for a reason. The GENIUS5 is well-built, easy to use, and works exactly as advertised. Whether charging, maintaining, or bringing a weak battery back to life, it consistently delivers reliable results. The smart charging features take the guesswork out of battery maintenance, and I appreciate knowing my batteries are being charged safely and efficiently.What really stands out is the quality. Unlike the other chargers I've owned, this one inspires confidence right out of the box. It's clear that NOCO put thought into the design and performance of this unit.Yes, it costs a little more than some competing brands, but in my experience, it's absolutely worth the extra money. I've wasted enough on cheaper chargers that didn't perform as claimed. The NOCO GENIUS5 does exactly what it says it will do, and it does it well.If you're tired of disappointing battery chargers, do yourself a favor and get the NOCO. Highly recommended! ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐"
    },
    {
