    {
        "review": "My third desk lap. More suitable for a teenager. 28\" width of which about 23\" is actual LEDs. The extension are gadgety, nothing more and I don't find the lighting pleasant or enough to cover my desk well however it's adjusted. Overseas adaptor plug (two flat pins). Vastly overpriced (paid 73 pounds, but 18 would be about right).I have several desks that need a wide lamp including a main one that a large monitor and has small bookshelves on either. At the price, I hoped this one might be the best. It's ok, just not the best, and I'm wondering whether to return it.The construction seems quite solid (though not as much as I had hoped. The controls are easy enough to reach though a bit gadgety. The total width is 28\" including the two extensions but subtract 5\" as the space between the main lamp and the extensions takes up some width that doesn't include LEDs (my printer lamp is 31\", but consists of two arms much easier to adjust, simpler controls, a nicer light and the best of three I've bought).(I bought this Galnolt after returning a Woputne, which was a better light but with an even wider selection of colours and brightnesses which, together with an unreliable bluetooth control that stopped working after a few hours was more like something for a child to play with.)The Galnolt is more a lamp for someone who uses a small computer monitor occasionally in the corner of the living room. It's easy to assemple. The little swivel lamps on the end are a funky add-on though with limited utility (not that clear from the photo but they only swivel through one plane, so whien your lamp is positioned downwards onto the desk, that means up and down. The end lamps are 5\" as quite a bit of length is taken up with the swivel joint. It's a bit fiddly to position to get my whole desk well lit without it shining in my eyes. However I adjust it, I don't find the light it emits that pleasant. To cap it all, it came with a foreign plug, the sort with two flat slots. I have written to the manufacturer for a replacement though I have got it to work just now by plugging it into an adaptor that goes to a standard 2-pin plus which is then plugged into another adaptor to go into a UK socket.I think it might be suitable say for a teenager. Otherwise I can't recommend it and wouldn't describe it as value for money."
