[
    {
        "review": "Admittedly, I am a Ninja fan, and have been using  a Ninja Foodi OL700 for the last 3 years. I have loved the versatility of this pressure cooker since it has only one lid and functions include Steam and Cook, Broiling, and so much more. Alas, my beloved pressure cooker has been giving me an error message that, according to customer service,  indicates the circuit board is going. Although I will continue to use it, I know it is dying and have been on a mission to find a replacement for it. Since the available Ninja Pressure Cookers have 2 lids, I don't want to have to store a separate lid.I did research a number of different pressure cookers and the options they provided, but none of them met my basic requirements. Since Instant Pot is a known and trusted brand, I found this pressure cooker. Granted, it doesn't have all of the functionality I had gotten used to, but it would accomplish most of the tasks of a pressure cooker with the options that are important to me. In my testing, it preheated for both steaming and pressure cooking in about 8 minutes which is faster than others I tested.So, what do I like about this pressure cooker?1)  The stainless steel cooking pot is easy to clean and doesn't stain or wear as the cooking pot provided for the Ninja OL700 pressure cooker. This is a definite plus over the Ninja pressure cooker.2)  The cooking pot has handles for removing the pot from the pressure cooker. This is definitely a plus as most pressure cookers, including Ninja, require heat-resistant gloves to remove the pot from the cooker.3)  The handles on the insert pot also hold the pot in place for stirring. With other cookers, you have to hold the pot with heat-resistant gloves when you stir the contents so that the pot doesn't spin.4)  I use my pressure cooker for steaming vegetables I grow at least 2 times a week. Some of the pressure cookers I found steamed under pressure. This pressure cooker does not steam under pressure. So, a vented glass cover can be used for steaming. This makes cleanup easier as the pressure lid is more difficult to clean and dry than a glass lid. I bought the vented glass lid to use for steaming in this unit. In addition, you don't have to empty or dry the condensation collector when using a lid for steaming since the steam and water stay in the pot.5)  The sauté' mode of this pressure cooker allows selection of multiple levels of heat whereas other have only low or high as options. Since I often make gravy using roast dripping, I have found that low is too low and high boils. Since gravy requires simmering, neither if these options works. I like that I can select the heat level I need with this model.6)  Settings are retained for each function. So, you don't have to reset setting when using the same function multiple time as I do for steaming large batches of veggie greens.7) Preheats faster than other units I tested. It took about 8 minutes to preheat for both steaming and pressure cooking starting with cold water.I appreciate that I don't have to use preprogrammed settings when I don't need to, and when I do, the settings are easily changed. Overall, I am happy with this pressure cooker. It doesn't have all of the functions my Ninja Pressure Cooker has, but it works for what I need, and it is the best I have found.UPDATE 08/10/25:I have been using this pressure cooker for about 4 months, and use it almost every day and sometimes twice in a day. I have grown to love this pressure cooker even more than my beloved Ninja pressure cooker. It lacks some of the functions of the Ninja unit such as air frying, but I have multiple ovens that air fry. My primary uses for t pressure cooker is for pressure cooking and steaming. The stainless steel pot is so much nicer and easier to clean than the non-stick pots, and it doesn't stain or wear out as the non-stick pots do. For steaming, I have the cover that fits the pot, so I don't have to clean a pressure lid. The handles on the pot prevent the pot from rotating when ingredients are stirred, and facilitate removal of the pot without heat resistant gloves. Multiple levels for the sauté mode are so very useful for making gravy from the drippings of a pressure cooked roasts which are cooked to perfection vs. other methods of cooking.I could go on and on about what I love about this pressure cooker. I can only say, if you are looking for a great pressure cooker with a lot of functionality, this is the one to buy.I hope this review was helpful for you."
    },
    {
        "review": "Review is for the 6 Quart Instant Pot Pro. I love this thing and plan to use it for every single thing that I can!Setup was easy and next to no effort other than cleaning out the pot to start. Thought I had to take the white rubber 'cap' off the red popup pressure indicator under the lid as it looked like some sort of cover but I did not try - thankfully. I tried the 3 cups of water test and let it run through 5 min. Everything worked fine and showed me what to expect! I highly recommend doing this.Made Red Beans to start and they came out great. I tried it on the Bean setting but it ran it for 20 min. Even with overnight soak it wasn't enough to be done but wow , they got soft already - would have taken an HOUR in the pot to get to this point! Ran another 20 min and the quick steam release let me check progress and the powerful nature of this PRO let me going again quickly! Even with all of the stopping, testing, checking, I still cooked the red beans faster than the pot!Finally the interface is simple and intuitive - did not even need the manual. The presets are just that and you can customize your times. It will flash BURN and freak out requiring to unplug - if anything goes wrong. Yes I tried to re-warm the beans the next night and - yeah don't do that if they're thick. Nice safety feature because you can't exactly SEE what's going on in the pot with the lid on under pressure! Also it's heavy and seems durable. The rubber handles for the pot - love love love!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I recently upgraded my old pressure cooker to the Instant Pot, and I’m thrilled with the improvements. The most noticeable difference is the wider cooking area, which makes sauteing, stirring, and lifting the pot out much easier compared to my previous model.In terms of cooking efficiency, the Instant Pot has been a game-changer. The display clearly shows the current stage of the cooking process (preheating, cooking, releasing), eliminating any guesswork. Additionally, the pressure release is significantly quieter and less messy than the older units I’ve used, which is a major upgrade that I didn’t anticipate.Cleaning up is also a breeze. The stainless steel exterior makes it easy to wash, and I appreciate not having to keep extra pans around since most tasks can be completed in one pot.However, there’s a minor downside: the Instant Pot takes up some counter or cabinet space, and there’s a slight learning curve if you’re new to the Instant Pot menus. But after a few uses, it becomes intuitive, and the time and effort saved are well worth it.Overall, I’m incredibly happy with my purchase and highly recommend the Instant Pot to anyone looking for faster and easier home-cooked meals."
    },
    {
        "review": "I upgraded from Instapot Duo to the Pro. The fact the Instapot Pro has handles makes a world of different. On the Duo it is was a pain pulling the pot out and straining it while hot. On top of that it inner-pot of this \"Pro\" has a heavy bottom making a great is steel pot, so it just work like a normal pot on the stove top giving me the options in using either the electrical device or on the stove top. When in doubt get the 6 qt, is fits most things for a 1-4 person household (I'd argue even 6). Might not hold a Large whole chicken, but I manage by being able to breakdown a whole chicken (it isn't hard, just watch a video)I cook professionally. But at home the Instapot is the best appliance I've gotten. I make Bean from dried at a fraction of the time, amazing quality fresh stock that doesn't require me to to wait 8 hours on a stove top, it takes the place of crockpot, rice cooker (*), and a normal pot. Stews, Gumbo, Chili, and Braises are done greatly when you are pinch for time making what would normally for 5 hour is only an hour or two and near same quality. Which has saved me during a dinner party where my sous dropped braised short ribs 2 hours before plating.It is an investment but if you eat (or plan to eat) legumes/beans, and you use stock/broth occasionally or consistently in your diet. This will save you some money and time while giving you a better product than store-bought stock/broth and canned beans. Both the beans and stock you make freeze well, and cooked frozen bean reheat/thaw fast. And if you freeze veggie scrap (like onion skin, carrot/parsnip peel, celery tops and bottoms) as well and save bones from you chicken (rotisserie, chicken thigns, or whole chicken) you can make stock with all the stuff you were going to throw way anyway. Better than store bought while also being basically free. Lastly it is great for meal prep and you can set it to have your ingredients in it before leaving to work and being done by the time you get home from work.If you cook consistently for yourself and/or for loved ones, or want to cook more at home. An Instapot makes thing easy at a press of a button, and the Pro has very convent features (like HANDLES I cant praise enough!) that make life much more easy. When you get one you will ask also ask \"Why didn't I get one sooner?\". I work late nights and early morning, this gives me more time and saves me from eating out.~*I've mentioned \"rice cooker\" and I want to expand on this.~The Instapot works great as a rice cooker but compared to an actual rice cooker, it is a little slower to cook (20min vs 30-35min) and the rice (especially short-grain/sushi rice) builds starch and stick to the stainless steel pot, requiring a slight bit more finesse to clean. I personally eat rice (and grains) consistently and i like saving 10-15min of what little time I have at home, so i have both a cheap rice cooker and instapot. However if you want to minimize your appliances (i.e. have less \"appliance clutter\") and need to conserve space in your kitchen, then having just an Instapot will work well enough and do every thing a rice cooker can, plus much MUCH more."
    },
    {
        "review": "My previous insta pot was a 6qt non-stick pot.  I really enjoyed that pot.  But many times I wished it was a little bigger.  And getting the pot out of the unit was a bit dicy, you have to use pot holders for the heat and it could slip out of your hands.  However I also became concerned about the non-stick coating on the pot. I had put a couple of scratches on the bottom so I was concerned about the coating.  Enter this new pot. Very nice 8 qt size.  I actually thought 8 qt might be too big but on my first use I almost filled it up, so it's not too big.  The stainless steal pot is awesome and the handles make it very easy to lift out(without pot holders) and I dont feel like it's going to slip out of my hands. First use was a stew and it came out great.  I did wash it in the dishwasher before use.  I found the silicon seal to be hard to put back onto the lid, I had to use a little water as a 'lubricant' to get it to slip back into place.  There are LOTS of options on this thing.....it took me a couple minutes just to pick one for the stew and I finally picked the 'slow cook' option which took six hours....but it was worth the wait....the meat was very tender and the potatoes and carrots were just right, not too soft and not too hard......by the way, if you shop Walmart, the organic carrots taste way better than the regular carrots......I just buy a bag of organic petite and dump them in....this time I also added a bag of frozen mushrooms and that was great.....too many times I buy fresh mushrooms and forget to use them within a day or two and have to chuck  them.10/17/25 updateStill loving this pot....the only issue I have is the silicone pressure ring can be difficult to put back in place.To show how large it is, just now I put in a whole 3 lb bag of potatoes, a whole chopped up celery bunch, a large onion, 8 oz of fresh mushrooms, bag of carrots, 64oz of chicken stock and about 2 lbs of chicken......I put it on an option for 6 hours of 'slow cook'......the slow cook option is pretty cool....."
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love this Instant Pot! It’s incredibly easy to use and has made cooking so much faster and more convenient. The food comes out delicious every time, whether I’m making rice, soups, chicken, or even yogurt. It heats evenly, saves time, and the quality is excellent. The large capacity is perfect for meal prep or family dinners. Highly recommended for anyone looking for a reliable and versatile kitchen appliance!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I we'll start this by saying if I could give this product zero stars or negative stars, I would. Absolutely.I have tried to order this product FOUR times.  Amazon will not let me individually review this item for each one of those purchases, although they are different experiences.The first time Amazon sent me a defective product with a broken lid and a damaged body (I knew that I was purchasing it from the Amazon sell, but I had no idea it was gonna be unusable)My second order was with a Christmas gift card for a brand new product. I purchased the 8 qt \"pro\" as an upgrade from my current duo nova instant pot. I have enjoyed that product, although I do get an error code fairly frequently stating that the pot is the wrong size for the base… Instant pot told me that there is no fix for this I just need to order a brand new pot which I refuse to do so I fidget with it and it will work.  Anyway, the brand new 8 quart would not come up to temperature on the slow cook function. I let it sit for four hours and it was still preheating and the food inside was raw. I had to take the food out and cook it in the oven. I sent the pot backThe third time I ordered a 6 quart instant pot pro which looked beautiful, but had the exact same problem.  There was also a problem getting the lid to come off the pot I had to take a butter knife and push down the steam release so that the lid would release and allow me to remove it even when I was not using the pressure cook mode.  The slow Cook function absolutely would not preheat I did an experiment by putting water in the pot and turning it on for eight hours in the morning with no food in it and it never came up to temperature. Seven hours later it was still blinking preheat and the water inside was like 150°F.  I contacted instant pot and they wanted me to do this almost tremendous task of taking a video showing the problem taking several photographs explaining the context of the problem blah blah blah blah. I said no I am just going to send it back until I can get one that actually works.That brings me to my fourth and final order of the instant pot Pro 6 qt.  I know that it will shock you, but I had the exact same problem. I could not believe it. I looked it up online (as I should have done in the first place)and found that this is a common problem with this product. It just will not come up to heat on slow cook.  One YouTube video said that it takes hours for this particular model to come up to temperature and you will need to have an instant thermometer handy so that you can check your food every few hours to ensure that it's actually cooking appropriately. I don't know about you, but when I slow cook food it is my expectation that that food is going to cook, even if I am out working in my garden or running a quick errand to the store. Why would I be on standby for eight hours when I can probably cook it in the oven in one or two and have a somewhat consistent result?Well, I did contact instant pot (again) and they required me to send photographs, and an explanation, and the whole rigmarole, which I did this time only to get a reply just a couple hours later where I was told the same thing :  if you were going to cook something on slow cook it can take up to four hours to preheat on a longer cook.My reply back to instant pot was that several sites on the Internet know that there is a problem with the instant pot pro in that the slow Cook function does not work correctly. The Internet knows that this is a flaw. Amazon is becoming aware that this is a flaw. And I believe that instant pot knows that this is a flaw yet they refuse to either correct the problem or honor their warranty.I am sad to say that I will never buy another instant pot product after the runaround that I was given by this company, who refused to assist me, will not honor the warranty, made excuses for a faulty product, and ultimately just basically said if you don't like it go return it (literally).  So I am.GOOD NEWS-- UPS will pick it up for free."
    },
    {
        "review": "I’ve been using this Instant Pot Pro for over 4 months, and it has become one of the most useful appliances in my kitchen. The 6-quart size is perfect for a family of four — enough space for stews, soups, and batch cooking, but not too bulky on the counter.Cooking results:• Pork and beef come out incredibly tender — the meat literally falls apart.• Potatoes with meat turn out soft and perfectly cooked.• Homemade beef stew cooks in about 30 minutes, which still surprises me.• I also made beef “tushonka” and it turned out excellent.• I tried making pilaf once — the rice overcooked and the bottom browned slightly, so this recipe needs some adjusting.Ease of use:• Very simple controls, intuitive interface.• I really like that the exterior has no physical buttons — it makes wiping the surface quick and easy.• The inner pot handled the dishwasher perfectly.What could be improved:• The lid is not very convenient to wash, especially around the silicone parts.• An extra sealing ring is included, but it doesn’t completely prevent the pot from absorbing strong smells from previous dishes (paprika, curry, etc.). I haven’t tested sweet or dairy recipes yet because of this — I’m sure the aroma would transfer.• The steam release is very hot and can splash, so you need to stand back when depressurizing.Overall, it’s a great appliance that saves a lot of time and consistently delivers delicious results. I rate it 4 stars, mainly because of the lid cleaning and lingering smells, but I’m still very satisfied with how well it cooks."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought the Instant Pot and it was genuinely one of the best decisions for my kitchen. I use it several times a week and it has solved things that were a pain before, especially when meat or chicken is frozen. Literally: from freezer to served plate, without drama. One of the moments where I loved her the most was at Christmas. I was able to make the entire dinner in about 2 hours, when normally I would have spent all night cooking. Soft, well-cooked meats, with flavor, without being stuck to the stove or stressed about time. The pressure works excellent, the results are consistent and it gives you a lot of peace of mind knowing that you can cook even when you didn't plan anything in advance. It is ideal for: chicken or frozen meat, stews, broths, rice, “everyday” meals and also for special occasions. At first the buttons are a little intimidating, but it is a matter of using it a couple of times. Then it becomes automatic in your routine. Plus, the inner pot is easy to clean and the safety system feels very reliable. I'm not exaggerating when I say it saves me time, energy, and stress. If you cook at home and want good results without spending hours, it is totally worth it. I recommend it without hesitation and I would definitely buy it again and in fact I am thinking of buying more sizes :D."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great product. Stainless steel inner pot that can be removed and used on the stove. It makes cleaning easier and will last forever. I make yogurt in pots in a bain-marie every week. I pasteurized whole eggs with the sous vide function. Quiet when using pressure, which works wonderfully."
    },
    {
        "review": "After my previous Instantpot stopped working one day. It was time for a new one.This one I chose as it had sous vide function. I have used this function twice now. I am quite impressed with it I must say.This Instantpot has ten functions. It is bigger than my precious one at 8qt. While I don’t cook for many I do not regret choosing the bigger version.What I enjoy about this particular Instantpot is the following:-The steam release is so much more easy to use. Just slide down to open and slide up to close.-The stainless steel liner with handles is the best update to this device ever. Silicone handles make it so easy to remove a scorching hot pot.-As with my previous pot this one has the dial to adjust time, temp and such. So easy and handy.-the cord is attached rather than having to plug it in the pot each time.As of yet I haven’t use this Instantpot for anything else. I can’t imagine it be any less than my other Pot.One thing I can think of that I’m not so impressed with:-There is a small slot to slide one thickness of your cord in the back, to hold it. It is fairly useless as being able to hold more cord would have been so much more useful. Even a nice Velcro strap to fold the cord up would have been so much better.Is this InstantPot worth buying?It is in my opinion a solid buy. It has more than one use and is a well built product."
    },
    {
        "review": "Product was working fine till unexpected malfunction happened. The Tokion team was then extremely helpful. Provided me multiple options for repair through warranty and when all solutions were exhausted, full refund was issued upon shipping back the item"
    },
    {
        "review": "The product demo was given by Mr. Zafar Khan. The demo was really good and detailed. Made things so much easier. And the product is really convenient and amazing to use. Must buy."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love this Instant Pot! The 4-quart size is perfect for one or two people. It doesn't take up much space on my countertop, but it's still big enough to cook a full meal.Before this one, I used a 6-quart Instant Pot to make bone broth. It worked great, but I actually prefer this smaller size. Now I can make fresh, smaller batches two or three times a week instead of one big batch. For some reason, it just feels so much better, and I enjoy having freshly made broth more often.It's very easy to use, and I love how versatile it is. I've already used the pressure cooker, sauté, and slow cook functions, and everything has turned out great. The stainless steel inner pot is also very easy to clean, which is a big plus.It also looks really nice on the counter. The Sea Salt color is simple and modern, and the compact size makes it easy to store or move around if needed.If you're looking for an Instant Pot that doesn't take over your kitchen but still does everything you need, I highly recommend this one. It has been a fantastic purchase!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I like the shape of this one and the death of this one much better than my old 3 quart instant pot let me explain. The old 3 quart mini instant pot was taller and smaller around. So if you’re a person that had a six or an 8 quart instant pot you had to re-buy all of your accessories. And I am in process of downsizing. I gave away my big six and my eight cord instant pots in favor of just having one smaller one and I chose the Rio. It’s is big around as the six and 8 quarts, but it’s not nearly as deep so it’s not as deep as the other instant pot mini design which I don’t think is available anymore I’m not sure this one is bigger around so it’s easier to get a chicken in there a whole chicken And maybe a little bit of carrot and vegetables. Don’t go over the line inside, but it’s much easier at least with your liquid. It works OK see if your chicken wing is sticking up or the very top of the breast, but it doesn’t work for the liquid to go up any higher than the line in there so be sure and note that because that will be a difference, but it holds 4 quarts in total so you should be fine but so far everything that I have made in it. I have been happy with and I like the ease of being able to put a small roast or a whole chicken in there without stuffing it up on its side or something like that I like to put it in the correct way so that it comes out easier. This is the second one of these Rios that I bought. I gave the first one away because someone came and wished they had one and they were in a situation that I think it could’ve been quite helpful so I gave them my first one and now I got one of these brand new beautiful white ones just very pretty."
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love this Instant Pot! The 4-quart size is perfect for small families or meal prep, and it doesn’t take up much counter space. It heats up quickly, cooks food evenly, and the 7-in-1 functions make it incredibly versatile. The controls are simple to use, and cleanup is fast thanks to the nonstick inner pot. It’s a stylish, reliable appliance that has made cooking much easier. Highly recommended!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I honestly didn’t expect to love this Instant Pot as much as I do. It has made cooking so much easier and faster for my family. The wide design is perfect because I can cook larger meals without everything feeling crowded. I’ve made rice, soups, stews, and even yogurt, and everything comes out delicious every time.The controls are simple to use, it heats up quickly, and cleanup is super easy. If you’re looking for a versatile, high-quality multicooker that saves time and delivers great results, this is definitely worth it. I highly recommend it!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my first Instant Pot. I wasn't sure how I would feel about using one for cooking, which is why I had not bought one previously, I am pleasantly surprised. It's smaller than others I've seen, which I appreciate because of lack of space. I've been able to cook a variety of food without complaint, which always comes out tasting great. It cooks the food perfectly as long as you use the correct amount of liquid and cook for the appropriate time. Worth every penny that I did not want to spend during these times of high inflation."
    },
    {
        "review": "Perfect size, just a bit bulkier than my earlier Instapot. This one is wider, while the earlier one was taller with same capacity so it looked sleek. My kitchen has limited space guess that’s y it hurts.But works beautifully & good quality of steel bowl."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been using this Instant pot almost every day now, and it has made cooking so much easier. I use it regularly for meal prep and to make soups, rice, beans, lentils, potatoes, and other quick meals. It's especially helpful on busy days because I can start it and walk away instead of standing by the stove.The Pressure Cook, Sauté, Steam, Rice, and Yogurt functions all work really well, and I love the Keep Warm feature - it keeps food at a good temperature until everyone is ready to eat.It takes a few minutes to come to pressure before cooking starts, and depending on the recipe, you'll also need time for the pressure to release. But once you know that, it's pretty easy to factor into your meal planning.It has saved me a lot of time and I would definitely recommend it to anyone looking for a versatile multi-cooker."
    },
    {
        "review": "I am so happy with my Instant Pot RIO Mini 7-in-1. It has quickly become one of my favorite kitchen appliances.The 4-quart size is perfect for small families, couples, or meal prep. I love how versatile it is—it works as a pressure cooker, slow cooker, rice cooker, steamer, sauté pan, yogurt maker, and food warmer all in one.Everything I’ve cooked has turned out great, from rice and beans to soups and tender meats. It saves me so much time, and cleanup is incredibly easy. The controls are simple to use, even for someone new to pressure cooking.If you’re looking for a compact, reliable, and multifunctional cooker, I highly recommend this one. It has made cooking faster, easier, and more enjoyable.Pros:✔️ Perfect size for everyday use✔️ Easy to operate✔️ Cooks food quickly and evenly✔️ Multiple cooking functions✔️ Easy to cleanRating: 5/5 Stars – Highly Recommended!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased the Instant Pot recently, and it has genuinely improved my daily cooking routine. The multi-function options are very well-designed — pressure cooking, sautéing, slow cooking, and steaming all work flawlessly.Rice, biryani, dal, soups, and even boiled vegetables are cooked perfectly with consistent results. The stainless-steel inner pot is sturdy and easy to clean, unlike non-stick pots that wear out quickly.I also appreciate the safety features: lid lock, pressure release valve, and auto temperature control. The appliance feels premium, user-friendly, and energy-efficient.Overall, the Instant Pot is a reliable, time-saving kitchen appliance, especially for busy households. Totally worth the purchase."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good and practical. As seen in the photo. Due to its very functional size."
