[
    {
        "review": "Had a All in One Ninja that finally gave out.  Tried this brand and no looking back.  Great for pressure cooking, roasting, and air frying. Do have to change lids but even that was fast, convenient and easy.  Quieter then the Ninja which surprised me. No leakage or odor issues even after use. Stainless steel inner pot make cleaning easy.  Its settings are very simple, pick cooking feature, set temp and time and wait till done.  Have cook lamb leg, lamb chops, ribs, ribeye, etc all in it and always left food cook as desired and each was always tender.  My Go To"
    },
    {
        "review": "I received this instapot/air fryer 8 qt. It is massive but I needed something this size.I used it the first day I got it.  I cooked 1 pot spaghetti in it. Three spaghetti turned out great.  I forgot to season the meat, because I was so excited about getting it in.  It cut my cut time in half.The only thing was coming the hamburger meat with the airfryer.  It cooked the meat but it browned it to much. Next time I will try cooking it on another setting.I haven't found anything that I don't like about it yet.  The size is great for my size family, it cools great for the first time using it.  I will try other settings to see how it works with the dehydrator, the pressure cooker, and the other settings.  I will update on those and how i like them."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was always pretty anti-kitchen gadgets. The InstantPot seemed like just another thing that did stuff I could already do on my stove or oven. Boy, was I wrong.I honestly am in disbelief how much I love using this thing. It is so versatile that I use it more than anything else in my kitchen, except maybe the sink.The reasons why? Well, for starters it’s super easy to clean. Instead of making a mess with multiple pans and pots and lids with food stuff splattering everywhere, the InstantPot is one thing that’s fully contained. When the timer ends I take out the food, clean the pot, and wipe down the lid and little “gutter” and that’s it. Two, it’s so FAST! Now, granted some things will feel like they take the same or similar amount of time when pressure cooking because the machine has to build pressure. BUT, the difference is that I can set this and LEAVE! When it’s ready it beeps and I can choose to keep the food warm or have it stop cooking when the timer is done.What really sold me on finally purchasing this is the air fryer attachment. I’ve been wanting an air fryer so this was much cheaper than buying everything separately. I will say that while I do love the air fryer it’s probably not the best on the market. When using a two-tiered basket, whatever is in the top portion *will* be more cooked than the bottom. This is a problem I will try to fix by simply removing the top portion when it’s cooked and letting the bottom cook longer until it’s done. Not ideal, but for delicious air fried food ans the convenience of having these capabilities from one machine at this price, I’ll make do.I recommend purchasing an InstantPot cookbook to accompany this. It will help acquaint you to how this machine can be used, even if the cookbook is more oriented towards one of the models without the air fryer.What do I use this for? So far I’ve made vegan chana masala (yum!), vegan banana bread (yum!), air fried Brussels sprouts (yum!), truffle fries (you get the idea). I’ve also used this to steam food to make dog food for my pup with allergies. One of my favorite things to use this for is dry goods!! I will dump a whole bag of dry beans or quinoa or whatever in the pot, add spices, water, set it, and it’s done to perfection so quickly! I’ll then can everything and freeze it so I have beans and quinoa on hand all the time whenever I need them for a meal! It’s the best! Not to mention the pressure cooker seems to really INFUSE whatever you’re cooking with the spices you choose. The beans I’ve made with this I’ve eaten plain they were so so tasty!I live with roommates but this has been the best purchase ever. I’m lucky to have the room to store it, but it’s also great because they can cook on the stove or in the oven if they want, and I have everything I need right in this InstantPot so no one has to wait on anyone to clear out before they can do their own thing.Things I wish it did differently:- I wish the display showed seconds. Minor inconvenience but the display in general makes it feel cheap.- I wish the display showed what setting it was on and what action it was currently doing. For example, a small icon for “pressure cooking” so you can remember that you chose the correct setting. Or a little icon that showed it was building pressure so you don’t think you didn’t hit “start” or something before waking away. Also, something that showed the current temperature inside instead of what you set it to so you know how things are cooking or heating up/cooling down.- A persistant beep every minute or so when food is finished cooking."
    },
    {
        "review": "After using a while I can say that I do love this appliance, I wanted one for a long time but didn't have space, I bought this one for it's air fryer capabilities. It airfrys OK, not as good as a standard air fryer but it does the job. I have not used the slow cooker function so I can't speak to that. I would not recommend purchasing this model for the air fryer honestly, just get a regular instant pot...I also love that I bought the bigger size even though it is massive! I like to prep a lot at once."
    },
    {
        "review": "I am an experienced pressure cooker user, so the Instant Pot posed less of a learning curve for me than for some.I  have used the slow cooker, pressure cooker, sous vide and air fryer functions so far.  Great performance in every mode. My family and I  love the yogurt I make in the sous vide mode.The air fryer does a very good job for fries and onion rings. I will soon be trying to fry some chicken, which I was not willing to try with our toaster oven air fryer.  The Instant Pot has a smaller fryer capacity than one might expect from an 8 quart device.The pressure cooker offers true ease of use as compared to my stove top pressure cooker.  Good capacity, easy to make plenty of whatever for a crowd.  The electronics make the device “set-it-and-forget-it” for most jobs (although I would never leave house with a pressure cooker in operation).Many people are concerned about the possibility of being injured by a pressure cooker and/or winding up with soup on the ceiling.  Such events are virtually impossible with modern pressure cookers, including the Instant Pot.  There is a lock system on the Instant Pot that will not allow the pot to be opened while under pressure. Pressure can be rapidly released through a valve provided for that purpose, if the user desires. Even so, the lock remains in place until the pressure is vented. Over pressurization is prevented by the electronics. Follow the instructions in the manual, and you’llbe fine.The slow cooker function also worked well, as did the sous vide function. One thing to bear in mind for these functions is that the pressure valve should be open since pressure is not required. That’s why there is a small hole in the glass lid of your old school crock pot.I have not used the device got dehydration. It appears, however, at least without some kind of hack I haven’t figured out, to have a very small capacity. This machine would not be a good choice for that function in my opinion. Check out some YouTube vids and you will see my point illustrated.Those are the pros. The one big con is the size of this device.It is well constructed, so it is heavy. Might be tough for a small person to lift. Also, has a big footprint. It has two lids (pressure/slow cook and air fryer),  both of which are the same diameter as the device. These means you need more than 24 inches of counter space (12 inches deep) to store the device and fryer lid. It’s about a foot to 14 inches tall with the pressure lid. The fryer lid, when in use, makes the thing over two feet tall.Had occasion to call customer service with a couple of questions. Excellent service. Found out you can use the fryer basket as a steamer, if you want.Overall, I am very satisfied with this purchase and the value offered."
    },
    {
        "review": "I love this pot,I almost didnt buy it because of aome.of the comments.But I bought it and it works perfectly great and my food is ready in no time.Just ensure when you get your package tou inspect it for damages..if theres damage, call Amazon customer service immediately and they will send you a link to take picture to send to them while youre kn the phone with them as proof. Then make your exchange. Other than that, product works wonderfully!"
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a fantastic all purpose cooking product! My son threw it, and it still works five years later!I can make a meal from frozen in less than a half an hour, and it's delicious using the pressure cooker.I have also used the saute abd steam functions. I will be trying the sous vide tomorrow.I can't say enough on its durability!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I had a different brand of a pressure cooker, but knew there was a lot of hubbub about Instant Pots and their new air fryer lids. My friend mentioned there was this newer model that doubled as an air fryer / roaster, and I got one! This machine is incredible, I'm being honest. We roast vegetables for almost every dinner and this air fryer lid shaves half the time off of that process and doesn't make the entire kitchen hot. Our oven takes about 10-12 minutes to get to the right temperature, then roasts for 20 minutes, then takes another hour to cool down. The air fryer option on the Instant Pot takes about 2 minutes to heat up, only needs to cook for about 10-12 minutes tops, then cools down very quickly. It's truly a fraction of the time and frees up the oven for the main course!So far we've loved it for cooking carnitas and then crisping with the lid once done, making delicious one-pot pasta meals and broiling the cheese on top, or making some nice stews / other dishes. I'm extremely, EXTREMELY impressed with the Duo Crisp. Pressure cookers are great to begin with, but having the simple addition of the air frying lid is just a total game changer for us. I also didn't realize that stand-alone air fryers are rather expensive, so this machine being able to do both pressure cooking AND air frying for us for less than the cost of a big air fryer has been amazing. I cannot recommend this machine enough!We've had it a month and use it about 5-7 times a week at the moment. It's also helpful for getting roasted vegetables at lunch as it's so quick about cooking. I'll update in a few months or a year on how it's doing - but I'd buy this again a few times with how useful it is! If you're on the fence - don't be. Just get it - you will be happy!"
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "This pressure cooker has become one of my favorite kitchen appliances. It’s very easy to use, even with all 12 functions — the control panel is simple to understand and the presets take the guesswork out of cooking. Food cooks fast and comes out tender every time, whether I’m making rice, soups, or meat dishes. Cleanup is quick since the inner pot is nonstick and dishwasher safe. It’s also compact enough to store easily but still has a good capacity for family meals. Overall, great value for how much it can do."
    },
    {
        "review": "so far so good.  Honestly bought this one becuase my power quivk  pot ressure Release Valve fell off and i cant find.  Placed order for the replacement only to be told on back order. So i found this one, and I am honestly impressed with it.  I am not sure which one I like better.  This one is  easy to use, the size is great there are two of us in the house unless son comes over for dinner.  Thi one  is  practical, and it helps make meals faster while saving time in the kitchen. The quality feels good and it works as described. It arrived well packaged and in perfect condition. It has become a addition to my kitchen and I would recommend it.”"
    },
    {
        "review": "While this is a pretty standard pressure cooker for this price point, I chose this one because most don't have a sous vide function. Overall I am happy with it. I have a higher end brand at home, but purchased this during an extended stay with family and it's been perfect in that context. So far I have not noticed a big difference between this model and the name brand. In at least one respect, this model is better. The non-stick pot seems far more durable than the  name brand. I haven't been careful with this pot, and in a few weeks of heavy use (like once a day), there's no discoloration or dinging through the non-stick coating.As noted, it has a sous vide function. Don't sleep on this, getting pre-marinated meat at the market and popping it in the water bath without even opening the packaging is incredibly nice; the meat turns out great, it's incredibly forgiving, and there's zero clean-up. It's particularly choice for dinner parties where everyone hangs out beforehand and you want to be able to serve hot food as soon as everyone sits down. Only down side compared to a standalone sous vide is that you're limited to the area of the pot (though the pot is big so it fits most jobs), and the time to heat the water is long compared to the standalone.A few minor quibbles: the yogurt function is not great.. There is no built-in boil function, so that must be done independently. I had been very happy with the name brand version, as there is very little scalding, which is the big down side of doing it stove-top. You can use the saute function to boil in this model, but you have to babysit it more. The non-stick pot does a pretty good job of avoiding scalding but I don't trust it enough to walk away like you can with other models. Given no boil feature, the Comfee basically holds the milk to the yogurt temperature. That means you have to have a thermometer to test the temp yourself prior to adding the cultures, or risk killing them. Other models self-regulate and let you know when you can add the cultures. You also cannot easily add time if you check the yogurt and it's still too runny. A minor issue, but a surprising one. I'll still use this to make yogurt, but I'm guessing the sous vide function is a better way to do the post-boiling steps.Second, the manual is pretty worthless. The model works like most pressure cookers, but the buttons are not quite  consistent with other brands (\"Level\" is not the best choice of description). So, I wouldn't give this model to, e.g., my mother who's never used a pressure cooker before, as it would likely be too confusing. I'm a frequent user of other models and have been for years, and I even found it a little less clear than I'd like. On top of this, unlike name brands, there is no real quick start guide or recipe book. Not an issue for me, but might be for some.Third, the pressure valve is a little subtle and a little spitty(?) It always takes me a second to feel out which way is sealed and which is open, as the markings are faint and the rise between the two is subtle. Fancier models either make this really clear, or automatically seal when you close the lid. The valve on this feels very old school. And because the release is manual (turning the valve by hand rather than sliding a button, e.g.), it requires more care to quick release the pressure without burning yourself. The release spigot tends to spit a little, so I find I need to be very careful. On other models, I would trust my pre-teen kid to be able to use it without any worry, but for this model, I'd be worried they'd hurt themselves from carelessness.Finally, the choice of included extras is a little unusual: a rice cup (3/4 cup, as you'd get with a rice cooker), a rice paddle, a deep serving spoon, and a trivet. I didn't notice markings inside the bowl that show how much water you add per rice cup serving, so I'm not entirely sure why the rice cup. The rice paddle is an unexpected positive, though I suspect most people who are frequent rice eaters probably already have one that came with a rice cooker. Still, perhaps a good thing for college students or others who have limited space for small kitchen appliances who may actually use this as a rice cooker. I do like the included spoon, as I keep it in the pot when not in use and thus always have it for serving directly from the cooker. The fact that it's right there is a good way to never use metal on the non-stick surface.All that said, the durability of the pot is a much bigger deal than the minor negatives, all of which can be worked around. At this price point, the features and build seem on par with more expensive models, other than the throwback valve."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is the best purchase ive ever made. As a Hispanic cooker i would be nothing without this. Zero complains i use it everyday. I make meat,beans ,rice and even bufallo chicken dips . Theres sooo much you can do and easy to use . Has not given me a single problem and the day it stops working i will purchase anotherone. Bought my mom one and my grandma because theirs didnt have enough strength and dint cook as fast as mines and they love it ."
    },
