[
    {
        "review": "I can't believe I waited this long to switch. This is my first time ever using an electric toothbrush, and I am seriously surprised by its effectiveness. My teeth have honestly never felt cleaner, it feels like I just walked out of a professional dental cleaning every single time I brush. One of the most useful features is the built-in timer. It pulses/vibrates every 30 seconds to let you know exactly when to switch to a different quarter section of your mouth. It completely takes the guesswork out of making sure you brush for the full recommended two minutes.Battery life has also been great. A single charge easily lasts for about a week, if not slightly longer, which is perfect since I don't have to keep the charging base cluttering up my bathroom counter all the time.Before buying, I noticed this model is highly recommended by many reviewers due to its incredible bang for the buck. Now that I've been using it, I totally agree. You don't need to spend hundreds of dollars on a fancy toothbrush to get amazing results. Highly recommend!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased “Oral-B Pro 1000 Rechargeable Electric Toothbrush” on the recommendation of my dental hygienist, who suggested the brand to help manage my gingivitis. After my previous Oral-B lasted over a decade, I was happy to stick with the same reliable brand.I find this model to be extremely effective; it does all the hard work for me, and the built-in timer works perfectly. It is a high-quality product that offers great value and dependability.While I am giving it a 5-star rating, there are three minor drawbacks: the battery life is somewhat short, the motor is noticeably loud, and the power button can be a bit difficult to press, especially with wet hands. Despite these small issues, I highly recommend this toothbrush and am confident it won't disappoint!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Oral-B Pro 1000 Rechargeable Toothbrush has not disappointed me. I’ve had my own since 2019. It still functions beautifully. The one thing I’ve noticed is the battery charge doesn’t last as long as it did when it was new. I could go 2 weeks or more without charging. Now it’s about 1 week & I need to charge. I do brush morning & night for the full 2 minutes. I love everything about it. I don’t have sensitive teeth thus my teeth can handle the deep cleaning it provides. I really don’t recommend this if you do have sensitive teeth as I feel it would be too harsh. The timing function works wonderfully for it tells me when to move onto the next area of my mouth. ••To prevent a yukky mess that drips down I have learned 2 things: I thoroughly run water under the brush while it’s running to fully clean it. This, however, doesn’t prevent yuk from your brushing from forming down where the brush head meets the toothbrush. I became tired of a toothbrush that had dried toothpaste on it even though I thought cleaning the head was enough. Unfortunately the yuk does collect at that area & begins the mess. To prevent that I do now take the head of the brush every time I use it now. I just sit in to side in a dry cloth. Hence a clean brush.Since my purchase I’ve purchased 2 more. One for my husband who was told by his Dentist he needed an electric toothbrush because he was developing gingivitis. He loves it because it’s so easy to brush now. The toothbrush does all the work. At first he was doing the brushing & ruined the head a little. So a reminder this does the brushing for you. You only need to move the brush around your mouth. Since having mine I haven’t had one cavity. One thing my hygienist did mention when I first got it was I wasn’t getting high enough to my upper gums. Now I make sure I do. I also purchased one for my 15yo Son as his Pediatric Dentist said it was okay & recommended that he start early with an excellent choice in toothbrushes. For $50- the price hasn’t changed since 2019 - this is well worth getting. For multiple reasons as I feel I thoroughly cleans my teeth. Once I began using this my mouth felt clean & fresh & it does every time I use it. I also feel using an electric toothbrush has saved $ I’d be spending on dental bills. The cost always swayed me away from making the purchase but I’m glad I did. Obviously since mine still works since 2019 there’s definite genuine quality with Oral-B. The box does state it comes with a 2 year limited warranty & there is a 60 day risk free trial. The paperwork details are in the box. For all I’ve written I highly recommend this Oral-B electric toothbrush & I don’t feel you’ll be disappointed. ••I do not purchase genuine Oral-B brush heads. From the beginning I’ve purchased a generic version from Amazon which have worked perfectly. My pictures reflect how nicely it’s packaged upon its arrival."
    },
    {
        "review": "+ Cleans very well - better than a hand brush obviously, not just because of the increased brushing motions but also because you can reliably reach places that are hard to get to (the very back of the wisdom/last teeth for one) with the manual toothbrush.+ Powerful+ Easy to clean- The replacement heads are HIDEOUSLY expensive- If you are travelling abroad, you will need to use a hand brush, since the charger will not work with different voltages (I had to buy a new one when moving to the US).General:- You should replace the toothbrush heads when the bristles are bent too much since they will start bothering your toothflesh. I increase their life span by binding the bristles together with twine or dental floss (forcing them to their original position) and dropping the head in a cup of boiling water (just like making tea). This kills off any bacteria and straightens the bristles. Can be done 2-3 times.- I have been reading reviews by supposes \"dentists\" in toothbrush forums on Amazon claiming that it is best to brush after every meal, not rinse after brushing to keep the toothpaste working, not rinsing after using mouthwash and so on.My thoughts (as a scientist with only one minor filling in my whole life) on that are the following:# Enamel is hard, but not indestructable. 2 times brushing (thoroughly but not excessively) and is enough. Washing out your mouth with water (like you would with mouthwash) after every meal helps.# It is more important to reach every tooth (also the hard-to-reach places like the inside of the upper front teeth and the back of the wisdom teeth) than trying to remove the flesh...# There are no studies that I am aware of, that consistently prove an advantage of using mouthwash over water (if the same amount of rinsing is used). That said, alcohol, sodium benzoate and essential oils all kill bacteria and if a mouthwash contains them in reasonable quantities, it is quite possible that they aid keeping teeth healthy. Obviously the best time to use it is after brushing (when bacterial growth has been removed and lower levels of the biofilm exposed). I do so after brushing at night (since there will be no more disturbing my bacteria for the next 8 hours it is best to reduce them as much as possible before. In the morning they will get new sustenance too soon for it to be as effective.)# Brushing before eating is useless. Yes, you do reduce the biofilm. But then you give the bacteria food and leave them alone? Better to reduce build up AND remove their resources by brushing after eating.# Toothpastes need to contain fluoride. Why? Because it hardens the enamel which makes it harder for bacteria to attach to it and dissolve it. The other ingredients are nice but not essential.# Toothpastes and mouthwashes are not food. Not rinsing after brushing and using mouthwash is essentially swallowing them. Since there are composed of chemicals that I have not seen in my food (or do not want to), probably not a good idea. Also: the fluoride went to work while brushing. No need to keep your teeth caked in toothpaste any longer. Unless you want to give your teeth an extra hardening session. Like they do after a professional dental cleaning. But: their paste contains far more flouride and you could only (possibly, not proven) achieve some effect by distributing copious amounts of toothpaste on every tooth and keeping it there for 30 min. at least. Not drinking, no rinsing. And then rinse it out afterwards.And finally (and most importantly): DRINK WATER! Pure water. Best: tap water (without tooth-destroying additives) if available. (Yes you can also use mineral water from far away places like France and Norway. But think of the environmental cost! And also: that water has been spending time (months?) in a plastic bottle...)  Benefits (scientifically proven): keeps teeth healthy (by repeatedly rinsing them), keeps kidneys, urinary tract... healthy, aids the immune system, aids mental performance, aids slimming and the list goes on...Disclaimer: I am not responsible for anything that results from following above advice."
    },
    {
        "review": "First of all I was very reluctant to get this because a lot of the reviewers were giving very negative feedback about this. I don't exactly agree that the problems are as bad. People were definitely complaining that the toothbrush is too slippery cuz there's less rubber grip and people are also saying that it's hard to turn it off. I definitely see those issues but they're not enough to say that it's not a good toothbrush since you can just get the next model up for closer to 75 to $100 or higher and get the one that's much better this is definitely more of a budget type toothbrush.The other thing is that it doesn't turn red when you're brushing too hard it just slows down instead and also there's no battery meter to tell you how much is left.These are all concerns but not if you're spending $40 on this device which I think is an amazing price for a machine that does the best clean of my teeth I've ever had. I literally feel like I just came back from the dentist every time I use this. This is much smaller than I'm used to but it's great it does get slippery but not enough to say that it's a flaw when you paid $40 for this really nice toothbrush.The toothbrush head is extremely soft and honestly I might only use this type of heads because it's just doing such a good job cleaning my teeth."
