[
    {
        "review": "I’ve been using the Sonicare 7300 for a few weeks now and it’s made a noticeable difference in how clean my teeth feel. The pressure sensor is genuinely useful — it lights up if I’m brushing too hard, which I didn’t realize I was doing with my old brush. The SmarTimer keeps me honest about brushing the full two minutes, and it pulses every 30 seconds to remind you to switch quadrants.Having 12 different brushing settings might sound like overkill, but I actually use a few of them — the gum care mode is gentler on my gums, and the whitening setting feels more thorough for the odd coffee stain.Battery life is excellent — I’m easily getting close to the advertised 21 days on a single charge, so I’m not scrambling to find the charger during travel. The included travel case is a nice touch and keeps everything protected in my bag.Only minor gripe: it’s a bit pricier than a standard electric toothbrush, and replacement brush heads add up over time. But for the overall brushing experience and results, it’s worth it.Bottom line: A solid, feature-packed electric toothbrush that’s easy to recommend if you want something better than a basic model without going full premium."
    },
    {
        "review": "5 StarsI like this Sonicare much better than the older model I bought at Costco. It feels more powerful, cleans my teeth better, and I really like the pressure sensor and different brushing settings. The battery lasts a long time, and the rechargeable travel case is a great feature for trips. Overall, it’s a great upgrade and I’m very happy with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "My Sonicaire Diamond became wobbly and loose. It was no longer taking care of tartar buildup. So happy I replaced it with this one. (The Diamond reviews at the time of my purchase were not good especially for the price point so I chose this one.) Love the travel charger, effectiveness I once had of tartar removal & overall cleanliness. To me, the value is good and it’s sleek and easy to travel with. The initial charge has lasted so far 2 weeks. Bristles are firm but will soften."
    },
    {
        "review": "This puppy is miles beyond my ten year old Sonicare, and also various newer models from other brands. Faster vibrations, stronger cleaning, more settings, much longer battery life. Definitely the best electric toothbrush I've used.Use it with a good tooth powder, activated charcoal, baking soda, clay, or better still alternate between a few of these, and your dental bills will quickly shrink.At fifty during prime week, this was a steal. Even at double that, it's definitely worth it.PS Use generic heads, they're 10% the price of Phillips heads and almost as good."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my THIRD Philips Sonicare in my lifetime.  I'm old.  I have good teeth and I take care of them.  My dental hygienist tells me I'm one of her favorite patients.  It's the toothbrush my dentist from years ago recommended and I absolutely love the brand.  It's great for my gums as well as my teeth.  It is GREAT value for the money and the fact that in my lifetime, I'm on my third (I'm 76), I think that's a very good brand recommendation.  And it's very lightweight and easily transported.  I like the white because I feel like I can see that it's pristine and clean.  Nice design that lets me twist the bristles up and down along each tooth.  Love it and highly recommend it.The whitening depends more on dentifrice being used, I believe, and the fact that one brushes multiple times a day.  Check with your dentist on details for whitening."
    },
    {
        "review": "This toothbrush appears to live up to the extravagant claim on the box. I have no way of knowing if the amount of plaque removed is actually 2000% more than what my previous Sonicare could do, but it is a huge improvement.  I used to believe the job of an electric toothbrush was to pound my gums into submission, but this model is far more gentle and does a better job of reaching the tricky trouble spots my dentist never tires of pointing out. So I do recommend this product with almost no reservations.But before you open the box you should know that the power cord is a USB-A plug, wall unit not included. Phillips says this is to cut down on electronic waste, which is such an obvious euphemism for \"it saves us money\" that you wonder why they even bothered.Also, the Sonicare 7300 has Bluetooth connectivity, and you can download an app and connect to, um, your toothbrush. I said what the heck I'll give it a try. The app says it values your privacy but then it doesn't let you complete setup unless you agree to send all data to Phillips so they can help you with suggestions for improving your dental hygiene. Then the home screen comes up and it's a dental chart, at which point I killed the app and uninstalled it. Perhaps you might find such detailed help useful, but I'm not interested in being an unpaid beta tester or belonging to a floating focus group for future products.I've been using a Sonicare for years and will continue to do so. But the way this product is packaged and presented seems to make it just another way that big business is trying to carve off a big slice of my conscious awareness. And I’m getting tired of that.But before you open the box you should know that the power cord is a USB-A plug, wall unit not included. Phillips says this is to cut down on electronic waste, which is such an obvious euphemism for \"it saves us money\" that you wonder why they even bothered.Also, the Sonicare 7300 has Bluetooth connectivity, and you can download an app and connect to, um, your toothbrush. I said what the heck I'll give it a try. The app says it values your privacy but then it doesn't let you complete setup unless you agree to send all data to Phillips so they can help you with suggestions for improving your dental hygiene. Then the home screen comes up and it's a dental chart, at which point I killed the app and uninstalled it. Perhaps you might find such detailed help useful, but I'm not interested in being an unpaid beta tester or belonging to a floating focus group for future products.I've been using a Sonicare for years and will continue to do so on the advice of my dentist. But the way this product is packaged and presented makes it just another way that big business is trying to carve off yet another slice of my conscious awareness, and I’m getting tired of that."
    },
    {
        "review": "this toothbrush is a good value for the money, since it is packaged with a charging base, travel case, and 3 replacement toothbrush heads. You can also set it up to auto deliver the heads when they get worn out. The auto delivery is easy to do, just follow the instructions that will be on the Amazon website. The appearance is black, with other colors available. The size is right for the use by family members of all ages that are capable of using it. The handle is durable and sturdy. The handle is light weight and not too heavy to use. The vibration will take some getting used to, as it tickles the gums, for first time users. It will clear up in about a week or so. It has green LEDs that show the current charge, then turn amber when you need to change the brush head. I recommend it to anybody that needs a reliable toothbrush, that isn't a hard-to-find item."
    },
    {
        "review": "The Philips Sonicare 7300 doesn’t just clean your teeth — it judges your entire dental existence. In the best way.The first time I turned it on, I swear it sounded like it was saying, “Move aside, amateur, I’ve got this.” And it did. This thing blasts plaque off your teeth like it’s clearing a runway. My mouth hasn’t felt this clean since the day I grew my first tooth.The pressure sensor lights up like a tattletale every time you brush too hard. The timer keeps you honest. The brush heads feel like they were engineered by NASA for enamel. And the clean afterward? Unreal. My teeth are so smooth I’m convinced I could butter toast with them.It’s powerful, it’s smart, it’s slightly condescending — and it’s absolutely fantastic.Five stars. My dentist is going to weep with joy."
    },
    {
        "review": "This toothbrush is amazing.  I've had an older model for years.  This model is a dramatic improvement.  From the function, clean teeth feeling, to less operating noise - this brush is a winner.It does not have a larger charging stand and unit for holding and cleaning multiple brush heads.  So be aware of that if you are upgrading from older units.  Also, the charging stand uses a USB cable that does not come with a plug-in charging block."
    },
    {
        "review": "You think you spent 200 dollars on a toothbrush, but you use it and you think, “Man, I should’ve gotten this sooner.” Your teeth never feel cleaner. It automatically shuts off after 2 minutes and it has a pressure sensor. Although I found myself not wanting to put much pressure on my teeth at all because this thing vibrates so loudly and fast that if I pressed any harder, my gums would be obliterated.The charging base is cool. It doesn’t come with a charging brick, though. You can also charge it with the included travel case, but that requires Philip’s proprietary charger. (Which is also included.) Luckily, it shares the same charger as Philip’s water flosser so I can just carry one charger while I’m travelling for both. Finally, it comes with two extra brush heads. So you have 9 months of brush usage before you’d buy a replacement."
