[
    {
        "review": "Love the look of these knives and they are very sharp! My only hesitation might be the wooden top being stabbed by the knives when you’re putting them away. The “wood” will get nicked and look bad."
    },
    {
        "review": "Just got this yesterday and already impressed with how sharp these are right out of the box. I own Wusthof knives and even when I first got them they weren't as sharp, used to use the bread knife to slice tomatoes. Used the paring knife and didn't have to pre-pierce the tomato in order to slice it. So glad I made this purchase. Right now the only downside is I wish the slate color was a little darker. Oh, and be careful when putting them on your counter. They have suction cups that really grip."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've had the original never dull knives since they came out and although the knives are still going strong, the handles are not so I decided to buy the pro knives. I like the solid body of them.The pro knives are extremely sharp and I have no doubt they will hold up.The ability to remove the sharpener to store away is a nice feature. The base itself is just plastic with a metal handle for the sharpener vs a plastic handle which for a pro set the metal handle is nice, but the storage base just doesn't feel like a pro item.The reason I'm only giving 4 stars is because Ninja did not include a slicing knife, but included a bread knife. Why would they make this an 11 piece set, not an even 12 and not include one of the most used knives in a set? I personally never need a bread knife, but do use a slicing knife daily. The pearing knife just doesn't work for larger items.The knives are really nice, but feel Ninja dropped the ball by not including a slicing knife."
    },
    {
        "review": "SHARP. Be careful opening up the packages. Lol We love them!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Lovely set with all the essentials, save for a butter knife, but who puts butter knives in blocks anyway. But yeh, I was thinking I could get rid of some stuff by using exclusively this set. Love that it has a paring knife. My very old set did, but no other blocks have since. I like this!There are no surprises as to what knives come with this set and how many. Take a look at the pictures, all of the text, all of this information is available before deciding whether to purchase or not. In multiple places.That being said, there are 11 pieces, 4 of which are steak knives.Then there is a bread knife, a santoku knife, a paring knife, a chef knife and a pair of scissors. That makes up 9 of the 11 pieces. The other piece is the sharpener. I *think* the block itself is considered the 11th piece. Personally I think it is odd to list it like an 11 piece, but again, there are no surprises in what we are getting with this. Just a minor mystery, haha.Or as the product description states,\"11-PIECE SET: Includes8\" Chef Knife,8\" Bread Knife,5\" Santoku Knife,3.5\" Paring Knife,4.5\" Steak Knives (x4),Kitchen Shears,Modular Storage Block, andRemoveable NeverDull sharpener\"but yeh, the picture is spot on.I like that this doesnt need to be used as a block. I think if you are ordering this set, then you likely are going to use it with the block stand. Yet it nice that this design offers options. You can have everything on the block, or you can choose what is attached to the block. Since I rarely have need of the sharpener at this point, I slid that into a nearby drawer. It gave me back a little real estate on my counter. I may bring it back out as my whim guides me.Or everything can be dismantled and slid into a drawer. All very easy to do.Looks otherwise sturdy, clean, and attractive. I like that the handle on the scissors matches the color of the base.The knives themselves are nice, solid, full body. To me, they are balanced. They feel good in the hand, the texture on the handle makes them feel secure while handling.The sharpener is neat. Uh, I am not good with sharpener tools. I like that this takes all the guesswork and skill out of sharpening a knife. Great that there are those among you who do a perfect job every time, but I am not. This is so easy--just put knife in, use the side trigger, which takes care of the sharpening, then done. Easy peasy.I dont rate on price. I just... dont. Everything is pricy to me and since prices fluctuate, sometimes there are sales, coupons, etc. We all have different budgets, brand loyalties, etc.. so I just rate on everything else as best i can. You decide if $299 is worthwhile to you for this set.I happen to have a positive bias toward Ninja products. I have many appliances, and even a single chef knife from their never dull line, which I have been using for years with good performance.These are a welcome addition, and I put them solidly at 4 stars. My hubs was surprised at how nice the are, and was impressed with how the whole thing looks and feels. So, 4.5 stars/5 stars. Going with 5. I will update if I experience anything worth sharing, especially if negative."
    },
    {
        "review": "I have a bad habit of putting off sharpening kitchen knives until I suddenly realize I am sawing through a tomato instead of slicing it. That is what makes the Ninja NeverDull Pro set so appealing to me. The sharpener is built right into the system, so maintaining the edge takes a few seconds instead of becoming another chore I have to remember to do separately.The knives are seriously sharp out of the box. The chef’s knife glides through vegetables and meat without much pressure, the Santoku is great for smaller chopping and prep work, and the paring knife gives me much better control for trimming and peeling. The bread knife handles crusty loaves without crushing them, and having four matching steak knives rounds out the set nicely. It covers pretty much everything I normally reach for without filling the block with a bunch of oddly specific knives I will never use.The sharpener is easily my favorite part. Instead of trying to hold the blade at the right angle against a sharpening stone or guessing my way through a pull-through sharpener, I just use the lever on the block and let the ceramic wheel handle the angle and pressure. It makes keeping the knives sharp much more approachable, especially if you are not someone who wants knife sharpening to become a hobby.I also really like how modular the block is. A lot of 10 or 12 piece knife sets take up a ridiculous amount of counter space, but Ninja lets you remove both the sharpening module and the steak knife storage and keep them in a drawer. With those pieces off, the footprint shrinks considerably. That flexibility is useful in a smaller kitchen or if you simply do not want a giant knife block dominating the counter.The knives themselves feel very well made. The German stainless steel blades have a nice balance between weight and maneuverability, and the riveted handles feel secure without being overly bulky. I can chop for a while without my hand getting tired, and the weight distribution feels natural rather than blade-heavy. The steel is also designed to resist rust and everyday wear, which is reassuring for a set that is meant to stay in regular rotation for years.The navy finish is a nice touch too. Knife blocks tend to be some combination of black, silver, or wood, so the darker blue gives this set a little more personality without looking flashy. It still feels polished enough to leave out on the counter.Durability is obviously difficult to judge over ten years on day one, but Ninja is putting a lot of confidence behind the set with its 10-year sharpness guarantee when maintained as directed. The blades, handles, storage modules, and sharpening mechanism all feel substantial enough that this does not come across as a disposable kitchen set dressed up with a gimmicky sharpener.At $300, this is definitely expensive compared with an entry-level knife block. The value makes more sense if you look at it as a long-term set where the sharpening system is part of the package. A good chef’s knife, bread knife, Santoku, paring knife, four steak knives, shears, storage, and an integrated sharpener can add up quickly if purchased separately.Overall, this is the kind of knife set I like because it removes one of the most annoying parts of owning good knives: keeping them good. The blades perform very well, the handles are comfortable, the modular block saves a surprising amount of space, and the sharpener makes regular maintenance almost effortless. If you want a complete set that is easy to live with rather than something you constantly have to baby, this one makes a strong case for itself."
    },
    {
        "review": "We are a very loyal JA Henkels / Zwilling kitchen knife family, with 4 sets.  When I was given an opportunity to order and review the Ninja NeverDull Pro, I was skeptical.  Well I am a believer now!  These are exceptional knives.Space: A primary issue we contend with is countertop space and knife blocks are a great way to store many knives conveniently but they can take up valuable countertop space depending on the size of your kitchen.  The modular nature of the NeverDull Pro allows you to moves steak knives into a kitchen drawer along with the separate sharpening tool segment which you only need periodically.  Two very nice features.Performance: These are some SHARP knives.  I was educated on the value (and danger) of a really sharp knife.  All of our knives are of higher quality now, and they hold or take a really sharp edge.  These Ninja knives feel as sharp as any we have ever owned.  The steak knives are of high enough quality that they don't need a serrated edge - it's unnecessary - the blade simply cuts with a smooth pull, just like you want it to through meat.  The same holds true for the remaining knifes.  And the cherry on top is a 10-year guarantee on holding an edge and staying sharp.  I haven't tried their built-in sharpener, as these are razor sharp out of the box, but it's interesting and seems to be fairly foolproof so I can't give you an opinion on that yet.  The knife block is plastic versus wood but the suction cups on the bottom of all ninja products are amazing.  This thing sticks to the countertop like it was bolted in place!Weight & Shape:  These are solid slabs of stainless steel with a fantastic weight in the hand.  We had steak twice this week and used the Ninja knives once and the Zwilling once.  The Ninja steak knifes actually felt better in the hand, they balanced nicely, had an excellent and comfortable grip and cut identically well as our Zwilling which did not feel as nice the second night after having gotten a feel for the Ninja the night before.Variety: The kit comes with 8 knives total, 4 of which are steak knifes, a Santoku knife, a Chef's Knife, a Bread knife and a Paring knife round out the other four.  In reality that will suffice for 95% of all the kitchen cutting a general household will ever do.  The 9th utensil is a nice pair of kitchen shears, which hang off to the side.  I'm guessing that they refer to the sharpener and the stand as the last 2 things to equal 11.Aethetics:  The knives themselves are solid stainless slabs with dimpled handles for grip and look fantastic.  As with anything stainless you'll need to keep up with them making sure that you polish out any blemishes you see with a scrubby and they should stay handsome for a long time to come.  The countertop knife block comes in a few colors to add some splash to your countertop and is ver handsome overall as well.Value:  At $300 this is not a cheap set of knives, it is an investment.  But when I think about the cost of my Zwillings it is pretty much right in line considering the variety and build quality.  And just because the 10-year warranty will have passed in a decade, these are most likely going to be good for decades more, if reasonably cared for."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great Knife Set With a Very Clever SharpenerI'm really liking this knife set so far. The knives feel substantial without being awkward or overly heavy, and the handles have a comfortable grip that makes them easy to control during food prep.The variety in the set is also great. Having a chef's knife, bread knife, Santoku, paring knife, steak knives, and kitchen shears covers pretty much everything I normally need in the kitchen. It's nice not having to mix and match knives from different sets.The blades are very sharp right out of the box and cut through vegetables, meat, herbs, and other foods very easily. The bread knife is particularly useful, and having four matching steak knives is a nice bonus for anyone who entertains or simply wants everything to coordinate.What really sets this set apart for me is the NeverDull sharpening system built right into the block. Instead of having to remember how to use a sharpening stone or keep a separate sharpener somewhere in a drawer, you can simply use the lever on the block to sharpen the knife. It's a very clever idea, especially for someone who doesn't want to fuss with knife maintenance.I also like that the sharpener and steak-knife storage can be removed from the block if you want to save counter space. That's a thoughtful design because knife blocks can take up a surprising amount of room.The German stainless-steel construction is another nice touch. The knives have a quality feel to them, and the blades clean up easily after use.Obviously, I can't personally verify a claim like staying sharp for ten years or surviving hundreds of thousands of cuts, but the knives are certainly impressively sharp so far. Long-term edge retention is something I'll have to see with continued use.Overall, this is a very nice, versatile knife set. The combination of sharp blades, comfortable handles, a good selection of knives, and the built-in sharpening system makes it feel like a thoughtfully designed set rather than just a collection of knives in a block."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I'm impressed with this knife set.. the handles look to be of quality wood or wood-like and is full tang with a very nice weight to it.. I really like the ergonomic design - I wasnt sure how they'd feel in the hand but I can tell they won't be slipping around as I cut meats and veggies.. They look very sharp the presentation was great for gifting.. they came in a nice shrink wrapped package so you know it is truly new vs so many other sets where the ends are taped and you've no clue if you're really the first..  Just really pleasantly surprised especially for the price"
    },
    {
        "review": "My initial review after one day using them.I buy cheap knives for $4 and use them as long as I can. My current set is ~4 years old, and for a while have been tearing through what I cut. Enough is enough. So I went hunting on Amazon for a better set of knives.That particular day, they were on sale for $29.99. After reading the specs, I decided they were pretty good knives for the price.First, they are very well made. The blade is a single piece of high carbon German stainless steel that goes all the way through the well-crafted handle, and the handles are thicker than the blades. They aren't going to fall apart at the hilt.About the steel: this is the same grade as used in Swiss Army knives. Anyone who's had one knows how well the steel holds up, and holds an edge. Now, these aren't as hard as professional kitchen knives, but I didn't pay a professional price for them, either.This grade of steel is a good balance between hardness, edge holding, and rust resistance. With a proper sharpening tool they should last for many years. Hint: do NOT run them through your can opener! Get a good honing stone when they start to lose their edge. This steel is easy to sharpen and get a good edge.As far as cleaning, a quick wipe with a soapy dishtowel or sponge is all they need. The blades are so slick they clean easily.Here's a hint on cleaning: Don't use HOT water. My Chinese FiL was a chef in a Chinese restaurant, and had professional knives. I saw him washing them in COLD water! I said something to my wife, and while he never got a good grasp on English, he said something to her in Chinese, and she told me hot water will dull knives quickly. Ya know, I think the old guy had something, because I started rinsing my razors in cold water and they last for MONTHS. Clean them lukewarm at best. I saw a review where someone said they put them in the dishwasher. Not a great idea...Now, a word on sharpness. They are sharp. REAL sharp. I got them, cleaned them, and then took the next smallest one to dice a chicken breast. In the process I felt the blade hit my finger. I looked and saw nothing.I finished supper and then cleaned things up. After I was done with the dishes I saw a tiny flap of skin hanging off my finger. It shaved the top layer cleanly. So cleanly that I didn't feel it, and the flap of skin has healed back in place in 24 hours. That's the sign of a cut made with a sharp knife.Not as important as quality and performance, but I love the design, too. I was wondering how they would feel to the hand, and the curve is very comfortable.All in all, I scored an excellent deal."
    },
    {
        "review": "I don’t actually own these knives. But I’ve purchased them twice as gifts for other people.My friend absolutely loves them!!! I actually watched her use the items, and handled them myself—trust—they’re razor sharp—so be very careful handling them.The weight and balance of these knives are substantial, fully forged from solid steel—formed into one entire piece; so there’s no chance of breaking.The spine is thick and the blade has a 15 degree angle, used by German and Japanese knives to cut razor thin—with little effort.That is, the knife does all the work!The Brewin CHEFILOSOPHI Japanese Chef Knife Set, looks (aesthetically) beautiful! with 5 PCS adorned with elegant Red Pakkawood Handles, with noticeable ergonomic design that awes!These knives are as professional as they come, as stated, made of ultra sharp High Carbon Stainless Steel that impresses right down to the last cut! with an 8” Chef’s Knife, 7” Santoku Knife for sushi and cutting fish, 8” Carving knife, 5” Utility Knife and a 3.5” Paring knife.You’ll love them—guaranteed! And the value for the money? SPOT ON!!! especially when they do 50% off sales!Like I said—I’ve purchased two sets as gifts. My family is away on vacation—so they’ve yet to receive or use them. But I can’t imagine a different experience.No need to think about this purchase! DO IT!Quality, durability, weight, ease of cleaning, sharpness and functionality are ALL 10 stars and beyond!Lastly, the packaging is truly mesmerizing? Can’t explain—you’ll just have to experience it for yourself!I’m a minimalist; so I’m not going to buy something I don’t need—already have GREAT German knives. But these Brewin knives ARE THE REAL DEAL!"
    },
