[
    {
        "review": "I received this camera today and am very excited to use it, so this might be a rushed review! LOVE the camera!It has great beginner friendly modes and is very simple to use and get the feel of. I will say that if you have never used any form of ‘professional’ camera, the different modes and buttons may confuse you for a bit, but once you get adjusted it’s very simple!The lens is nice and allows ample zoom to allow close ups!However, if you are looking for zoom quality from ample distance, you’re going to need another lens. I ordered a telephoto lens, so I’ll see how that works out.In terms of pricing, the camera is amazing quality, though I did end up finding bundle kits that come with a bit more for the same price. If you’re looking for quality pieces only, then this is definitely the right pick!The camera is fairly lightweight in itself, with lenses it will obviously get heavier, but not bulky and very easy to handle with the grip.All the different modes and settings allow for a variety of pictures! I’m more focused on nature pictures, but this does great for people and animals.The camera has GREAT picture quality and refined details. I’m able to magnify into my photos and see crystal clear details you can’t see with the naked eye.I do hiking occasionally, so this camera is going to be great for it! Very excited to see how it does as we plan on going to upstate NY for a hiking expedition and visiting multiple spots.I’ll update further on its performance if needed!"
    },
    {
        "review": "my favorite. the quality of the pictures are great, the controls are super easy to use, and everything work fantastic. battery last for so long."
    },
    {
        "review": "I bought this camera as a cheap way to take good pictures and the pictures and better than expected. I recommend."
    },
    {
        "review": "As someone who wanted to level up from phone photography but wasn’t quite ready to dive into $2,000 camera bodies, the Canon EOS Rebel T7 was the perfect bridge — and it has exceeded expectations.⸻Image QualityThe 24.1 MP sensor captures crisp, clean images in both daylight and low light. You’ll instantly notice the difference in depth, clarity, and color accuracy compared to any smartphone. Whether you’re shooting portraits, street scenes, or nature — it delivers professional-looking results.⸻Ease of UseThis is where the T7 shines. The controls are intuitive, even for someone brand new to DSLRs. The built-in Feature Guide explains settings in real-time, helping you learn as you shoot. It’s like a camera and teacher in one.⸻ConnectivityBuilt-in Wi-Fi is a huge plus. I can snap a photo and send it to my phone instantly for social media or editing. The Canon Camera Connect app works smoothly with iOS and Android.⸻Build & Features• Lightweight but solid — feels durable without being bulky• Interchangeable lenses give you room to grow (the included 18–55mm lens is a great start)• 3 FPS continuous shooting — not blazing fast, but great for beginners shooting action• Full HD 1080p video recording — smooth, with decent autofocus⸻Who It’s ForIf you’re a beginner who wants a real DSLR experience without feeling overwhelmed — this is your camera. It’s also a great backup for experienced shooters who want something reliable, portable, and affordable.⸻Final VerdictThe Canon EOS Rebel T7 is proof that entry-level doesn’t mean low quality. It’s a fantastic intro into the world of serious photography, and once you start using it, you’ll wonder why you waited. Sharp photos, simple learning curve, and real Canon reliability."
    },
    {
        "review": "Although it was an open box [not taped shut] and the battery was in a plastic bag[ no protective cap] and no paperwork whatsoever included ,for a supposedly new camera , i,m keeping it as it has enabled me to take 110 good quality pictures so far! Easy to use if you like or you can be creative with it! Download the manual from the internet! reminds me of my canon AE1 but much more capable!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Now, just for some context, I've never even held a \"fancy camera\" (what I'm calling anything above the little handheld digital cameras) It was a little hard for me to figure out the setting and how to use it, but I watched some YouTube videos and got a slight grasp of everything. For some reason the camera will tend to not focus and actually take the picture. It ends up with me awkwardly yelling at my family to hold their pose until the camera will finally decide to click, but I'm going to blame the focusing issue on me lacking knowledge on how to actually use a camera. Another issue I have with the camera is the photo transfer feature (the thing that hooked me into getting this camera was the fact that it could easily transfer photos from your camera to your phone): it's a little annoying to have to go into the settings to even begin a transfer and make sure both the camera and your phone are connected under the same wifi and once you're there the camera is left on a blank screen which makes me scared I'm gonna mess something up. Last minor complaint, I promise. The picture I added with that black kitten, it was actually really hard for the camera to decide to take that picture for some reason, like the lighting wasn't good enough so I had to put my camera light over her just for camera to see there was a subject to aim at and for the flash to go off. Now overall I'm happy with the camera. I love the photos I took with it and I think I just need some more practice with it. It makes me feel like I'm a little professional photographer even though I have no plans of making them look like legit photos one would take.Update 12/2/22I'd recommend getting a backup battery. It does have a decent battery but there are times when I'm in the middle of using it and it needs a charge. Getting the battery to fill takes maybe a couple hours. Side note, *facepalm* there's literally a low lighting mode on the camera. It'll do a short flash to brighten up the subject/area and then it'll take the picture. Getting the camera to focus is still a slight issue for me but it's not as bad as it was when I was first settling in with it."
