        "review": "It arrived quickly and looks excellent"
    },
    {
        "review": "i love how well the camera performs, i was nervous at first ordering a used camera for the first time. but the quality is great. it did take a while to ship but i mean it was def worth the wait."
    },
    {
        "review": "Camera is nice. Works great. Nikon is a fine brand."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a great camera to get started in professional photography. The camera is really nice. Put some money into better lenses, The stock lense is decent but your going to want more."
    },
    {
        "review": "A small conclusion first: there is no more camera for the money in the amateur or middle class. In this respect, the Nikon D5100, like the D7000 (the latter in the semi-professional league), is a bargain. The operating concept is, by Nikon standards, relatively menu-heavy. But that's not a serious disadvantage, as all photographically relevant settings can be accessed by pressing the \"i\" button twice. The sharp display also shows tips and hints in a very beginner-friendly manner, and technical functions such as aperture and shutter speed are presented in a comprehensible manner using successful symbols. As is usual with Nikon, the camera is very well equipped. In addition to pure configuration options, creative photography is also not neglected. So is e.g. For example, a multiple exposure function is also available, in which two shots merge into one. In combination with the foldable monitor and the perspectives that can be achieved with it, the artist can let off steam here! What I also like is the very neat workmanship of the camera, which is subjectively more valuable than that of the big competitor's \"plastic bombers\". Plastic is common in this price league, but that also has a weight advantage. The robustness of the D5100 is still right. The crucial thing: as a great connoisseur of the Canon system (including full format), the built-in image sensor of the D5100 completely surprises me! The sharpness of detail and resolution is already great with the small but very usable 18-55mm VR. Really good at f/8. Extreme weaknesses, for example when the aperture is open or in the corners of the image, which are often seen in the inexpensive lenses of the competition, are not present here. Surprisingly, the annoying color fringes on contrast edges (chromatic aberrations) occur rarely or in a non-disruptive manner. A hobby photographer can't go wrong with Nikon's kit lens. And even ambitious people may like to stash the 18-55mm in their backpack as a light addition to high-quality fixed focal lengths. As expected, the D5100 works at its best with fixed focal lengths such as the Nikon 35mm 1.8. Even when compared to a full-frame camera from its major competitor, the 16 megapixel image sensor doesn't have to hide. The images appear very clear and are simply brilliant and beautiful - with Nikon, great image quality doesn't just start at €1,600. This also applies at high ISO settings. At least up to ISO 4000 there are still nice prints in A4 or A3 - that wasn't the case a few years ago. Regarding the autofocus: the 11 measuring fields are arranged very sensibly in the viewfinder. You can easily focus on the “golden ratio”. The accuracy is convincing; when the AF assist light is switched on, even at dusk. If you want even more flexibility, you have to choose the D7000 or D5200. Its 39 measuring fields have to be selected first for difficult subjects, which slows down the workflow a bit. Conclusion: small, cute camera with great qualities. With a \"kit\" lens, it's just right for a stroll, a weekend trip or a vacation. With high-quality lenses, it's almost outrageously smart in terms of the image appearance. The very successful contrast distribution with great detailing of even the critical lights as well as the vibrant colors are exemplary - the brand with the big \"C\" can create as much hype about its own new releases. Purely subjectively: the compact, slightly beefy housing with typical Nikon design elements makes the D5100 an \"eye-catcher\" and popular figure. The little one has it all - and I say that with a completely professional attitude."
    },
    {
        "review": "super"
    },
    {
        "review": "The images: very beautiful, fine, contrasty and with good sharpness, very good rendering of the depths of field: it's a real pleasure.... and the sensitivity: incredible! no noise, excellent performance! So ideal when you avoid the flash! Use: easy to handle with its handle, easy settings and in French (almost as practical as the settings of the Lumix from Pana!...). As for the possible presets and options, they are more numerous than necessary. The screen swivels in all directions, robust as they say, allows you to photograph from all angles. I've had it for a year, I make the manual settings and am very happy with this camera. I only regret not having taken an 18 - 105 lens, because the zoom is a little limited on this model. Charger: without cable, it attaches directly to the wall plug, which is practical: it takes up little space."
    },
    {
        "review": "Ok firstly let me say this is not a technical expert review, rather a review of an averagely intelligent electrical engineer that has taken up DSLR photography.My first and most important point is going to talk about Camarthan Cameras. I have had some technical issues with the camera, that turned out to be partly due to my still learning and a dodgy lens i was using. The level of support/service i got was nothing short of excellent. It has been an absolute refreshing change to receive prompt responses to problems, to answer questions on the phone and provide an excellent after sales care, to the point i've even bought a book for the D5100 and another lens of them. I have absolutely no affiliation with the company, in fact i don't think i could further away from them. I would recommend them to anyone wanting camera equipment on here 100%Now to the cameraI bought a Bridge camera and knew instantly i wanted a full DSLR, so against the wive's better judgement, i sold my Bridge camera and bought the Nikon D5100 after reading a thousand reviews on several different cameras.I came to the conclusion that all the major DSLR makers have plus and minus points, you have to decide what is right for you.The camera arrived with the 18-55mm lense and after reading the manual i carefully attached the lense. (trust me you are so nervous about damaging the sensor, the glass the cpu, but just line it up correctly, ensure your camera is off and insert the lense.Charged battery, which only took 45mins inserted battery and then SDXC card 32GB. Turned on and set the camera to program P. (Get off auto and use program until you are happy to move onto A,S, & M modes, which doesn't take long).Secondly after having POS and Bridge cameras i spent 5 mins trying to operate the zoom, LOL. You operate it manually by turning the zoom part of the lense.I am still waiting to get out in the field and really test the capabilities of the camera, but so far all the pet photos ive done are crisp and clear. ( and when they are not it is not the cameras fault.)I have since ordered a sigma 70-300mm lense and tested it and it is a superb budget second lense and should be enough for your needs for now.I am currently following books and video tutorials on getting the best settings for different situations then trying them and recording them on paper until i can embed them in the brain.One thing i am surprised about is the lack of auto ISO in S, A & M modes, but personaly i cannot see any graining on ISO settings up to 3200. But i dont inspect photos at A3 size with a magnifyinh glass either.Ok waffle overConclusionThis camera is seriously cheap for what you receive. It will perform all of the actions you require if you are not a proffessional photographer. Dont listen to some that say dont get the 18-55mm lense it is a good little lense to start off with and comes with VR and AF-S.There are a ton of settings and ways to get to them. Honestly ive spent more of the week learning these than actually taking photographs, as i beleive you need to be slick with settings to capture the moment.This really is a superb camera.If you get lost in the settings and think the camera is playing up, use the two green buttons to reset all the camera settings before thinking its broken (it will save you sending it off. speak to Camarthan Cameras on the phone, they will tell you what you have done _DOH)FinallyGo to the Nikon website and create an account, then register your camera for a full 2 year manufacturers warranty.Hope this helps newbies and anyone who is thniking of buying this camera."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "I purchased this a few days ago here on Amazon. I brought a slightly used one with just over 3,000 shutter actuation's. Saved a LOT of money. I came from the delightful rank-beginner D3200 camera to this upgrade. Here are my thoughts after 2 years of shooting with a D3200 and upgrading to the D5300:1. Stellar upgrade in sharpness! Without a doubt, the most noticeable thing. 39 autofocus points over the 11 on D3200 really helps! That irritating OLPF is gone! I use a 37\" monitor to develop in Lightroom, and pixel peeping on the D5300 is a delightful experience. My friend who owns a D7200 thinks the image quality is identical. It is close, my friends. Too close to call.2. Smaller and lighter than D3200, but still substantial enough in the hand. Anyone with slightly smaller hands will really enjoy this camera. Me? I am 6 4\" with big hands, and it still felt just great. No issues there as far as I can see.3. Your EN-EL-14 batteries will work in it. The D5300 is recommended and comes with EN-EL-14a. Battery life is just fine. To make a guestimate from the 150+ images shot and no budge on the battery meter...500-600 may just be possible without heavy flash use. I should not admit this, but even an aftermarket battery I used in my D3200 for 2 years works perfect in my D5300. No issues at all.4. A surprising find: This much better body has really helped me get a LOT more from my lenses. I kid you not, friends. The 35mm f/1.8 from Nikkor remains sort of a disappointment, but still shows improved image quality on the end of the D5300. The \"Fantastic Plastic\" Nikkor 50mm f/1.8 really shines though on this camera! sharp sharp sharp!!! Lots of light through that big 58mm thread size. It was a performer with the D3200, and really pairs well with the D5300. My old 55-200 VR likes my D5300 as well and has also shown a sharpness bump. If you can believe it, even the kit lens 18-55 from the D3200 takes better pics on the end of the D5300. We all know good glass is important, but often times it seems that good quality bodies get down played even though they are also a very important part of the overall photographic equation.5. You are going to have to make some out of the box adjustments. The irritating focus lock chirp for one. I had to shut that off. The AF illuminator is irritating as well and also had to be manually turned off.6. Control lay-out: For God's sake, take the time to learn the differences if coming up from from D3100/3200 series cameras. It takes a while to learn the new placements and new hand motions to adjust on the fly. David Busch's guide to the D3200 was great for me, and I have purchased his D5300 guide and expect similar excellent tutoring as well. I highly recommend, and you can buy here on Amazon and read on Kindle.7. ISO is a LOT less noisy on this body. Seriously. 1600 ISO looks great compared to many D3200 shots at that sensitivity. Even the one 3200 ISO shot I took for giggles was way quieter than I expected it to be.Overall my fellow Nikon geeks, this is a great upgrade still within most of our financial means. Next gen performance free of the OLPF, and just a real joy to work with. Some people complain about slow Live View, but I could care less. The good friend who got me into photography suggested I learn to get it as right as I could in the camera first, and to stop using anything but the view finder. 3 years later, I am glad for the wisdom he shared. Live View needed maybe for video or some other sorts of shots...but for us View Finder clickers who peep it later, this camera is going to be a happy surprise for you.Bottom Line: If you can afford it (new or used from a reputable seller here on Amazon)...do it! You won't be disappointed in your next step up the DX ladder. The D3200 got you hooked, and now the D5300 is going to take you to even better places and greater enjoyment and satisfaction from our chosen hobby."
    },
    {
        "review": "I spent a good couple months looking for cameras I thought would be an upgrade from my Nikon D60, without breaking the bank.  I must have tested numerous cameras in store, but happened to find that the features of the D5300 meshed well with what I was doing.  I'm not a professional photographer at all, and I wouldn't even really classify as an enthusiast, but here are my observations from a layman.The shutter speed is quick, with much less time required between shots - flash or not - than the D60.  You notice this especially under low light conditions.  I primarily take photos in low light situations such as dinners and restaurants where the ambiance is generally romantic.I have little to compare this to aside from my D60, but it does appear colors are much more vibrant, and the addition of having a liveview screen is nice.I wanted to use this for video too, but I have yet to really delve into taking quality videos with this.  I have a video camera that seems much more reliable when it comes to focusing, and pan speed.  That being said, I have very limited knowledge on using this as a video camera, and I'm sure with additional usage I'll get better at it.Overall I'm extremely happy with my purchase."
    },
    {
        "review": "I got a refurbished unit from a reputable vendor at a great price.  It is a rather light camera and smaller than I thought but has such a nice rubberized grip.  I bought it with the 18-55 mm VR kit lens since I'm just starting out and I graduated from a mirrorless Sony NEX-3N.  I then bought the 55-200 mm zoom lens, and the 35 mm 1.8 prime lens.After a week of figuring things out (it really did take that long to get a decent shot),  I am won over by it's amazing sensor, the lovely articulating screen, solid yet light construction, peer-to-peer wifi, fast autofocus, and many more adjustment features that were lacking in my Sony NEX-3N.  I took pictures from my old camera and new and there is a significant difference in quality in low lighting situations especially.  Also, the additional 8 megapixels to 24 from 16 clarifies the images considerably and a reasonable post processing cropping does not degrade the image.  I've taken some pretty amazing images and with the right settings and lenses you can get professional quality photos.I was not wowed by the camera initially, but once I got the hang of using it, I am very happy with it.  I feel it has enough headroom in which I can grow as a photographer.  This is not a professional camera by the lack of more refined adjustments (like front and back autofocus adjustments),  one control dial and only one SD card slot-but it's not a basic model.  I would hazard to say it has enough for an intermediate level photographer, but it might be too complicated for a beginner.In short here are my likes and observed weaknesses with this camera:ProsIt's the right size and weight to fit in my bag so it's always with me.  \"The best camera is the one that's with you.\"  Chase JarvisIt feels great in my handA view finder with a diopter adjustment and accessories shoe (totally lacking in a lot of mirrorless cameras).An articulating display that's pretty detailed and bright.An advanced 24 megapixel  APS-C  1.5 crop sensorPhase-detection autofocus and contrast in live-view with up to 39 pointsThe removal of the optical low-pass filter for a higher level of detail (that won't be realized until I get some optically superior lenses that can fully resolve the 24 megapixel detail).2016 pixel RGB TTL exposure meter sensorPeer-to-peer wifi to transfer photos to my tablet and smartphone, and also remotely fire the camera.Availability of lenses at relatively dirt-cheap prices that are pretty sharp and autofocus quicklyBattery lasts pretty long when using viewfinderAn actual wall charger for the battery (it was in-camera charging with the Sony)ConsNo touch screen so toggling feels a little dated with everything being touch screen nowDial function operates shutter speed and you need to depress other buttons at the same time to adjust aperture and ISOSome strange locations for certain buttons like shutter release settings and programmable function button.Poor documentation about more advanced featuresNo built in motor so many legacy f mount lenses are manual focus onlyThe viewfinder is a little small and has 95% coverageLive-view is slower than molassesGPS function is junkYou can't make adjustments to the camera settings while controlling it from your tablet or smartphoneA little on the high side if you pay full priceBattery drains quickly when using wifi and in Live-view mode.Some iffy focus issues with the Nikon 35 mm 1.8 prime lens at the widest apertures in low light.  Some have complained online about this combination presenting minor problems.My advice for beginners is to buy the d3300.  It's considerably cheaper, has the same sensor, removal of the optical low-pass filter, and has a built in lens motor.  Granted it only has 11 auto-focus points, a 420 pixel RGB light meter sensor,  a smaller fixed screen, and no wifi or crappy GPS.  You can buy the wifi module separately but it does protrude from the side of the camera body."
    },
    {
        "review": "My review is geared more towards beginners like myself who are just getting into the DSLR world.I've been shooting with the D5300 for a few months now. I bought this camera because it was at a lower price point for an entry level amateur photographer, and I've been having a great time with it.I've used this camera mainly for photographing friends and family, nature/scenery, my dogs, and random still-life scenes that captured my attention. I haven't really captured action except with the video.Pros:1. Light weight (for a DSLR)2. Good beginners camera. Controls not too difficult to learn (the user interface helped me get used to different ISO, aperture, and shutter speed settings)3. Swivel screen is very useful, especially when taking videos low to the ground or photos above a crowd.4. Great battery life (new to me, compared to point-and-shoots)5. WiFi useful for quickly transferring photos to a tablet and emailing to friends and family.  I haven't used the GPS function though.Cons:1. DX format (starting to learn about the limitations versus a full frame (i.e. more expensive) camera2. Video focus tough to use (I guess I still have a lot to learn :P)3. Lenses are expensive and there are soooooooo many accessories out there (my wallet is much lighter than I anticipated)4. Settings can be a bit cumbersome and overwhelming for a beginner, but I'm getting the hang of it.I definitely recommend this camera, but I would recommend getting the only the body if you plan on picking up additional lenses (starting with the $200 35mm f/1.4 Nikkor). I bought this camera with the 18-55mm VRII lens, which is a good beginners lens. I've stopped using that lens since I got the 35mm f/1.4 Nikkor and haven't used the kit lens ever since.Overall, this is a great camera, and I've been having a great time with photography. At the end of the day, the camera is only a tool, but I'm glad I got this one and have no regrets about it."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my third Nikon camera. I previously have owned the d80 and d300s. What I like most about the d5300 is the compactness and weight of the camera. I wanted a camera that would be easy to bring along with me on vacations and walk abouts. This camera far exceeds my expectations.I have not tested the camera's specifications, and I am sure it has been noted in other reviews already. However, I can state the camera takes excellent shots and will not disappoint, amateur or pro alike! The swivel LCD is a huge plus with this camera. Though this is not the first of Nikon's line to have one it is a very big plus it does. The LCD is excellent for extremely awkward shooting positions (such as ground level or above head).This camera will not replace a pro camera, but it will deliver very impressive capabilities. I recommend to amateurs as this would be a wonderful entry level camera. The d5300 also makes for the perfect 'hobby' camera or for the very budget minded individual as a back up. Do not let this camera's entry level tag deter your decision for the d5300 stands as a fierce competitor."
    },
    {
        "review": "It's perfect! It looks almost completely new, I am very happy with this purchase, so far it has worked excellent. As for the accessories, it is necessary to keep in mind that it does not come with a USB cable or memories so you have to buy them separately."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great camera no visible defects takes great pictures overall exactly what I was looking for"
    },
    {
        "review": "I've only had the camera few days but so far so good.  Everything works as advertised and I can use my other Nikon lenses on the D5300 body.  The quality of the camera is very good, actually better than expected. 👍Refurbished can be just as good as new!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I recommend"
    },
    {
        "review": "I received the Nikon D5300 and the camera body itself appears to be in good condition and is working properly so far. Unfortunately, the included 18–55mm lens arrived with visible signs of damage and does not work at all. When attached, the camera displays “Lens not attached.”I tested the D5300 with several of my own compatible Nikon lenses, and they are recognized and work perfectly, so the issue appears to be with the included 18–55mm lens.Although the additional cost of getting the camera with the lens was relatively small, that is not really the point. If a lens is included as part of the purchase, I expect it to be functional. If it is defective, it should either be clearly described as such or simply not included.I’m disappointed that a visibly damaged, non-working lens was shipped without the issue being identified during inspection. I will continue testing the camera body during the return period and may update this review accordingly."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is a very good camera from the Nikkon brand and it is at an excellent price unlike other places where the price is more expensive.  Unfortunately there are two details that take away points for me: 1 the microphone that the camera has integrated is very bad, it is preferable to buy another one. 2. The accessories are more expensive, a 1.8 lens is around 3000 to 5000 Mexican pesos compared to other lenses or other brands. What helps a lot is its rotating screen that will help you take photos in very uncomfortable situations or in which it would be too much work to take them through the viewfinder."
    }
