        "review": "Old Reliable! Never failed me, brews quick, looks minimal and clean. For the size and value this is amazing. Never had issues. Always descale for peak life.Had to switch to this because me $500 Philips coffee maker broke from a $7 part cracking. It was a tedious task and I left it for a year. This drip machine has been a champ.My Philips is back in action now so it’s time to retire this Old reliable."
    },
    {
        "review": "Rating this as a 3 as \"okay\". I plan on keeping this but here are the things you need to know that I learned within 2 days of having it:I bought this coffee maker to replace a single Keurig maker that I have had for over a decade. I began drinking coffee made from fresh ground coffee from a local roaster recently, so using K-cups is out of the question. Also using the K-cup insert for more than one cup is a pain to clear and clean. The Keurig consistently poured 9 ounces at the large setting or 8 ounces for the middle setting, but I always used 1 heaping scoop of beans to one 9 ounces water (and 1/2 ounce of creamer). I then used a separate pour-over drip system to pour the hot water from the Keurig into my cup, which takes 3 or so minutes each cup.  My new job is in-office so I needed to brew one \"cup\" to wake up, and two \"cups\" to take in a coffee thermos.  I have to run the drip system 3 times in order to leave the house. This is why the Black and Decker was necessary. Two coffee systems to make coffee was a pain and even worse for the days I needed to drive into the office.First con: As a test I poured two cups (16 ounces) and one cup (8 ounces) into the Karaf to prepare for my use for Monday morning thinking the lack of an ounce would just make my cup a bit peppier.  I was annoyed that the measurement on the Keraf (and water reservoir) showed the waterline as being more than \"4\" -- the water line was just over the top of the painted number 4 which was above the painted line.  So in what universe does 24 ounces of water equate to more than 4 units??  It's not equidistant between the 4 and 6 so it doesn't correspond to 5 units. It corresponds to maybe 4.5.  So is each unit equal to 5 ounce pours? Well surprisingly that is what Google tells me. Coffee makers use 1 \"cup\" = 5 ounces, not 8 ounces to leave room for cream.  What?? 3 ounces of cream. Gross.  So I am measuring 4.8 units for 24 ounces of water according to the Keraf, but logically this should be closer to the midpoint of the 4 and 6 line (ie very close to where \"5\" would be), but it isn't.  If the Keraf or water reservoir stated \"4 = 20oz\" this at least would make sense. Minus one star for incorrect real-life measurements and propagating this dumb measurement into the 21st century.Second con: The benefit of the machine is that you can prepare your water amount with the ground coffee the night before, and set an alarm for it to brew (ie 5:55am) so that when you get up (ie at 6am) the coffee is already brewed. This feature is great.  The first thing you need to do is set the actual time by pushing the hour or minute buttons. The con is that you can push these buttons at any point. Ie you don't need to select any other button in order to set the actual time. If you are right handed, the \"hour\" button is very close to the re-entry of the keraf and any accidental bump to the hour button will move the clock forward. The \"Program\" button is strictly for the programming of the alarm, not the clock. The Program button should be used as a dual clock setting and \"brew alarm\" setting. What benefit is it if the machine brews an hour off than the intended time? Minus one star for not designing the button programming to protect the auto-brew setting system time.Lastly, this isn't really a specific con, as I have seen this with other coffee brewers...paper filters get easily moved in the brew process resulting in coffee grounds in the keraf. I tested it today with a reusable filter, and no grounds made it into my keraf.  I recommend using a reusable filter to ensure no grounds in the coffee (also it's better for the environment).TLDR: make sure the time on the clock is correct before you go to bed as you might accidentally bump the time an hour forward. Measure your water from a measuring cup unless your brews are based on a 5 ounce cup measurement."
    },
    {
        "review": "It is an excellent coffee maker for the price, the flavor is very intense (personal taste is a very good thing), it is very easy to use and the automatic ignition is very easy to use. The appearance is very modern, I would buy another one without thinking about it!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Beautiful. It is serving what it is intended to do. I always use it to make 4 to 6 coffees."
    },
    {
        "review": "sorry very dissappointing experience, when it was switched on we were able to see the displayes , but the hot plat was not worhing."
    },
    {
        "review": "Sellers were very good but unfortunately it’s a 110 v coffee machine"
    },
    {
        "review": "Does a fabulous job making coffee, Day in day out.Looks great. It’s also easy to clean. Great product."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "As a parent who needs coffee asap as possible in the mornings, I cannot emphasize how much I love this. It is so fast, easy to program, simple to use and clean, and brews a good cup of coffee - no burnt taste. The side handle for the scoop is really handy, and I love that the water reservoir can come off. The lid on the carafe is a very light plastic and I am not sure how long it will hold up, but overall I am very happy with this. After about 10 years with a beloved Cuisinart that went to coffee maker heaven recently, I was curious to try a new drip maker and I'm glad I gave this one a whirl!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I got this coffee maker a little over 2 years ago, mainly because of its aesthetics, specifically the design and layout were visually pleasing to me compared to other coffee makers. Also all my appliances are black and stainless.On the two cup setting, it takes about 6 minutes to brew. When it's done the coffee is really hot, and I have to wait about 5 minutes before drinking it, or drop in an ice cube and I can start drinking in about 30 seconds to a minute.I found a combination of things with this coffee maker that turns a good cup of coffee into a great one. The ninja branded measuring spoon that comes with it is more important than you might think. They've literally hit the nail on the head regarding the exact amount of coffee grounds that should be used. It's very simple, one flat scoop for every standard size cup of coffee you want, and remember the word flat. Also there's markers on the water tank so scoops and water match up.I personally found if I use a flat scoop of coffee grounds, not letting the coffee grounds be higher than that, it tastes perfect every time for me. So I would say that's a huge key to a great cup of coffee on this device.Next up, paper filters improved the taste significantly! The goldish mesh metal filter that comes in the box does not filter out all the coffee grounds. I even tried other brands of metal filters to see if they improved that, but it didn't. You will likely have some coffee grounds in the bottom of your coffee cup if you stick with the included metal reusable filter.At first I didn't think it was very many coffee grounds, but then one day I immediately poured the coffee into a glass after brewing it. Then I held the glass above my head and shined a flashlight at the bottom, and there were a lot more grounds that I saw that way, than when I was getting close to finishing a cup of coffee. Meaning, I was likely swallowing a lot of grounds and would have never known that.So I stopped using the Ninja metal filter and started buying and trying #4 cone paper filters and that did 4 things. 1. No more coffee grounds in my coffee cup. 2. No more upset stomach after drinking coffee. 3. The oily stuff that kind of floated to the top of the coffee was gone. 4. The taste of the coffee significantly improved.I would strongly suggest trying a box of paper filters to see if it improves the taste for you.Do not attempt to use a paper filter and the mesh filter at the same time. I tried that once and it significantly reduces the flow of the coffee out of the filter area. This causes the water to build up in that filter area and overflow from there on to your countertop.In closing, this is a good coffee maker, but if you use flat scoops, a good brand of paper filters, and purified water, I think those might be the keys to turning a good cup of coffee into a great one."
    },
    {
        "review": "I've owned this coffee maker over a month now and love it. Intuitive-to-operate controls and a very attractive appearance are among its virtues. Of course, hot water dripping over coffee grinds isn't rocket science and is something most coffee makers do comparably well, and the Ninja likewise makes excellent-tasting coffee.Other reviews were mildly critical of the \"sneak a cup\" functionality, but I've found it on par with that of my last two coffee makers (Krups and Cuisinart). Yes, a couple of drops of coffee will still splash on the hot plate while you pull the carafe out, but I've never used or seen a pot yet that didn't do that, and the spring mechanism on this carafe is at least as robust as any I've seen on other coffee makers.Where this pot really shines is in the clear, detachable water reservoir. That obviously offers you the (completely?) unique option of detaching the tank to fill it over the sink before brewing, which I've done a few times and anticipated doing regularly. Frankly, however, I find it unnecessary because the reservoir itself provides an unusually large opening/target for pouring directly from my filtered water pitcher.But there are other benefits to the reservoir design, including the fact that it allows you to easily monitor the conditions inside . . . i.e., when the moist environment inevitably leads to mildew, you will know it (and, because the reservoir is transparent to any ambient sunlight, mold/mildew should theoretically take longer to manifest in many kitchens.) More importantly, you'll be able to easily clean the reservoir in a sink of hot soapy water (with a little bleach) without repeated electric cleaning and rinse cycles for the whole brewer, followed by awkward attempts to wipe the nooks and crannies of the integral, typically black reservoir with a white paper towel to see if any undesirable residues remain.But perhaps the greatest advantage of this design is in what it will save you if you should *ahem* forget to put your carafe in place before you hit \"brew.\" I've only done this twice in my life but, ironically, both times occurred within the last six weeks: once with my last Krups pot and once with this new Ninja. (Put aside for the moment what such forgetfulness might be saying about me.:-) At the very least, I gained valuable, first-hand experience in how the Ninja's design saved my a$$ while the typical integral design on the Krups resulted in a ruined coffee maker, which prompted my purchase of the Ninja in the first place.)When you fail to put the carafe under the filter spout on a coffee maker that offers the increasingly ubiquitous \"sneak a cup\" feature, the spring mechanism on the filter holder is never engaged and the water that's dripping down over your grinds to make coffee is never given proper egress below. So your mistake may go unnoticed for quite some time since you won't hear the kind of gushing and sizzling sound that an older model without the sneak a cup feature would produce when dripping coffee is merrily dispensing and burning on the naked hotplate below. Instead, the water stays in the filter holder, eventually having nowhere to go but over the sides, which, on the vast majority of drip coffee makers, means that the brewed coffee--complete with floating grinds--will spill over and back into the reservoir itself. That is exactly what happened to my Krups, and, despite hours of repeated cleaning cycles and an aborted effort to dismantle the innards, I realized the pot was shot (no pun intended) and would never process water properly through its (clogged) pump pathway again.Enter the Ninja. After making delicious coffee for a week or so, I, once again, got distracted by something (probably my Yorkie yapping to get out for a morning pee) and neglected to actually seat the carafe on the coffee maker before hitting brew. When I got back in, I noticed the problem just before the brewing coffee was about to overflow the filter holder. The difference was, even had I been a bit later, I would have only had to deal with a messy countertop and not a ruined $80+ coffee maker. In other words, if the Ninja coffee filter overflows, the brewed coffee will NOT go back into the reservoir because the latter is physically higher and isolated from the former in a way that would not permit that. So, should you repeat my negligence, you will hear coffee suddenly dripping on your hotplate and counter top, but you won't have to buy a new coffee maker.The only \"negative\" to the pot is that the carafe lid does not flip up via the common thumb depression above the handle. You have to slide/turn it to remove, which is more easily done with two hands. But this is a very, very minor ding in my view and not worth deducting a star for an otherwise very well-designed, highly practical, and elegant-looking appliance."
    },
    {
        "review": "My last coffee maker was a \"premium\" brand — three of them, actually, over six years, none making it past three years. When the latest one died, I had exactly two days of patience left before I needed a replacement, so I grabbed this one based on our family's good experience with Shark/Ninja products being reliable long-term.First impressions out of the box were mixed. The removable water tank and its lid feel like lightweight, cheap plastic — not the sturdier, higher-impact plastic I was used to. The lid especially isn't hinged or attached in any way, so it's a little awkward to get on and off, especially before I've had coffee. The carafe glass is thinner than my old machine's, and it doesn't have cup-line markings, which turned out to bug me more than expected since I like to glance and see how much is left.None of that matters once the coffee's actually made. It's hot, it stays hot, and — I genuinely didn't expect this — it tastes better than what my more expensive machine produced. The \"Rich\" brew setting gives a noticeably stronger cup without needing extra grounds, which matters if you go through coffee as fast as we do.I can't speak to long-term durability yet since I've only had it two weeks, and that's exactly where my last coffee maker let me down repeatedly. But replacement parts look easy to find if that becomes an issue down the road. For now: better tasting, hotter coffee, at a lower price than what I paid before. That's the part that actually matters to me, and it's why this is a 5-star review despite the cheaper feel of the hardware.This is standalone and Amazon-appropriate — no links, no brand mentions, no outside references, just the product experience. Want me to also trim it down further for Amazon's shorter-review norm, or is this length good?"
    },
    {
        "review": "UPDATE: For our second brew, I ran it on \"Classic\" instead of \"Rich\".  I really was a significantly superior brew.  It tasted good with the old machine, but really fine with this one.  Now after over a week of use- it's a consistent result every day, and a cup of coffee is, indeed, superior with this machine and all else being equal.I found another issue, though.  There's a gap between the back of the carafe lid and the handle. Steam escapes there, and it gets very hot.  I have fairly large hands and I tend to put my thumb there.  I have gotten burned a couple of times.  It's painful but not long lasting.  It's not the steam alone, but the fact that the little bit of steam heats the material for the entire brew time plus serving time.  Cleaning is getting burdensome. The carafe needs a re-design.I got this to replace (?) a very basic Mr. Coffee that is about 5 years old, that is one of my favorite examples of design.  It's as simple as can be: an on/off switch and a carafe that is capacious and very easy and fast to clean.  It has very few right angle areas that would make it hard to clean. (They have changed the design since we bought it, and not for the better.) It still works.This Ninja is nice, and the filter basket will barely sit on the scale while I weigh the grounds into it, but it's going to tip over, and often.  Once the coffee is made, you can't pour all of it from the carafe, because the plastic rim extends around the edge and into the lip, and it has a square edge.  The lid of the carafe, has a strange pipe that extends to near the bottom of the pot.  Why?  You'd have to use a round brush to clean it well.  I've made one pot of coffee so far.  I used the rich setting.  My wife liked it, but I thought it was much more bitter than yesterday's coffee. It was visibly darker (remember, I weigh the grounds, so that's constant). I found the liquid measure to be the same as the coffee make we've been using. Tomorrow I will try the \"classic\" setting.  I don't really need another clock to set, or a timer, or really any of the features this sports beyond \"on/off\".I will also mention that the reservoir works well.  But it's clear plastic.  So with our regular water, it will get mineral spots (and it's virtually impossible to clean with all the strange grooves and right angles).  So we have to use distilled water.  I like a spotless kitchen.  (The reservoir in the old one doesn't need hand cleaning because it's mostly black plastic with a frosted window to gauge water level.  It much much easier to see the water level with this Ninja, though.)"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great product so far, I bought this coffee pot totally on reviews that were on Amazon. After receiving it I quickly read the instructions and made some coffee, very good, and I do believe Ninja thought of everything. My carafe lid stays on, reservoir is easy to fill, electronics are easy to set, and the coffee coming out of the machine goes to the bottom of the pot instead of on top of the brewed coffee. It also has a cleaning mode and a formula to clean it with. I brew with basket out just using paper filters, it comes with a measure which I measured into 1/3 cup for six cups coffee actually I thought it was a little strong so I’d say 7 cups to 1/3 cup measure of coffee. After using this coffee pot for about two weeks I found the power button difficult to turn off and on without using my fingernail.Charlie"
    },
    {
        "review": "I've been using this coffee maker every day for over a month, and I have zero complaints. The coffee comes out consistently great, and since I usually brew 4 cups, the Small Batch setting has been perfect.I use disposable paper filters instead of the included reusable one because it's more convenient for me to just throw them away after brewing. Cleanup is quick and easy.I've also tested the Keep Warm and Delay Brew features, and both work exactly as expected. The coffee stays at a good temperature without tasting burnt, and the scheduled brewing has been reliable every time.Overall, it's been a fantastic coffee maker with no issues, and I'd definitely recommend it to anyone looking for a dependable machine that consistently makes great-tasting coffee."
    },
    {
        "review": "I LOVE the Ninja drip coffee maker!1. The coffee tastes fresh.2. The heat plate keeps the coffee in the pot hot.3. The pot design does not spill or drip when you pour the coffee into the cup.4. The maker doesn't steam while brewing. I can keep it pushed under my cabinet without worrying about it ruining the bottom of my cabinets.5. The removable water reservoir means I dont have to store the maker by my sink.6. It's easy to clean."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "This being the first espresso machine I have ever had/used, it seems pretty simple and straightforward, although I did appreciate the instruction book, because I did have to reference it a couple of times when I couldn't figure out how to (1) change out the \"cup\" size, and (2) get the wand thing IN to the correct position. This machine works well, heats up really quickly, and the water/coffee comes out boiling hot of the main part, with the steamer steam/water not *as* hot (which is honestly a perk for me because I am clumsy). I do like that it comes with a water tank as well, and that it's pretty small - smaller than our current coffee pot (which is a double maker, pot + single cup). I like that you can use any ol' fine ground coffee, something I didn't know. I now have to play around with it and try recipes and combinations. I got this because I had always been excited over the idea of being able to make my own \"coffee shop\" coffee at home - and now have to figure out if this is \"for me\", since it does feel a bit messier and more time consuming that just making a super strong pot of coffee that I can make last all week. However, I do see benefits of using it even for just hot water for tea - it's SO MUCH FASTER than heating up water through our single-serve coffee maker or with a traditional teapot (I have never used an electric kettle to compare that speed though). This machine makes super hot water in roughly a minute from the first turn on, but in less than 30 seconds if you're making more than one cup back to back. But the one thing I'm not too keen on is that I can't put a taller mug in it - it has to be shorter than a traditional coffee cup, or it just doesn't fit."
    },
    {
        "review": "I probably shouldn’t be anywhere near espresso, much less own an espresso machine, but as basic coffee has started to have little impact on my midday sleepiness it is time to try something new. At the advice of a friend, I gave this machine a try and it has been fun learning how the device functions and how I like my espresso.While shopping for and experimenting with beans has been its own adventure, the machine is actually pretty straightforward in terms of parts and functioning. I read the instructions twice and then had my former friend (a former barista) come over to walk me through the steps. I was a bit disappointed to realize that some of the cute accessories shown in the photos are not included but that’s a fun and easy fix. We ran the water cycle and then set out to experiment with brewing (?) and frothing.The machine is reasonably quiet and quite effective. The results are reliable and consistent. I find the clean-up process manageable and I know that investing in a man espresso machine will be a money-saver over time. Plus I can control the ingredients and ratios and customize them to my precise liking. I hate to keep saying “in this economy…” but the truth is that going out for a coffee treat is just no longer within budget, so the investment in a machine and ingredients will definitely pay off over time.My friend/former barista said that this seems like a solid machine for a home or office and is simple enough for a beginner while still possessing enough features that most anyone would be satisfied with it. I consider her endorsement to be more valuable than my own."
    },
    {
        "review": "No More Coffee Shop RunsI’ve been wanting an espresso maker for a while, so I finally decided to try this one and I absolutely love it. It’s a simple, basic espresso maker, which is exactly what I wanted. Setup was simple, and it was much easier to use than I expected for my first espresso machine. It comes with a large instruction booklet that explains everything and walks you right through it.It’s about the size of a regular coffee maker, so it fits nicely on the counter without taking up too much space. It has a nice sturdy feel to it, and the black and stainless steel looks really nice. I love that it matches my other kitchen appliances. The clear removable water tank lets you easily see how much water is left, and the power cord has a good amount of length for placement on the counter.The controls are pretty straightforward. It heats up quickly, doesn’t take long to make a shot, and isn’t very loud while running. The espresso comes out nice and hot, and the milk frother works great.It comes with the portafilter, two filter baskets, and a coffee scoop with a tamper on the end. One basket is for a single shot and the other is for a double. A little tip for first time users, if you have trouble removing the filter basket, it twists out of the portafilter rather than pulling straight out.It does not come with espresso cups or shot glasses, so you’ll need your own. They also recommend using a stainless steel frothing pitcher that isn’t too deep with the steam wand. It’s easy to clean, and the removable drip tray makes cleanup quick.I’m still learning, but that’s part of the fun. I’m excited to try different drinks and learn how to make lattes and cappuccinos at home. It’s been really easy to learn how to use, and I think it’s a great option for anyone wanting an espresso maker, especially beginners. I also think it’s a great value and I’m very happy with it."
    },
    {
        "review": "I don't drink expresso often but wanted to try a home machine. This 20 bar espresso machine is a great addition for anyone who enjoys making coffeehouse style drinks at home without having to leave or pay over $10 a cup. It has a nice combination of features without being overly complicated, and the machine has a solid feel to it. Despite being less expensive, it functions well without leaking at any of the key areas, and it seems to be able to last for a long time. It’s great for making espresso, lattes, cappuccinos, and other favorite coffee drinks.The pressure provides a nice espresso extraction with good flavor and a satisfying crema. It also gives you the ability to steam milk for making creamy cappuccinos and lattes. The controls are straightforward, and the overall design is compact enough to fit nicely on a kitchen counter without taking up too much space.This is a convenient and enjoyable espresso machine for home use. It’s a good option for someone who wants to make espresso-based drinks without having to visit a coffee shop every time. A nice combination of functionality, convenience, and value for the price."
    },
    {
        "review": "I found this expresso machine to be a little disappointing honestly. I'm getting zero foam production from it and the coffee just isn't getting the same rich flavor I get from my machine at work. My number one complaint is the size - its a squat little machine that is very restrictive in the size of cup you can use. Yes, espresso cups are small, but what if you're making a latte or americano?"
    },
    {
        "review": "I got this because I wanted to be able to make espresso at home instead of always going somewhere to get one. I've used a couple different coffee makers over the years, but I wanted something that could actually make espresso rather than just regular coffee.Getting it set up wasn't difficult. I ran a few cycles through it first to clean it out and then started making drinks. Once I got the amount of coffee and water figured out, it was pretty straightforward to use.The machine heats up quickly enough that I'm not standing there waiting forever, and the espresso comes out with a good amount of flavor and crema. I've mostly been making straight espresso and adding milk for different drinks depending on what I'm in the mood for.It's also been easy to clean afterward. I rinse things out and wipe everything down after using it, and so far I haven't had any problems with buildup or anything leaking.I'm sure somebody who is really serious about espresso could find things to complain about, but for making drinks at home without turning the kitchen into a coffee shop, it's been great. I do wish the frother was a little more straightforward, but it's not bad and I think it just takes some practice.I've been using it regularly and haven't had any issues. It's definitely gotten plenty of use since I got it."
    },
    {
        "review": "This little espresso machine is awesome!  It has a nice, small footprint to sit on my countertop.  The water reservoir is on the back of the machine and is easy to access and refill.  The milk frother works really well, whipping up steamed milk in no time!  The machine itself heats up quickly and the water reservoir holds 50 ounces of water -- no need to constantly refill.  The best part of this entire machine is the fact that you can brew up to two cups of espresso at the same time.  It comes with two separate filters -- one for one cup and the other for two.  The measuring utensil has a built-in tamper, a convenient add.  The espresso is perfect!  A perfect temperature and brewed properly for a perfect taste!  The LCD screen is nice and bright and is activated by the push of a finger, super easy!  The drip tray is easy to remove and easy to clean.  Overall, this is a great little espresso machine at a great value given the quality and the price!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I got this to start making espresso at home instead of running out all the time, and it has been great for learning how to pull shots and steam milk. Setting it up out of the box was very simple and the included instruction booklet explains everything clearly. It fits nicely on the counter without taking up too much space and matches other kitchen appliances. The clear removable water tank makes it easy to see the water level. The controls are straightforward and it heats up quickly. The espresso comes out hot with good flavor and crema, and the milk frother works well for lattes and cappuccinos. One thing for first time users is that the filter basket twists out of the portafilter rather than pulling straight out. It does not come with cups or a frothing pitcher so you will need your own. The frother takes some practice to get used to, but overall it is a solid machine that makes good drinks and is very easy to use."
    }
]

