[
    {
        "review": "So I own the 40oz and 24oz. The 40 is great for long term backpacking or all day drinking but will not fit into standard car small vehicle cup holders.maybe truck and suv. The 24oz will fit everywhere. Both keep ice cold for 24hrs. Just make sure you don’t lose the straw. If you use ice balls or cubes they last a lot longer. Great product and doesn’t leak. I got the black ones don’t care about the other colors. Do scratch over time but if you don’t drop them great value for the money you spend"
    },
    {
        "review": "This bottle does exactly what it's supposed to do. Yes, it's small, but that's the point. There are definitely pros and cons.The obvious downside is that I have to refill it a few times throughout the day. On the other hand, it's incredibly convenient. It's not too heavy when full, fits easily in my bag and my spin bike's water bottle holder, and the locking latch doubles as a carrying handle. I've even clip it to my fanny pack with a carabiner for hands-free trips to the gym.It keeps drinks cold exceptionally well. I usually fill it with ice, and it lasts all day and overnight.One unexpected benefit is that it fits perfectly in the portable \"ADHD caddy\" I carry from room to room, which has actually helped me stay hydrated because my water is always with me. (I wrote a separate review about that caddy if you're curious.)If you want a bottle that travels well, doesn't leak, and keeps your drinks ice cold, this is a great choice.I also get a ton of compliments from both men and women on the color (Pink/Purple, Darling Diva). It's a less common color. So, it's a great conversation starter at the gym especially!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I purchased this bottle just to try out in the summer of 2023. It is now the summer of 2026 and I still use it multiple times a week (only because I now have multiple Owalas and I have just ordered my third Owala FreeSip). I have used so many cups—Stanleys, Yetis, Hydrojugs, Hydroflask, etc—and the Owala is still my favorite. Here’s my favorite things about it:LEAKPROOF! This is the biggest one. I can throw it my bag and trust that everything will stay dry.THE SPOUT! Hands down the best spout I’ve ever used. I love that I can sip through the straw but then close the lid and keep my straw clean and not have any spills if it tips over. And then it also gives me the option to absolutely inhale water if I’m about to shrivel up from dehydration. I think this alone is why I now own three in different sizes.STAYS COLD! The 32 ounce holds ice for quite a while. It varies depending on how often I refill it but if I’m not constantly refilling it, it will hold ice for about 24 hours.THE SIZES! I love the size options. My original one was a 32 ounce and is a great middle ground for me. It holds enough water that I’m not refilling it every hour but it’s not huge. It’s great for work and this bottle will be going to live at my job. I just ordered the 40 ounce for home because I want a little more volume when I’m outside gardening but I can’t say much about the 40 ounce size yet. I also have a 16 ounce (kids size) that I use when I leave the house. It fits in a lot of my bags and it’s leakproof so I always have cold water with me. It’s perfect especially since I’m on medication that causes dehydration if I’m not careful.DISHWASHER SAFE! This one is self explanatory.It’s amazing and I love it. There are a few cons though. The first one is that the 32 ounce doesn’t fit in my cup holder. Not a huge deal since it’s leakproof—I just keep it on the passenger seat. But Owala does have cup holder friendly options now if that’s something you want.The bottle also does dent and scratch fairly easily. I dropped it getting out my car one day and it’s a little beat up now. Kind ugly, but it’s still my fave. It’s kinda disappointing because I have Yetis and Stanleys that have been through far worse and still look new. Just be mindful of that and baby it a little. Still 100% worth it though.Long story short, it’s a great bottle that will keep your water cold and everything else dry. As someone who has used this bottle regularly for years, I highly recommend!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely adore these water bottles compared to any other I’ve come across. Let’s start with the pros: you have the option to drink using the straw or tilt the bottle back like a cup. The mouthpiece is covered and leak-proof when closed, and it has a handle that locks the cover securely, then when open, it can be used to carry it with your fingers. I’ve dropped it multiple times, and surprisingly, it hasn’t dented. It also keeps my drink cold throughout the day.Now, let’s address the cons. If you open the bottle with the straw and pour in carbonated drinks, some of the liquid will splash out. (Lo and behold, there is a warning on the bottom to not use with hot or carbonated drinks…so there’s that.) Also, the carrying handle is made of plastic and appears to be a bit thin. While I haven’t tested it yet, it seems like it could break if hit the right way. It is sturdy so a tad heavy but lighter than any other I’ve encountered in its class. Considering these minor drawbacks, I’m so happy, I wouldn’t even deduct a star from this product"
    },
    {
        "review": "I absolutely love this Owala FreeSip water bottle! The 40 oz size is perfect for keeping me hydrated throughout the day without having to constantly refill it. The FreeSip design is my favorite part because I can either sip through the built-in straw or tilt the bottle back when I want a bigger drink.It keeps my water cold for a long time, and the stainless steel construction feels sturdy and high quality. The lid closes securely, and I love the sleek Very, Very Dark color—it looks simple, stylish, and goes with everything.It’s perfect for the gym, work, errands, or just keeping next to me throughout the day. Great size, keeps drinks cold, comfortable to use, and absolutely worth it."
    },
    {
        "review": "This is my first Owala and I am more than happy with it. Everything about this water bottle is perfect. It keeps the drink cold for a very long time, I like the mouthpiece where you can either sip or drink. I thought that the shape of the sip part would bother me as it’s not shaped like a straw. I actually like the shape of this better. And to drink instead of sip is nice also as neither part interferes with the other. The cap seals tightly and I turned it upside down and did not have any leakage at all. The handle is also a plus. I can carry while hiking or hook it to my backpack. Very satisfied with this water bottle. I will buy another."
    },
    {
        "review": "I got this Owala for my son and really like it so far! My favorite feature is that you can drink from either the built-in straw or use the chug opening. It gives him both options without having to switch out lids or pieces, which is really convenient.The bottle itself feels sturdy and good quality, and the lid closes securely and has a handle that makes it easy to carry. The colors are also really fun and vibrant.My only concern is the coating on the outside. It feels like it may be somewhat easy to scratch, especially since this is going to be used by a kid and tossed into bags, taken to school, etc. We’ll see how it holds up over time!Overall, though, I’m really happy with it.I’ll update my review once I find out how sturdy it is."
    },
    {
        "review": "The whole thing about being able to choose whether you use the straw or tilt it to chug is awesome and all, but mainly I was scouring the internet for a bottle that had some kind of straw mechanism that wasn't (1) on a spring-loaded flippy thing that would eventually wear out and stop working, (2) wasn't open to the air where it could collect other peoples' geeeeerms when I took it around everywhere with me, and (3) didn't have some kind of screw-top or other cap that I wouldn't be able to open one-handed. DO YOU KNOW HOW HARD THAT IS TO FIND? I do.Literally, Owala was the only brand/bottle I found that checked all three of those boxes. THE ONLY ONE. So even though it was a little more than I would normally pay for a water bottle and I hated that I couldn't customize my colors, I decided to give it a try. And lo, I found the light and the heavens blessed me with a (near) perfect water bottle.I've used my bottle pretty much all day every day since purchasing it several months ago and it works as well now as it did when I first bought it. I do worry about the spring-loaded lid release eventually wearing out, but it hasn't yet with constant use (and more than occasional extra use for funsies). The only sign of wear over time is that there are a few small (aesthetic/surface) scratches on the outside from a couple times when I dropped my bottle on concrete and asphalt. I wish they weren't there but it doesn't affect anything except my delicate eyeballs.My only qualm with the bottle's near perfection, which seems entirely unavoidable from a design perspective, is that ice cubes inside of the bottle make a lot of noise when sloshing around and bumping up against the metal interior. When you work a job that sometimes requires near-complete silence, that noise can be an issue. For most, it probably wouldn't matter in the slightest (when I'm at home, I don't even think about it). I don't blame Owala for this. But if they solved it, then it would be the holy grail of sheer perfection designed to hold ambrosia for the gods. Until then, I feel honored to partake.The handle is convenient for carrying. The shape of the bottle (flat on the sides) is a clever touch that makes it easier to grip for twisting off the top and such. It fits in average cup holders. It's leakproof when the lid is secured well and the cap is clicked into place.The button to open the cap is strong (works well) and is easy to press. If the handle is down (it gently clicks into place if you fold it down), it covers the button and prevents any accidental presses. That said, while it could theoretically be pressed accidentally if you leave the handle up/open all the time (like I do), it has never happened to me.The inside is metal, which keeps the insides cold for ages (with ice). The straw is plastic, which stops the water from tasting like gross metal water bottle. The outside is... not-metal, which means the sun can be directly on the bottle and warm the outside while the inside is still ice cold and, more importantly, that there's no condensation on the outside getting everything around it wet (bane of my college existence :| ). Vinyl stickers adhere well to whatever the outside material is.It disassembles into straw, lid, and bottle for cleaning, and the the shape of the lid makes it a lot easier (and way more sanitary) to clean than any other straw-based bottle I've had/used. I run the lid and straw through the dishwasher occasionally. I think the bottle recommends hand washing but I forget; in addition to hand washing, I occasionally do a polydent cleaning to eliminate any odors and ensure all bacteria are dead af. It works like a charm.This is a 100% honest review and I generally try not to fangirl or gush excessively: this bottle is /that/ good. I legit recommend it for anyone looking for a water bottle (unless they prefer the big mouth/screw-top kind) and am very seriously considering buying one for everyone in my family."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good, doesn't leak, keeps the temperature."
    },
    {
        "review": "Did not include the green ”cover” as shown.Other than that, I adore this bottle!"
    },
    {
        "review": "Great for keeping water cool during hot days. Easy to drink!"
    }
]

