[
    {
        "review": "I have been a huge fan of CamelBak for many years. I refuse to travel without a reservoir or an insulated bottle, and this brand has never let me down.Currently, I have several different water bottles from this company, and I could not be more satisfied.  Among them are a 25 oz and a 64 oz that I use regularly. Sadly, I recently tested positive for Cyclosporiasis, and my doctor urged me to drink as much as three gallons of water a day. One of the side effects of the disease, as well as the antibiotics, is an intense sense of fatigue. I struggled to even lift my 64 oz insulated bottle and was just barely able to handle the much smaller 25 oz, also insulated.  On a whim, I looked to Camelbak for something lighter, and this water bottle turned out to be just perfect! Full of water, it's actually lighter than either of the other 2 when they are empty. This saved me a tremendous amount of pain and stress for the nearly two weeks it took for the antibiotics to provide some substantial relief. It is now my go-to bottle for home.  It was a terrific value and is so easy to clean, as it's safe in the dishwasher.  I purchased the clear color, and I am glad I did, as with a simple glance I can see just where I am in my quest to finish four of these a day.  The drink hole size is also just perfect for me, and I truly appreciate the magnetic lid, as I once dropped it without screwing it closed and not a drop spilled.  I also appreciate that the lids are interchangeable with my other bottles, making them so much easier to use."
    },
    {
        "review": "With over 5000 reviews of this product, which in no small part was one of the major reasons I chose it - people don’t buy stuff that’s not any good, so anyway the chances anyone will read my review are slim.  So here goes if you are one of the lucky ones.Remember what I just said, that people don’t buy junk? It’s also true that people buy what is easy and familiar. So why is it that it is so excruciatingly difficult to buy a simple water bottle to replace the disposable ones you buy in stores?  After much too long spending money on increasingly thinner disposable store bought water, which in most cases is just tap water that’s been filtered, I invested in an expensive under sink filtration system so I can refill my own plastic bottles. My own filtered water tastes awesome and I never run out of it. The only trouble is those cheap thin bottles are not good for refilling so I wanted to get one that’s clear, simple and works exactly like I’m used to, unscrew the cap and chug. Guess what, almost no one makes that sort of thing. Now I’m as environmentally conscious as the next person but most brands want you to buy their bottle because it’s somehow going to save the planet. And instead of making it the same way people already drink water they come up with a thousand gimmicks to try to improve on simplicity.To cut to the chase, I think I finally found one here that’s basically like a 20 ounce store bought water bottle with a cap that unscrews and everything and the only think that’s gimmicky about it is the dubious addition of a magnet to hold the cap so you don’t lose it. I’ve never lost a cap so I can’t see why that’s such a big deal.WHAT I DON’T LIKEIt’s not really a singular cause not to buy this bottle just a dose of reality. I spent a lot of money to make my home-filtered water as tasteless as possible but unfortunately for all the hype about Tritan (which is as pure as dimethyl terephthalate (CAS 120-61-6), 1,4-cyclohexanedimethanol (CAS 105-08-8) and 2,2,4,4-tetramethyl-84 1,3-cyclobutanediol can be) it still has an odor when new. And it’s a pretty stron odor. I would never use such a product without washing it first and with this it took several washings with a baking soda solution to reduce the odor to a tolerable level.WHAT I LIKEi do like that it has the overall familiar feel of a typical water bottle, it just the right size to wrap my hand around, and the 20 ounce size is only slightly larger than the usual 16 ounces so it was a logical size to buy. Most of all I like that they didn’t try to play games with the bottom, it’s flat, no weirdly formed bumps so it takes quite a shove to accidentally knock it over.YES OR NOBuying a simple water bottle shouldn’t be so difficult. I spent hours searching these pages until I found this one and even then I wasn’t 100% convinced.  The fifteen buck price helped, it was reasonable and for those who care it comes in four different sizes and twelve different colors.  I went simple, just 20 ounces and crystal clear.  After washing most of the odor out I replaced my used plastic store bought bottle with this on my bathroom counter, where I always need a water bottle for taking meds and vitamins. After the first day I didn’t even notice I was using it. I learned I didn’t have to screw the top down tightly between uses, it has a slight snap to it to keep it closed (but not spill-proof) so it is actually easier to use than a regular bottle. And the magnet isn’t something I have to think about, when I open to top it just flops back by itself.  I like the plain simplicity, knowing it can go into the freezer if I want to have ice cold water and that I can use hot water to clean it. It’s pretty much the perfect personal water bottle and for that reason it earns a five star rating."
    },
    {
        "review": "I was looking to replace a five-year old 20 oz water bottle with a flip cap that had served me well, but the cap seal had worn and would open unexpectedly when on its side, creating a mess.  My needs required a durable 20-25oz bottle with a screw cap, but no straw, as I prefer a more generous flow when working out.  I also use my water bottle to mix and consume pre workout drink and don’t want the hassle of keeping a straw clean.In all my research, this Camelback bottle with the magnetic cap seemed the most promising so I ordered a charcoal 20 and 25 oz size to evaluate.  While the 25 oz bottle was a bit larger than the 20 oz bottle I had before, I preferred it over the 20 oz Camelback, which seemed a little top heavy with the durable cap.So I returned the 20 oz bottle and have been using the 25 oz. Camelback for over two weeks now, and I love it.  It’s the perfect water bottle for my needs, and the magnetic cap is great to insure that it isn’t falling back into your face when drinking the contents.  I also like how the threads of the cap are internal and not on the exposed surface of the drinking port.  This was an ingenious idea.  If you’re working out and don’t want to screw the lid completed down, the magnetic cap will stay in place once returned to the port, not providing a waterproof seal, but keeping out any airborne debri and essentially making it spill proof if knocked over.One thing I do recommend before using this water bottle is adding a tablespoon of baking soda to the bottle filled with warm water and letting it sit for 30 minutes or so.  This will neutralize any plastic taste to the water.A concern I had before purchasing this 25 oz water bottle was if it would fit my vehicle’s cup holder as the smaller 20 oz bottle did, and yes, it fits fine.In conclusion, I find the 25oz Camelback Chute Mag water bottle to be the perfect canteen for the gym or general purposes.  I love it so much I ordered a second one in case they discontinued production of this model if mine ever dies or I leave it somewhere.  I still prefer to refill regular 20 oz Aquafina water bottles when hiking for a lighter, more compact option for my day pack, but this is the only instance that I would not use the 25 oz. Chute Mag."
    },
    {
        "review": "I rarely review anything but this water bottle is perfect! It's the perfect size for me. It holds 50 ounces of water which is equivalent to 3 bottles of water. It's also very well made. It's made with thick plastic heavy duty parts. It has the perfect sized handle that's easy to hold. Both parts of the top (the large base where you fill it with water and the attached small nozzle where you drink from) have heavy duty threads that form a perfect seal so nothing spills out if you drop it. There may be some rubber parts on it. It is also dishwasher safe. It also looks nice (I got blue) and just looks well made..like a German car. No idea where it's made. Camelbak may be a German or French (or French Canadian) company because the label is written in French, German and English. Anyway it's the best water bottle I've ever used. It's a top shelf product at a bottom shelf price!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I like the bottle, its heavy duty, the handle feels very sturdy. It's a pretty color, and I like the 'water flow' , not just sipping through a straw. & as stated easier to clean.  Teeny problem I had was I couldn't figure out how the 'snap/click' of the the magnetic cap was to work, actually had to look up a video, there is a little indent in the handle that you put the cap up next to and THAT holds the cap- would be nice if they put the word cap on it or made that part actually look magnetic.  The other thing , it doesn't fit in my cup holders in my car, too big, but this is great for everything else."
    },
    {
        "review": "Love this bottle. 1) Holds 32 oz but still fits in my carrying sleeve that used to hold a 24 oz. plastic jug. 2) I can also use the carry clip built into the bottle attached to a large carabiner, attached to a belt loop, my work harness, etc. 3) Love the ability to gulp down water from the opening. 4) Like the magnetic snap-on top, which you can also screw down. It stays out of the way when you're drinking. Since I carry it on a belt etc. and bend over while working, the bottle isn't always upright so I find the magnet cap can leak a little unless I screw it down further, but no big deal, I have to stop work to drink in any case, and the magnetic cap closure seals out debris getting into the bottle. 4) Like the quality rigid plastic bottle--glass or stainless are not practical with the type of outdoor work I do. I've tried another couple bottles recently that just didn't work for me, but I finally found The One. I'm guessing this will hold up to rough use."
    },
    {
        "review": "No leaking, even when dropped. Good size for active 5 year old. Light weight and made of a safer plastic."
    },
    {
        "review": "I like this water bottle.  I had another one that was grey, and for reasons I can't remember, I decided I wanted a clear one instead.  Since the cap screws on, it takes two hands to get a drink, but then you can take gulps.  The magnetic cap and living hinge work well, and so far the hinge shows no signs of fatigue or failing.  It has been dropped a few times without drama.  It does not fit well in the cup-holders in my wife's car, but does fine in my car.  Again, since it takes to hands, it is a good passenger's bottle, but is not a driver's bottle."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very good for drinking, easy fill and strong product"
    },
    {
        "review": "It can be washed in the dishwasher, it doesn't taste bad or leave a smell, it is very comfortable to use, it is resistant, the magnet to keep the lid open is great... Is it more expensive than other brands? Yes, but he deserves it. I like it so much that I bought another one in a different color. The only drawback I have is that if you don't screw the lid completely on (even though it looks like it's closed) it won't be completely sealed and liquid can escape from there, so it's not ideal if you're looking for a very quick way to open and close the container. Personally, I don't care about this, but for athletes, moms, etc., it may be something to take into account and I would recommend looking at other models from the same brand (of which there are quite a few)."
    },
    {
        "review": "Thick, robust plastic and the spout is wide, you drink as if it were the mouth of a soda bottle."
    },
    {
        "review": "Nice product as you would expect from CamelBack"
    }
]

[
    {
