[
    {
        "review": "The guitar arrived in perfect condition (7-16-2024) packed in its gig bag and cardboard box.  I chose the Cedar top.  My first impression was that the finish is beautiful.  I could not find a blemish or defect anywhere.  The neck is arrow straight and the set up is ok; action is just a little high (about 5 mm) at the 12th fret.  The fretboard top and edges are very smooth.  I only found one sharp fret wire end-- on the 14th fret.  The strings are ok, not the greatest.  The tone is good.The gig bag  has a some padding to protect the guitar if you carry it around in your car.  I think it would cost close to $30 to buy it separately.  The tuner is very good; I keep it clipped on the headstock since nylon strings need frequent tuning in the first couple weeks of use.  The capo is \"so-so\", really not that great.  If you use a capo a lot you might need a better one.  I would say the same for the foot rest; its a little unstable but useable..  All in all, the accessory package is a really good deal!My only other complaint is that they used a black stain on the Bridge which rubbed off on the included cleaning cloth.  I really had to rub it a lot to get all the stain out.  I think they also used a black stain on the fretboard but it doesn't seem to rub off.I won't hesitate to give it 5 stars because it is really beautiful, sounds good, and is a great value at $169."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very pleased with this guitar. Tone is nice. Action is low (a consistent height of strings above frets) which makes the pressing of nylon strings comfortable. The action is adjustable since it has a truss rod and included allen wrench but I don't need to adjust it.  Also included is a guitar case, clip-on tuner, capo, picks, extra strings, strap, cleaning cloth, and a footstool. I was surprised to see how accurate the tuner is and how easily I could tune the guitar. More surprisingly, I'm pleased with how long the guitar stays in tune. The tuner was not just for guitars. It is for chromatic, guitar, bass, violin, and Ukulele tuning. I’ve used it to tune my B-flat trumpet. Pitch range is listed at 440Hz.If you plan to hang the guitar on a wall, the width of the headstock above the nut is slightly over 2 in, so the first wall hanger I got did not fit. I ended up purchasing the Onmiwod black walnut wall hanger. It has a hook opening of 2.13 in. It works great and was easy to install."
    },
    {
        "review": "My grandson love it it came with a lot of accessories ❤️💜"
    },
    {
        "review": "I am going back to guitar after a long absence. This had twoo things you don't usually find in a less expensive guitar, a solid top and a bone nut and saddle. Both of these qualitied should improve the tone. It comes with a lot of extras including a nicely made soft case. One of the tuning peg handles was partially broken but that did not effect the guitar. I was surprised how the supplied strings maintained their tune after a couple of days.It's a nice looking guitar and seems very well made tho' I haven't heard of the brand before. I was disappointed that the punticello did not work on the 7th fret. Might be the strings. There is a slight buzz in the D string. I am frankly afraid to play around with the truss rod to adjust the action. Figured I would let the guitar \"break in.\" Also, I am trying to get back a familiarity with a guitar so I can discover what is me and what is the guitar.Kind of disturbed at the number of people who got the guitar for free. I had to order the guitar twice because the first time it never arrived. Overall, even with the price rise, I would say it's worth the money especially as a beginner guitar."
    },
    {
        "review": "Not bad, and or the money quite good.  It is a bit heavier than my more expensive Cordoba, and less resonance, but i got it for practice and the lower volume is just fine for that.  String height seems right at least for the first 5 frets, and the adjustment rod should take care of the rest.  Finish is very good.  Tuners hold well.  Comes w extra string set and tuner of unknown manufacturer, padded case and strap (for a classical guitar?) and footstool.  Recommended for a practice guitar."
    },
    {
        "review": "It will be a year next month, since I got this guitar. I average 30 minutes a day fingerstyle play. I was really pleased with the construction. The frets were well done. Only one was a little high. I did have to adjust the truss rod to eliminate some buzz on the 6th string at the 3rd fret. 3 strings required rewinding.I am really surprised how well it stays in tune. After the first week, the strings settled and has only required tuning after humidity changes in my area.The body of the guitar is well made and all the fit and finish is really quite beautiful. I work with wood as part of my trade, and an ill fitting piece stands out. This guitar is lovely and the tones are clear and strong. The sound comes out true and each open string pluck is it's own musical piece.I highly recommend this guitar!"
    },
    {
        "review": "I've owned several different classical guitars ranging from $150 \"cheapos\" up to mid-level student guitars ( Yairi CY118 and Takamini Hirade ). I can say this is a very nice guitar for what I paid for it! I've owned a couple of inexpensive Yamahas and I want to say my last model was a c40 if memory serves me and this is every bit as nice and honestly a little bit nicer than that one. Fit and finish are good, the frets are finished well with no sharp edges and it has a nice laquer job. It has a warm and balanced tone and while not the loudest guitar it's loud enough for practicing or playing in small groups if you have decent acoustics in the room. Actions nice and low over the first fret and a tad high over the 12th fret but interestingly enough its not difficult at all to play, the strings are very easy to push down and I'm not going to even tamper with it,  I usually shave down my saddles to lower the action but this one won't be needing it. I went ahead and ordered a set of diadario ej45s but honestly the strings that came with it sound pretty darn good so I'm not in a hurry to change them out. It also came with the bag tuner, footrest etc. normally I don't care about stuff like that but the accessories seem like decent quality and I will use them. Overall for the price you really can't beat this instrument and if you're trying to decide between something like this or one of the Yamahas for an inexpensive guitar or for a student don't be afraid to give this one a shot, I don't think you'll be disappointed. Hope this helps."
    },
    {
        "review": "Build quality is really good. The 2\"+ nut gives everyone space to place their fingers. My fingers are wide and old👴and it allows me more room to play . The action is good. The sound is solid for nylon strings. It is visually appealing as well! You won't be disappointed. The included gig bag is handy for travel and storage. Comes with a lot of extras for beginners."
    },
    {
        "review": "It is a good purchase, very well protected in its packaging, the accessories are very good and useful, the tuner could be improved, but it works"
    },
    {
        "review": "For a low-cost beginners guitar, the sound is quite good, the guitar is easy to play, with no obvious defects, it keeps its tuning well enough, and so far (after about 4 months) nothing has broken."
    },
    {
        "review": "I just wanted a practice guitar with a wider fretboard. I did not expect such a great guitar. The finish is amazing the accessories that it comes with are great, even the gig bag which is not cheap. I picked the cedar one because I wanted the particular sound and it sounds absolutely amazing. Now I know it's supposed to be a beginner guitar but I have 11 other guitars ranging from expensive to mid-range and this guitar surprises the hell out of me."
    },
    {
        "review": "Great guitar with lots of accessories. Arrives very well packaged. The bag is also very high quality. Perfect for starters"
    },
    {
        "review": "My guitar arrived a day earlier than expected and in perfect condition, it must be said that it was particularly well packaged, guitar and accessories in a suitable box, itself perfectly placed in another large box. I'm delighted, it's very pretty and sounds very good. It is a real pleasure and I am very satisfied with my purchase."
    }
]

[
    {
        "review": "better than I expected. Solid construction, easy to assemble.  Versatile.  The neck and bracket sit perfectly for a guitar or five string, but I hacked off about four inches so it would accomodate a Uke or a mini Banjo.  Works well.The instrument sits up straight, having a rubber guard  strap that will keep it from falling forward, but I cut about an inch off the rear leg so the instrument will lean a little far backwards.This product was well worth the price and quite functional"
    },
    {
        "review": "There is NOTHING about this stand that I dislike. I already plan to buy another one next month to put in my home studio. This one sits out by the amp and pedal board.So, obviously, the pictures show that I do not have a guitar on this particular guitar stand. That is the Korg RK100-S2 keytar. And it is a pain in the butt to place on a stand. It's odd shape makes it unstable, and the power on/off switch is right there between the two forks. Along with audio in jacks, the MIDI out, the USB port and a gain switch for a microphone.I have never heard of the CAHAYA music company before, and I wasn't sure if I wanted to get the stand for that reason alone. But, for the price, I figured that I could just leave it on the curb if it didn't work out. My main concern was needing the neck forks to be offset so much (as can be seen in the picture). I've had other stands (much more expensive) that had a kind of tongue in groove system that didn't let the neck rotate at all. That was my main concern. As you can see, no matter how screwy a position you need that neck fork to be, it can accommodate.I did have to make a slight alteration to the base forks. Again, see the picture. I used a couple of zip ties to pinch the ends of the base forks together approximately 1/2 inch. The pictures and dimensions provided by the company were at the top of the page were correct. What I didn't take into account was that my strap lock takes up about 1 inch of the bottom of the keytar. The instrument still rested easily on the base, but just barely. So I did this just to be safe.The rubber on the forks seems to be the standard stuff you will find on a lot of lower to mid-range stands. I definitely like the padding that you can find on the higher tier stands from Gator and On Stage better, but those stands come at a premium, and wouldn't have held this instrument well anyway. My concern is that this type of rubber gets tacky when it's hot, and that MIGHT damage the finish of a really nice instrument. Time will tell if it damages the finish of THIS really nice instrument, but it doesn't feel tacky at the moment, and the keytar is super stable when resting on it.Assembly was a breeze. I didn't even look at the instructions. Although, I did notice that there were both words and illustrations for anybody that needs a little help. The neck hinge is secured with a nut and bolt. And that is nice, because even some of the $80+ stands just have a squeeze kind of grip that is tight and stable, but also can be easily collapsed if hit at the right (or completely wrong) time!As for the base forks, they also secure with a nut and bolt, but there are also (2) two washers provided that allow you to tighten the base on really well, but still allows the forks to rotate into a self-leveling position when you place your instrument on it.Honestly, with the exception that I wish it had a better quality foam rubber padding, this might just be the most well engineered guitar stand that I have ever owned. It is certainly the most versatile."
    },
    {
        "review": "This review covers two CAHAYA products:- 5 in 1 Dual Use Sheet Music Stand- Guitar Stand Floor UniversalI bought them together, use them together, and have similar feedback on them, so am cross-posting this review for both products.I am an amateur, non-gigging player and have no need of (and cannot afford!) professional quality stands. However, I am tired of purchasing lower-end products that are poorly-designed and poorly manufactured using inferior materials.CAHAYA has hit the sweet spot offering decent quality stands at affordable prices.This music stand came as part of a kit including a desktop adaptor, travel bag, music clip, and sheet music folder. Personally I have little use for the folder, but it's a nice touch to include it. The desktop adaptor and clip work well when you need them. The bag is great and I use it whenever I need to quickly vacate the family room for visitors. It even accommodates my CAHAYA guitar stand. Everything (except the sheet music folder) breaks down quickly and goes into the bag in about a minute.The most important piece--the music stand itself--is well-designed and well-manufactured. Most important for me is that it easily accommodates thick three-ring binders full of music. It adjusts to any height I need, whether sitting or standing.For daily, same-location amateur use, this music stand kit is a bargain. If you expect to break it down, pack it up, throw it in a vehicle and reassemble it several times a week, you should probably go more high-end. (But if you just don't have the budget to do so, this kit can probably survive some length of pro service as long as you don't abuse it )The CAHAYA Universal Floor Guitar Stand is similar to the music stand in terms of materials, reliability, and value: an absolute bargain for a single-location amateur. If you expect to set it up in different venues every week where it might get knocked over by a drummer stumbling toward his or her kit or by a drunk audience member, you should probably go more high-end. But this stand is light years better than some of the products that are just $10 cheaper.Some people complain that it holds the guitar too high, making it too unsteady. I was a little concerned about this at first, but I use it for a relatively heavy bass, and have never had a problem. In fact, the cradle appears to be high to accommodate the adjustable tripod legs, so it was designed this way for a reason.My only minor complaint is that you need to completely remove bolts, knobs, nuts, and washers when fully collapsing the stand. If you expect to be doing this a lot, there's a good chance you're going to drop and lose a piece at some point. Otherwise, this stand is a great solution for anybody on a budget that won't be abusing their equipment."
    },
    {
        "review": "Got it fast, cheap and it works.  Can't say it's the sturdiest stand, and be careful of the plastic and don't over tighten.  Would I buy it again?  Probably not, I'd try something else, but in this price range, they're probably all of similar quality."
    },
    {
        "review": "Excellent guitar stand for either acoustic or electric. It gets the guitar off the ground and is adjustable."
    },
    {
        "review": "Stand works great, well made and holds my bass securely.  Has padding in the correct places to protect the instrument, and all for a great price."
    },
    {
        "review": "Very stable and light perfect"
    },
    {
        "review": "Purchased this for an electric Fender Loog which has a thin body, very narrow neck and short scale. The cradle on most \"standard\" guitar stands and foldable A-frame type stands don't seem hold, support or keep small and odd shaped body guitars balanced reliably balanced.  If you've ever put put a guitar on a stand and had it tilt sideways because of the cradle or not enough support from behind then you know what I'm talking about.The CAHAYA stand is very well made, has great padding, is very stable and would be hard to knock over. The added safety buckle is a nice touch. This stand does a great job of securely holding my electric Fender Loog in place (especially after I pushed the cradle arms a little closer together. If you're a gigging musician, this would be a decent stand to have; it's portable enough and not overly heavy. For home, studio or school use, it's great! For the money (I paid $18.00), I definitely recommend this stand over the other options I've seen online and purchased in the past."
    },
    {
        "review": "Bought for my grandsons new guitar. He loved it"
    },
    {
        "review": "Good quality, performs its function well"
    },
    {
        "review": "Product with a very good price, it has the defect that the arms where the guitar rests move in position as well as where it rests on the arm. Although it is the only detail that is solved by being careful when placing the guitar, the product feels solid and the price is affordable."
    }
